Two years after the documentary novel *The Binh Xuyen Man* came out in 1985, I got my hands on *Bay Vien, Le Maitre de Cholon* by Pierre Darcourt. Hachette published it back in '77. It's one of those rare books in the Collection Les Grands Aventuriers series, profiling international outlaw adventurers — Mafia bosses, Italian Black Hand guys, German Nazi kingpins, Mexican drug cartel chiefs.

Thanks to that book, Bay Vien became a household name all over France: a gangster who rose to become a general for the French, bold enough to go toe-to-toe with the tyrant Ngo Dinh Diem, and when he lost, the French whisked him out of the Forest of Sac special zone and brought him to France to live out his days — same as they did for most of their "loyal friends of France."

Pierre Darcourt was a French journalist who'd spent decades holed up at the Continental Hotel — basically a clubhouse for international war correspondents covering the French-Vietnam and Vietnam-American wars. So he really knew his Vietnamese figures. Plus he had one big advantage that Vietnamese journalists and writers didn't: the French Second Bureau and the Secret Police fed him files on whoever he wanted to write about.

The author of *The Binh Xuyen Man* wanted to redo Bay Vien's story once he gathered more of those rare documents the French Deuxième Bureau and Sûreté had released. That's the French side of things — their interest in Bay Vien. What about the Americans?

Just recently, an American tech specialist named Randy Liebermann from Falls Church, Virginia tracked down the author of *The Binh Xuyen Man*. He's planning to shoot a short film about a relative of his — a photojournalist who got killed in the firefight between Bay Vien's Binh Xuyen and Diem's army over the skies of Chanh Hung on April 30, 1955. That photographer was working for an American newspaper and went up in a small American plane that day. Both the pilot and the photographer caught Binh Xuyen bullets just minutes after flying over the Y-Bridge.

Liebermann bought a copy of *The Binh Xuyen Man* in the States, had it translated, and wrote a screenplay. He needed to meet the author to nail down some details for the film, especially about Bay Vien — a general who started out as a bandit. He flew to Hanoi, stayed seven days, and someone told him the author lived in Ho Chi Minh City. So he flew south. Careful guy — he went to Vietnam Tourism and hired an interpreter named Truong Quang Cuong to help find the author's address. When they met, he laid out his plan and asked the author to help review the script and the shoot, scheduled for late 1998.

Those two events are why we decided to write Bay Vien's life story one more time — this time as a documentary narrative, not a novel.

Truth is, in the Binh Xuyen gangster hierarchy, Bay Vien ranked third — behind Brother Ba Duong (Duong Van Duong) and Muoi Tri (Huynh Van Tro).

Ba Duong was the one who raised the flag of patriotism highest. He pulled together a good ten thousand Binh Xuyen fighters across seven battalions — 2nd, 3rd, 4th, 7th, 9th, 21st, and 25th — known as the Binh Xuyen Interzone. Ba Duong led the Binh Xuyen across the Soai Rap River from the Forest of Sac into Ben Tre to save the An Hoa-Giao Hoa front in early '46. But he died too soon — a Spitfire shot him dead in Chau Minh commune on the sixth day of Tet, Year of the Dog 1946. They named the old Lagrange Canal after him — that's in Moc Hoa, Tan An. The government posthumously awarded him the title of Brigadier General, the first martyr from the Southern region.

The number two Binh Xuyen outlaw was Muoi Tri — Huynh Van Tro, Bay Vien's blood brother in some legendary heists. Like the raid on the Binh Trieu lumberyard, where they rolled up in a brand-new sedan borrowed from Chinese millionaire Tran Tang, using pistols borrowed from "Cau" Nguyen H. Nhon. We called him Cau because Nhon was a big landowner's son, educated in France — nobody knew what degree he had, you'd just see him hanging around doing nothing, carrying an air rifle for hunting in the evenings like those rich plantation owners back in the West.

Right as they were pulling out, they ran into some Frenchman's car coming back from hunting in Bien Hoa. Bay Vien was driving and wouldn't let the Frenchman pass. They raced at full speed, and when Bay Vien took a sharp turn at Cay Thi crossroads, the car dropped into a ditch. That's when the brains of the operation, Muoi Tri, pitched in: "Leave the car there, grab the money, run home, then borrow a winch to pull the car out, fix it up real nice and return it to Tran Tang." Too bad Bay Vien and Muoi Tri were slower on the draw than the police inspector at Ba Chieu. So they both went to court and got twelve years of hard labor, shipped off to Con Dao. Muoi Tri was a cut above Bay Vien in brains and character, which is why he stuck with the revolution to the end, regrouped up North, got his education through seventh grade — equivalent to the old Saigon Diplôme before 1945. He became a Colonel, Deputy Director of the Military Training Department, and was recognized as a Southern Intellectual.

Still, in novels, cai luong plays, and movies, the bad guys always steal the show. So we're writing about Bay Vien first, then weaving in the straight-up heroes: Battalion Commander Nam Ha (Ba Duong's half-brother on his father's side), Ong Tam Manh (Nguyen Van Manh) — a martial arts master from Chanh Hung and the Cay Kho Canal area — and his son-in-law Hai Vinh, who took over his father-in-law's command of Battalion 7. Hai Vinh pulled off big feats, like capturing Ba Nho with his bare hands — Ba Nho was a warlord with close Binh Xuyen ties, sentenced to death by Lieutenant General Nguyen Binh at Long Thành in mid-'47 to set an example of revolutionary military discipline. And of course, we won't leave out the two big brothers — Ba Duong and Muoi Tri.


---


The Secret Police files still had Bay Vien's record clear as day: a repeat offender, in and out of jail more times than anybody could count. His real name was Le Van Vien, born 1904 in Phong Duoc, Cho Lon province — that's District 8 of Ho Chi Minh City now. His father was Le Van Dau, half-Chinese from the Teochew community. So Bay Vien had Minh Huong blood — Chinese father, Vietnamese mother.

He was born in the Year of the Dragon, 1904 — the year of the worst flood in the history of the six Southern provinces, which the Mongolian Min Dam newspaper covered in an article back then. That terrible flood year somehow brought into this world one of the most infamous outlaws ever to shake up the rich folks, the military, and the French colonial cops. But Bay Vien's early years were marked by a string of unglamorous arrests and convictions.

His first time in jail was February 14, 1921 — he was seventeen. Reason: stealing a bicycle. Sentence: twenty days. Then the second, May 31, 1927: two months for assault. Third, August 28, 1936: twelve years — that was his first armed robbery.

After escaping from Con Dao, Bay Vien got more professional about his robberies. He finished village school, then hit the streets, learned martial arts, got his body tattooed: a dragon across his back, the dragon's head on his neck, its tail running all the way down to his backside. Both shoulders were tattooed with naked women, charms, and a snakehead on the tip of his dick. Physically, Bay Vien was big — one meter seventy tall. His father Le Van Dau had been in the Heaven and Earth Society, the Yihetuan movement — full of "Support the Ming, Destroy the Qing" spirit. So Bay Vien had that gangster blood in him from childhood.

Back when he was working as a bouncer at a gambling den in Cho Lon, one day the boss chewed him out and Bay Vien beat him half to death. Court gave him two months. Every time he got out of jail, he moved up a rank in the underworld. He put together his own crew, claimed the turf around Binh Dong Market, protected the cockfighting arenas. When his power grew, he took over the bus station on Schreiner Street — that's the one alongside Ben Thanh Market, now Phan Chau Trinh Street.

In a jewelry store heist at Gong Ong To, he scored six thousand piasters — back then one piaster could buy you five bushels of rice. But they nabbed him at the Louvain streetcar station, near the Nguyen Van Hao theater on Tran Hung Dao Boulevard. On August 28, 1936, the criminal court sentenced him to twelve years of hard labor, to be deported to Con Lon Island.

In 1936, Con Lon was under the rule of the French island warden Bouvier. Bouvier ran the prison island for two terms — first from 1927 to 1931 (four years) and again from 1935 to 1942 (six years). Bouvier was fat, round as a ball, couldn't handle the tropical heat at all. He spent all day in his warden's office wearing striped pants, shirtless, his body dusted with baby powder to stop the heat rash. He left all the prison management to the guards. Back then they had this practice of using Cambodian prisoners to rule over the Vietnamese ones.

The most notorious thug was the "striped tiger" of Cellblock 5 — a guy named Khăm Chay, a mountain bandit from the Ta Lon range on the Vietnam-Cambodia border. This guy was a top-notch fighter, had that Tra Kha iron-body technique, and all kinds of magical charms.

When they threw Bay Vien into Cellblock 5, the guards wanted Khăm Chay to do their dirty work for them — wipe out the famous Binh Xuyen gangster from Saigon-Cho Lon.

But let's follow Bay Vien's journey from Saigon's Grand Prison to the hell of Con Lon.

The French kept things secret until the last minute — they only told the prisoners at 5 AM on Day X to get on the windowless truck from the Grand Prison down to the Saigon pier next to the Ba Son shipyard. There, the inmates were handcuffed in pairs and herded onto the boat. All prisoners stayed in the hold. Only the guards and crew were on deck. If a storm hit and the ship went down, the prisoners would die first — trapped in the hold with their hands cuffed. From that very first moment, Bay Vien was furious and swore he'd escape by any means, no matter the cost.

The ship reached Con Dao, but big vessels couldn't dock — prisoners had to transfer to small barges. From up high, they had to climb down a vertical rope ladder. Hard enough going alone, but they were cuffed in pairs, both climbing at the same time — like a circus act. Bay Vien watched with his own eyes as old prisoners missed their footing, fell into the sea, and became shark bait.

Malabar Indian guards herded the prisoners onto canoes that took them to the pier. From the big ship to the dock was five hundred meters. Onshore, guards and their families gathered to watch the new arrivals like it was some big entertainment event on the island. Political prisoners lined up on the left — the guards marched them to bagne 2, which was what the French called the camp. Common criminals lined up on the right — they went to bagne 1.

Bay Vien kept his cool, taking in everything on the island that he'd heard about from old Con Dao hands. He noticed that the road along the shore was called Quai Andouard — Andouard Wharf. A few days later, Bay Vien learned that Andouard was the most brutal warden ever, running the island for two years from 1917 to 1919, until a prisoner named Tu Nho killed him on December 3, 1919. When Bay Vien heard that, it gave him a real lift. Ah — so even in prison there were men bold enough to die for a righteous cause. The story of Tu Nho sacrificing himself to take out the warden Andouard kept Bay Vien's spirit strong through the brutal trials in that earthly hell.

Back then there was something strange: the guards let prisoners bring tobacco and lighters into the cells, but absolutely no knives, razor blades, or nails. Every time they came in, the prisoners had to strip naked, spread their arms and legs so the guards could search for hidden opium. Later on, the prisoners called this the "phoenix dance." Since Bay Vien was tattooed all over, when he stood naked the whole cellblock stared. The head guard Santini and the half-French guard Bonifacy got a kick out of it, always chatting Bay Vien up.

Bay Vien arrived on the island in 1936 — the year the Popular Front launched its movement to expand democracy and freedom in the colonies. Prison conditions improved too. At Con Dao, the prisoners got better at looking out for each other, mainly led by the example of the political prisoners showing the common criminals how it's done. The total prisoner count when Bay Vien was there was 7,863.

A few months after Bay Vien got to the island, a big stroke of luck came his way.

The passenger ship *Gougal Pasquier*, running the Saigon-Singapore route, stopped at Con Lon for a few hours. On board was a lawyer named Kim. Bay Vien's wife's family — his father-in-law was millionaire Huynh Dai, who started out as a scrap dealer like Chu Hao and struck it rich — had sent, through Lawyer Kim, a thousand piasters for Bay Vien: ten hundred-piaster bills. These ten bills were rolled up real tight and stuffed inside a hollow ivory chopstick. Bay Vien could hide it in his asshole. Whenever he needed money, he'd go into the latrine, pull it out, take one bill, and that would last him months. With that secret stash, Bay Vien was extra careful in how he carried himself.

Bay Vien didn't have much schooling — just village school — but he learned plenty from life. He knew the saying "Money that goes ahead is smart money," so he'd cleverly break big bills into small ones for easier spending. Whenever a guard needed cash for gambling, Bay Vien would gladly lend it, then act like he forgot — never asked for it back, building up goodwill.

Life was going smooth — until suddenly they transferred Bay Vien to Cellblock 5, where the striped tiger Khăm Chay was king. His friends, even the guards, were worried for him. One guard tipped him off: "When you go over there, keep your shirt on, even in the heat. Don't let Khăm Chay see that dragon tattoo on your back. He'll kill you for sure."

Bay Vien's blood ran cold. How in the hell was he supposed to go mano a mano with that striped tiger of Cellblock 5?


---


Right from his first day in Cellblock 5 — they also called it Ward 5 — Bay Vien was quietly sizing up the striped tiger Khăm Chay. The guard's warning was dead on: this guy was nothing to mess with. He stood over one meter seventy, built like a brick shithouse, his body covered in Cambodian and Indian charm tattoos that zigzagged like a centipede. He was sprawled out on a tall stack of mats, with two underlings attending him — one giving him a massage, one fanning him.

The minute he spotted Bay Vien coming in, he raised his hand and crooked a finger, calling him over to report. Bay Vien remembered the guard's advice: don't let Khăm Chay see that dragon tattoo on your back. If he did, he'd put you in the ground for sure — he'd know Bay Vien was a notorious gangster and a serious rival. So even though the cellblock was stifling hot, Bay Vien kept his shirt on. He watched Khăm Chay sitting there bare-chested in nothing but shorts, getting fanned by his flunky, and it made his blood boil — but he kept his voice humble, like a man with no cards to play.

"I'm new to this cellblock, just wanted to offer the big brother a little gift."

As he spoke, Bay Vien pulled a silk sleeveless undershirt out of his bag — a "Deer Head" brand from Shanghai — and held it up with both hands in a respectful offering.

Khăm Chay's eyes lit up. He nodded.

"You're smart, boy. I love a good Shanghai silk sleeveless undershirt. In this heat, it wears cool as a breeze."

He snatched the shirt out of Bay Vien's hands, sat up, and put it on. It fit perfectly, showing off his powerful upper body like a weightlifter's.

Thanks to greasing the guards' palms, Bay Vien got assigned to cleaning duty at Camp I's kitchen.

It was there that Bay Vien met some big-name characters, the top one being Thomas Phuoc.

This prisoner was a damn good-looking man. A "cậu" type from My Tho — rich kid who had his Brevet diploma but couldn't be bothered working as some desk jockey. He preferred the outlaw life. In 1922, Thomas Phuoc robbed the Saigon-My Tho train. But his biggest score was the Grand Magasin Charner in Saigon — at the corner of Charner Boulevard (now Nguyen Hue) and Bonard (now Le Loi), right across from the Rex Hotel. The Charner store was massive, three stories, each floor divided into sections: silk section, leather goods, jewelry, watches, fountain pens, the works. The most valuable was the high-end watch section — Omega, Rolex, some encrusted with diamonds for the贵族 and new-rich wannabes. The owner put a beautiful Frenchwoman in charge of that section. Thomas Phuoc played the part of a dandy who loved dressing up, hanging around every day checking out the latest Paris fashions — shirts, cufflinks, Italian shoes, Swiss watches.

After casing the place for a while, Thomas Phuoc made off with several hundred premium watches from the top floor of the Charner building.

It was a clean heist, drove the cops crazy. But the lid was tight and the nail was sharp. France sent over a private detective to back up the French police inspectors in Saigon. This detective went old-school — start with the women, find the leads. He tracked down the night girls at the store and learned about a guy who'd just gotten friendly with a beautiful young woman. Traced it back — it was Thomas Phuoc's sister. Phuoc had used the honey trap, sending his own sister to seduce the night watchman right when Phuoc was pulling the job.

In court, Phuoc didn't hire a lawyer. He defended himself:

"I admit my guilt. I had a gambling addiction. To pull off that watch heist, I stooped to involving my own sister in a life of crime. I'm not asking for leniency for myself — only for my sister. She has her Baccalaureate, she was a student at the Couvent des Oiseaux..."

Thomas Phuoc got straight to the point:

"So, boy, what do you know about that striped tiger in Ward 5?"

Bay Vien nodded. "I've known a guy named Dao for over ten years. He's in for murder, got life in 1931. Dao told me Khăm Chay is the most vicious bastard he's ever seen. He steals money from everybody in the cellblock. When guys run out of cash, he pries their gold teeth out..."

Thomas Phuoc nodded. "What else?"

Bay Vien kept going. "Ward 5 has a gambling den run by Khăm Chay. He takes thirty percent of every pot. Every month he pulls in what, three hundred piasters easy — same as a French warden's salary."

Thomas Phuoc laughed. "We already knew that. What else did Dao tell you?"

Bay Vien chuckled. "There's one more thing. Even in prison, you can get laid — rare as hen's teeth. The Malabar guards' wives will 'parachute' for fifty cents. Top-shelf goes for one piaster. Dao says Guard 76's wife is a real beauty, only twenty-two. Price is one piaster."

There was a long pause. Then Thomas Phuoc's voice turned dead serious:

"That Dao is worthless. Khăm Chay humiliated him and he didn't fight back. Don't bother with him."

Bay Vien asked, "So what's your advice for dealing with the striped tiger Khăm Chay?"

Thomas Phuoc thought for a minute. "Taking down Khăm Chay takes strategy. First, you gotta be smart about building alliances with some solid prisoners who can handle Khăm Chay's flunkies. Let them go head-to-head, while you go mano a mano with Khăm Chay himself. I don't know which of you is tougher, but if you want to make sure you win, you strike first. The martial arts manuals say 'attack when least expected.' That way you seize the upper hand..."

Bay Vien nodded eagerly. "I know the drill. Strike first, strike hard. That's my specialty."

Thomas Phuoc handed Bay Vien a little packet. "You might need this, in case you can't take him."

Bay Vien bristled. "How do you know I can't take Khăm Chay? What's in this?" He opened it to look. "Ah — salt and pepper. Alright, I'll take it. I'll keep it on me, in case things go sideways."


---


Bay Vien walked back into Cellblock 5 with a lighter step than before.

Thomas Phuoc had taught him lessons worth their weight in gold. Sure, the underworld knew all these tricks already — but what made this teacher special, stranded on this faraway island, was that he laid it out at exactly the right time and pointed him at exactly the right target.

Bay Vien's mortal enemy was Khăm Chay. To beat him, he had to know his man and know himself.

Bay Vien kept his shirt on every time he came into the cellblock. No way was he showing Khăm Chay that dragon inked across his back. Walking past the striped tiger's mat, he saw Khăm Chay wearing that brand-new silk undershirt — *his* shirt. He swallowed hard and choked down the rage.

Bay Vien went to his own mat, off in a dark corner. Lying there, he turned things over and over in his mind: Where was Khăm Chay strong? Where was he weak? After a night of watching, Bay Vien saw it plain as day. Cellblock 5 held over a hundred and twenty common criminals. The guards turned a blind eye to gambling — it was practically out in the open. Anybody with money could try their luck, and most of the inmates had that gambling itch. Funniest part? Even the guards got in on it — sometimes they'd lose their whole month's pay.

One day, Guard 76 came by flat broke, asking to borrow from Bay Vien. Bay Vien didn't miss the opportunity. He slipped him a five-piaster note, and the next day, Guard 76 spilled the beans: the gamblers in Ward 5 didn't like Khăm Chay because he was riding the coattails of Deputy Warden Jean Jacques to run the place. Warden Bouvier couldn't stand the tropical heat — he just sat in his office with the fan going full blast, always an iced coffee on his desk, leaving everything to Deputy Warden Jean Jacques. And this guy used Cambodian prisoners to keep the Vietnamese in line.

The most dangerous of all was the Recherche unit — all Cambodian soldiers. These boys specialized in climbing mountains, crossing streams — they knew every spot where prisoners cut lianas to build rafts, every beach where prisoners carried their rafts down to the sea. Most escape attempts got caught by these guys.

As for Khăm Chay himself, he sat back directing his thugs to collect protection money from the gambling tables — a full third of every win. Counting it up, Khăm Chay pulled in three hundred piasters a month — the same salary as Warden Bouvier himself.

Bay Vien smiled inside. *There* was Khăm Chay's weak spot. The guy was like a giant python lying in one spot, swallowing prey two or three times his size. Choking was inevitable. Bay Vien would strike when the python was choking — that's how you win.

The next day, Bay Vien went out again with a guard to clean the kitchen. He told Thomas Phuoc about his discovery.

Thomas Phuoc grinned. "I've got good news for you too. Head guard Santini doesn't like Khăm Chay — he wants you to take him out. So now you've got the green light. The guards will look the other way while you boys settle your score."

Bay Vien made his decision on the spot: tonight. If he waited, Khăm Chay might catch on and get ready.

That night, Bay Vien stripped off his shirt and went to shower — deliberately letting Khăm Chay see that fiery dragon emblazoned across his broad back, tough as a butcher's block.

It was a provocation, pure and simple. Khăm Chay had been watching Bay Vien for a while — this guy who always kept his shirt on like a TB patient scared of a draft, now suddenly stripping down like he was daring the world. And this wasn't just some punk — the dragon covered his whole back, blazing red. In that instant, Khăm Chay shoved himself up on his elbows and crooked his finger, calling Bay Vien over.

Bay Vien saw clearly how cocky Khăm Chay was being, but he pretended not to notice.

Khăm Chay lost his temper and ordered his flunky to go tell Bay Vien to "present himself" to the striped tiger.

Bay Vien had been planning this showdown all day. The packet of salt and pepper was stashed under his mat — but he decided not to use it. What would people think of Bay Vien if they heard he'd used a cheap trick to bring down the striped tiger Khăm Chay? That pepper-and-salt packet would cheapen his victory.

Seeing Bay Vien drag his feet, the flunky sneered. "Scared, huh? If you know what's good for you, make Khăm Chay one promise — you'll get rid of that fire dragon on your back."

Bay Vien laughed. "Maybe I should do just that."

Then he took his time walking over to "present himself" to Khăm Chay. He put on a humble act.

"What does the boss want with me?"

Khăm Chay spoke with contempt: "Turn around. Let me see that fire dragon of yours."

Bay Vien let out a big laugh, his voice full of swagger:

"You're supposed to be a gangster too — so why are you asking me to turn my back so you can make the first move? Here's my fire dragon — take a good look!"

And with that, Bay Vien drove a kick straight into Khăm Chay's face like a thunderbolt from heaven. The kick came so fast — Khăm Chay was sitting on his stack of mats, couldn't get his hands up in time. It caught him square on the temple. And lucky for Bay Vien — that was an old wound from a gang war in Can Tho not long before. The injury had healed, but not enough to take a thousand-pound kick from Bay Vien. Blood burst out like a faucet, streaming into his eyes. Khăm Chay clutched his face with both hands and crumpled to the ground.

He'd taken down his most dangerous enemy — too easy, too fast. Just one kick, and it was over. Bay Vien was completely stunned, and more convinced than ever in his "Year of the Dragon" destiny. He couldn't help humming under his breath:

*"The Dragon of the Dragon Year dwells in the Dragon Palace, / Flying back and forth, hiding himself in the clouds."*

Hiding in the clouds — then who the hell could touch him?

The minute Khăm Chay was down, they crowned Bay Vien the new striped tiger of Ward 5. Most of the prisoners still thought like feudal subjects: "A day without a king, and the world turns upside down."

A nation needs its king. A prison cellblock needs its striped tiger.


---


"Hello, Sister Chau."

Bay Vien only managed those three words, then stood frozen for a long time. He was completely unprepared for a woman this beautiful — beyond anything he could've imagined.

"Hello, Brother Bay. Please come in."

Sister Chau closed the door carefully and led him to the sitting area in the middle of the house. She poured hot tea and handed it to him, smiling.

"It seems like Brother Bay has something on his mind..."

Finally Bay Vien spoke his thoughts: "Forgive me, Sister Chau, for staring at you so rudely just now. The truth is, I never imagined that on this godforsaken island there could be someone as lovely as you. And especially that such a beauty would be the wife of Guard 76 — a man I've run into a few times in Cellblock 5."

Sister Chau laughed. "I bet you never thought a guard's wife would be sneaking around receiving visitors like this, did you?"

Then her voice dropped, heavy and sad. "It's a shame we've only just met and I'm already unloading my troubles. But since you know my husband, you must understand something of the situation..."

Bay Vien nodded. "I know. Guard 76 has gambling fever. He's always broke and up to his ears in debt. I've even lent him money myself to try to get back on his feet."

Chau shook her head. "From now on, please don't lend him anything. That money's as good as thrown to the ghosts."

Then she changed the subject. "But let's talk about tonight. Brother Dao passed me your calling card as a gift. I don't know you yet, but I'm touched by your gentlemanly gesture. I'm ready to be your wife for the whole night."

She handed Bay Vien a towel and pointed toward the bathroom. "Take a shower, it'll do you good. There's Co Ba soap here."

Bay Vien took off his white ba ba shirt, folded it neatly, and put it on the table. "You're so thoughtful, Sister Chau. A woman with both beauty and virtue like you, stuck with someone like Guard 76 — forgive me for saying it — it's like a jasmine flower planted in a mud pit..."

Chau was getting the bed ready and was about to take off her shirt when Bay Vien stopped her.

"That won't be necessary. To be honest, when I came here, I had that on my mind. But now that I've met you, let's set that aside. I want us to be true friends for the long haul — not just a 'pay-for-play' transaction."

Chau looked a little surprised and buttoned her shirt back up. "But Brother Bay, we've only just met. You don't know anything about my character. I've got the looks on the outside, but inside I have plenty of bad habits — and top of the list, I've got the gambling bug almost as bad as Guard 76."

Bay Vien took her hand, looked at the lines in her palm, and nodded. "You really do have the fate of a beautiful woman marked by hardship — a life full of troubles, just like me. That means our two lives coming together makes a lot of sense..."

Chau looked uneasy. "I'm ready to be your friend, Brother Bay. But I can't just take your gift and spend the whole night just talking, can I?"

Bay Vien put his arm around her waist and laughed. "You're too practical, aren't you? You want everything fair and square, huh? Well, that's easy. The night has five watches — plenty of time for everything. But I want to hear you tell me stories of this island, to soothe a banished man's soul. That need is just as important as the other one. Let's lie down together, and you tell me about life on this island..."

Chau giggled. "I've never met a guest as strange as you, Brother Bay. I thought you were lacking one thing, but it turns out you need something else. But I'll humor you. What should I tell you about?"

"Tell me about the prisoners who came before. How did they live? Did they just take it lying down, or did they fight back against the guards? Tell me whatever you know. I need to hear it so I can figure out how to get through this place in one piece."

Chau thought for a bit, then began:

"There have been a lot of escape attempts on this island, Brother. Every warden's term had at least one. Let me tell you about Tam Te Thien and his wife. This happened about ten years ago. There was this prisoner nicknamed Tam — but he called himself Tam Te Thien. People said 'Te' means 'equal' and 'Thien' means 'Heaven.' So Brother Tam thought he was as talented as Heaven itself. And he really did have talent. He'd been on the island three years, and he built a raft to escape every single year. But skill can't beat fate — sometimes he'd get spotted in the mountains, sometimes a patrol boat would chase him down and catch him."

"Did he ever make it out?"

"He did, but he needed his wife's help."

Bay Vien sat up. "His wife's help? That's quite a story! Tell me about it."

Chau laughed delightedly. "People say Tam Te Thien's wife was as beautiful as I am — but when it came to real ability, she was a whole different world ahead of me. When she heard her husband had tried to escape three times and never made it back to the mainland, she stepped in to save him. She started hanging around the Grand Prison and the police stations, getting friendly with the guards who rotated back to Saigon for leave from the island. She was gorgeous and fearless — she started an affair with a Corsican cop named Moritz. His wife had died, so he was crazy about her. When his leave ran out, he begged her to come to the island with him. She acted reluctant, but the truth was she'd set the trap perfectly.

Once on the island, Sister Tam secretly gathered information, found out where her husband was being held. She watched carefully, bribed a few of the prison guards — gave them money, asked them to smuggle in essentials: Nhị Thiên Đường oil, medicated balm, salt and pepper, cold medicine, cash. For the first time, she exchanged letters with her husband, telling him that when the monsoon winds came, the two of them would escape and be home for Tet.

On the appointed day, the couple set off on a raft with three other prisoners, riding the waves out to sea. One single night, carried by the monsoon winds, the raft drifted all the way to Ganh Hao, near the Ca Mau cape."

Bay Vien nodded slowly. "A woman like that — she's one in a million."

Chau continued, "But here's the real kicker. Before she followed her husband back to the mainland, Sister Tam Te Thien wrote a farewell poem to her 'temporary husband.' A letter that was also a poem. It went like this:

**Farewell — to Mr. Moritz**

*I gave my body to be your fake wife*
*Only because circumstances gave me this chance*
*Now we part, east and west*
*You keep your fate, I return to my true husband*
*Don't stand at the door waiting and watching*
*Let the boat return to its old dock, fulfilling its eternal duty*
*One day together still counts as a bond of blanket and mat*
*But this silk thread of fate only stretches this far*
*Farewell, I leave you these few words*
*Find yourself another — I'm going with my husband.*



**— Into Independence Palace —**

Diem once told Lansdale: "It's the Binh Xuyen, with Bay Vien at their head, that I worry about most."

Lansdale smiled confidently. "I've already put the negotiations in General O'Daniel's hands. Not sure how he's progressing. How about we invite Bay Vien to the Palace and test the waters?"

Ngo Dinh Diem took power in Saigon on July 7, 1954 — right when Vietnam was at its most volatile. The French had just fallen at Dien Bien Phu and the Geneva Conference on Vietnam was underway in Switzerland.

Diem lamented: "I returned to the country at a time when Vietnam was like France in the age of Joan of Arc."

But Diem was a shrewd politician.

On the Geneva Conference, Diem foresaw that Vietnam would be divided for a long time. So he ordered his Foreign Minister, Dr. Tran Van Do — head of the "State of Vietnam" negotiating team — to make a final declaration: "The State of Vietnam reserves for itself the freedom to act in order to defend the sacred rights of the Vietnamese people: territorial unity, national independence, and liberty."

Once he was in the Prime Minister's seat, Diem pushed aside those loyal to the French and brought in his own people. From the start, he faced heavy opposition from the religious sects. Their main muscle was the French-backed army led by General Nguyen Van Hinh — son of former Prime Minister Nguyen Van Tam, both father and son pro-French. On Lansdale's advice, Diem ordered General Hinh to France. Hinh not only refused but started planning a coup. Lansdale saved Diem's skin by cabling President Magsaysay to invite Hinh's staff officers on a week-long junket to the Philippines. That killed the coup. It was mid-September 1955.

After that, Lansdale moved to buy off the other generals — Trinh Minh The, Nguyen Thanh Phuong, and even General Tran Van Soai (Nam Lua).

But Bay Vien was harder to buy because he was tight with Chief of State Bao Dai. American journalist Robert Shaplen swore Bao Dai had sold the exclusive exploitation rights to the Grand Monde, Kim Chung, and Binh Khang casinos for a paltry forty million piasters — barely eighty days' revenue from the Grand Monde alone.

Since Bay Vien was such a tough nut, Diem played a smarter game: he invited the Cao Dai to send two representatives into the government. The Hoa Hao also got two seats in Prime Minister Ngo Dinh Diem's cabinet. But these four ministerial posts were figureheads only — all real power stayed in the Ngo family. Still, by showing favor to the Cao Dai and Hoa Hao, Diem wanted everyone to see that the Ngo family had isolated Bay Vien's Binh Xuyen.

Taking Lansdale's advice, at the end of December 1954 Diem reluctantly invited Bay Vien to Independence Palace. The Binh Xuyen command worried it was a trap Diem had set for their leader, but Bay Vien was determined to go.

"Turning down an invitation makes us look scared. Besides, behind the Binh Xuyen stands General Paul Ely — Commander-in-Chief and High Commissioner for France in Indochina."

So Bay Vien got in the car and headed to Independence Palace. Nam Tai immediately called Lieutenant Colonel Savani to alert General Ely to intervene if Diem tried to kidnap Bay Vien and hold him inside.

When Bay Vien arrived, the hostile atmosphere was unmistakable. The Prime Minister's security officers were armed to the teeth, glaring at the Binh Xuyen leader like they wanted to tear his throat out.

Bay Vien smiled arrogantly, the look on his face saying, "I walked in here, so I treat you like grass."

When he met Diem, Bay Vien said, "The Prime Minister summoned me — it must be something important."

Diem: "The Geneva Accords have split the country in two. The North has fallen to the Communists. We must not let them swallow the South too. To defeat Communism, we need unity among the religious sects. The Cao Dai and Hoa Hao have already agreed to bring their forces into the Army of the Republic of Vietnam. What about the Binh Xuyen?"

Bay Vien replied calmly: "Fighting for our country's independence has long been the goal of the Binh Xuyen army. We've fought the Viet Minh the same as we've fought the French. We have our own way of fighting — suited to the times, the terrain, and the people. If the Prime Minister wants to merge us into the Republican army, I'm afraid the Binh Xuyen troops won't fight as effectively as before. You'd be better off doing what the French did."

Diem was no diplomat, so Bay Vien's rambling answer irritated him. The meeting ended quickly — nothing came of it. In less than thirty minutes, Bay Vien walked out of Independence Palace.

Not ready to give up, twenty days later Diem sent General O'Daniel to Bay Vien's headquarters to try again. But the situation had changed. The National Unity Front had turned up the pressure — all four Cao Dai and Hoa Hao ministers had resigned, demanding Diem change a cabinet that was too heavy with family members. Saigon's newspapers called it an ultimatum: the sects were giving Diem five days to overhaul the administration.

Ngo Dinh Nhu immediately organized the National Revolutionary Civil Servants movement to back the government, while lobbying the Americans for more aid money.

At the start of 1955, Diem struck a mortal blow at the Binh Xuyen: he ordered the Grand Monde entertainment complex shut down. That bottomless cash cow of Bay Vien's — and Bao Dai's — was suddenly cut off.

Bay Vien immediately flew to France to meet Bao Dai and plan a counterattack.


---


# Escape Plot

Escape Plot
After that night he cleaned out the mã tà 76 in cards, Bảy Viễn grinned to himself and muttered:
"Luck beats skill any day."

Bảy Viễn figured the wives and girlfriends of the prison guards were the key if they'd help a prisoner break out of the cage.

And he immediately thought about getting close to Châu, slowly turning her into a useful accomplice for his escape.

Bảy Viễn tested the waters:
"Listen to how Tám Tề Thiên's wife helped her man escape — I can hardly believe it's true, it's that incredible. A woman willing to spit in the face of public opinion, marry a Westerner just to get onto the island and try every way to save her husband from this living hell. How many women in this world can measure up to Tám Tề Thiên's wife? Could you do the same, Châu? If I were your husband, would you dare help me escape?"

Châu sat bolt upright:
"So you came to see me tonight for that, not for... the other thing?"

Bảy Viễn gently pulled her back down, stroked her chin:

"Don't misunderstand me, girl. Like I told you when we first met, I came to you tonight to make a friend. Landed on this island with a 12-year hard labor sentence, I need someone close to help me get through all this suffering. As for escaping — I was just testing you. I'm the boss of Block 5 now, taking over for Khăm Chay. Why would I throw away a good thing chasing shadows? Right?"

Châu nodded slowly:
"Yeah, you're right... Why throw away a sure thing. Ten escape attempts, seven fail. Only three make it. If you tried to escape, Brother Bảy, I'd be worried sick. I'd be scared of losing such a charming, gallant sworn brother."

Bảy Viễn thought to himself:
"Rushing won't get you there. This is life and death, gotta take it slow. I need to own this girl body and soul before she'll be any use for the big job." He leaned over, blew out the little oil lamp, and turned back to pull Châu close...

October 9, 1936. Bagne 2 — the political prisoner block — buzzed with the news that the government was granting amnesty to 250 people, most of them serving under five years. The lifers just got their sentences reduced and stayed rotting on the island. Those were the heavy hitters — men like Tôn Đức Thắng, Phạm Hùng...

Bagne 1 held the common criminals. Nobody got amnesty there, but the buzz was just as loud.

Tôn Đức Thắng was from Cù lao Ông Hố, Long Xuyên town. He was a mechanic at Trường Máy, worked at Ba Son a few years, then went to France, served as a mechanic on the battleship Paris. He joined the mutiny when the French fleet was sent to the Black Sea to rescue the Tsar's army getting torn apart by the Russian Revolution. Tôn Đức Thắng raised the red flag to the top of the Paris's mast to show the French battleship backed the revolutionaries. For that, the French shipped him back home. Then he got arrested and exiled to Côn Đảo in the Barbier affair of 1919. When he arrived, the French wardens threw him into the rice-grinding pit, hoping the common criminals in there would waste him. But he was clever enough to win those guys over by reorganizing the work so it was less backbreaking and building solidarity and mutual support.

As for Phạm Hùng, he was from Long Hồ, Vĩnh Long province. He studied at Mỹ Tho High School until his second year, then dropped out to join the revolution. Got two death sentences. He left behind one hell of a statement at the Sài Gòn Superior Court: "Every man only has one head. You've given me two death sentences — so I guess you're planning to chop off the head between my legs too."

But the Red International of Labor Unions managed to get Phạm Hùng's death sentence commuted to life.

After the political prisoners boarded the boat back to the mainland, Côn Đảo went back to its daily grind of hard labor.

Bảy Viễn was dead set on prepping his escape. Those secret nights sleeping with the mã tà 76's wife, he'd always ask her to tell him escape stories, quietly picking the best approach. Some guys stole the Fisheries Service's motorboat and reached the mainland in one night. Hard to pull off though. Option two was swiping a boat from the Fisheries Service too. Also tough.

The most common method was transferring to the Timber Station, going up the mountain to chop trees, saw them into firewood, and turning it in every day. A strong prisoner could double his workload to let his buddies strip rattan for a raft.

In the end, Bảy Viễn settled on building a raft. For that, he had to talk smooth to the guards and get himself transferred to the Timber Station.

As for the Block 5 boss job, Bảy Viễn handed it off to his trusted man, Dao.

First day in, Bảy Viễn already saw the danger lurking for any prisoner thinking about escaping by raft. Up in the hills there were Khmer ranger squads under the command of a Frenchman. Those bastards knew all the empty beaches where prisoners would launch rafts to reach the mainland.

He was still looking for allies when one day somebody spoke up:
"Brother Bảy, I know who you are even if you don't know me. You were the block chief at Block 5, Camp I, and now you're asking to transfer here — I can figure out what you're planning."

Bảy Viễn sized him up:
"Let's say you've got some idea of what I'm up to. But first — who are you, what's your name..."
"My name's Tư Điền, but in prison I'm just number 2140."

Bảy Viễn laughed:
"In prison you're not a man anymore, just a number. Go ahead, tell me what you want to say."

Tư Điền said:
"I'm a logger from the Three Borders region. I'm saying this so you know I've got the same plan as you, meaning..."

Bảy Viễn looked around, didn't see any guards, and shook Tư Điền's hand:
"What's your plan? Strip rattan and bamboo to build a raft?"
"Up in these hills there's a tree called 'búng' — real light but strong. Building a raft out of that beats rattan and bamboo."
"Is that right? First time I've heard of 'búng' wood. But I trust you. Still, we need to round up a couple more guys."
"I've already picked them. Trust me on this, Brother Bảy."


---


# Back to the Mainland, First Time

Back to the Mainland, First Time

After three failed attempts, Bảy Viễn and Tư Điền's crew finished their raft and launched it at Bến Dầm. But before the raft could even push off, the tracker dogs went wild barking. The Khmer rangers came running down, opened fire, and chased them down.

Bảy Viễn fought back hard. His left arm got a nasty stab wound. He had to throw in the towel and surrender.

The French were thrilled. They gave the Khmer rangers a big reward and locked up four dangerous prisoners — Bảy Viễn leading the pack.

From then on the French called him "chen de file" — the ringleader.

After 12 days in the hole, the escape crew got dragged out for hard labor, hauling timber from the mountain down to the Timber Station. Later they got switched to crushing coral rock. Since the French knew Bảy Viễn had a gift for organizing prison breaks, they didn't keep him in one place long — they shuffled him between blocks constantly. Timber Station, Fisheries Service — wherever Bảy Viễn went, he stuck to one rule: cash comes first, cash is king. Those hundred-franc notes rolled up in hollow bamboo tubes, stashed up his ass, were his "never-leave-home-without-ems" — no guard ever found them.

No matter what situation Bảy Viễn was in, he always kept himself in shape by exercising at least an hour a day. If you want to escape, health is priority number one.

Three years on the island flew by. Bảy Viễn had failed four times, but he still wouldn't quit.

He planned the fifth attempt carefully. This one had to work. His right-hand man was still Tư Điền. Bảy Viễn gave Tư Điền five hundred to buy supplies. This time Tư Điền picked a killer crew. Five guys: one logger and four fishermen.

They quietly went up the mountain, stripped rattan, and built the raft. Alongside the raft-building, Bảy Viễn shelled out cash to have the mã tà 76's wife buy supplies: 100 kilos of drinking water, 5 kilos of sugar, 2 kilos of fresh limes, 10 kilos of dried bananas, 3 kilos of salt...

To launch the raft safely, Bảy Viễn picked a special date: exactly the 30th day of the 12th lunar month — New Year's Eve of the Year of the Dragon, which fell on February 8, 1940. A smart pick. First, the 30th — pitch black night. Second, New Year's Eve — everybody's home enjoying family time, even the Khmer rangers got lazy about patrols. But the best part was the escape hit the middle of monsoon season. Catch one gust of wind and that raft would fly across the waves — one night and you'd be at the tip of Cà Mau.

When one of the fishermen told him the monsoon winds had helped Côn Đảo prisoners escape plenty of times before, Bảy Viễn felt even more confident. From where they hid the raft on Núi Chúa, it took four hours just to reach the beach.

One weird thing: that New Year's Eve it poured rain — rain so hard you couldn't wipe it off your face. But luckily the sea stayed calm. The water on the 30th night was black as coal. The raft was ready. Tư Điền's job was basically done. He handed things over to a prisoner named Nhan, a fisherman who knew the waves and how to navigate.

Not long after they hit open water, the sea got rough. Waves two meters high. The raft bobbed around like an egg.

Nhan moved fast, tied lines to the mast, barked orders for everybody to sit in position and bail out the seawater flooding the raft.

Bảy Viễn carefully pulled his bamboo tube of hidden cash out of his ass and put it in a rubber pouch tied around his neck.

The crazy storm weather threw all of Bảy Viễn's calculations out the window.

The storm lasted five days and nights. Their food ran out completely. No drinking water left. Everybody was dying of thirst. But Hai Nhan knew how to handle it. He'd brought along half a dozen fishhooks.

Five days later, the storm passed. The raft was still drifting. Nhan dropped lines and caught enough fish to keep the crew alive. Just before dawn on the tenth day, Nhan yelled out when he scooped up a leaf floating on the sea: "We've reached land, brothers!"

Half an hour later the raft hit shore.

Nobody knew where the hell they were. Bảy Viễn jumped onto the beach and walked into a village. He found a temple. Asked around and found out they were 10 kilometers from Phan Thiết, 8 kilometers from Mường Mán station. The head monk told them to take it easy, rest up at the temple, get their strength back, then move on.

Bảy Viễn asked two of the crew — Tư Đen and Hai Nhan — to come back to Sài Gòn and work with him. Both turned him down. Tư Đen went back to the Three Borders for his logging. Hai Nhan stuck with the sea. So Bảy Viễn went alone to Mường Mán station and caught the train to Sài Gòn.

The day Bảy Viễn came home — man, what a happy day. The court had given him 12 years hard labor. He'd done 4 on the island. Only one third. He got home 8 years early. His wife and kids were over the moon. His firstborn son Paul was 14 years old, in his first year at Pétrus Ký High School. That boy was Bảy Viễn's pride and joy. He hadn't been lucky with schooling himself, so he wanted his son to do better, so life wouldn't screw him over like it did his dad. Bảy Viễn's wife ran a little trading business, enough to get by.

Bảy Viễn was relieved and went looking for his buddies in the underworld.

Most of Bảy Viễn's friends were Bình Xuyên men. The top guys were Ba Dương, Mười Trí, Sáu Đối, Tư Ty, Tư Hoạnh, Năm Bé.

Ba Dương was a martial arts teacher from Bến Tre but lived at Rạch Đỉa bridge, Tân Quy village, Nhà Bè district.

Mười Trí was from Bà Quẹo, Hóc Môn district, the leader of the Cửu Long Chín Rồng gang.

Sáu Đối was the big shot of Tân Thuận harbor.

Tư Hoạnh was a bandit who ruled around the Ông Thìn bridge area.

Tư Ty ran things in Bình Đông, Câu Bót neighborhood.

Năm Bé was a player from Hải Phòng who drifted down to Xóm Chiếu and Kinh Tẻ.

When he saw Bảy Viễn again, Ba Dương said happily:

"You took a long-ass vacation on that island, Brother Bảy. Four years — a lot of water's flowed under the bridge. Let me fill you in on the big stuff. The short Japs have already jumped into Indochina. The French are scared shitless. France is losing back home so they're scared the Japs will take the colonies. For now the Japs haven't made their move because the French are giving ground on everything... I'm telling you this so you know we've gotta watch both the French and the Japs now."

Bảy Viễn went looking for Sáu Đối, and Sáu Đối gave him a Colt pistol with a mare engraved on it:
"Here's a fire-spitting bitch with six clips, Brother Bảy. For protection. Just got out of prison, I figure you're short on cash?"

As he spoke, Sáu Đối pulled a stack of bills from a drawer and stuffed it into Bảy Viễn's pocket.

Bảy Viễn went to Bà Quẹo to visit Mười Trí. A big warm greeting. These two were a deadly duo — they'd known each other since before 1930. Mười Trí looked Bảy Viễn over for a minute and asked:

"What's the plan for work going forward?"

Bảy Viễn laughed:
"Gonna kick back for a couple weeks, then start 'singing' again like before. I cooked up a lot of new songs on that island."


---


# Robbing the Kim Khánh Gold Shop

Robbing the Kim Khánh Gold Shop

Back with Mười Trí, Bảy Viễn laid out a big-time scheme:

"Now I'm Monsieur Vincent. I'll pass for a Frenchman and make the cops think twice. Makes it easy to work. I'm asking you to be my advisor. You in?"

Mười Trí shook his head:
"Truth is, after escaping Côn Đảo, I want out of the game. This life's got no future. It's just one big circle — 'singing,' getting caught, doing time, escaping, then singing again... My old lady's doing okay in business, runs a mushroom farm in Bến Tranh up in Dầu Tiếng. I figure I'm not desperate enough to keep taking risks."

Bảy Viễn laughed loud:
"One trip to the island and you're already cooked, buddy?"

Mười Trí got thoughtful:
"Even now, looking back at that escape, my gut still clenches. I was so damn eager to get home I crossed during storm season. The gale blew us all the way to Hải Nam island. We were drifting out at sea when a fishing boat picked us up and dropped us at Móng Cái. The boat owner gave us money to get to Hải Phòng. Lucky for me, a couple of smugglers who'd been locked up in Sài Gòn's Khám Lớn recognized me as someone who'd done them a favor, so they asked me to be their advisor moving goods down south."

Bảy Viễn grinned with delight:
"So you've already been an advisor for the opium smugglers running from the North to the South. You've got all the experience in the world — how can you retire? Listen to my plan: I'll pretend to be a gold shop owner from Cần Thơ coming up to Sài Gòn to stock up. I'll rent an American car, a box truck, put a fake sign on it to make it look like a private vehicle. I'll carry the Colt that Sáu Đối gave me. I'll time it for noon when the cops change shifts. Walk into the shop, ask to see the merchandise, scoop everything into a leather briefcase, leave a few warning bullets behind, jump in the truck and peel out. It's something new — bold and lightning-fast. The shop owner won't have time to react. By the time he gets his wits together and screams, I'll be miles away."

Mười Trí nodded slowly:
"That's one fresh plan. Surprise is everything. But you've gotta act the part. Fake being a gold shop owner so well the owner doesn't suspect a thing. If he gets suspicious, the whole thing's blown."

Bảy Viễn grinned, full of confidence:
"I've rehearsed. I looked in the mirror — I look like a real bourgeois. Watch, let me put on the suit and show you."

In a flash, Bảy Viễn turned into a gold shop owner — impressive in an egg-yolk yellow suit, red tie, gold-rimmed glasses, Fletcher hat, two-tone shoes, a leather briefcase in hand, a cigar clamped in his mouth...

Mười Trí was pleased:
"Your act looks damn good. Number one safety concern is that American car. The engine better be solid. If it stalls, you're dead."

"Of course! I've got a driver who's also a first-rate mechanic. When I'm inside the shop, he stays in the truck with the engine running. The moment I step out with the briefcase, he takes off like a rocket. Good enough?"

Mười Trí shot back:
"Your role and the driver are okay, but you need two more guys. A bodyguard in the front seat with the driver to signal you about what's happening outside. And another guy pretending to be a pedestrian — he'll go to the intersection where the traffic cops are posted. When they change shifts, he'll wave his hat as your signal to move fast..."

The Kim Khánh gold shop was on Rue des Manns (later renamed Đồng Khánh, now Trần Hưng Đạo B) right in the middle of the busy commercial district — with police posts at both ends of the street.

But Bảy Viễn and Mười Trí had done their homework. They picked exactly the guard-change at noon, the time when the streets were emptiest, to hit the gold shop.

Just like they'd planned, Bảy Viễn played the Cần Thơ gold shop owner perfectly. The American truck pulled up in front of the shop. Bảy Viễn stepped out with his leather briefcase, looking important, walked in, tipped his hat to the owner:

"I meant to come up early but the ferries at Cần Thơ and Mỹ Thuận held me up, so here I am at this hour. Past noon already! Hope you'll forgive me, ma'am." Bảy Viễn pulled back his sleeve to check the time, flashing his gold Omega — "Quarter to twelve. You haven't closed for lunch yet, I hope?"

The shopkeeper looked at Bảy Viễn, then at the American truck parked out front, knew he was a big spender, and smiled warmly:

"It's exactly twelve — we usually close for lunch. But when a customer comes all the way from the provinces, we're happy to serve you well past the lunch hour."

Bảy Viễn pulled out a business card:
"This is my shop. Opened last month. Business is pretty good, thanks to heaven, so I've come up to Sài Gòn to restock. Heard from colleagues that the Kim Khánh gold shop is a trustworthy place, so I wanted to make your acquaintance."

Turning the card over in her fingers, the shopkeeper's chest swelled at the stranger's compliment:
"Thank you for your patronage. Please, let me show you the merchandise."

Bảy Viễn sat chatting with the owner, occasionally glancing at the truck where Mười Trí sat in the front pretending to read a newspaper but really watching Ba Rùm, who was drinking coffee on the sidewalk at the intersection where the cops were stationed.

The gold shop owner offered Bảy Viễn lotus-scented tea while he looked over the goods. Bảy Viễn admired every piece — clever compliments, called them beautiful, and asked to see absolutely everything in the display cases.

Every now and then he'd glance at the truck. Suddenly he saw Mười Trí roll up his newspaper and fan himself — the signal that no cops were at the post. Instantly Bảy Viễn snapped open his briefcase, swept every gold bracelet off the display counter, and stuffed them into the case.

He slammed the pistol hard on the table and said:
"Don't scream! If you scream, this gun'll shut you up for good."

The moment Bảy Viễn got in the truck, the driver floored it. By the time they hit the intersection, the gold shop owner was just starting to scream.

Five minutes later, Bảy Viễn's truck was already at the Phú Lâm ferry terminal.

Buzzing from how easy the score was, Bảy Viễn suggested to Mười Trí that they knock over the Bình Triệu carpentry workshop — the top boat-builder in all of Sài Gòn. The owner was a Chinese man from Chợ Lớn.

At the workshop, it was just the owner and a safe full of cash that hadn't made it to the bank yet.


---


Robbing the Bình Triệu Carpentry Workshop

Three honks sounded from the alley — two short, one long. That was the signal: Bảy Viễn had come to pick up the strategist Mười Trí for a "singing gig."

Mười Trí hopped nimbly into the car and sat up front next to Bảy Viễn. In the back sat Ba Rùm — his nephew, who called him Uncle — and Tư Nhị, one of Bảy Viễn's boys.

"Damn, Hoảnh — where the hell did you find a brand-new car like this?"

Bảy Viễn laughed, pleased with himself. "Fresh off the lot, belongs to that Trần Tăng, a big shot wholesaler at Bình Tây Market. The car's only just been run in, and when I asked to borrow it for a night's 'hunting,' he didn't hesitate. Even offered his driver. But of course I turned that down — having his man along would blow the whole operation."

Mười Trí grilled him carefully: "What's the plan? Lay it out for me."

"It's six PM now. We head straight for Bình Lợi. Out in the suburbs, six or seven in the evening, it's dead quiet — perfect conditions for us to 'grab some goods.' The Bình Triệu carpentry workshop sits right at the three-way intersection, diagonal from the train station. By this hour the owner's gone home to Chợ Lớn, only the foreman's left minding the place."

"What about the cash? The owner might've taken it all to the bank."

"We checked thoroughly. The owner only deposits at the bank once a week, on weekends — he doesn't like making small trips to the vault. Today's Friday, so we're scooping it all clean, nice and easy."

Still cautious, Mười Trí pressed: "What about the cops, the patrols at the Bình Triệu intersection and the checkpoints along the road?"

"Don't worry about a thing. Tư Nhị spent days keeping tabs on those cops at the Bình Triệu station. They just loaf around at coffee shops and drinking holes. We'll do our business on this side and they won't know a damn thing. As for the escape route, there's a police station in front of Bà Chiểu Market. We'll swing around by the pig slaughterhouse way..."

The car sped straight to the Bình Triệu carpentry workshop. Bảy Viễn honked. The foreman came running out. Bảy Viễn drove into the yard and put on his important-man voice:

"Where's the owner of this workshop? I need to discuss some business."

"The owner's gone home to Chợ Lớn. If you wouldn't mind coming back tomorrow, sirs."

Bảy Viễn got out, pretending to inspect the sawmill and workshop. "We're thinking of going into business together — need to have a look at the facilities. Would you be so kind as to show us around?"

The foreman hesitated, but seeing these guests dressed sharp and riding in a gleaming car, he gladly took them on a tour.

After they'd made a full round, Bảy Viễn pulled out his gun and pressed it into the foreman's side.

"I'm here to borrow your boss's money — I'm in a tight spot. If you wanna live, take me inside, open the safe, and hand over everything."

The foreman went white as a sheet, frozen stiff like a pole.

"Hurry it up!"

Bảy Viễn shoved the gun barrel hard into the foreman's back. The man shuffled obediently into the house and opened the safe. Tư Nhị stuck close behind Bảy Viễn and scooped all the cash into a burlap sack they'd brought along. Meanwhile, Mười Trí kept watch inside and out. Ba Rùm jumped into the car and turned it around toward the alley for a quick getaway. In just a few minutes, all the money in the Bình Triệu workshop safe had flowed into the pockets of Bảy Viễn's crew.

Everyone climbed back in. Bảy Viễn grabbed the wheel and laid down the law:

"Don't you dare yell. Wait till we're long gone, then you can holler all you want. Got it?"

Before he'd even finished talking, Bảy Viễn floored it. The car shot onto the road and turned onto Bình Lợi Bridge. But when they were halfway across, headlights flared up behind them.

Ba Rùm turned to look. The headlights were getting closer.

"That Frenchman coming back from hunting up in Biên Hòa! He's flashing his brights, wants to pass us, anh Bảy!"

Bảy Viễn hit the gas, refusing to let the car behind overtake. The two cars raced at top speed like it was a drag race.

Suddenly, lightning flashed, thunder rolled, and rain started coming down. Bảy Viễn flicked on the wipers — they swept frantically across the windshield — but his foot stayed heavy on the gas. The car behind kept stubbornly on their tail.

Mười Trí said: "The road's slick in this rain. Push it too hard and we'll skid out. Just let him pass."

Bảy Viễn shot back: "No way! If he gets past, he'll tip off the Bà Chiểu station and they'll come after us."

Then Mười Trí's face went pale. Up ahead: a bullock cart crawling along the shoulder, and right behind it a horse-drawn carriage. The carriage swung out to pass the cart — and the road was too narrow for a car to squeeze through. He turned to gauge Bảy Viễn's reaction. Cold sweat broke out on his skin when he saw Bảy Viễn's speedometer still reading 120 km/h. The trees on both sides felt like they were hurtling straight at him. Just as the car reached the narrowest point, the horse-drawn carriage finished passing the bullock cart and hugged the right shoulder. Bảy Viễn's car shot through like a bolt of lightning. Only then did Mười Trí, Ba Rùm, and Tư Nhị finally exhale.

But their troubles weren't over. At Băng Ky Bridge, Bảy Viễn suddenly swung onto the Cây Thị fork, figuring he'd shake the Frenchman — the guy would surely keep going straight, and Bảy Viễn would lose the bastard for good. But he took the turn hard without easing off the gas or braking, and the car lurched violently. Bảy Viễn wrestled the wheel to keep them from plunging into the water spinach ponds and catfish ponds lining both sides. He managed to keep the front end on the road, but the back end slid off — the right wheel sank into a catfish pond. The three of them jumped out and tried to pull the wheel up, but the rain had turned the mud into slippery grease and they couldn't haul the rear end back onto the road.

Bảy Viễn barked: "What now?"

Mười Trí sighed: "Only one thing — carry the cash home and leave the car here. First thing in the morning, hire a crane truck, fish it out, take it to the garage, get it fixed up proper, and return it to Trần Tăng."

So the whole crew flagged down a horse-drawn carriage, paid for the whole trip, and headed back to Bảy Viễn's place.

But Mười Trí's plan couldn't outrun the city police machine. That French hunter had stopped at the Bà Chiểu station and reported the Bình Triệu workshop robbery. Cops fanned out looking for a brand-new car. They found it sitting pretty in a catfish pond in Cây Thị village. From the license plate they traced it back to Trần Tăng. And just like that, the "singing gig" was blown. One by one, four gangsters got nabbed and landed in court — 12 years hard labor, exiled to Côn Đảo. Bảy Viễn got an extra 8 years stacked on top of what he already owed. And so Bảy Viễn was shipped off to the island for the second time.


---


Back to Côn Đảo the Second Time

Bảy Viễn was arrested after the Bình Triệu carpentry workshop heist on the 8th of September, 1942, and shipped back out to Côn Đảo. He went back to the island the way a man returns to a familiar boarding house after a long trip away. On this exile voyage he had plenty of company — his close friends Mười Trí, Ba Rùm, and Tư Nhị. The sentence of 12 years hard labor, plus the 8 years still left from before — 20 years flat — meant nothing to him. Once you've made up your mind to escape, the number of years doesn't matter.

Mười Trí was no stranger to the prison island lost in the middle of the ocean either. He'd been there many times and had escaped many times. The first time, his raft made it all the way to Rạch Gốc, but while he was hitching a ride on a fishing boat from Cà Mau to Chợ Lớn, he got caught at Chợ Nhỏ in Trà Vinh. He bribed the local chief with two hundred-piastre notes, but the man wasn't interested. All he wanted was to capture an escaped prisoner so the provincial chief would write him up — "bon serviteur de la France." That chief was so desperate for French praise that the locals called him Chief Boong (twisting the French word "bon" — good). So Mười Trí got sent back to Khám Lớn, went to court, got another sentence, and was shipped straight back to Côn Đảo. A few months later, he escaped again. He got impatient and didn't wait for the right monsoon season, so a storm blew him north and washed him up on Hainan Island. He begged sweet potatoes from fishermen to survive, then pushed his raft back out to sea. Adrift again, he got lucky — a fishing boat picked him up and dropped him off at Móng Cái. The boat owner gave him money to go down to Hải Phòng, where he joined a smuggling ring and caught a train back to Sài Gòn.

Hearing Mười Trí's escape story, Bảy Viễn asked: "When you got back to the mainland, did you settle scores with that Chief Boong bastard?"

Mười Trí nodded. "Right when he was checking my ID papers at the checkpoint, I slipped him two hundreds and he turned his nose up at it — big shot saying, 'I don't need money. All I want is the provincial chief's signature with the word 'Boong' on it.' I swore to myself I'd teach that leech — a man willing to be a slave for the French — a lesson. So when I joined an opium smuggling run from Hải Phòng down South, I got off at Trà Vinh, went straight to Chợ Nhỏ, and torched that rotten Chief Boong's house. Watched the fire swallow him inside that house he'd built with money stolen from the local folks. It felt good."

But on the road back, the gruesome death of Chief Boong haunted him. It satisfied the gangster code — an eye for an eye, a debt repaid. But was it too cruel? Too inhuman?

Bảy Viễn laughed. "I don't waste time on thoughts like that, buddy. Way I see it, life's law is big fish eat little fish. But sometimes fish eat ants, and sometimes ants eat fish."

The prison ship reached the island. The four friends got split up into four different places. Bảy Viễn went to Sở Lưới (the Net Camp), Mười Trí to Sở Củi (the Firewood Camp), Tư Nhị to Sở Rẫy (the Farm Camp), and Ba Rùm — since he was a craftsman — was sent to Bản Chế, the workshop where they handled carpentry and light metalwork to repair tools.

All four had stashed money on them for protection.

Mười Trí and Bảy Viễn played the smart move: "spend money to win hearts." Inside the prison, any inmate — whether an old-timer or a newcomer — who needed cash for gambling or buying supplies could come borrow from either of them, and they'd always help. Five or ten piastres was nothing special. Then they'd conveniently "forget" about the loan. Never mentioned it, never asked for it back.

Bảy Viễn, for his part, was thrilled to get back in touch with Châu — his old flame, the prison guard's wife from Block 76. When they reunited at night, the girl was happy as a kid at Tet.

She smothered her dashing lover with kisses. "Anh Bảy, you know what? Last Tet, when I heard you escaped in the middle of New Year's Eve, I was happy and scared at the same time. Happy because it was pitch black on New Year's Eve and nobody was out on the streets. But scared because there was a huge storm that night — I didn't know if your raft could take the waves."

Bảy Viễn laughed. "Getting caught and sent back here — that's a good thing too. You and I have some kind of karmic bond. Coming back out here to visit you every now and then makes perfect sense."

Châu asked him: "Are you planning to escape again, anh Bảy?"

"What kind of question is that? You've never heard the saying 'a horse knows the way home'?"

Châu looked sad. "When? Do you need my help?"

Bảy Viễn shook his head. "Thanks for what you did last time. I might still need your help down the line. What's new in the escape game these days?"

"After your escape, the French beefed up their Khmer patrol unit. They're handing out bigger rewards to keep them motivated — crawling through the jungle, climbing the mountains, looking for bamboo rafts hidden in the hills. You should be careful, anh Bảy."

Bảy Viễn nodded to himself. "I need to think of a different approach — something besides going up the mountain to cut bamboo and build a raft."

After only a short time doing hard labor at Sở Lưới, Bảy Viễn hit on a new idea: steal one of the boats right here and sail out.

He found excuses to hang around the pier, studying the number of boats moored there.

In those days the dock always had a high-speed motor launch ready to chase down escaping prisoners, plus a few barges for transferring prisoners and supplies from the big ships. On top of that, three or four fishing boats that prisoners used for daily fishing.

One strange thing: there was a small sampan at the dock, big enough for five or six people, and nobody seemed to know what the inmates used it for.

At night, Bảy Viễn tossed and turned, his mind fixed on stealing that sampan to get back to the mainland. A few more months and the monsoon season would hit — one night's sail and he'd reach Sóc Trăng, Bạc Liêu. Crossing the ocean in a sampan was risky, but he had to try his luck — building bamboo rafts up in the mountains was a thing of the past.

Right away Bảy Viễn found a way to contact Mười Trí. He suggested Mười Trí request a transfer to Sở Lưới so they could talk things over. Mười Trí came to Sở Lưới, heard Bảy Viễn out, and agreed on the spot.

Bảy Viễn asked the prison guard's wife from Block 76 to prepare drinking water, dried rice, sticky rice flakes, lime, and sugar. Mười Trí got in touch with Ba Rùm and asked him to file down a "master key" so they could steal the sampan from Sở Lưới. Tư Nhị was also contacted and told to get ready.

The escape night was set for New Year's Eve again — pitch black. Everybody got down into the boat — everybody except Ba Rùm.

Mười Trí called: "Come sit next to me, boy."

But strangely, Ba Rùm shook his head.

"Wishing the three of you a safe journey. I'm staying behind — in case you ever come back out here, I'll be here to help."


---


The Second Prison Escape

The guys had already climbed into the boat, ready to head out to sea, when Ba Rùm raised his hand to wave goodbye.

"And this here is anh Năm Bé — also a skilled worker, asking to hitch a ride with you back to the mainland. I'm giving up my spot for anh Năm Bé. Hope he'll be useful to you on this escape, and once you're back on the mainland too."

Everyone turned to look at the prisoner Ba Rùm had introduced as a hitchhiker. He was a stocky middle-aged man with a high crew cut like a fighter's. He was carrying a pretty heavy tin box.

Bảy Viễn shot a quick look at Mười Trí and got the signal. "Sure. The more the merrier. Come on down."

Năm Bé set his tin box down gently in the sampan. "Dried rice I've been preparing for months. Call it my contribution to the provisions."

The sampan pushed off. Ba Rùm stood silently watching until his friends disappeared into the hazy mist over the sea before turning back to the prison.

Bảy Viễn was puzzled. "Ba Rùm handled everything for this trip. Why's he staying behind? I don't get it."

Mười Trí laughed. "Only I really understand him. Ba Rùm's my nephew. He came along with me for the thrill — wanted to try 'singing gigs' and see what the adrenaline rush was like. But when he got arrested, he had a change of heart. He told me his thinking: the sentence is 12 years for all of us. He's only 25 now. When he gets out, he'll be 37 — still plenty of time to turn his life around."

Bảy Viễn roared with laughter. "That kid's got more moves planned than I thought. Not bad!"

The sea was calm, the water lapping gently against the sides of the sampan like a quiet melody.

Bảy Viễn pulled a cloth bag out and took out a Chinese chessboard with two sets of pieces carved from beautiful ebony. "Let's play a few rounds to pass the time."

The two gangsters matched wits over the board. The chess pieces played just like the men: Bảy Viễn was hot-tempered, quick to sacrifice his pieces, while Mười Trí sat in deep contemplation, calculating every move. As they played, he was also studying the temperament of this famous Phú Thọ racetrack gangster. When you're in business with someone, you need to know their strengths and weaknesses.

They played until noon, then rested, ate, and drank. That's when Bảy Viễn suddenly remembered the prisoner who'd hitched a ride.

"So what's your story, friend? What's your name, where are you from, what landed you on the island?"

"My name is Năm Bé, from Hải Phòng originally. The name Năm Bé I picked up when I hit Sài Gòn — sounded like everyone else. I worked as a mechanic on Bạch Thái Bưởi's passenger ships. But I got tired of the same routine day in and day out, so I decided to head South and see what all the fuss was about. Lots of guys who'd gone to work on the rubber plantations came back alive and told amazing stories — endless rows of rubber trees stretching like a forest, the Mekong Delta with rice fields so wide a stork could fly straight across and a dog would tuck its tail running. The people there were filthy rich. Heard about the Bạc Liêu playboy who bought an airplane just to fly over his rice fields. I was dreaming about heading South when the government started recruiting non-career soldiers — called ONS — to go to France and fight the Germans. The ship was docked at Hải Phòng and was stopping in Sài Gòn before crossing over to France. I volunteered, but when we got to Sài Gòn, I jumped ship. Settled in the laborer neighborhoods of Khánh Hội and Xóm Chiếu. Later I landed a job as assistant manager at a teak sawmill. I was doing well then — always had twenty or thirty piastres jingling in my pocket. So I started playing the big shot..."

Bảy Viễn and Mười Trí laughed and asked: "In your days as a big shot in Xóm Chiếu, anything good happen? Tell us."

"Plenty! Like running protection for the ferry terminals in Chợ Lớn — where Nguyễn Văn Kiệu's boats carried cargo and passengers to Cà Mau, Rạch Giá, the Nam Vang dock... Had to fight it out with the other hoodlums, winner takes all, loser bows down."

Năm Bé's eyes lit up. "There was this one time I had a heated showdown with anh Ba Dương at the Long Kiểng ferry. That was a brutal fight, but it ended in a draw — we each stayed in our own territory. I was king on my side of Xóm Chiếu, but I never crossed the Kinh Tẻ canal — that was anh Ba Dương's turf."

Bảy Viễn yawned. "Fighting, stabbing, slashing — boring stuff. Got anything fun?"

Năm Bé thought for a moment, then laughed happily. "There's a good one. I cursed out some French fedora salesmen on Catinat Street. Here's how it went. Back in 1930, wide-brimmed fedoras were all the rage. The most expensive brand was Borsalino from Italy. Twenty-five piastres each — that was a month's salary for a courthouse clerk. I was strolling through Sài Gòn, thinking I'd buy myself one for the hell of it. Walked into a hat shop on Catinat Street and saw half a dozen on display — black, grey, brown, white, you name it. Soon as I stepped inside, I caught those French guys looking down their noses at me.

At the time I was wearing black silk trousers and a green khaki jacket — plain working-class clothes. Didn't give a damn. Walked right up to the glass display case.

'Let me see a hat, friend.'

The Frenchman shook his head and pointed at the price tag. 'Too expensive. You can't afford it.'

I got hot under the collar. How the hell do you know I can't afford it? Get one for me — now!'

Seeing me get tough, he reluctantly pulled one out. I felt the felt — thick, nice style — tried it on, it fit. I asked one more time just to be sure: 'How much?'

'Twenty-five piastres. Got enough to buy it, old man?'

I had to teach these cheeky French bastards a lesson. I bought the one on my head, then told him to wrap up the four still in the case. Five in total. Pulled out my wallet and paid one hundred and twenty-five piastres right on the spot — watched their faces turn green."

Năm Bé cackled with glee. "Bought 'em, took 'em home, and had no idea what to do with the extra four. Ended up giving them to my younger brothers and sisters — they were cluttering up the house!"

Bảy Viễn slapped Năm Bé on the shoulder. "We don't usually take a liking to Northerners, but I'll make you our brother — because you've gone full Southerner!"


---


# Anh Hùng kết nghĩa

Sworn Brothers

There ain't no place as unpredictable as the sea. One day the water's flat as a stone slab, and the next day a storm comes roaring in. Black clouds appear on the horizon. Then the wind whips those menacing clouds in fast. Before you know it the sky goes dark as an eclipse. Wherever the wind goes, the waves follow. The little sampan was rocking like an egg in a pot of boiling water.

Then Tu Nhi yelled out:
"Mother of God, that pair of 'sacred geese' is showing up over there! We're dead men, brothers!"

All three stared hard at what Tu Nhi was calling the pair of "sacred geese."

But they didn't see a damn thing—just waves as tall as rows of houses roaring toward them. Thunder exploded across the sky, and lightning split the heavens open, revealing a waterspout dropping down from above.

Nam Be cried out:
"That's a dragon drinking water, brothers. We're gonna get sucked up and thrown down just like the fisherfolk always tell about."

Muoi Tri and Bay Vien stayed calm in the middle of the danger. Both of them focused their minds to deal with the disaster coming their way. Their two hands gripped the planks of the boat tight.

Muoi Tri told his friend:
"Gotta hold on to the boat planks. Whatever happens, we'll stay afloat."

No sooner had he said it than a wave crashed over them, shoving the sampan down into a deep trough and then tossing it back up, light as a leaf. All four men on the sampan felt their hearts stop. Once they caught their breath, they braced for the second wave. But they waited and waited and nothing came—all they saw was the sampan flying along on top of the waves. The strange thing was the sampan wasn't getting pulled under deep like before. Lucky as hell.

About fifteen minutes later Bay Vien figured out the reason for the miracle: a big whale was hiding right against the side of the sampan.

He shouted: "The whale saved us!"

Tu Nhi opened his eyes, saw it, and cried out:
"Namo Amitabha Buddha! Nam Hai General protect and guide the four of us brothers. If this prison break brings us back to shore safe and sound, we'll slaughter a pig in thanks."

When the storm died down, everybody was overjoyed to realize they were still alive and they all held to Tu Nhi's vow—they'd roast a pig in thanks.

But the mainland was still far, far away. The storm had knocked them off course. Still, hunger and thirst were the problem right in front of them. The waves had flooded the sampan and washed away all the tin cans of dry rice and drinking water.

Hunger didn't break the escaped prisoners as bad as thirst. All that ocean water and you're dying of thirst—that's just cruel. But you can't drink seawater. By the next day, they were so thirsty they had to drink each other's piss.

Bay Vien laughed and said:
"I've heard stories about prisoners drinking each other's piss. That's happened before. But I never thought I'd end up in this damn situation myself."

Tu Nhi laughed with amusement and said:
"I've read Chinese novels, I remember when the trio Luu Quan Truong swore brotherhood in the peach garden. Now I've got an idea—the four of us brothers are drinking each other's piss, so why don't we make a ceremony to become sworn brothers? If we make it back to land, we'll help each other out, lean on each other to survive."

Bay Vien looked at Muoi Tri:

"So brothers want to do a makeshift ceremony, like the peach garden pact of the Luu Quan Truong trio? What do you think, Brother Muoi? You agree?"

Muoi Tri said:
"Swearing brotherhood as wanderers, loving each other for life—that's serious business. We've gotta know each other inside and out, every last detail, before we can trust each other. Among us four brothers, Brother Nam Be is the new one. We've only known each other a few days, since Ba Rum introduced him for a ride the day before yesterday."

Nam Be looked a little sad, forced a smile and said:
"Brother Muoi is right—you've gotta know each other to make a true bond. It's true, I've only known you brothers for three days. So leave me out of it. The other three have known each other a long time, go ahead and do the peach garden ceremony like Brother Tu Nhi wants."

Bay Vien stared hard at Nam Be and said:
"Nobody's doing anything as crazy as what Brother Nam Be just said. Four of us on this sampan, sharing the same fate, living and dying together—how can we swear brotherhood with just three? The one left out is bound to feel ashamed and hurt. I'm no fortune-teller, but one look at Brother Nam and I can tell he's a good man. Then I heard about how he cursed out those stuck-up French bastards in the hat shop on Catinat Street, and I knew he was a solid dude. Plus, with Ba Rum's recommendation—he's Brother Muoi's nephew—I think we can let Brother Nam Be into our crew. Sure, he's a Northerner, but he's been in Saigon so long he's practically Southernized, no different from us Southern boys. What do you say, Brother Muoi?"

Muoi Trí nodded slightly:
"I was just stating the principle. But every principle has its exceptions. Brother Nam Be is a good man. These past few days I've been watching how he carries himself, and I can see he fits right in with us brothers. I agree with Brother Bay."

Tu Nhi heard his two seniors talking it out, happy as could be. He found a coconut shell, scooped out water, scrubbed it clean as a whistle, held it up in front of everybody and said:
"The sky is bright and clear today—this is truly a blessed hour. Please, eldest brother, piss into this first, then the two younger brothers after. We'll perform the blood-oath ceremony prison-break style, replacing red blood with piss, which is also water that once flowed through our bodies."

Bay Vien said:
"I was born in the Year of the Dragon, Giap Thin, 1904—I'm thirty-six this year. And Brother Muoi?"

"I was born 1903, Year of the Cat, Quy Mao."

Tu Nhi:
"So Brother Muoi is the oldest, then Brother Bay is second, then Brother Nam is third, and I'm the youngest. Eldest Brother, say a few prayers for our oath ceremony today."


---


# Trường đua Phú Thọ

Phu Tho Racecourse

After his second prison break, Bay Vien went back to Phu Tho dead set on making it big. Trouble was, making it big takes capital, and Bay Vien was flat broke at the time.

How was he supposed to get big money?

Phu Tho had a racecourse. Every week the whole town would flock there to "test their luck."

So Bay Vien started studying this form of public gambling that the government officially recognized.

He read the newspapers with "Race Tips" until they were crumpled to shreds. Bay Vien decided to test his luck on Saturday afternoon. He scraped together his money and bet on the pair "first Xich Tho, second Hoang Long."

The papers were calling these two horses divine steeds—they'd been taking first and second for months.

After buying his betting tickets, Bay Vien went up to the grandstand to sit. He brought binoculars to track the two divine steeds he'd picked, hoping they'd give him his starting capital.

The result: Hoang Long beat Xich Tho by a nose. So Bay Vien lost a few hundred bucks for nothing.

"That's what I get for being greedy," Bay Vien scolded himself. If he'd bet each horse individually he'd have had a better chance than betting the pair. Sure, betting the pair paid big—but it rarely hit.

In the middle of the blazing hot summer, Bay Vien started walking home. Passing by the race betting office, he saw a tall, well-dressed man stepping out.

As soon as he saw Bay Vien, the guy called out:
"Brother Bay! You look like you got skinned!"

Bay Vien shook the man's hand and asked quietly:
"That you, Brother Tu?"

The other man laughed loud:
"That's right. Tu Thien here. I'm a turfiste—a horse bettor—I come here every week to 'cut grass and feed the racehorses.'"

Bay Vien let out a sigh:
"Whoever came up with that phrase 'cut grass and feed the racehorses' is a genius. I just got burned clean on those two horses Hoang Long and Xich Tho."

Tu Thien pulled Bay Vien up to the grandstand and chatted leisurely:
"I've been deep in this gambling racket so long I know all the tricks the horse owners and jockeys pull. No offense, but they're a bunch of crooks."

Bay Vien exclaimed:
"How can you lump them all together like that, Brother Tu? There are good horse owners too!"

Tu Thien nodded:
"Yeah, there are good ones. Those are the intellectuals who studied in France and see horse racing as a noble sport. I know Dr. Nguyen Chi Nhieu, owner of the Nguyen Chi Duoc Cuoc company in Da Kao—a real straight shooter, wouldn't know how to fix a race if he tried. But good horse owners are damn rare. Most of them have 'money blinding their eyes.' They order the jockeys to pull back and 'place second.' You know, Brother Bay, a horse coming in first doesn't pay the owner as much as fixing the race and buying up all the betting tickets. The money comes in ten times more..."

Bay Vien burst out laughing:
"All this time I thought I was a bastard. Now I see these people are bastards ten times worse than me. One hell of an expensive lesson: seven hundred bucks."

Tu Thien looked at Bay Vien with a hint of pity:
"Don't be sad! Let me help you make it back." He reached into his pocket and pulled out a stack of pair-bet tickets: "I'm giving you ten of these pair tickets. In five minutes we'll watch my two divine steeds take the prize. And in half an hour we'll go collect the money."

Bay Vien looked at Tu Thien, half believing, half doubting:
"Brother Tu talks like he's pulling money out of his pocket!"

Tu Thien laughed:
"To know how to bet on horses, you gotta know how to catch the tuy-do—French for inside tip. I hang out with a lot of the horse owners, and especially the jockeys. I treat the jockeys real sweet. Plain talk: I 'buy' them. None of 'em dare cross Tu Thien. They give me the tip, I give them money. When the result matches what they told me, I pay 'em ten times extra. That's my whole secret. Just like the Chinese have taught their kids for thousands of years: 'Phong tai hoa, thu nhan tam'—spend your money and goods generously to win people's hearts."

Bay Vien chuckled to himself:
"I know that chant all too well. Back on the island I bought off the warden and the guards' wives—all 76 of 'em—so they'd help me escape safe and sound."

The fix went down exactly like Tu Thien said.

Tu Thien and Bay Vien went down to the betting office to collect their winnings.

With those ten tickets Tu Thien gave him, Bay Vien not only got his stake back but walked away a few hundred bucks ahead. And Tu Thien? The guy cleaned up like you wouldn't believe. From that Saturday afternoon on, Bay Vien became tight with Tu Thien.

The two had a lot in common—both were Chinese-Vietnamese mixed, both had taken French citizenship (Tu Thien went by Maurice, Bay Vien picked Vincent). Learning more about Tu Thien, Bay Vien found out he was the son of a customs official, from a Catholic family. Tu Thien was well-educated, had his French high school diploma, and married a Chinese-Vietnamese girl (the daughter of a big shopkeeper in Binh Tay Market).

Maurice Thien was the kind of petit bourgeois intellectual famous for partying hard, in line with the "Be Strong to Serve" movement of the time, led by Lieutenant Colonel Ducouroy.

Before the patriotic youth movement started by Southern student groups—which students from Saigon, My Tho, and Can Tho high schools all joined—the French colonialists got scared and launched the "Play Hard, Stay Strong" movement to split up the North, Central, and South regions. Maurice Thien was a well-known cyclist racer all over the South. On top of that, Tu Thien was a footballer for Ngoi Sao Gia Dinh, the oldest football club in the South. He had a thunderbolt kick that made goalkeepers tremble.

One day Maurice Thien brought Bay Vien some great news:
"The French are organizing a bicycle race around all of Indochina—the Tour d'Indochine. It's gonna be huge. Over a hundred racers from Northern, Central, and Southern teams, plus the French military team. Following the race there'll be 200 motorbikes and cars from the teams handling supplies for the riders along the way."

Bay Vien asked:
"In this race, can we get in on any concession deal?"

Tu Thien nodded:
"Damn right. I'm bidding on a Duvette drink truck. I'm putting you behind the wheel of that truck. Two birds with one stone. We get to travel, we get spending money—you in?"

"Hell yeah. I need to broaden my horizons so I've got stories to spend later on."


---


# Lịch Sử xe Xích Lô

The History of the Xich Lo

The more Bay Vien hung around Tu Thien, the more strange things he discovered: this Chinese-French mixed guy, married to a Chinese girl, into sports—he was also a sharp businessman.

Back then Saigon had just gotten the xich lo (pedicab) to start replacing the rickshaws, which the newspapers trashed as "man-pulling-man" transportation, uncivilized. The early xich lo were crude and clunky, uncomfortable to sit in.

Tu Thien designed a new-style xich lo. It looked modern, with cushioned seats.

This was the Coupeaud-style pedicab that had shown up in Phnom Penh a few years earlier, and later came to Saigon.

Tu Thien jumped on it. He had a whole fleet of the new-style pedicabs built and gave them a flashy name: "photey three-wheeler."

The government authorized the "photey three-wheeler" for public use.

The total number of new-style pedicabs running all over Saigon-Cholon in the early 1940s was thirty—and every single one of them belonged to one owner: Tu Thien.

Later on, Tu Thien founded the Association of Photey Three-Wheeler Owners. Membership grew to a few dozen, and Bay Vien was among them.

All of Saigon-Cholon ended up with over two hundred of them.

Thanks to knowing how to do business big, Maurice Thien got rich. He drove a green Citroën and won a bunch of fat concession deals—like the Duvette drink truck supply contract for the Tour d'Indochine bicycle race.

Bay Vien just drove that drink truck. But during that month of riding the thousand-mile road, Bay Vien saw the real size of his Chinese-French buddy: Tu Thien was an international smuggler.

Following the Tour d'Indochine was just a cover for smuggling gold and opium—the two commodities that brought in the biggest profits for the smuggling trade.

But circumstances wouldn't let Bay Vien keep living easy off Tu Thien's connections. The Japanese army landed in Indochina, and the French got nervous about another uprising like the Nam Ky Uprising of November 1940. So the French started cracking down on elements dangerous to French law and order. Everybody on the blacklist got arrested—first the nationalist activists, then the politicized religious movements like Cao Dai and Hoa Hao, and finally the gangsters and underworld figures like Bay Vien and Muoi Tri. So it was "of all thirty-six stratagems, fleeing is the best."

Bay Vien followed Muoi Tri to "lie low" in Ben Tranh. But who would've guessed that in this godforsaken backwater they'd run into a famous Communist intellectual fighter: Bay Tran.

Bay Tran was from Cho Dem, Binh Chanh District, Cho Lon Province. He was studying for his baccalaureate at Petrus Ky High School when he dropped out to follow older comrades into journalism, then into politics. The newspaper Dan Chung that Bay Tran worked for was the most famous paper back in 1938.

That newspaper pulled a revolutionary move: it didn't ask the colonial authorities for permission to publish. This was a tricky legal issue. French press law back in the motherland didn't require permission. To start a new paper you just had to file a copy and complete the formalities—declare the paper's name, the manager's name, and the address. But the colonial rulers were scared of letting the colonized people enjoy press freedom like people back in France, so they issued a decree requiring permission before you could print anything.

The famous lawyer Phan Van Truong, who'd practiced for decades in France, tipped off the Southern nationalist activists: just go ahead and publish without asking permission, because according to the spirit of the law, an arrêté (decree) from the Governor-General of Indochina couldn't override a loi (statute) passed by the National Assembly—the highest legislative body in the land.

Taking lawyer Phan Van Truong's advice, the Southern revolutionaries secretly put out the newspaper Dan Chung without permission. When the colonial authorities started rounding up dangerous elements, Bay Tran got out fast and went up to Ben Tranh, near Dau Tieng, to lie low.

Around the same time, the folks in Ben Tranh noticed an old Frenchman who called himself Teacher Tu Hoanh-Xang spending all day wearing a sarong and silk T-shirt, hanging out at Councilor Thi's place to "chill."

Councilor Thi was a big shot in Ben Tranh—they called him "le coq du village" (the rooster of the village, meaning the richest man around).

Councilor Thi's brother-in-law was Commune Chief My, another rich guy who spent all year just "moving troops and commanding generals" (playing four-color cards). Nowhere was safer for card sharks than Councilor Thi's house.

So that's where all the players gathered—including Teacher Tu Hoanh-Xang, who'd drifted in from god-knows-where.

Besides the old Frenchman, there was Mr. Nam Mam. His wife had a solid business going, so Mr. Nam Mam didn't need to do a thing—just sat around "crystal clear." But sitting around doing nothing gets boring, so he'd drift over to Councilor Thi's to "chill" and kill time.

So heroes met: four players gathered around the card table—the host Councilor Thi, the old Frenchman who always wore a sarong and silk T-shirt, Mr. Nam Mam, and Teacher Nam Dau Tieng.

The villagers didn't know the background of these three players, but the secret police sniffed them out after the bold heist of the Dau Tieng Rubber Plantation owner—robbed right at the Saigon bank on the riverside street (Bach Dang Wharf).

The old Frenchman calling himself Teacher Tu Hoanh-Xang was Bay Vien. Mr. Nam Mam was Muoi Tri. And Teacher Nam Dau Tieng was none other than the journalist Bay Tran.

Let me tell you about that bold robbery of the Dau Tieng Rubber Plantation owner. Two underworld figures lying low in Ben Tranh, playing four-color cards day after day, got bored stiff.

One day one of Bay Vien's underlings reported: he'd been tailing the Director of the Dau Tieng Rubber Plantation, who went to the bank every week to withdraw payroll money for the workers. So Bay Vien huddled up with Muoi Tri to "sing a number"—pull a job for fun. They planned it carefully: Bay Vien rented a US-made truck, swapped the plates to make it look like a private car, and drove it himself. Muoi Tri played the bodyguard.

Right on schedule—Saturday morning—Bay Vien drove over and parked right next to the French director's car, alongside the Saigon bank. Bay Vien walked into the office carrying a leather briefcase, sat down in the customer chair, and pretended to flip through ledgers doing calculations. Meanwhile he kept his eye on the French rubber plantation boss. When the guy came out with the cash and headed for his car, Bay Vien put his papers away and watched. The moment the Frenchman stepped onto the street, Muoi Tri rushed over to the Frenchman's car and asked to borrow a lighter for his cigarette. The driver unsuspectingly reached for the lighter, and faster than lightning, Muoi Tri chopped him hard on the back of the neck. The driver slumped over the wheel, unconscious. Bay Vien pulled the French plantation boss over to his car and hit him with a packet of chili powder right in the eyes. The guy freaked out, dropped the briefcase, and grabbed his face. Bay Vien snatched the briefcase full of cash, tossed it into the truck, gunned it, and took off. Since both the boss and the driver were down, nobody raised the alarm.

A whole week later, back at the Dau Tieng hideout, the newspapers finally started buzzing about the daring heist.

All the reporters agreed: a robbery using a getaway US truck was Bay Vien's specialty. There was a secret police agent named Doi Cau from Ba Queo who, together with Muoi Tri, figured the two big shots were lying low in the area—that's how they knew the Frenchman's every move. So the net was cast, with Dau Tieng and Ben Tranh as ground zero.

But Muoi Tri managed to escape.

That morning Muoi Tri's bicycle ran over a nail. He had to stop at a roadside repair shop to patch the tire. Suddenly a truckload of soldiers rolled up. Muoi Tri knew the secret police had caught his scent, so he bolted back to Saigon that same day. Bay Vien was still "chilling" at Councilor Thi's place. Thanks to Councilor Thi's informants tipping him off that soldiers were coming, Bay Vien and Bay Tran jumped into a boat in time and paddled across the river to safety...


---


# Going to Côn Đảo the Third Time

Going to Côn Đảo the Third Time
After that bold robbery of the Dầu Tiếng rubber plantation owner, Bảy Viễn and Mười Trí got nabbed again and sent to Côn Đảo.
For Bảy Viễn, this was his third trip out to the island.

The old-timers and inmates spotted the hero who'd once dropped the undefeated champion of Chùa Tháp Khăm Chay with a single lightning kick, and they welcomed him back with open arms. Plenty of guys still owed him money, shaking his hand and joking:

"What, you came back out here to collect, old man? I still owe you a few dozen đồng."

Bảy Viễn shot back:

"The French brought me out here like they were snatching a cat and throwing it on the plate."

He patted his pants pocket:

"I was doing fine back in Sài Gòn. I didn't come out here to collect from you fellas — I could still lend you more to pass the time gambling. How about it, gents?"

Of course the inmates were easygoing with honorable outlaws like Mười Trí and Bảy Viễn. They trusted these two were cut from the same cloth as the 108 heroes of Lương Sơn Bạc, men who robbed from fat cats and helped the poor.

Out loud they'd shoot the breeze with the inmates, but night after night, Mười Trí and Bảy Viễn were itching to hatch an escape plan. Things seemed like they were about to take a major turn. World War Two was winding down. The Allies were counterattacking like a storm. The German-Italian-Japanese Axis was on its last legs. Reading the newspapers, Bảy Viễn and Mười Trí had some idea of what was going on in the world. And the more they knew, the less they could stand lying around on that island much longer.

Luckily, Ba Rùm was still in Bản Chế, ready to help the big brothers whenever they asked.

Bảy Viễn was hoping to see Châu again, the wife of Mã Tà 76. She was both his lover and the inside contact who bought all the stuff they needed for the island breakouts.

But this time, Bảy Viễn was out of luck. The Mã Tà 76 couple's tour of duty on the island had ended, and they'd been transferred back to Sài Gòn a few months earlier. Sure, there were plenty of other Mã Tà wives who'd "parachuted in" to earn some gambling money, but Bảy Viễn had gotten tired of "paying for his bread." Now he realized how hard it was to find a "lotus in the mud" like Châu.

Ba Rùm went all out helping the two big brothers, Mười Trí and Bảy Viễn, with this third escape.

Same as before, Ba Rùm helped but didn't step onto the raft. He was still dead set on paying off his full 12-year debt and starting a clean life.

They hit the mainland at just the right time. The Japanese Emperor announced unconditional surrender. The Japanese in Indochina were more depressed than if their own father had died. Plenty of officers committed harakiri. According to the papers, the British-Indian army — made up entirely of Indian soldiers — had the job of disarming the Japanese in the South, while up North, the Chinese troops under General Lư Hán from Southern China were handling the disarmament up there.

Things were changing so fast in Sài Gòn you couldn't keep up. The French had been running things when the Japanese overthrew them on March 9, 1945. A week later, Japanese troops landed on the island, rounded up all the French and took them away, leaving just one platoon of Japanese soldiers to run everything. This was when the pro-Japanese faction was on the rise. A few intellectuals like Professor Trần Văn Quế and some pro-Japanese Cao Đài followers were sent back to the mainland. The Japanese put a commissioner named Lê Văn Trà in charge of the island's administration. Trà's assistant was Sơn Vương, a writer turned bandit.

But the Japanese only got to play king of Vietnam for a few months — then "a monkey's still a monkey even in silk." After America dropped two atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki, the Japanese Emperor surrendered.

Down in Nam Kỳ, the Japanese army was disarmed by the British and taken prisoner.

Watching all this, Bảy Viễn said to Mười Trí:

"Right — fish eat ants, but sometimes ants eat fish too."

Mười Trí was more practical, worrying about what to do next:

"In this situation, what should we do? Best we go ask someone who knows what's what. If you've got connections with someone big, go ask them. Me, I'll head to Tân Quy to visit Brother Ba Dương."

Bảy Viễn thought immediately of Maurice Thiên, the guy who'd helped him "earn pocket money" on Saturday afternoons at the Phú Thọ racetrack, and later hooked him up as a support vehicle manager for the Tour of Indochina race.

Seeing Bảy Viễn again, Tư Thiên was delighted:

"You've come back to the mainland at just the right time. Things are crying out for someone to shake things up! You know, the World War is over. The German-Italian-Japanese Axis lost, the Allied powers — Britain, France, Russia, China, America — won. The British are disarming the Japanese south of the 16th parallel. Up north it's been handed over to General Lư Hán's Chinese troops..."

Bảy Viễn nodded along:

"I read the papers, I know the big picture. But Brother Tư, tell me — what do we need to do right now?"

"The Việt Minh are rising up, seizing power up North. Word is there are Việt Minh down South too. The Việt Minh came up at exactly the right time — the Japanese lost the war, they're more depressed than a corpse that hasn't been buried yet. And the French are still locked up in the Ondème camp by the Japanese. I hear French troops from Calcutta and Bombay are heading straight for Sài Gòn to retake Nam Kỳ from the Japanese. Don't know whether the Việt Minh or the French will get here first."

Bảy Viễn didn't know much about politics, so he pressed for more:

"Who are the Việt Minh? I never heard anyone mention them out on the island."

Tư Thiên explained:

"Việt Minh is short for Việt Nam Độc Lập Đồng Minh — a political and military organization set up by the Communist Party to seize power and give birth to an independent Vietnam. They're on the Allied side, anti-Fascist, they've worked with the Americans in Southern China — rescuing American and British pilots shot down by the Japanese in North Vietnam and handing them over to the Allies in Vân Nam. With those contributions, the Việt Minh have built up a reputation internationally. Word is the American President doesn't want the French to retake their old colonies in Indochina."

Bảy Viễn thought it over for a long time before saying:

"From what you're telling me, Brother Tư, I predict there'll be shooting between the French and the Việt Minh. So who do we side with in this fight?"

Tư Thiên laughed:

"We Chinese don't like talking politics — we just worry about making a living. But since you asked, I'll tell you. Take it or leave it, Brother Bảy. The French — we know too much about them. We learned French since we were kids. We copied their way of life: coffee with milk and omelette sandwiches in the morning, butter-fried chicken and red wine at lunch, soup at night to sleep easy."

"And if the Việt Minh win?" Bảy Viễn asked.

Tư Thiên cracked up:

"That'll never happen! The Việt Minh only just formed their army a few months ago — how could they stand up to an expeditionary force that's fought all over Europe and North Africa? It'd be like throwing an egg at a rock."

"But back in the Trần and Lê dynasties, we beat enemies a hundred times stronger than us plenty of times. There's that saying: 'It's funny how a grasshopper kicks a cart — you'd think the grasshopper would fall, but the cart tips over instead.'"

Deep down, Bảy Viễn was thinking: shelter from whatever sun is shining. He made up his mind — he'd sell gold, buy guns and ammo, and raise his own army.


---


# The Bình Xuyên Army

The Bình Xuyên Army
After sounding out Maurice Thiên's take on things, Bảy Viễn met back up with Mười Trí.
The two blood brothers talked over what needed to be done.

Mười Trí asked:
"Brother Bảy, who'd you go see to hear the news?"
"I met Tư Thiên — that guy knows everything under the sun. And you, Brother Mười, have you seen Brother Ba Dương yet?"
"Yeah, I did. Brother Ba Dương's story is long and wild. I'll keep it short. He was standing at the Tây Ninh–Nam Vang bus station when the French declared a state of emergency, rounding up all dangerous elements — Communists, Cao Đài, Hòa Hảo, Tứ Ân Hiếu Nghĩa, Tịnh Độ Cư Sĩ, and they grabbed the underworld types too. Brother Ba had to run to Cần Giuộc to lay low. But someone snitched, sent the soldiers to grab him and haul him to the district office. The district chief was a bastard — he forced Brother Ba to swallow a whole clump of hair before letting him go. Hair inside you will wreck your digestive system, kill you slowly, but he had no choice — he had to go through with the sentence. Lucky for him, one of his loyal students took him in and treated him with a family remedy, so he survived.
When we seized power, Brother Ba was teaching martial arts to the Youth Vanguard in Cần Giuộc, joining in the takeover. When I asked him what we should do, he said happily:
'We outlaws have a tradition of standing tall, going back generations. We only became bandits because we were weak. Now the government's in our hands — why wouldn't we raise our own army to fight the French and get our fill of satisfaction? Brother Mười, you should head to Bà Quẹo, buy guns and ammo, recruit militiamen. The Japs are selling guns dirt cheap. Be stupid to hand them over to the British-Indian army!'"

Bảy Viễn jumped in:
"When Brother Ba raised his army, did he settle the score with that rotten district chief who made him eat that hair?"

Mười Trí exclaimed:
"Oh, I forgot the best part! When we seized power, the local officials and cops all crawled out to surrender. Then that district chief saw Brother Ba was now the commander of the Ba Dương army — his face went green as a leaf. He dropped to his knees and kowtowed to Brother Ba like he was praying to the stars. But Brother Ba handled it like a true gentleman. He just said: 'Your crime — I ought to chop off ten of your heads to feel better. But times have changed. You've lost your rank, your power. Revenge is for when you're still on top. What would I get out of taking revenge on you now? I spare your life.'"

Bảy Viễn nodded:
"So the whole underworld is of one mind — we're raising an army. Brother Mười, you got money?"

Mười Trí nodded:
"Yeah. Piles of it. A hundred taels, you hear?"
"What job netted you a hundred taels?"
"That was back in 1942. The Japs opened a shipyard building sea vessels out of ironwood to replace the warships the Allies had sunk in the naval battles across Southeast Asia. They hired me to provide protection for a few ironwood rafts on the Kinh Đôi canal, right across from the Japanese Navy Headquarters on Galiéni Street [Trần Hưng Đạo]. Six months in, the Japanese navy admiral was killed in a sea battle. Nobody paid attention to the rafts floating in the Kinh Đôi anymore. And nobody paid me either. Then a middleman offered to buy all the ironwood rafts. He thought I was some big shot because he saw me going in and out of the Japanese Navy HQ every day. I sold on the spot, had my wife bring a bag of green thread to stuff the money in. Took it home, bought gold, buried it in the garden — safe that way. Sold in the morning, I was gone by afternoon."

Bảy Viễn let out a breath:
"You're set, then. Money can buy anything — even more money. Gold's better than cash anyway. Me, I never managed to hold onto much... But lots of money buys lots of guns, little money buys a few. No big deal. Long as we've got a couple squads to make a show, we'll figure out the rest later."

Mười Trí laughed:
"Figure what out?"
"Easy. I'll take the title of army commander and ask the people for support and supplies. Who can just shrug when you ask them to support the troops? I'll target the rich bastards in the villages. If they're stingy, I'll use strong-arm tactics."

A few weeks into raising his army, Bảy Viễn ran out of money. He dragged Mười Trí along to find rich folks and ask for troop support.
Mười Trí didn't approve of Bảy Viễn's strong-arm approach, but he tagged along anyway to see just how far his friend would go — if he went too far, he'd step in to keep him out of trouble.

Bảy Viễn found a car. The two of them got in and drove through Xóm Củi.

Mười Trí asked:
"Where the hell are you taking me?"
"To Hội Đồng Đống's place. I hear he's got a daughter who's a real looker."

Mười Trí cracked up:
"So you're going wife-hunting, not asking for troop support!"

"Hey, doing two or three things at once — that's talent! You go to market for meat but see fresh fish still jumping around — why wouldn't you buy it? This guy... I'm just joking, don't take it to heart, buddy..."
"Just say it straight, don't beat around the bush. You scared your girlfriend will call you a 'squad leader' who's loyal only to his mama's kids?"

Bảy Viễn laughed, pleased:
"You've already called yourself Lãnh the pig-seller, so fine. Let's make a deal. You like drinking, I like the other thing. So when Hội Đồng Đống receives us, you sit upstairs and chat with him while I slip down to the kitchen and sweet-talk his precious daughter."

Mười Trí shook his head:
"You're a real battleship, man. No matter how many you've got, it's never enough."

That night's visit went exactly as Bảy Viễn had directed. Mười Trí sipped French liquor with Hội Đồng Đống while Bảy Viễn went to the kitchen — first to light a cigarette, then to chat up Miss Lúa. And Bảy Viễn sure knew how to sweet-talk a girl. On the way back, Bảy Viễn announced: he'd marry Miss Lúa no matter what it cost.


---


# Getting Married

Getting Married
A few days after Military Commissioner Lê Văn Viễn came by to discuss supporting the Phú Thọ troops, Hội Đồng Đống received an unexpected gift. It was a box that looked like an I-U biscuit tin, wrapped in pink paper with a red bow, addressed: "From: Military Commissioner Lê Văn Viễn. To: Mr. Hội Đồng Đống, Đa Phước Village."

Opening the box, Hội Đồng Đống's eyes nearly popped out: a brand-new six-shooter, fully loaded.

Hội Đồng Đống was crazy about guns. For a long time he'd been lusting after those revolvers the French cops wore low on their hips — thought they looked badass as hell. He'd been wanting a "pig's leg" to show off to the whole district. Now holding this Colt with the horse engraved on it, the steel gleaming blue, untouched by any hand, he was so overjoyed he hollered: "Lúa! Where are you, girl?"

Lúa came running from the kitchen: "What is it, Ba?"
"Look here — that Military Commissioner Lê Văn Viễn gave this to me."

Hội Đồng Đống waved the gun, showing off to his precious daughter.

But Miss Lúa stepped back, raised both hands, and screamed:
"Ew! That thing kills people — I'm scared!"

Hội Đồng Đống laughed loudly:
"What's there to be scared of! This is a weapon for self-defense. These are lawless times — we're prominent people, a target for bandits. Tư Ty at Cầu Ông Thìn or that Mười Nhỏ bunch over in Xóm Cỏ. That Military Commissioner giving me this gun — he really knows what I want. I wouldn't be this happy if he gave me gold!"

While Hội Đồng Đống was admiring the gun, Miss Lúa looked in the box and found a business card printed with: "Lê Văn Viễn, Military Commissioner." Flipping it over, she saw a few handwritten lines: "Grandson wishes to marry Miss Lúa and this is the first betrothal gift respectfully offered to Uncle."

She cried out:
"Ba, read this paper, Ba!"

Hội Đồng Đống was startled when he realized that the Military Commissioner hadn't just given him a gun out of the blue — there was a condition: "You must give me your precious daughter's hand in marriage." What a strange way to propose!

He immediately turned to his daughter:
"That night he went down to the kitchen to light a cigarette — what did he say to you that now he's asking for your hand?"

"He didn't say anything, Ba! He just asked about this and that, making small talk to break the ice, that's all."

Hội Đồng Đống was worried:
"What do you think about this marriage proposal? I'm really worried — if you don't agree, we'll have trouble with him. In the old days he was an outlaw, in and out of prison, and now he's a Military Commissioner. No matter what era, he's always got a gun..."

Miss Lúa said shyly, her voice low:
"You're always scared of anybody with a gun, Ba. But I think there are two kinds of people who hold guns. There's the kind that robs and kills, and there's the kind that gets rid of traitors and enemies. The Military Commissioner is the second kind..."

Hội Đồng Đống nodded thoughtfully:
"If he can straighten himself out like that, then good. So you're saying you... accept him, right?"

Miss Lúa nodded. Hội Đồng Đống sighed with relief:
"That takes a load off me. If you didn't agree, I wouldn't know what to say to him."

So a week later, Bảy Viễn was officially the prized son-in-law of Hội Đồng Đống.

Mười Trí, his blood brother, was invited too.
Out of friendship, he went to share in his buddy's joy, but deep down he didn't approve of Bảy Viễn's womanizing ways.

But in these chaotic times, no matter how lively a wedding was, it got swallowed up by the waves of history. During the honeymoon week, Bảy Viễn still worried about supporting his troops. Once you've swung the axe, you've got to carry the log. The more followers he recruited, the more it cost. His personal money ran out fast. The people's donations weren't enough either. Bảy Viễn had to think of tougher measures: confiscating rice boats and pig boats at Cầu Ông Lãnh market, along the Cây Khô canal, the Bình Điền river, and so on. But the trading boats were getting scarce too because of all the robbery along the routes.

Bảy Viễn then figured he'd squeeze money from the rich folks in Sài Gòn - Chợ Lớn.

Back in the day, Bảy Viễn used to gamble big at places like Sáu Ngọ's casino. When the revolution blew up, the casinos shut down fast, but Bảy Viễn still remembered the big-time gamblers who'd bet fortunes. One of these gambling kings was Cậu Ba Huy, nicknamed the Bạc Liêu Playboy, son of Hội Đồng Trạch — the richest man in Bạc Liêu province. That old man named his kids after aquatic creatures. The father was Trạch (Otter), the firstborn was Hai Đinh (Softshell Turtle), the third son was Ba Quy (Turtle), the eighth was Tám Bò (...). 

Cậu Ba Quy studied in France and came back. Not liking the name Quy, he changed it to Huy — Trần Trinh Huy.

Nobody knows what Cậu Ba studied in France. When he got back, he just loafed around, traveled here and there, spent money like water — even bought a plane to fly his father from Bạc Liêu down to Cà Mau to check on the rice fields. At that time, only two people in Vietnam "played" with planes: Emperor Bảo Đại and Cậu Ba Huy.

A guy who'd been to France, used to the high life, a dandy, found the province lacking amenities — no nightclubs, no dance halls, no casinos — so Cậu Ba moved to Sài Gòn, rented a house, and stayed there permanently, only going back to the countryside now and then to pick up money and blow it all.

Bảy Viễn sent men to scout things out and learned that Ba Huy lived in a villa in Phú Thọ Hòa, with five or six bodyguards and a pack of German Shepherds. Attacking that villa wouldn't be easy...

Among Bảy Viễn's troops was a printing worker and martial artist named Tám Tâm.

When Big Brother Bảy Viễn asked who dared break into Ba Huy's villa to deliver a letter of "invitation" from Military Commissioner Lê Văn Viễn, Tám Tâm raised his hand.

"Do you even know Ba Huy before volunteering?"
"Yes. I know the Bạc Liêu Playboy well. He's got a young wife named Tuyết Nhung who owned the Tuyết Nhung casino on Chasseloup Street. I've seen Cậu Ba go there every evening to take Tuyết Nhung to Chợ Lớn for Chinese dinner."
"Then I'm assigning you the mission — go invite Ba Huy for me."

Nguyên Hùng


---


Công Tử Bạc Liêu

Bảy Viễn asked Tám Tâm, "How'd you get inside Ba Huy's villa?"

Tám Tâm was sure of himself. "I got my own plan. Just need you to sew me two fancy suits, get me a gold-tipped cane, and one thousand đồng. Once I snatch Ba Huy and bring him back here, I'll need to borrow your American car and a driver too."

Bảy Viễn's eyes went wide looking at Tám Tâm. "That's a hell of a list you're asking for. Two suits, a gold-tipped cane, a thousand đồng cash money. And now the American car on top of that..."

Tám Tâm laughed. "That ain't much, ông Bảy. Think of it like this — you're throwing out a shrimp to catch a fish. The Young Master of Bạc Liêu'll pour millions into your Phú Thọ army, a hundred thousand times what you're putting down now."

"You mind telling me how you're gonna spend that thousand đồng? Money ain't the issue, I just wanna know your method."

"I like keeping my plan under wraps. Long as it gets the job done. Afterward I'll lay it all out for you with every detail. The surprise'll make it even sweeter, ông Bảy."

Bảy Viễn grinned. "Alright. But remember one thing — get it done, and I'll shell out whatever it takes. But if you fuck this up, don't bother coming back to see me. You hear?"

Three days later, Tám Tâm was decked out in his fancy suit, swinging a gold-tipped cane, walking into Tuyết Nhung's gambling den on Chasseloup street. He introduced himself as a big landowner from Cần Thơ up in Sài Gòn for a change of scenery. Heard Tuyết Nhung's joint was where all the heavy hitters hung out, so he figured he'd try his luck and rub elbows with the high-class crowd of the Pearl of the Far East.

Seeing Tám Tâm's high-roller threads, Tuyết Nhung let him right in.

At the tables, win or lose, Tám Tâm carried himself smooth and classy, making a name as the most refined gentleman in the gambling scene. After three straight days and nights of playing, he casually mentioned to the house boss, "Couple days I'm heading back to Cần Thơ. I'd love to hit up Cậu Ba's — the Young Master of Bạc Liêu's — place for a few days, just to satisfy the itch."

Tuyết Nhung beamed. "Cậu Ba's real picky about who he lets in. But an ông Tám like you — you got the credentials to get into his club. Let me give you the address and the signal."

"Got a signal too?"

"You bet. Keeps uninvited guests out. And remember — roll up in a car. Park in front of the gate, honk three times — two short, one long. The guard'll ask who sent you, you say Tuyết Nhung, and he'll open the gate for you to drive in."

Now that he had the address and the signal, Tám Tâm went back to Bảy Viễn. "Tonight I'm borrowing your American car, ông Bảy, to bring the Young Master of Bạc Liêu back here."

Bảy Viễn looked Tám Tâm over — sharp in his egg-yolk-colored tussor suit with a red tie — and nodded approvingly. "You told me within a week you'd bring Ba Huy here. If you drag his ass out here tonight, I'll give you a fat bonus for finishing ahead of schedule."

That night, Bảy Viễn's Chevrolet pulled up in front of the Song Tùng villa.

The driver honked three times, just like the signal. A guard came out, Tám Tâm flashed Tuyết Nhung's business card, the guard opened the gate, and the car rolled into the courtyard.

Ba Huy came out holding Tuyết Nhung's card introducing the big-spender guest, stepping forward to welcome him.

Tám Tâm sat down proper in the living room before pulling out the official invitation from the Military Commissioner Lê Văn Viễn.

Ba Huy's heart dropped, but he pulled himself together in a few seconds and called out, "Guards!"

Tám Tâm instantly drew his six-shooter and aimed it at Ba Huy. "Calling your security? Don't think so. You're better off coming with me to meet the Military Commissioner and see what this is about. I give you my word — after you see ông Bảy, I'll bring you back safe and sound."

Ba Huy stood up and said, "I already know what the Military Commissioner wants. Here — come here, I'll pay my contribution to the troops." He opened his safe and pulled out several stacks of big bills.

Tám Tâm shook his head. "Cậu Ba's got the wrong idea about ông Bảy. This ain't some old-school kidnapping for ransom. These days, ông Bảy is the Military Commissioner. He invited you here to talk about matters of national importance. Lock your safe back up and come with me."

Thanks to this job, Tám Tâm earned Bảy Viễn's trust and got put in charge of the office. Later on he found out that because he brought the Young Master of Bạc Liêu in so politely, Cậu Ba happily donated three million đồng — a huge sum back then. Through this move, the Young Master of Bạc Liêu proved that the big landowners had just as much love for their country as anybody else.

With his hands on the office and all the paperwork, Tám Tâm got wind of a lot of inside info about Bảy Viễn, both before and after the day they seized power — August 25, 1945. Bảy Viễn's political teacher was the saloon-style politician Trần Văn Ân. Back in his student days, Trần Văn Ân went to France to study Law at Aix-en-Provence, down in the south of France. Graduated with a law degree, came home in 1938, and joined Bùi Quang Chiêu's Constitutionalist Party.

Ân got into politics with a pro-Japanese streak, so the French arrested him and shipped him off to Bà Rá camp, but it wasn't long before the Japanese stepped in and forced the French to let him go. The Japanese took him to Singapore to work as a broadcaster for Radio Shonan. Then Japan brought him back home, where he put out the Hưng Việt newspaper, pushing propaganda for the Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere. It was Trần Văn Ân who introduced Bảy Viễn to Lâm Ngọc Đường, the Director of the PSE (Eastern Region Special Police).

When Bảy Viễn set up the Phú Thọ army, he headquartered it on Hoàng Cung street (formerly called Thomson street by the French).

At first, Bảy Viễn's head treasurer was Maurice Thiên.

When the French retook Sài Gòn on September 23rd, Bảy Viễn pulled back to Cầu Xàng, Đức Hòa. Maurice Thiên stayed behind — he figured he couldn't handle the rough life out in the countryside with none of the comforts.

Bảy Viễn ordered his troops not to fire, to preserve their strength.

The locals watched Bảy Viễn's Phú Thọ boys clutching their guns and running for the hills and they were pissed. The people only gave supplies to the units that actually stood up to the French, that kept the French from rolling into their villages torching chickens and stealing pigs.

Right from the start, Bảy Viễn's army didn't earn much trust from the folks in the outer districts of Gò Vấp and Hốc Môn. But when it came to medical supplies, Bảy Viễn's boys got lucky — they stumbled onto a stash of medicine and medical equipment from Chợ Rẫy hospital. That medical supply cache was put together by head nurse Nguyễn Văn Tư, aka Tư Cao, and his hospital staff, who smuggled it out for the resistance. Before they could figure out which unit to give it to, they ran into the Phú Thọ army.

So head nurse Tư Cao handed the whole lot over to Bảy Viễn.


---


Lực lượng Bình Xuyên

Back in the early days of the resistance, Sài Gòn was crawling with military units named after their home turf — like the Thủ Thiêm unit, the Tân Quy unit, the Bà Quẹo unit — or named after their commander, like Mười Trí's unit, Bảy Viễn's unit, Ba Dương's unit.

On top of all that, there were four regular divisions: the First Division was made up mostly of French colonial troops left behind, commanded by Kiều Công Cung, Trần Tử Oai, Trương Văn Giàu, Nguyễn Văn Quan... The Second Division under Vũ Tam Anh, the Third Division under Nguyễn Hòa Hiệp, and the Fourth Division under Lý Huê Vinh, a half-Chinese guy with a decent education (had his secondary school diploma).

But these divisions were only good for dressing up pretty and marching through the streets — when the bullets started flying, they'd drop their rifles and haul ass. Meanwhile, the guerrilla units wore whatever they had, carried sharpened bamboo spears, daggers, and machetes, but when it came to a real fight, they were like Cáo Lãnh fighting rooks — they'd rather die on the battlefield than run away.

The Bình Xuyên underworld crew got special attention whenever they marched through the streets showing their strength. A whole bunch of scattered groups from Thủ Thiêm, Tân Thuận, Phú Xuân, Tản Quy, Chánh Hưng, Bình Đăng, Cần Giuộc all banded together and elected Ba Dương (Dương Văn Dương) as their commander-in-chief.

The Sài Gòn front was set up by Chairman of the Southern Administrative Committee and Military Commissioner Trần Văn Giàu, organized into five defensive fronts at the five outer city gates.

The Bình Xuyên crew was assigned by Anh Sáu Giàu to hold the area from Tân Thuận bridge to Bình Đông's Cầu Sập, called Front Number 4. Anh Bảy Trân (Nguyễn Văn Trân) — who studied in France and then went to Russia for the Eastern School (also called the Stalin School back in 1927-1930) — was the military commissioner. In the Bình Xuyên command structure, Ba Dương was named commander-in-chief, and ông Tám Mạnh (Nguyễn Văn Mạnh), a martial arts teacher from Chánh Hưng, was the political officer. The departments were set up like this: Ba Dương ran the intelligence unit; Sáu Đối ran weapons procurement; Ba Bang (Trương Văn Bang), district chief of Cần Giuộc, handled logistics; Sáu Tùng, a gangster from Xóm Chiếu, ran the pro-resistance mobilization unit.

The units under Front Number 4 included: Ba Dương's troops at the Rạch Đỉa bridge ferry; Sáu Đối (Trần Văn Đối) and Sáu Thơ's troops at Tân Thuận; Chín Phải (Quách Văn Phải), Tám Mao, and Năm Mười Ba's troops at Tân Quy; Hai Soái's troops at Phú Xuân; Đoàn Văn Ngọc and Chín Mập, Dương Văn Đức's troops at Tân Thuận; Chín Hiệp's troops at the Tân Thanh bridge ferry; Mười Đen's troops at the Khánh Hội docks; Mười Lực (Ngô Văn Lực), Bảy Môn (Võ Văn Môn), Tám Hoe (Nguyễn Văn Hoe)'s troops at Thủ Thiêm; Ký Huỳnh (Nguyễn Văn Huỳnh)'s troops at Bình Đăng; Tám Mạnh (Nguyễn Văn Mạnh)'s troops at Chánh Hưng; Tư Hoạnh's troops at ông Thìn bridge; Ba Bang's troops at Cần Giuộc. Among those leaders, plenty were former bandits and outlaws — like Tư Hoạnh, Ký Huỳnh...

Most of these gangsters were at least somewhat acquainted with Bảy Viễn. So Bảy Viễn wanted to grab the whole underworld bloc that'd joined the resistance and climb up to be their leader. But when it came to talent and character, Bảy Viễn knew he was way behind Ba Dương.

Anh Ba Dương was from Bến Tre, came up to Sài Gòn for work since he was a kid. His main trade was teaching martial arts near Rạch Đỉa bridge in Tân Quy village. Since he had a handle on all the gangs operating along Cây Khô canal, right at Sài Gôn's doorstep, he also ran a side business in informal insurance — meaning if a merchant boat got robbed, he'd track down the goods if someone hired him to.

Sometimes Bảy Viễn would let his real intentions slip, and the Bình Xuyên boys would push right back. Bảy Rô, part of Ba Dương's security detail along with Năm Mười Ba and Chín Mập, would always badmouth Bảy Viễn as a "landlubber" — meaning no roots whatsoever with the river outlaws of Nhà Bè and Cần Giuộc. And they weren't wrong — Bảy Viễn was a strictly land-based crook, knocking over jewelry stores in the outskirts, occasionally getting bold enough to do a heist right in the middle of Chợ Lớn.

In those early resistance days, Bảy Viễn only cared about preserving his forces — whenever the French showed up, he pulled back.

Meanwhile, Ba Dương and Tám Mạnh's combined forces hit a convoy of tugboats on Cây Khô canal (Phước Lộc village, Nhà Bè). They crushed it, capturing a tugboat, a barge, and four boats packed with supplies.

Word of the victory reached the ears of Khu trưởng (Zone Commander) of Zone 7, Nguyễn Bình.

The Zone Commander sent a letter of commendation:

"The whole country has heard how the Bình Xuyên troops fought the enemy with bravery. You men deserve to be called the Southern Liberation Army. In the name of the Zone Command, I send my praise and the sum of three thousand đồng to reward you all."

Signed: Nguyễn Bình.

Seeing that the Bình Xuyên force was the biggest outfit in the Eastern region, Anh Ba Bình dispatched Lương Văn Trọng and Nguyễn Văn Lội as zone representatives to work alongside Ba Dương. It was a smart pick, because anh Trọng was a worker originally from Hải Phòng who'd come south to live since early 1940. When the resistance broke out, Hai Trọng joined Ba Dương's troops and earned anh Ba's trust since he had education (high school diploma) and political savvy. It was Hai Trọng that Ba Dương sent to the An Phú Xã conference (Gò Vấp) when the central representative Nguyễn Bình had just arrived in the South via the Central Highlands. At this military conference, Nguyễn Bình brought together the scattered military units to form multiple chi đội (battalions), numbered 1 through 25. Ba Dương's troops got organized into two chi đội (numbers 2 and 3). Later on he handed command to Năm Hà (Dương Văn Hà), his half-brother. Chi đội 2 was commanded by Năm Chẳng, and Chi đội 3 by Mười Lực (Võ Văn Lực). Nguyễn Bình took a liking to Hai Trọng and appointed him the Zone representative alongside Ba Dương's Bình Xuyên forces.

Beyond Chi đội 2 and 3, there was Chi đội 4 under Mười Trí, the chi đội of father-and-son ông Tám Mạnh and Hai Vĩnh (Mai Văn Vĩnh), Chi đội 9 under Bảy Viễn, Chi đội 21 under Tư Hoạnh, and Chi đội 25 under Tư Ty — all Bình Xuyên, placed under Ba Dương's command. These seven chi đội were collectively called the Bình Xuyên Inter-Zone, which later played a massively important role in the early years of the anti-French resistance.

As for Tám Tâm, thanks to his exploit of kidnapping the Young Master of Bạc Liêu, Ba Huy, and securing three million đồng to fund the troops, Bảy Viễn promoted him to Chief of Staff of Chi đội 9. Tám Tâm had a gift for shooting a pistol with either hand — dead accurate. Every time they pulled back to preserve their strength, Tám Tâm was always the one covering the rear for Bảy Viễn. But after the French attacked Cầu Xàng, Tám Tâm lost his post as Chief of Staff, and Bảy Viễn stepped into a historic turning point in his life as a military commander.


---


Tướng Leclerc tới Sài Gòn

October 5, 1945 — General Leclerc rolled into Sài Gòn. This was a historic day for the French in the biggest city of Cochinchina. Ever since the Japanese coup — March 9, 1945 — the French had been getting slaughtered and locked up by the Japanese in the Ong-dèm camp (IIe Régiment d'Infanterie Coloniale, the 2nd Colonial Infantry Regiment).

It wasn't until General Gracey's British-Indian forces arrived in Sài Gòn to disarm the defeated Japanese that the French finally got freed.

Facing the fierce spirit of the Vietnamese people who'd just snatched power back from the Japanese, the French in Sài Gòn couldn't strut around like they used to. As the common folk put it, the French back then were "like drowned cats." They were terrified of the guerrilla squads — sure, they were armed with sharpened bamboo spears, daggers, and machetes, but their willingness to die for independence was plain for everyone to see. In that situation, the warship Triomphant pulling into Sài Gòn packed with soldiers was a huge morale boost for the French. Next came General Leclerc touching down at Tân Sơn Nhất. A Jeep immediately whisked him to the Governor's Palace (now the Reunification Hall).

Right there, thousands of people stood in the blistering heat and rain to welcome the famous general who'd liberated Paris and ended Germany's occupation of France. Full of himself, Leclerc bragged: "We'll wipe out the Việt Minh in three weeks."

And Leclerc started pushing outward, taking the Eastern provinces like Biên Hòa, Thủ Dầu Một, then turning his spearhead toward the West, hitting Long An, Mỹ Tho...

Reading the situation — the French backed by British-Indian troops and even the defeated Japanese — Military Commissioner Trần Văn Giàu ordered all armed units to pull back to the outskirts and fight guerrilla-style. During this time, Bảy Viễn's Phú Thọ forces withdrew to Bến Phân bridge (Gò Vấp), then retreated deeper to Cầu Xàng (Đức Hòa).

Facing the hurricane assault of the French expeditionary force, Bảy Viễn had his troops bury their weapons and disguise themselves as civilians, scattering to the winds.

Once the enemy pulled out, Bảy Viễn ordered Tám Tâm to go collect all the guns they'd hastily buried during the retreat. The job took three days.

When Tám Tâm got back, he found two strangers in the office. Bảy Viễn introduced them: "Anh Tám, these are anh Tư Sang and anh Năm Tài — anh Tư Thiên introduced them to me. These two are highly educated, they've got the chops to give our Phú Thọ army some real firepower. So I'm putting anh Năm Tài in charge of the office. Anh Tám, you'll stay on as deputy chief. You okay with that?"

Tám Tâm sized up the new guys before answering Bảy Viễn. "If anh Bảy found people with the skills to help our troops, I oughta be happy, not offended. Whatever job I'm given, I'll give it my all to push this resistance to victory as fast as possible."

He said that, but inside, Tám Tâm started quietly digging into those two characters that Maurice Thiên had introduced to Bảy Viễn.


---


# Even Shoes Have Their Luck

Even Shoes Have Their Luck

Following orders from Regional Commander Nguyễn Bình, every unit out in the Eastern region set up a city operations squad — part intelligence, part reconnaissance, part fund-and-medicine collectors for the troops, part liaisons to the deep-pocketed sympathizers still living inside the city. Tucked inside those squads was always one or two armed cells packing pistols and grenades, working the outskirts and sometimes slipping deep into the working-class neighborhoods of the inner city — markets like Bình Tây, Hòa Bình, Bàn Cờ, Cầu Muối, Cầu Ông Lãnh.

One day the team leader reported he'd run into a man named Thomas Phước, just back from Côn Đảo, an old friend of Bảy Viễn.

Bảy Viễn jumped on it: "Thomas Phước was my teacher on the island. Must've come back on that Phú Quốc ship that went out to pick up prisoners in September '45. Where's he at now?"

— Cầu Muối, Boresse street — what they call Ký Con now.

They met up in a little alley off Dumortier (Cô Bắc street). Bảy Viễn was damn glad to see him. The two of them sat around a few Tiger beers and traded stories, talking from the gut.

Thomas Phước said:

"I'm happy for you, kid. You picked the right road. Used to be outlaws, now you're a bush fighter. If I was still young, I'd sign up for your Unit 9 in a heartbeat."

"Never too late. Come be my advisor."

Thomas Phước laughed:

"Don't mix peacetime with wartime, kid. In peacetime, sure, I could advise you. But wartime — here today, gone tomorrow — how the hell am I supposed to keep up with your marches? Let me tell you about Côn Đảo instead. You remember that bastard Dao? After the Japs pulled out of Côn Đảo, Dao killed Bonifacy — the worst villain on that island. End of August '45, word came the Việt Minh had taken power in Sài Gòn. Everybody was fired up, getting ready to head back to the mainland. But I told 'em to keep their hopes in check: if a ship shows up to pick up prisoners, it's only gonna take the political ones. Why'd they bother hauling common criminals back? Same shit, different day. And sure enough, the Phú Quốc and twenty-odd boats that came end of September only took the political prisoners. The common criminals were crushed. A bunch of 'em built their own boats to get back — including Sơn Vương, the guy who'd been locked up longest on the island. A few days later the Phú Quốc went back out. This time they cleaned house — politicals AND common criminals. The ones still there elected an Administrative Committee for Côn Đảo. Sơn Vương won Chairman. See what I mean, kid? Everybody's got a number written for 'em. From prisoner straight to chairman..."

Bảy Viễn nodded, taking it in:

"Even shoes have their luck — how much more so a man? Anyway, let's talk about fighting the French. Big brother, you're sitting right in the middle of occupied territory — that's dangerous as hell. The cops'll grab you sooner or later. If they catch you, you're shipping back to the island, and this time you'll be buried at Hàng Keo. Come with me instead."

Thomas Phước shook his head:

"I ain't some crab hiding in a hole waiting for people to poke a stick in and drag me out. I'm running a suicide squad. Those grenade tosses at the bars around here — that's us."

Bảy Viễn shook his hand:

"Salute you, big brother. You're the real teacher of Bảy Viễn. On the island and on the mainland, you've always been solid, a real man above the rest. I better get going. If you need ammo backup, just say the word — I'll get it to you fast."

A week later, bad news reached Mười Trí:

"Thomas Phước was shot dead by the French during a sweep on Boresse street. His suicide squad fought like hell, shooting and pulling back toward Kitchener street (Nguyễn Thái Học)."

Hearing this, Bảy Viễn ordered Unit 9's operations section to avenge Thomas Phước immediately. Bảy Viễn himself joined in and led one wing.

On Marchaise street, also in the Cầu Muối area, Bảy Viễn shot dead the Gurkha squad leader. Three more Gurkhas dropped on the spot. Afterward, Bảy Viễn held a solemn memorial for the man who'd been his brother in prison and was now a fallen soldier, Thomas Phước.

Thanks to Unit 9's operations squad, Bảy Viễn rebuilt his reputation and made up for those earlier retreats.

As for Tám Tâm, he kept quietly tracking the movements of those two "new recruits" named Lai — new recruits, but somehow they'd landed themselves positions above the old hands.


---


# Major General Ba Dương

Major General Ba Dương

Answering Ba Dương's call to action, the Bình Xuyên units fell over each other killing the enemy and scoring merit points. Gurkha and Japanese soldiers caught walking alone on empty roads — our boys would jump out and snatch their rifles clean.

Bảy Rô and Ba Bay, part of Ba Dương's protection detail at Rạch Đỉa bridge, baited a bunch of Gurkhas to chase them across Rạch Giới bridge, then shot straight into a wasp nest hanging under the middle pillar. The wasps swarmed and stung those Gurkhas a lesson they'd never forget. One got so spooked he threw his rifle and jumped into the river — still couldn't escape the winged suicide squad.

In the attacks on the Khánh Hội customs post, they captured post commander Paul Jean alive, then rode the momentum straight to post number 6, busting out sixty young men locked up inside.

Bảy Viễn's troops hit the Fire Station on Galliéni boulevard (Trần Hưng Đạo), climbed the hundred-meter watchtower, and raised the red flag with the yellow star. Four fighters died, but they got what they wanted: a Việt Minh flag as big as a mattress flying off that tower top.

The battleships Triomphant, Suffren, and the flagship Richelieu landed French troops in Sài Gòn, giving the French army a serious leg up. They took Thủ Dầu Một and Biên Hòa provinces quick and easy. The Euro-African battalion, the Commando Ponchardier company, plus marines with heavy firepower — they rolled in like a storm. Around the same time, the British 20th Infantry Division landed in Sài Gòn. The French were over the moon, like a tiger that just grew wings. By the end of October 1945, Biên Hòa, Thủ Dầu Một, Tân An, and Mỹ Tho had all fallen.

With things looking that grim, General Nguyễn Bình decided to hit back big — win back some of the Resistance's credibility.

The plan was to strike Biên Hòa town right at the start of the new year, while the French were resting easy on their victory high.

The attacking force pulled in all the Eastern units: Unit 1 under Huỳnh Kim Trương (Thủ Dầu Một), Unit 10 under Huỳnh Văn Nghệ (Biên Hòa), Tô Ký's troops (the Hóc Môn–Bà Điểm–Đức Hòa coalition forces), and Đào Sơn Tây's troops (Dĩ An).

The battle went exactly as planned — total victory. What stood out was a women's volunteer squad all the way from Quảng Ngãi, specialists with machetes. How many traitor heads rolled under those blades...

The win electrified the people of Biên Hòa. After that, a flood of intellectuals and respectable folk abandoned the city for the resistance zones.

Right before Tết 1946, Nguyễn Bình got an urgent message from Bùi Sĩ Hùng — a medical student from Hà Nội who'd come south to fight on the Bến Tre front — saying the An Hóa–Giao Hóa front was surrounded by the enemy and needed reinforcements.

Nguyễn Bình left Tân Uyên immediately for Long Thành and ordered Ba Dương:

"Brother Ba, take a strong force down to Bến Tre, break the siege at An Hóa–Giao Hóa. Let the men celebrate Tết early, then march within the three days of Tết."

After the holiday, the column set out, crossed the Soài Rạp river, cut through Gò Công territory straight to Bến Tre, fighting whatever enemy got in their way, morale through the roof. At Châu Bình they ran straight into a big enemy sweep.

An office secretary shoved a stack of personal tax papers in front of Ba Dương:

"The French got us surrounded on all sides. Only way out is to ditch the uniforms. Here — take one of these, pretend you're a civilian, slip through the ring."

Ba Dương tore the tax paper to shreds and said, stone-cold serious:

"A commander who runs when he meets the enemy ain't a commander at all! Listen to me, men: hide your weapons, ditch the uniforms. We'll rally after."

A couple of Spitfires strafed the area to clear the way for the infantry.

Ba Dương didn't duck into a trench — he ran circles around a haystack instead. But he was a step too slow. A bullet caught him and he sprawled face-down into a paddy ditch.

With the commander gone, the troops lost their spirit. All they could do was find their way back to Rừng Sác.

The day Ba Dương fell was February 20, 1946 — the 19th day of the first lunar month, Year of the Dog (according to another source, he died on the 6th day of Tết, i.e., February 7, 1946).

The news of Ba Dương's death sent Bảy Viễn right back to his old ambition: becoming the supreme leader of Bình Xuyên.


---


# His Excellency the Deputy Regional Commander

His Excellency the Deputy Regional Commander

Ba Dương was shot dead by a Spitfire at his command post in Bình Phương hamlet, Châu Bình commune, on February 17, 1946. Deputy Commander Trần Văn Đối — Sáu Đối — led the Bình Xuyên troops (Joint Units 2 and 3) back to Rừng Sác. A week of fighting through a bloody corridor and they made it back to their own turf.

When the news hit, Năm Hà and the other Bình Xuyên commanders hurried to hold a memorial for their big brother. During the ceremony, Năm Hà read out the posthumous promotion — Comrade Dương Văn Dương, Major General of the Government — and read President Hồ's letter of condolence to the family.

The news of Ba Dương's death stirred up an ambition that had been brewing in Bảy Viễn for a long time.

Bảy Viễn figured he was the one to replace Ba Dương as leader of the Bình Xuyên Inter-Zone — which covered seven numbered units: 2, 3, 4, 7, 9, 21, and 25. Almost all the commanders of those units were close friends of his: Mười Trí, Tư Hoạnh, Tư Ty. The only holdouts were Năm Hà — Ba Dương's half-brother on the father's side — and the father-son pair of ông Tám Mạnh and Hai Vĩnh, who didn't like where Bảy Viễn was heading.

Just as Bảy Viễn had predicted, the Bình Xuyên boys elected Năm Hà to replace Ba Dương as head of the Bình Xuyên Inter-Zone. But Bảy Viễn and his two right-hand men — Tư Hoạnh and Tư Ty — all said Năm Hà didn't have the stature to command.

That night, Bảy Viễn called a meeting of everyone from platoon leader up to sound out opinions on him running against Năm Hà for the Inter-Zone Command. Under Bảy Viễn's banner were two characters we've met before: Ba Rùm — Mười Trí's nephew from Bình Thới, the guy who'd been part of the "singing party" at the Bình Triệu lumberyard and pulled a twelve-year Côn Đảo sentence — and Năm Bé, a Hải Phòng boy who'd drifted south, talked tough at the Khánh Hội docks, got shipped to Côn Đảo, and hitched a ride back to the mainland on Bảy Viễn's raft.

Bảy Viễn trusted these two especially — he'd known them since before the war. He put Năm Bé in charge of personnel and Ba Rùm on weapons.

In the meeting, most agreed with Bảy Viễn that Năm Hà wasn't fit to command Bình Xuyên. The man with the stature to raise the Bình Xuyên banner high had to be Bảy Viễn. So Bảy Viễn drafted letters to all the units in the Inter-Zone, calling a meeting to pick a replacement for Brother Ba Dương.

Before that big meeting, Bảy Viễn had already decided to move his command post from Vườn Thơm (Tân Nhựt–Bình Chánh) down to Rừng Sác — a tangle of rivers and canals wedged between two big waterways, the Lòng Tàu and the Soài Rạp. Rừng Sác's position was critical: it sat right across the water route from Vũng Tàu into Sài Gòn, and from the Western provinces into Chợ Lớn. But it came with serious drawbacks — no fresh water (it was all saltwater mangrove), no solid ground for houses, so people built huts on stilts floored with melaleuca or mangrove planks. On the flip side, there was shrimp and fish year-round.

Why'd Bảy Viễn pick Rừng Sác over Vườn Thơm? After the French hit Sài Gòn and pushed out into the provinces, the Bình Xuyên forces scattered — each unit found its own best spot to set up for the long haul. Joint Units 2 and 3 headed to Rừng Sác. Ba Dương picked Phước An in Long Thành district. Ông Tám Mạnh and his son-in-law Hai Vĩnh left Chánh Hưng for Long Sơn island — also called Bà Trao or Nứa Mountain — to set up their base. Long Sơn island sat between Bà Rịa and Vũng Tàu, a strategic spot for both sea and land routes.

Rừng Sác also had the old Lý Nhơn base — also called Xóm Tiều — which was the headquarters of the great Bình Tây marshal Trương Định, who a hundred years earlier had burned the French ship Espérance on the Nhựt Tảo river.

Moving the headquarters was tough and dangerous. On the roads, military trucks ran day and night. On the rivers, patrol boats blocked traffic.

Bảy Viễn was still worrying when luck smiled on him.

The Democratic Republic of Vietnam signed the Preliminary Agreement of March 6, 1946, with France.

On March 5, British troops pulled out of Vietnam — one less dangerous enemy to deal with.

The conference to elect a commander for the Bình Xuyên Inter-Zone went ahead. Only the unit commanders and deputy commanders within the zone were invited. Knowing what Bảy Viễn was after, the father-son pair Tám Mạnh and Hai Vĩnh lobbied Mười Lực, Nam Chẳng, Năm Hà, and Sáu Đối not to vote for Bảy Viễn.

Without enough support, Bảy Viễn had to shelve his bid for the top spot.

But come May 1946, luck swung back Bảy Viễn's way.

Regional Commander Nguyễn Bình signed the order appointing Bảy Viễn as Deputy Commander of War Zone 7. The second deputy was Huỳnh Văn Nghệ — Tám Nghệ — commander of Unit 10 operating in Biên Hòa province.

That was one hell of an honor for an ex-gangster who'd joined the resistance! All the small-timers and big-fish under Bảy Viễn's command decided to throw a massive celebration for their chief's new title.

Appointed Deputy Regional Commander, Bảy Viễn threw a party.

The news that Unit 9 commander Lê Văn Viễn had been promoted to Deputy Regional Commander — standing right behind Regional Commander Nguyễn Bình and above the second deputy Huỳnh Văn Nghệ of Unit 10 — came as a surprise to Bảy Viễn and his men alike.

Those two — Tài and Sang — figured this was a move to tie Bảy Viễn tight to the resistance, so they started hinting around: "Don't you think there's something off about this promotion, ông Bảy?"

"What's off about it?" Bảy Viễn snapped at Năm Tài.

"What's off is that Unit 9's combat record doesn't hold a candle to Unit 10's. Tám Nghệ fought some huge battles at Tràng Táo, Bảo Chánh, Đồng Xoài, Bàu Cá, and so on. Yet Nguyễn Bình only made Tám Nghệ second deputy, ranking below you. Ain't that strange?"

Bảy Viễn nodded slowly:

"You've got a point. I don't have as many achievements as Tám Nghệ. But who knows — maybe Nguyễn Bình put me ahead of Tám Nghệ for some reason. Maybe because I'm notorious in the underworld. He wants to raise me up as a banner — call out to all the hotshots still in the city to follow Bảy Viễn's example and join the resistance out here."

Năm Tài thought it over:

"Communists don't do anything without an angle. They're just looking out for their own interest."

Bảy Viễn nodded:

"True. But their interest is our interest too. When the flag comes to your hand, why wouldn't you wave it?"

Năm Tài:

"Agreed — ông Bảy's gotta wave it, and wave it big. We should throw a real grand ceremony to receive the Deputy Commander title, show off our power. If you're on board, I'll head up the organizing committee."

"Fine! Fine! Invite every unit commander from the Eastern region. Make it a spectacle."

Năm Tài:

"And the big shots from the city too, ông Bảy. I'll draft invitations to Lâm Ngọc Đường, Maurice Thiên — and the religious leaders too: the Pope of Caodaism Phạm Công Tắc, the Hòa Hảo founder Huỳnh Phú Sổ..."

Bảy Viễn nodded:

"Right! Invite the VIPs from the city. We gotta make this ceremony big enough to match the guests."

Tư Sang chimed in:

"I'll handle the guesthouse construction and the stage. We'll have a generator running all night, a full band playing both traditional and modern."

Bảy Viễn lit up:

"Good! I love traditional music. Get the city musicians down here to perform — make it lively. Especially vọng cổ. Late night, sipping a drink and listening to six verses — that's the real deal."

Tư Sang hurried off to find Hai Dậu, head of Unit 9's cultural troupe.

Hai Dậu was from Trà Vinh, had been learning the moon lute since he was a kid. He'd played for big troupes like Huỳnh Kỳ and Phước Cương until the war broke out. He was jobless for a while, then went up to Sài Gòn to play in cafés with live music. When he couldn't stay in the city entertaining folks anymore, Hai Dậu packed his lute, headed into the war zone, and joined Bảy Viễn. Around the same time, the musician Ngọc Thới — Bảy Viễn's wife's nephew — also came out to the bush and joined his uncle-in-law's unit. The two of them — one modern, one traditional — came together to make Unit 9's cultural troupe a real crowd-pleaser.

Hai Dậu followed Tư Sang's orders and went into the city to recruit famous musicians for the party celebrating ông Bảy's promotion to Deputy Commander. Hai Dậu was close with the famous singer Năm Cần Thơ, so he went straight to her restaurant to sign a contract. Worth noting: Hai Dậu was a ladies' man, had been sweethearts with the lead actresses of many big troupes, including Năm Cần Thơ. When Hai Dậu asked her to come out to the zone for a few days, Năm Cần Thơ agreed on the spot.

She said:

"You're out there fighting up a storm, and I'm stuck in the city, singing every night for the war-profiteering fat cats. Makes me ashamed. I'm coming out with you, anh Hai, at least once — got to see what ông Bảy Viễn looks like."

With Năm Cần Thơ on board, Hai Dậu swung by the Tabarin dance hall — now called Văn Cảnh, on Phạm Ngũ Lão–Calmette street — and picked up two musician friends: Chín Minh and Lê Yên.

Chín Minh was a famous artist, could play all kinds of instruments — wind, violin, guitar.

Lê Yên was a trumpet specialist. Both were happy to come out to the zone for a "change of scenery."

So Hai Dậu completed his mission for Tư Sang.

Năm Tài drafted the invitation letters to the city big shots, all to build up the reputation of His Excellency the Deputy Regional Commander.

The word "Excellency" in front of "Deputy Regional Commander" sounded strange. At first Bảy Viễn found it odd, but Tư Sang explained:

"We're dealing with city VIPs — we gotta use their language. Can't talk to 'em like peasants. The educated classes live by proper titles — every position has the right form of address. A common soldier or a cook or a foreman, they're 'anh' or 'chú' or 'thầy đội' or 'ông quản.' But from district chief on up, it's 'ngài.' Deputy Regional Commander is equivalent to a colonel — calling him 'Excellency' is spot-on. Don't you worry, ông Bảy."

Năm Tài had a glib tongue, kept buttering him up, and pretty soon Bảy Viễn stopped feeling uncomfortable when Tài called him "His Excellency the Deputy Regional Commander."

But every cadre and resistance fighter found it jarring. And from that point on, the men started keeping their distance from the Deputy Regional Commander with the gangster roots.

— Nguyên Hùng


---


# The Enthronement

The Enthronement

That desolate, mangrove-swamped backwater suddenly turned into a bustling hub of high society — all because of the enthronement ceremony for Deputy Regional Commander Lê Văn Viễn.

They'd stuck the generator way off somewhere so the noise wouldn't bother the guests. Electric lights blazed down onto the river. The reception hall was thrown up with melaleuca poles and nipa-palm leaves, no walls — just like a country market pavilion — strung with flowers and lights that made it look absolutely gorgeous.

The band struck up: all Western music, dance tunes, because the ringleaders of the orchestra, Chín Minh and Lê Yên, were the pillars of the Tabarin dancehall band.

City folks were thrilled, of course — it was what their ears knew. Country folks loved it too, just for the novelty, their hands and feet twitching and itching, going "chaka-chaka-chaka" like monkeys with Saint Vitus' dance.

Tiger Beer, soda pop, sarsi — the tables were loaded. Drink whatever you like.

The grandest moment came when the loudspeakers introduced the distinguished guests who'd come from everywhere: company commanders Năm Hà, Mười Lực, Năm Chẳng, Hai Lung, Mười Trí, Tám Mạnh, Hai Vĩnh, Sáu Đối, Tư Huỳnh, Tư Hoạnh, Tư Ty.

Then came the VIPs down from the city: Lâm Ngọc Đường, Maurice Thiên, Protector Phạm Công Tắc, and教主 Huỳnh Phú Sổ with his trusted disciple Trần Văn Soái, alias Năm Lửa.

Năm Tài, decked out in full formal regalia, was solemnly introducing the dignitaries. Right at that moment, a burst of heavy machine-gun fire ripped through the air. The women guests shrieked and scrambled into their men's arms, looking for cover.

But Năm Tài announced it was just a salute for the newly arrived Second Deputy Regional Commander, Huỳnh Văn Nghệ.

Everybody turned to look at the surprise guest.

Bảy Viễn stood up, gestured to the armchair beside him for Tám Nghệ, then straightened up, swept his eyes across the audience, and gave a slight bow.

Anh Tám had come as an invited guest — but also as the eyes and ears of the Regional Command.

For a long time now, the Region had been getting reports about Bảy Viễn's comings and goings: his close ties with folks from Sài Gòn and the two big religious leaders — Cao Đài and Hòa Hảo. That volley of gunfire just now — was it a welcome, or a warning to the Second Deputy Commander?

A thousand hearsays aren't worth one look. Tám Nghệ had now seen with his own eyes the four city VIPs who'd come down for Bảy Viễn's enthronement.

Năm Tài introduced Lâm Ngọc Đường and Tư Thiên as the chairman and vice-chairman of the Support Committee for Company 9, based in the city.

As for the two biggest religious leaders in the South — Protector Phạm Công Tắc and教主 Huỳnh Phú Sổ — they were special commissioners on the Southern Resistance-Administration Committee. Everybody had clear titles and positions.

Năm Tài solemnly invited Deputy Regional Commander Lê Văn Viễn to speak.

The speech had been written by Tám Tâm and approved by Năm Tài. But a few lines made Năm Tài nearly jump out of his skin: "The Bình Xuyên troops are mostly poor working people who once had to become bandits just to survive from day to day. But now, following the resistance, their hearts and minds have changed. From river wanderers, they've become patriotic soldiers. However, they should watch out for those lowlifes who live it up on the blood and bones of their comrades."

Năm Tài distinctly remembered the speech he'd okayed — those lines definitely weren't in there. So when the hell had Tám Tâm slipped them in?

The moment Bảy Viễn finished reading, Năm Tài asked for the speech back to check:

"Who added these lines?" Năm Tài whipped out a pen, crossed them out, and handed it back to Bảy Viễn.

Bảy Viễn just laughed:

"Nobody added nothing. The speech was typed up clean by Tám Tâm, with your signature at the bottom of every page. And why you fussing over it anyway? It's 'cause of those exact lines the crowd went wild clapping like crazy!"

Smooth as it was, Năm Tài knew Tám Tâm had pulled one over on him. From that day on, he watched Tám Tâm's every move, night and day.

Early evening, modern music had the place jumping and buzzing. But come late night, traditional music took over.

Hai Dậu on the moon-shaped lute, Paul Thin on guitar, Mười Nguyên on the zither, Mười Một on violin, Cô Năm Cần Thơ and then Cô Ba Bến Tre taking turns belting out their signature numbers (already recorded on Asia label records and sold all across the Six Provinces of the South).

The guests, the cadres, the soldiers — most of them were farmers at heart, so they ate up the vọng cổ melodies. The later it got, the more the strings and voices tugged at people's hearts. Bảy Viễn grinned and said:

"To really appreciate vọng cổ, you gotta hear it right here, in the middle of nowhere, in this godforsaken empty plain — that's when it hits you! Listening to vọng cổ in Sài Gòn? Man, it falls flat. Ain't that right, folks?"

The party finally wound down. But for the two religious leaders — Cao Đài and Hòa Hảo — the night was still going. Once all the other guests had gone home, those two stayed behind to discuss confidential matters with the Deputy Regional Commander.

The political groups and religious sects wanted to band together and form an interfaith front — to fight the colonialists, and also to oppose the Việt Minh. They wanted to stand in the middle. Huỳnh Phú Sổ wanted to visit the Bình Xuyên companies, like Joint Company 23, to get acquainted with Năm Hà, Sáu Đối, Mười Lực, Năm Chẳng. Bảy Viễn immediately arranged contact to take the Huỳnh教主 over to Phước An right away. As for Phạm Công Tắc, he wanted to form a brotherhood alliance with Company 4 of Mười Trí.

When Thầy Tư Hòa Hảo (the common name for the Huỳnh教主) and Năm Lửa reached Joint Company 2-3, Chief of Staff Ba Xuân was furious. He'd already heard about the Hòa Hảo's attempted takeover of Cần Thơ town on September 9th, led by Năm Lửa's son Trần Văn Hoành, along with the教主's own cousin Huỳnh Phú Mậu and the poet Việt Châu (special advisor to the Huỳnh教主).

The coup attempt failed. The French caught all three leaders and had them executed. So now what was Thầy Tư Hòa Hảo doing coming to Joint Company 2-3? On high alert, Ba Xuân grabbed his submachine gun, ready to waste the教主 right then and there. But anh Năm Hà, always level-headed, waved him off:

"The man came as a guest. We treat him polite. Whatever business there is, we'll deal with it when it comes. No need to rush. Haste makes waste."

You could say the Huỳnh教主's number was still up that day. Otherwise, Ba Xuân would've sent him to meet his maker right then and there.


---


# The United National Front

The United National Front

Here's a funny thing: during his entire term as First Deputy Regional Commander, Bảy Viễn never once went to Region 7 Headquarters for a meeting.

Bảy Viễn never left his base in the Rừng Sác mangrove forest. Region 7, meanwhile, was first stationed at Tân Hòa, Lạc An (Zone D).

When the French decided to wipe out Zone D with coordinated naval, land, and air assaults — including paratroopers — Nguyễn Bình moved his general headquarters to the edge of the Đồng Tháp Mười wetlands, along the banks of the Vàm Cỏ Đông River. By 1947, they'd pushed deeper into the heart of Đồng Tháp Mười, at the village of Nhơn Hòa Lập, along the canal named after Brigadier General Dương Văn Dương.

So why didn't Bảy Viễn go to regional meetings? In the decision promoting Bảy Viễn, the Regional Command had clearly stated: every command decision must carry two signatures to be valid. And since Bảy Viễn was stationed far from Regional HQ, any decision he made had to be hand-carried to Regional Command to get the additional signature of either Regional Commander Nguyễn Bình or Deputy Commander Tám Nghệ.

On this detail, Năm Tài had whispered to Bảy Viễn:

"Don't want to get scolded by ông Bảy, but if you ask me, this whole thing of Nguyễn Bình making you First Deputy Regional Commander — it's just a pie in the sky!"

Bảy Viễn's temper flared:

"A pie in the sky? Break it down for me, what do you mean?"

"Here's what I mean by pie in the sky: Ông Bảy makes a decision, signs it proper with your First Deputy stamp on it — but that decision ain't worth a damn! You still gotta send it to Nguyễn Bình or Tám Nghệ to countersign before it means anything. So it's a damn pie in the sky, what else?"

Bảy Viễn furrowed his brow:

"The way you spell it out so clear, now I see it. But when Tám Nghệ signs off on something, he still needs Nguyễn Bình's signature too."

Năm Tài gave a sly grin:

"Yeah, but see — Nguyễn Bình and Tám Nghệ are on the same team, two hands of the same body, and that's the Communist party. Ông Bảy, on the other hand, is a patriotic nationalist soldier, no party affiliation. So the nationalist side is the minority, getting squeezed out. You see that or not?"

Bảy Viễn pulled out a letter:

"Nguyễn Bình's inviting me to a meeting at Ba Thu. I'm worried about meeting up with that bastard."

Năm Tài raised both hands like he was blocking him:

"Whoa, no way! Ông Bảy shouldn't go! If you have to, send a representative. Word is, a lot of folks went in there and never came out. Could be they got liquidated, who knows."

Bảy Viễn tossed the letter onto the table:

"Alright then, I'm not going. I'll send Năm Hà from Joint Company 2 to scope things out first."

Năm Tài was delighted — just like the old folks said:

"Better to be a rooster's head than a phoenix's tail. Ông Bảy's already king of Rừng Sác here — ain't that better than being deputy to some river-driftin', market-lost bastard like Nguyễn Bình?"

Bảy Viễn winced:

"You talk too much, I can't keep up! Let me think."

Those poison seeds Năm Tài kept dropping slowly sank into Bảy Viễn's mind.

At the start of 1948, the old fox Bazin and Lieutenant Colonel Savani received orders from High Commissioner Admiral D'Argenlieu to push ahead with forming a United National Alliance Front, made up of reactionary parties opposed to the Việt Minh. Naturally, Cao Đài, Hòa Hảo, and Bình Xuyên were the top priorities.

Leading the charge to side with the French was Trần Quang Vinh. He'd gone over to the French and signed an agreement with the Tây Ninh province chief, pulling Companies 7 and 8 — under Nguyễn Văn Thành and Nguyễn Thành Phong — to guard the Tây Ninh Holy See back in May '46.

Then in August '46, Protector Phạm Công Tắc was released by the French from his exile on the Comoros Islands and came back to build a Cao Đài army to fight the Việt Minh.

Năm Tài had been ordered by the Deuxième Bureau — Military Intelligence — to win Bảy Viễn over to join this Front.

"Ông Bảy's been getting a lot of mentions from people up in the city."

Bảy Viễn laughed:

"Mentioning me for what?"

"People want ông Bảy to take command of a very important organization — the United National Front."

"What front is this?" Bảy Viễn asked.

"Like the name says — it's a united front of all the nationalist parties, fighting the French but also fighting the Communists, meaning the Việt Minh."

Bảy Viễn was startled:

"You got nothing better to do, Năm Tài? I'm the First Deputy Regional Commander of Zone 7. That makes me Việt Minh through and through. How you gonna suggest I stand up against the Việt Minh?"

Năm Tài tried to persuade him:

"Ông Bảy doesn't know the Communists yet. You think they trust your riverfolk brothers? They're just using the wandering men to follow the resistance for as long as it suits them. Even when they gave you the Deputy Commander title, Nguyễn Bình was finding ways to clip your wings. Any decision needs two signatures. One signature from ông Bảy — worthless."

Bảy Viễn furrowed his brow again:

"Tell me more about this United National Front."

"It brings together the strong religious sects — Cao Đài, Hòa Hảo, Catholics, Tịnh Độ lay Buddhists, Protestants — plus all the nationalist anti-Communist parties. They're uniting to force both the French and the Việt Minh to share power with them. Nobody gets a political monopoly... There's gonna be a preliminary meeting soon at Bà Quẹo. Protector Phạm Công Tắc and教主 Huỳnh Phú Sổ will be there."

Bảy Viễn thought about it for a long while:

"I'm sending you to represent me. Go to the meeting, come back and report. Then we'll figure out what to do."


---


# Tax Collection to Feed the Army

Tax Collection to Feed the Army

The Preliminary Accord of March 6, 1946 was signed, under which France recognized the Democratic Republic of Vietnam as a free state within the French Union, with its own government, parliament, army, and finances. Reunification was to be decided by a referendum.

But the colonial bastards never respected that agreement.

Admiral-High Commissioner D'Argenlieu declared the Accord had nothing to do with the South.

Two months later, on May 7, 1946, Colonel Cédille — the Republic's Commissioner — came to the South under D'Argenlieu's orders and set up an autonomous government of Southern Ky, putting Dr. Nguyễn Văn Thinh in as Prime Minister.

Next, they established the United National Front (UNF) to support the puppet Nguyễn Văn Thinh government and, at the same time, to wreck the Việt Minh.

From deep in the swamps, Regional Commander Nguyễn Bình kept tabs on the colonialists' and their lackeys' moves through the newspapers and reports from the Urban Action Committees. Seeing the reactionary role of the UNF, Nguyễn Bình ordered the organization dissolved. The Urban Action Committees scattered leaflets and tossed grenades into the Front's headquarters as a warning to the diehards.

Faced with the strong operations of the army and Urban Action Committees right in their Sài Gòn stronghold, the traitors got spooked and didn't dare strut around like before.

Prime Minister Nguyễn Văn Thinh himself realized he'd taken the wrong path. Victim of political naivety and the French colonialists' scheming, he ended his own life by hanging himself in his private residence in the early hours of Sunday, November 10, 1946.

When Nguyễn Bình dissolved the UNF, Bảy Viễn was worried day and night, not knowing if Nguyễn Bình would make a move against him. But he waited and waited — the Region never said a thing about him "breaking ranks" and "dealing behind the scenes" with the Cao Đài and Hòa Hảo leaders. So Bảy Viễn relaxed a little and, on his own, stopped mingling with those anti-resistance organizations.

The biggest thing on Bảy Viễn's mind at that time was fighting for the position of Bình Xuyên Inter-Region chief — a spot that Ba Dương had held before, and then Năm Hà had taken over.

But by mid-April 1946, Bảy Viễn's dream had gone up in smoke. Anh Năm Hà, Commander of Joint Company 2-3, received an official dispatch from Regional Commander Nguyễn Bình regarding the election of a replacement for the late Brigadier General Ba Dương as Commander-in-Chief of the Bình Xuyên Inter-Region.

The original text read as follows:

"VNCĐH — National Guard — No. 7/3/KB
General Headquarters, April 12, 1946
Nguyễn Bình, Regional Chief, Zone 7, Vietnam

To comrade Dương Văn Hà, Commander of the Bình Xuyên National Guard.

Regarding your appointment to replace comrade Dương, I fully approve. I hope you will not betray the trust and hopes placed in you by the Bình Xuyên soldiers, by myself, and by our late comrade Dương Văn Dương — above all, preserve the reputation of comrade Dương, a man of distinction, unlike the reactionary and factionalist 3rd and 4th Divisions. Only by doing so will you truly be worthy of the spirit of comrade Dương Văn Dương, who sacrificed himself so gloriously for the Fatherland.

Furthermore, I do not approve of borrowing money from the people. The troops should increase their own production so the people's burden is lightened.

Also, I do not permit wives and children to follow the troops, to avoid the chaotic, motley situation seen in the 3rd and 4th Divisions, HT29..."

The news that Năm Hà was officially recognized as Commander-in-Chief of the Bình Xuyên Inter-Region, replacing anh Ba Dương, spread across the entire Eastern region.

Bảy Viễn swallowed his spit and bided his time, waiting for his chance in the years to come.

Though the Region didn't make a move against Bảy Viễn for his involvement with the UNF, Nguyễn Bình started watching his every move closely. Bảy Viễn's leadership style still carried that river-gypsy flavor. There was a wall between the commanders and the soldiers. Most of the company-level officers were gangster types who treated their soldiers like underlings. Battalion-level cadres kept themselves distant from those beneath them. Word was, Bảy Viễn lived like a feudal lord — with plenty of retainers under his command, ready to do whatever he ordered. Luxury goods from Lâm Ngọc Đường and Maurice Thiên never ran short. Out in the middle of the forest, they had red wine, white wine, Martell, Cognac, champagne, Tiger Beer — drank like water. Coffee, canned milk, cocoa — everything the city had, Rừng Sác had too.

But what concerned Nguyễn Bình most wasn't Bảy Viễn's kingly lifestyle — it was how Company 9 collected taxes to feed its troops. Sitting right at the chokehold of the two main rivers leading into Sài Gòn-Chợ Lớn, Company 9 stopped every merchant boat coming from the Central and Western regions and levied taxes on them.

To make sure nothing slipped through, Company 9 set up tax stations at every river mouth and canal junction.

Seeing that tax collection was a goldmine, Bảy Viễn appointed Ba Tuấn — son of Hội đồng Thì from Bến Tranh, Dầu Tiếng — as head of the tax bureau.

Ba Tuấn had his high school diploma. When the resistance broke out, he didn't know where to enlist, so his father told him to follow Uncle Bảy Viễn, an old acquaintance.

Once he became Chief of Tax Affairs for Company 9, Ba Tuấn started to see how hard it was on the merchant folk plying the rivers.

Every military unit had the right to flag down trading boats and collect taxes. So a single boat carrying fish sauce from Phan Thiết up, or one hauling duck eggs from Mỹ Tho down, had to pay tax at how many different checkpoints?

Ba Tuấn called a meeting with all the companies to divide up tax collection zones — so they wouldn't step on each other's toes and, more importantly, spare the river merchants some misery.

But the turf war over tax collection eventually led to open conflict between Company 9 and the Zone's Finance Department.


---


# There and Back... Not!

There and back... not!

While the other units out in the Eastern region were busy hustling on production under orders from Zone Commander Nguyễn Bình — trying to lighten the load on the people so they wouldn't have to fork over so much to feed the troops — Unit 9 was out collecting taxes from the merchant boats.

The districts of Nhà Bè, Cần Đước, Cần Giuộc all had their own tax collection boards. Those boys hit up every boat that floated by. The river traders were crying bloody murder!

Then Ba Tuấn took over as head of Unit 9's tax department and finally brought some order to the mess.

But the biggest tax cow for the resistance was right inside Sài Gòn - Chợ Lớn itself. The big companies and factories were sending in money every year to support the troops.

The ones coughing up the most were the Western pharmacy owners. They didn't just send cash — they shipped medicine into the war zone too.

Later on, two big-shot pharmacists with Western drugstores in Sài Gòn — Hồ Thu (from Phan Thiết) and Bùi Quang Tùng (from Bến Tre) — figured money and medicine weren't enough. So they ditched the city and headed into the resistance zone themselves.

As for the Chinese in Chợ Lớn, some paid up, some didn't. The owner of the Đại Thế Giới restaurant used the excuse that he'd already paid taxes to the French, so he couldn't pay the Việt Minh too. Well, that called for strong-arm tactics. The Action Committee hatched a plan to kidnap him and bring him into the zone, where they sat him down and explained: any businessman making money on Vietnamese soil had an obligation to pay income tax to the Vietnamese government. The French were just temporary occupiers. The Sài Gòn - Chợ Lớn Special Zone had to hold a full-blown trial and drag that Đại Thế Giới owner into court before he'd finally shut up and accept it.

Back to Unit 9's Tax Board in Chợ Lớn. The man in charge was Mr. Bồ Văn Kiểu.

Now Kiểu was an artist at heart. When he was young he studied boxing, he was crazy about music and singing, he ran with cải lương performers, and he was tight with all the athletes — soccer players, tennis players, cyclists.

When we seized power, Kiểu was put in charge as Warden of Sài Gòn's Big Prison. When the French attacked Sài Gòn on September 23, 1945, they put him in charge of the Hiệp Hòa Sugar Factory. Later on, Bảy Viễn asked him to handle tax collection at the rice warehouses in Chợ Lớn.

Most of the rice mills were down at Bình Đông wharf, far from the city center and right on the waterways, right next to the canals. The rice barges from the Mekong Delta hauled their loads up there to sell to the warehouses.

Almost all the warehouse owners were Chinese.

If you wanted to collect taxes, you had to be there on the spot, ready to go — like the country folk say, "hit wherever you land" or "put a name to a face." The Chinese didn't give a damn about politics, they just wanted to do business. So collecting taxes was no walk in the park.

Kiểu was practically there day and night at the warehouses.

In peacetime that wouldn't be a big deal, but this was wartime. The French were always patrolling, especially in the outskirts — what they called "xôi đậu" — meaning in the morning it belonged to the French, but from dusk onward it was ours. Several times a week Kiểu had to "chém vè" — slash and run — whenever he ran into a French sweep.

Because he was so aggressive about collecting taxes, the revenue Kiểu brought in for Unit 9 was massive.

Word got around to the Zone's Finance Committee.

The head of the Zone Finance Committee was a guy with no long-term vision. All he saw was his own unit's interests, so he figured his boys should grab this fat revenue stream for themselves. He reported to the Zone that the tax station at the Bình Đông rice warehouses should be shut down — claiming all that money was just letting Bảy Viễn live high on the hog while Unit 9's soldiers were still going without everything.

But here's where it gets ugly. Nobody knows exactly what he reported, but one pitch-black night, Kiểu's boat was tied up along the canal bank when several bursts of submachine gun fire ripped through it. Bồ Văn Kiểu died right there on the spot.

Nobody claimed responsibility for those gunshots. All anybody knew was that a capable, courageous tax officer — a man willing to stick his neck out in the outskirts to collect taxes so the troops could eat — had died in some mysterious hit.

When Professor Hồ Văn Lái — who used to teach art at Pétrus Ký High School and was now heading up the Propaganda Department of the Sài Gòn - Chợ Lớn Special Zone stationed in Vườn Thơm — heard the news, he was stunned. He told his friends:

"I knew Bồ Văn Kiểu. The man had an artist's soul. For him to stick close to those rice warehouses in Bình Đông took more guts than most — the French were coming and going every single day. What a shame — the tax boards fighting each other over turf got Kiểu killed for nothing."

When Bảy Viễn got word that Bồ Văn Kiểu had been killed, he roared:

"You bastards find out which son of a bitch snuffed my tax officer. I'm betting it's that prick on the Zone Finance Committee. He wants to grab the tax revenue for himself. I'm ordering you boys to waste him, and if there's heat I'll take it!"

The turf war over tax collection between Unit 9 and the Zone Finance Committee got uglier by the day.

Sometimes both sides would fire warning shots at each other.

The Zone had to call a meeting to sort out this mess of unauthorized tax collections. Nguyễn Bình sent a letter inviting the First Zone Deputy Commander Bảy Viễn to come to Zone HQ for a sit-down. But Bảy Viễn was scared Nguyễn Bình would waste him — just like Tài and Sang kept whispering in his ear every day and night — so he sent Năm Hà instead.

At first Năm Hà was scared to go to Zone HQ too — he'd heard the rumors: "There and back... not!"

Năm Hà demanded that the Zone send Nguyễn Bình's personal secretary, anh Hai Trọng (Lương Văn Trọng), down to Liên chi 2~3 as a hostage before he'd agree to go. The suspicion between them was ridiculous at first, but really, it was understandable — the Deuxième Bureau was deliberately sowing division between the Communist Việt Minh and the Nationalist resistance elements.

When Năm Hà got to Zone HQ and saw Nguyễn Bình and Tám Nghệ treating him like family, his suspicions melted away. Coming back to Phước An (Long Thành), Năm Hà laughed and told Hai Trọng:

"There was nothing to it! Up there's just like down here — everybody's got the same goal of fighting the French. There's no split between intellectuals and gangsters, or between nationalists and communists. Clear as day — we've been had by the divisionalists."


---


# Every Soul for Themselves

Every soul for themselves.

Even though the United National Front (also called the National Unified Front) had been broken up by Lieutenant General Nguyễn Bình, the Pope-Protector Phạm Công Tắc kept pushing the Cao Đài army hard, attacking the mountain and jungle zones and grabbing territory from the liberated areas. The Protector was in a "ride the tiger" situation — once you climb onto that tiger's back, jumping off means the colonialists will eat you alive.

On our side, Zone 7 and Tây Ninh Province were pushing for broader unity. A lot of people reached out to the high-ranking clergy in the Holy See, inviting them to join the Việt Minh and stirring up Cao Đài followers to join the resistance like they did in the first days after seizing power. But the high-ranking Cao Đài clergy sided with the Protector against the Việt Minh and turned into effective tools for the colonialists. With the French supplying them guns and ammo and paying them monthly salaries, the Cao Đài army kept marching non-stop, putting the squeeze on the free zones.

In that situation, Zone 7 formed allied forces to stand up to the Cao Đài army that was driving deep into the free zone.

Through intelligence, Zone 7 found out the French were going to hold a weapons-handover ceremony for the Cao Đài army at the Tây Ninh Holy See on New Year's Eve of the Đinh Hợi Year of the Pig (January 21, 1947).

Zone 7's Chief of Staff Huỳnh Kim Trương pushed for a strike right at the enemy headquarters to bust up the weapons handover. But the attack didn't achieve what they wanted — neither militarily nor politically.

Pope-Protector Phạm Công Tắc passed out leaflets explaining that the Holy See had faked surrender to get the French weapons so they could later fight the French themselves. He called on the Việt Minh to hand over the Tây Ninh battlefield to them. At the same time, the Holy See invited the Việt Minh to send representatives into the Holy See for talks.

The Zone appointed three men: Dương Minh Châu, Nguyên Hữu Dụ, and Trần Văn Đẩu to go in and negotiate with the high-ranking Cao Đài clergy over two days.

In the end, the meeting produced nothing.

But the aftermath was a damn shame: no sooner had they gotten back to base than the French launched a sweep and hit the exact command post. Lawyer Dương Minh Châu — Chairman of the Provincial Administrative Resistance Committee — was killed!

The purges got uglier and uglier. Innocent people caught between two warring forces just kept falling.

Those were the dark years on the Eastern battlefield.

Seeing all this, Bảy Viễn pulled further and further away from Nguyễn Bình.

Năm Hà still went to Zone HQ for one more meeting, but Bảy Viễn figured it was "every soul for themselves."

The Cao Đài - Việt Minh conflict was still unresolved when the Hòa Hảo - Việt Minh feud broke out.

This feud started back in September '45, when教主 Huỳnh Phú Sổ showed up at the Southern Administrative Resistance Committee and demanded that Comrade Nguyễn Văn Tây hand over four provinces with Hòa Hảo followers for him to run.

Mr. Nguyễn Văn Tây pointed at the map of Vietnam hanging on the wall and said:

"The country of Vietnam is one. Nobody has the right to carve it up. What makes you think I can grant your request?"

Then on September 9, Trần Văn Thành — son of Trần Văn Soái (aka Năm Lửa) — together with the教主's younger brother Huỳnh Sanh Mậu and the教主's advisor, the poet Việt Châu Nguyễn Xuân Thiếp, led a group of Hòa Hảo followers in attacking the Cần Thơ fish market. By April '47, with French army backing, the Hòa Hảo rose up and grabbed a bunch of villages in Tân Châu district (Châu Đốc), Chợ Mới (Long Xuyên), and Châu Thành (Sa Đéc). Zone 9 organized two flanking forces to mop up the Hòa Hảo.

These were also dark years — the country drowning in "kinfolk cooking kinfolk," all from the colonialists' divide-and-rule schemes.

Bảy Viễn learned the old wisdom of "sit on the mountain and watch the tigers fight" — observing and taking notes.

And Bảy Viễn found his own path: "wading between two currents" — not following the French, not following the Việt Minh either.

This was the "master stroke" of his strategist Năm Tài.

Bảy Viễn listened to his advisor Tư Thiên and chose the stance of "sitting still, not moving." No attacks on the French along the Lòng Tàu and Soài Rạp rivers — and of course no tangling with Việt Minh troops either. He turned the Rừng Sác war zone into a safe haven where no guns fired.

The immediate payoff: he got to take it easy while supplies kept coming in steady. A real warlord living like a king in chaotic times.

But Zone intelligence had already picked up signs that Bảy Viễn was "walking the night" — and they urgently reported it up the chain.


---


# Who Walks at Night Meets Ghosts

Who walks at night meets ghosts.

After the last emperor of the Nguyễn dynasty — a man who knew which way the wind blew — handed over the royal seal and sword and dropped a line for the ages: "I would rather be a citizen of an independent country than a king of a slave nation," the Hồ Chí Minh government invited the former emperor Bảo Đại — now going by his civilian name Vĩnh Thụy — to serve as an advisor to the Democratic Republic of Vietnam. But it didn't last long. During a diplomatic trip to China, Bảo Đại flew to Hong Kong and stayed there. Never came back.

When the money ran out, Bảo Đại was ready to be a political tool for the French colonialists.

Bollaert flew to Vietnam with the former emperor Bảo Đại solution — a way to counter the Hồ Chí Minh government.

The French propped Bảo Đại up as Head of State and formed a central government, putting Colonel Nguyễn Văn Xuân in as Prime Minister. Alongside the administrative machinery, the colonialists set up "national war zones" right inside Việt Minh territory, calling on resistance fighters to come back to the national government headed by the former emperor Bảo Đại.

One of the first national war zones was the Bình Quới Tây National War Zone (now Bình Thạnh).

Our intelligence sent reports back about the colonialists' new strategy — to split the Việt Minh from within, peeling off those with nationalist leanings from the Việt Minh Front's leadership.

Lieutenant General Nguyễn Bình sent people to Bình Quới Tây to scope things out.

By mid-1947, the enemy moved Cao Đài soldiers in to set up a post at Bình Quới Tây.

They picked this spot because Bình Quới Tây sits like a peninsula along the winding Sài Gòn River, just past where it bends around Bình Lợi Bridge.

Earlier, the Lê Văn Hoạch government had taken the demoralized deserters who'd surrendered to the enemy and used them as stool pigeons — slipping into the free zones, spreading lies about the resistance, stirring up discontent, and pulling the waverers into the Bình Quới Tây National War Zone.

Once the Zone had the full picture, they assigned Unit 1 to pick someone to plant inside this national war zone's command structure. Since this was a Tây Ninh Cao Đài outfit, they needed someone with close ties to the Cao Đài.

The man they picked was Hoàng Của.

The enemy figured Hoàng Của was "good people" and gave him the rank of chief of staff.

Hoàng Của brought in his own "Cao Đài troops" — about a company's worth. It took six months to build up our "chém vè" strike force inside the Bình Quới Tây National War Zone.

When things were "ripe," we launched the "big smash" at two in the afternoon on December 10, 1947.

Same old classic trick: organize a soccer match between two neighboring teams.

Right when both sides were deep in the game, we fired the signal shot, grabbed the dangerous elements, and took over the ammo depots — our people had already been placed inside, and they handed out captured weapons to our boys. Five enemy officers were shot dead on the spot.

The "sleeping" force packed up the whole warehouse of war booty, crossed the Sài Gòn River — where our troops were waiting to receive them — under the command of anh Lương Đường Minh (later Major General Trần Hải Phụng, Commander of the Special Forces and City Commandos of Sài Gòn).

Alongside the Bình Quới Tây National War Zone, the enemy reached out to Bảy Viễn about setting up the Rừng Sác National War Zone. According to Lieutenant Colonel Antoine Savani of the Deuxième Bureau — a sly old fox from Corsica, a notorious ex-gangster who married a Southern woman, ate raw fermented fish, and spoke Vietnamese like a native — the Rừng Sác National War Zone was ten times more important than Bình Quới Tây.

First, strategically, Rừng Sác was the throat of Sài Gòn. Second, Rừng Sác was the Bình Xuyên stronghold — tougher fighters than the Cao Đài army. Get Bảy Viễn on board, and you turn Rừng Sác into the number one national war zone for Head of State Bảo Đại's Vietnam.

Following proper procedure, Savani passed his orders down to Lâm Ngọc Đường.

Đường passed them on to Maurice Thiên.

Tư Thiên went down to Rừng Sác himself to have a quiet word with the lord of Rừng Sác.

No surprise there — the "strategist" Năm Tài had already planted the seed with Bảy Viễn about "sitting still, not moving" — staying right in the middle between the French and the Việt Minh while the French kept supplying guns, ammo, and supplies...

Maurice Thiên laid out the terms of the treaty the Deuxième Bureau wanted to sign with Bảy Viễn: Unit 9 would "sit still, not move" — no attacks on French ships coming from Vũng Tàu into Sài Gòn or from Sài Gòn out to Vũng Tàu. At the same time, no attacks on French supply ships running between Sài Gòn and Phnom Penh.

The French would not launch any operations into Rừng Sác for as long as the treaty was in effect. On top of that, the French would supply guns, ammo, and provisions to Unit 9 on demand.

Bảy Viễn chewed over the three terms of the treaty for a long time. Unit 9 — that was easy enough. But Rừng Sác had two big rivers — the Lòng Tàu and the Soài Rạp — with a lot of other troops stationed there. Lý Nhơn had Gò Công troops who'd fled over to bunk there, Unit 7 of Hai Vĩnh, Tư Hoạnh's soldiers. If this bunch got it into their heads to ambush a French ship or a cargo boat, how the hell was he supposed to explain that to boss Savani?

Maurice Thiên immediately calmed Bảy Viễn down:

"Every man looks out for his own. Your Unit 9 part is as good as settled. The other units, we'll deal with later. If anything unexpected happens, I'll make sure they know it was beyond Bảy Viễn's control."

So Bảy Viễn quietly started "walking at night" with the Deuxième Bureau, using his closest friend Maurice Thiên as the go-between.

But who walks at night meets ghosts.

The Zone had already gotten wind of this top-secret business — even though Bảy Viễn was keeping it absolutely under wraps.


---


# The Little Scheme

Lieutenant General Nguyễn Bình called in Sáu Hoàng—back then everybody knew him as Cao Đăng Chiếm, Deputy Director of the Southern Police Department and Chief of the Sài Gòn-Chợ Lớn Special Zone Police—to figure out their next move.

Sáu Hoàng offered his take:

"Without solid evidence, the police can't just arrest anybody. Best we keep a quiet tail on 'em..."

Nguyễn Bình was impatient:

"A tail till when? Let's move fast, huh?"

Sáu Hoàng grinned:

"Military boys always want the 'quick strike, quick win.' But us police types, we take it step by step—rush it and you'll trip. I've got a little scheme that'll make tailing Bảy Viễn a whole lot easier."

"What kind of little scheme?"

"Bảy Viễn and Mười Trí are thicker than thieves—raided together, did time together in Sài Gòn's Big House, got shipped off to Côn Đảo together, busted out together... So here's my pitch: promote Mười Trí—he's commanding Chi đội 4 right now—to district representative for the Bình Xuyên region on the Kháng Chiến Hành Chính Committee for Sài Gòn-Chợ Lớn. Mười Trí's a good man. Get him in our pocket, we get Bảy Viễn too."

Nguyễn Bình nodded slowly:

"Not bad. Not bad at all. But we gotta run this by Phạm Ngọc Thuần, the acting Chairman of the Southern Committee. My lane's just military."

With Nguyễn Bình giving him the green light, Sáu Hoàng moved fast.

On February 2, 1948, an emergency meeting was called between the Bình Xuyên commanders and Professor Đặng Minh Trứ, Chairman of the Sài Gòn-Chợ Lớn Committee, with Nguyễn Hộ (in charge of militia affairs) and Deputy Police Director Cao Đăng Chiếm sitting in. In attendance: Lê Văn Viễn (Deputy Zone Chief, Zone 7), Dương Văn Hà (Bình Xuyên Commander), Trần Văn Đối (Deputy Bình Xuyên Commander), Huỳnh Văn Trí (Chi đội 4 Commander), Lâm Văn Đức a.k.a. Tư Ty (Chi đội 25 Commander), Nguyễn Văn Hoạnh a.k.a. Tư Hoạnh (Chi đội 21 Commander), Lê Văn Chàng (Chi đội 2 Commander), and Ngô Tấn Lực (Chi đội 3 Commander).

Bảy Viễn chaired the meeting. Nguyễn Hộ took the minutes.

Professor Đặng Minh Trứ laid out the purpose: per Decree No. 8-ND from the Southern Government, they needed to appoint a Bình Xuyên representative to the Sài Gòn-Chợ Lớn Committee to smooth things out.

Most agreed. Bảy Viễn himself nominated Mười Trí as the candidate. The vote came back: 25 out of 28 ballots cast in Mười Trí's favor.

Bảy Viễn beamed:

"Now that we've got Bình Xuyên sitting at the table in the Sài Gòn-Chợ Lớn Committee, things are gonna look up. We can sort out all the messy business—especially tax collection. Before this, we were stepping all over each other. The river traders suffered, and we had those damn shootouts nobody wanted. I'm hoping that with this new setup, the Southern Commodity Board can really get moving, bring in enough cash to keep our whole army down south properly supplied."

At that same meeting, Professor Đặng Minh Trứ brought up something the Southern Committee had been wanting: Deputy Zone Chief Lê Văn Viễn ought to come down to a big meeting in the South. Since getting promoted, Bảy had never once shown up at Zone HQ for a briefing.

Bảy Viễn cheerfully promised he'd make it to Đồng Tháp Mười one day—see the Resistance's southern command post for himself:

"One of these days, I'll head down South like the Standing Committee's been asking. Settle the whole thing about how Bình Xuyên operates and clear up whatever misunderstandings there are between Zone Chief Nguyễn Bình and me."

The meeting wrapped up with everybody trusting each other, united in the resistance.

Nobody was happier than Sáu Hoàng. His little scheme had worked perfectly: plant Mười Trí inside the Sài Gòn-Chợ Lớn Committee so they could keep tabs on Bảy Viễn.

Step two: get close to Mười Trí, and find a way to crack open the mystery of Bảy Viễn's "night dealings."

This kicked off a brutal mind game between the Southern Police Department and that wily fox Antoine Savani—head of the Deuxième Bureau, a Corsican old dog who'd married a Vietnamese woman, ate raw fermented fish like it was nothing, and spoke Vietnamese as good as any native.

In Savani's grubby hands, Bảy Viễn was just a pawn.

Sure, the man had no real talent or virtue, but by now Bảy Viễn commanded Chi đội 9 and held the post of Deputy Zone Chief of Zone 7. Knocking the lord of Rừng Sác off his throne? No easy feat.

The smartest play: let Savani's own Deuxième Bureau do the dirty work and humiliate Bảy Viễn for them.

That's what Sáu Hoàng chewed on, day and night, hoping Mười Trí would lend him a hand.


---


# One Stone, Four Birds

Sáu Hoàng was racking his brain trying to figure out how to block Bảy Viễn's double-dealing when opportunity came knocking:

Mười Trí showed up asking the Southern Police for a handful of travel passes—from the Eastern Military Zone (Đức Hòa–Đức Huệ) all the way down to Long Xuyên.

"We're gonna ambush the Thanh Vân boat and take its guns. She's carrying a 20-millimeter cannon, two FM machine guns, two submachine guns, and about a dozen rifles. The Thanh Vân runs passengers from Phnom Penh back to Sài Gòn, and she's towing a whole string of barges loaded with rice, grain, buffalo, pigs—you name it."

Sáu Hoàng smiled:

"How many passes you need? And why go all the way to Long Xuyên?"

Mười Trí dropped his voice, even though they were alone:

"I'm sending Hai Bạc—he's my recon squad leader from Chi đội 4—with a few boys to Cù Lao Ông Hổ at Mỹ Hòa An. They'll pose as passengers and board at Long Xuyên dock. That way—you know—recon, counter-recon, intel—we'll know how many soldiers are on board, how many Frenchies, their meal schedule, sleep times, when they'll hit our kill zone..."

Sáu Hoàng:

"Don't you worry, big brother. I'll get your recon team their passes, plus a French laissez-passer for good measure. Run into French soldiers? You got a French charm. Run into us? You got our charm. But where exactly are you planning to hit this boat?"

Mười Trí:

"Vàm Phong Mỹ. From there we haul the cannon and the smaller guns a few hours into our territory at Ba Sao, Cái Bèo."

Sáu Hoàng's eyebrows shot up:

"Why only grab the guns and leave behind all those barges full of buffalo, pigs, rice? What a waste!"

Mười Trí jerked:

"You're right—what a damn waste. My boys are hungry. I was just dead set on the big gun. Guess we gotta round up some more boats and sampans to haul the rice, grain, pigs, cattle too..."

Sáu Hoàng pulled Mười Trí over to the map tacked on the leaf wall:

"The spot you picked ain't great for a clean getaway. That Nguyễn Văn Tiếp canal runs straight as an arrow. Enemy planes will come buzzing, drop bombs, strafe with machine guns—you'll all be dead. You need to rethink the location—pick somewhere that works for both the attack and the retreat. And don't you forget about all that other loot besides your 20-millimeter cannon."

That night, Sáu Hoàng tossed and turned figuring out the perfect ambush spot for Chi đội 4. Around cockcrow, the idea hit him like a gold nugget: why not convince Mười Trí to hit the Thanh Vân right on the Soài Rạp River, smack in Chi đội 9's territory? One stone, four birds: militarily safe, politically victorious, and it'd bust up the unholy alliance between Savani and Bảy Viễn.

Couldn't sleep at the start of the night from racking his brain on Mười Trí's behalf. Come near-dawn, couldn't sleep neither from being so damn happy he'd cracked the code to break Bảy Viễn's shady dealings. And yet when Mười Trí showed up the next morning, Sáu Hoàng was sharp as a tack:

"Like you advised, Brother Sáu, I picked out a new spot—the mouth of the Kỳ Hôn River..."

Sáu Hoàng pulled him back to the map:

"That spot's still a pain for retreat. How fast can our rowboats go against a Spitfire? You're looking at heavy casualties, brother."

Mười Trí sighed:

"Then where do you say we hit, Brother Sáu?"

Sáu Hoàng glued his eyes to the map, pretending to think real hard, then slapped his hands together like he'd just struck gold:

"Brother Mười! Why not set the ambush on the Soài Rạp? That's Bình Xuyên turf. Rừng Sác is thick jungle. Hit the boat, cut the tow lines, let the barges drift into the little creeks—easy as pie. Even the L-19 spotter planes won't find you in there."

Mười Trí lit up:

"Brother Sáu, you're a genius! Hitting the Thanh Vân at Vàm Sác—can't miss. Let me head down to Tất Cây Mắm and talk joint operation with Bảy Viễn. Us two joining hands on this one—we'll hoist the victory flag and ride home triumphant."

Sáu Hoàng laughed:

"Joint operation, my ass. I figure your Chi đội 4 can handle this yourself. Plant a half-squad as passengers, wait till they're eating lunch, then let loose with the submachine guns—clean and simple. You just need to borrow Bảy Viễn's landing spot to haul away the loot."

Mười Trí nodded vigorously:

"You're right—we keep full command. But if I just ask to borrow his spot, he might get his pride hurt. So I'll call it a 'joint operation'—makes the boys feel good."

As Mười Trí got ready to head down to Rừng Sác, Brother Sáu gripped his hand:

"You and Bảy Viễn might be sworn brothers, but I figure you'll have a tough time talking him into this joint operation on the Thanh Vân."

Mười Trí's eyes went wide:

"Why would I have trouble? I'm laying the feast out right in front of him—why wouldn't he eat?"

Sáu Hoàng spoke carefully:

"You forget that people are shaped by their circumstances? In a gourd you're round, in a pipe you're long. Three years now, Bảy Viễn's turned into the lord of Rừng Sác—when's the last time he did any real fighting? His mindset these days is 'sit pretty and eat off gold plates.' I'm just guessing here—could be wrong. But you ought to pick your words carefully. Convincing Bảy Viễn to let you use the Vàm Sác landing to 'butcher' the Thanh Vân—that ain't gonna be easy. Still, I trust you'll pull it off, Brother Mười."


---


# Holding the Line

The Thanh Vân got ambushed by the joint forces of Chi đội 4 and Chi đội 9 at Vàm Sác, just like Mười Trí had planned.

The boat pulled into Vàm Sác late in the afternoon. Hai Bạc's recon squad swarmed up to the upper deck all at once and opened fire.

The French second lieutenant manning the 20-millimeter cannon was lying in his cabin with his Vietnamese girlfriend—both of them dead before they hit the floor after the first burst. The two FM gunners got wiped out too. Caught completely off guard, the Cambodian rifle squad on board dropped their weapons and surrendered.

Hai Bạc directed the pilot to hug the forest edge. Every time they passed a little creek, he'd send a barge drifting in so the troops and locals could haul off the spoils.

Rice filled up whole warehouses. Buffalo, cows, pigs—more than they knew what to do with, had to string ropes around them out in the fields. The stuff Bảy Viễn loved most? The luxury goods.

Mười Trí got exactly what he wanted: the 20-millimeter cannon and two FM machine guns. As for the rice, pigs, cattle—he turned it over to Bảy Viễn to split evenly among all the Bình Xuyên units, with a portion going to the local Gò Công troops who were stationed on Lý Nhơn land.

Back in the Eastern Military Zone, Brother Mười told the story of how he'd talked Bảy Viễn into lending them the Vàm Sác landing.

At first Bảy Viễn pitched a fit:

"No way! No damn way! I've had a quiet understanding with the French all this time. You don't hit me, I don't hit you. Now you go attack the Thanh Vân on my turf? You've set me up!"

Sáu Hoàng nodded to himself:

"Our intel was right. Plain as day—Bảy Viễn's been sneaking around with the French."

He asked:

"What'd you tell him to get him to agree to the joint operation?"

"I told him: 'Just blame it all on me—say Mười Trí did it without telling you. If you'd known, you never would've let it happen!' With the French, that explanation works fine. And for us? The benefits are huge. Chi đội 9 hasn't had a real victory in a long time. Now you've got the Vàm Sác triumph—captured the whole Thanh Vân with a 20-millimeter cannon, plus twenty barges of rice, pigs, cattle. Split it up and you'll be eating for a year, easy."

Sáu Hoàng shook his hand and praised Brother Mười for pulling off his "little scheme" without even realizing he'd been playing his part.

A few days later, intelligence reported that Lieutenant Colonel Savani was practically beside himself when he learned Bảy Viễn had broken their ceasefire agreement.

The French army scrambled an emergency operation into Rừng Sác to retake the Thanh Vân and rescue the passengers taken hostage.

As for Lâm Ngọc Đường and Maurice Thiên, Savani slammed his desk and tore into them:

"Why didn't you two keep a tighter leash on Bảy Viễn? He gave me his word and he ate it. You're nothing but a pack of thieves—can't trust any of you!"

Both of Bảy Viễn's advisors parroted Mười Trí's line:

"This was a unilateral action by Mười Trí, commander of Chi đội 4. They borrowed Vàm Sác as their ambush point. Bảy Viễn had no idea—let alone a joint operation. Please, Lieutenant Colonel, calm down and let us straighten Bảy Viễn out. We won't let anything like this happen again."

Around the same time, Hai Dậu—head of the Arts Unit for Chi đội 9—got handed a script from Năm Tài.

"Here's a play that Brother Tư Sang and I wrote. We're putting it on for Tết to give the boys something to watch. Take a look and assign the roles to the actors."

Hai Dậu was a musician, not much of a theater man, but when Ông Năm handed him something, he had to take it. "Above him is Ông Bảy, below him is Ông Năm"—that's how it worked.

Hai Dậu read the script over and over, five times, seven times, and still couldn't figure out what was so great about it.

He slipped the script to Tám Tâm, the deputy chief of Chi đội 9's office.

After reading it, Tám Tâm turned the question around:

"You're the head of the Arts Unit. What do you make of it?"

Hai Dậu shook his head:

"I don't get it at all. We're Uncle Hồ's soldiers, but this play is glorifying the former emperor Bảo Đại."

Tám Tâm gave a cryptic smile:

"What do you want to do, Brother Hai? Put it on or not?"

Hai Dậu let out a breath:

"Ông Năm ordered it performed for Tết as entertainment. But I'm really stuck here."

Tám Tâm spoke vaguely:

"Performing it is hard. Not performing it is also hard. Your call."

Hai Dậu thought it over for a long while:

"If I put it on, Ông Năm's happy... but I'm afraid it goes against Việt Minh policy. Best thing is to stall and think up a plan."

Tám Tâm shook Hai Dậu's hand:

"Good! You've found the perfect trick: hold the line, don't move a muscle."

Năm Tài checked in with Hai Dậu every day, pushing him to get the play staged. Hai Dậu suggested passing it off to Sáu Hiếu—Bảy Viễn's trusted man, who'd just been promoted to replace Hai Dậu.

"It'd be better if Ông Năm handed this play to Brother Sáu Hiếu. Me, I'm just the music lead now. Truth is, when it comes to theater, I'm hopeless."

Năm Tài frowned:

"What the hell does Sáu Hiếu know about art? Ông Bảy put him there to keep an eye on you people. I trusted you with this. Don't pawn it off on somebody else."

Năm Tài was pissed, going on and on about it, but the play never got staged. Little did he know—that very script was how they found out Bảy Viễn was leaning toward the former emperor Bảo Đại.


---


# Deadly Secret

Hai Vĩnh, the commander of Unit 7, was in a meeting with Regional Commander Nguyễn Bình when an urgent message came in from Trịnh Văn Tài, Unit 7's deputy commander, reporting they'd just arrested Phán Huề.

Hai Vĩnh filled Nguyễn Bình in on what this was all about:

"That Phán Huề — real name Lê Văn Huề — used to be Chairman of the Bà Rịa Provincial Revolutionary Administration Committee when we first took power on August 25, 1945. Back in the French days, he was a senior administrative secretary at the Vice Admiral's Palace in Sài Gòn, what they called the Governor's Palace of Cochinchina. In 1945, Phán Huề retired and they trusted him enough to make him provincial chairman. When the French recaptured Bà Rịa in October '45, all the provincial offices moved out to Phước Bửu, Long Ðiền, Ðất Ðỏ. Phán Huề asked to step down so he could go up to Núi Dinh and practice his religion — he's a Cao Ðài follower. Word later came he was captured by the French and taken back to the city. Now somehow he's snuck back into our zone, and Unit 7 picked him up in the Phước Hòa area."

Nguyễn Bình had been getting a lot of intelligence reports that the enemy was secretly sending former resistance fighters into the zone to call on our boys to rally to the national government with Bảo Ðại as Chief of State. Suspecting this guy was working for Nguyễn Văn Xuân's government, Nguyễn Bình went with Hai Vĩnh to Bà Rịa to investigate.

Hai Vĩnh pitched his plan to Nguyễn Bình:

"Brother Ba, let me handle Phán Huề alone. If he's really working for the other side, I'll play the part of a fed-up soldier sick of the Việt Minh's monopoly on leadership, ready to march my men back to the city and back the national government of the former emperor, now Chief of State Bảo Ðại. To keep this act running smooth, Brother Ba shouldn't let Phán Huề see you at Unit 7 at all."

Nguyễn Bình nodded thoughtfully:

"Your plan's a good one. Make him trust you so he spills everything about who he's been trying to recruit. Could be Bảy Viễn's on that list."

As soon as they got back, Hai Vĩnh chewed out Trịnh Văn Tài right in front of Phán Huề:

"This here is our honored guest. I apologize to you, Mr. Chairman, for the rude treatment my deputy gave you. This afternoon, you and I will have dinner together and discuss some important matters."

Over roast duck and red wine, Hai Vĩnh broke the ice:

"I don't know what brings you out here, Mr. Chairman, but I'll be straight with you — me and most of our boys who come from the underworld are sick and tired of the Việt Minh's patriotic monopoly. I hear there's a bunch of national-minded brothers who've had it with the hard life and lack of freedom in this zone. If somebody fires the first shot and raises a flag, we'll follow."

Phán Huề was cautious:

"I never expected a decorated commander like you to talk like that! I can hardly believe it."

Hai Vĩnh's face turned serious:

"You don't believe me, Mr. Chairman? I'll write a letter to Prime Minister Nguyễn Văn Xuân right now, expressing my wish to march my men back to the city and support the national government of Chief of State Bảo Ðại — on the condition that Prime Minister Xuân set aside a separate base for us."

Hearing that, Phán Huề lit right up:

"I'm overjoyed to hear those heartfelt words. To be honest with you, I came here looking for like-minded comrades. I've already met some national-minded fighters who are on board. Mr. Lai Hữu Tài, advisor to Deputy Regional Commander Bảy Viễn, received me and promised to relay my message to his excellency..."

With that, Phán Huề reached into his pocket and pulled out a pre-printed form, handing it to Hai Vĩnh.

Hai Vĩnh took out paper and ink and wrote a letter to Prime Minister Nguyễn Văn Xuân following the template. He showed it to Phán Huề, then signed and stamped it. That night Phán Huề slept like a baby with no idea he'd just spilled a deadly secret. After Phán Huề was asleep, Hai Vĩnh sat down and rewrote the whole letter from memory, showing Nguyễn Bình how he'd faked the letter to Prime Minister Nguyễn Văn Xuân to reel Phán Huề right in.

The next morning, Nguyễn Bình finally showed his face.

Phán Huề went pale when the Regional Commander said:

"Lê Văn Huề. You're under arrest for sneaking into our zone and inciting soldiers to desert and rally to the puppet Bảo Ðại government. What a damn shame — a religious man like you choosing the path of betraying your own people. To show genuine remorse, you need to name everyone you've been trying to seduce. Help us avoid heavy losses. Your life depends on how honest you are."

Phán Huề hunched over the blank page in front of him.

On that list of wavering men was Bảy Viễn's own advisor: Lai Hữu Tài.

As they were escorting Phán Huề back to Nam Bộ to face the Supreme Court, Nguyễn Bình said to Hai Vĩnh:

"For the past year, our intelligence has been telling us the Deuxième Bureau has been working aggressively to get a hold on Bảy Viễn. First step: a gentle agreement, sit tight and stay put, slowly setting up a national resistance zone, turning Rừng Sác into an enemy base. The colonialists are clever with their word games — they don't call it 'surrendering' or 'siding with the French.' They call it 'returning to the national cause' or 'joining the Free World.' That's how they hook a lot of shallow thinkers.

As for the Bảy Viễn problem, we've got to handle it carefully. Rush it and we'll spill blood for nothing — and it'll cost the resistance big time."


---


# The Persuader

Now that they had enough solid evidence that Bảy Viễn was sneaking around behind their backs with the Deuxième Bureau, Lieutenant General Nguyễn Bình — who'd just been promoted to Military Commissioner of Nam Bộ and Vice Chairman of the Nam Bộ Revolutionary Administration Committee — called a special meeting to figure out what to do about Bảy Viễn.

In front of all the committee members, Lieutenant General Nguyễn Bình laid out the intelligence reports showing Bảy Viễn was in close contact with French intelligence. He wrapped it up:

"Our Deputy Regional Commander Lê Văn Viễn has been dodging meetings for a while now. Neither side understands the other. So here's my idea: I've just been named Military Commissioner of Nam Bộ, which leaves the Khu 7 Commander spot open. I propose we appoint Bảy Viễn as Khu 7 Commander. Once he takes the job, he'll have to come back to Nam Bộ. Best way for us to finally sit down face to face and sort out all those grudges that've been simmering."

Everybody agreed. Acting Chairman Phạm Ngọc Thuần drafted the decision promoting Bảy Viễn to Commander of Khu 7.

They sent the telegram. Days passed. No word back from Bảy Viễn. Then intelligence reported that Bảy Viễn got the good news but wasn't happy about it the way he was the year before when they made him Deputy Commander. Turns out those two cronies Tư Sang and Năm Tài had told him this was a "lure the tiger out of the mountain" scheme — pulling his excellency the Deputy Commander away from his Rừng Sác kingdom.

Bảy Viễn himself had a feeling that was the case. Hell, the "trick the tiger into the open field" plan was so obvious anybody could see through it. So what now? Putting Tám Nghệ in as Commander would've made perfect sense — Tám Nghệ had way more battle honors than Bảy Viễn, not to mention he was a Party member. But they couldn't just throw Bảy Viễn aside.

The leadership in Nam Bộ took the attitude "as long as there's water, keep bailing." Bảy Viễn wasn't as rotten as the Third Division Commander Nguyễn Hòa Hiệp or those Vũ Tam Anh, Bùi Liễu Phiệt types. They should help him, pull him back, keep him from making the kind of mistake he'd regret.

Political Commissar Hai Trí — Nguyễn Văn Trí — nodded at Tám Nghệ:

"Bảy Viễn comes from the underworld. He's got that old-school gangster pride thing going on. Gotta stroke his ego to get anywhere with him. Brother Tám might not be from the underworld, but he's a legendary badass all over the Eastern region. I say Brother Tám goes down to Rừng Sác as our persuader."

Nguyễn Bình nodded along:

"Brother Tám went down there once for Bảy Viễn's Deputy Commander inauguration. Let's see how it goes a second time."

Tám Nghệ hesitated:

"I went to Rừng Sác last year, sure, but back then Bảy Viễn hadn't started thinking about betraying the resistance. Going down now won't be as smooth as before."

Hai Trí jumped in:

"Brother Tám, how many bodyguards you want to bring? A platoon? A whole company?"

Tám Nghệ laughed:

"Even my best unit, Unit 10, wouldn't mean squat down there — Bảy Viễn's got the whole Bình Xuyên Interzone, seven units total. No. I'm not taking a single escort."

Hearing Tám Nghệ say that, everybody knew he was on board to go down to Bảy Viễn's headquarters alone and talk the Bình Xuyên boss into coming to Nam Bộ to take the Khu 7 Commander post. Everyone breathed a sigh of relief. Nguyễn Bình shook his hand, gripping tight:

"Wishing you success, Brother Tám. Hope to see you and Bảy Viễn together at the inauguration ceremony for Commander Lê Văn Viễn on the Nhơn Hòa Lập canal."

Tám Nghệ went solo — one man, no backup — traveling from Lạc An down to Bàu Bông in Phước An commune, then hopping a covered boat to Tất Cây Mắm to meet Bảy Viễn.

Long Thành District Commander Phạm Tự Do was worried:

"Brother Tám, you're going alone? Let me send a squad to escort you."

Tám Nghệ waved him off:

"Thanks, brother. A squad wouldn't make a difference anyway. I'll go alone — easier to maneuver that way."

When word got out that Tám Nghệ was coming down to Rừng Sác by himself, those two Sang and Tài put their heads together:

"This bastard's asking for it, walking right into our territory. Let's waste him right now, don't even let him see Mr. Bảy."

Tư Sang posted three sharpshooters with submachine guns in a covered boat to tail their target. Tám Nghệ strolled right into Bảy Viễn's headquarters, warm and friendly like an old comrade. Bảy Viễn was genuinely happy to see Tám Nghệ pay him a visit.

Tám Nghệ unholstered his pistol, tossed it on the table, and threw both arms around Bảy Viễn:

"I'm here to congratulate you, Brother Bảy, on your promotion to Commander."

Bảy Viễn shook his head:

"I don't think I deserve it. That title should go to you, Brother Tám."

"What are you saying? You're the number one Deputy Commander. The one getting promoted to Commander has to be you. Second reason: you represent the Southern underworld. You taking the Khu 7 Commander post is an honor for every outlaw brother in the South. The Central leadership and Nam Bộ don't just make decisions on a whim — they study everything carefully, with full understanding of the situation."

Bảy Viễn let out a sigh:

"I'll be straight with you, Brother Tám. I'm not happy about being promoted to Commander. I feel like there's something fishy behind that telegram. Could be one of Nguyễn Bình's poison pills."


---


# Tám Nghệ Enters the Tiger's Den

Bảy Viễn busted out some imported liquor for Tám Nghệ:

"Truth be told, Brother Tám, I can't stand Nguyễn Bình. This guy's a nobody who washed up here and started acting like my daddy. Who the hell's gonna put up with that?"

Tám Nghệ slammed his Cognac glass on the table and cut him off:

"Let me say something, Brother Bảy. North, Central, South — we're all Vietnamese. Us Southerners, we're the descendants of the Ngũ Quảng folks who followed Nguyễn Hoàng down here to make a life. Splitting into Northerner versus Southerner is falling right into the colonizers' divide-and-conquer trap."

Bảy Viễn was fuming:

"But Brother Tám, what's Nguyễn Bình got that I don't, that he gets to be my boss?"

Tám Nghệ got serious:

"A hell of a lot! First of all, real leadership. Down here in the South, anybody with troops under his command acts like a king. Big fish eat little fish. Like the days of the Twelve Warlords — nobody respected anybody. But Nguyễn Bình shows up fresh off the boat in the Eastern region and pulls together the An Phú xã conference in Gò Vấp, unifies all the local military units, sets up over a dozen units — including all seven units of the Bình Xuyên Interzone. If it wasn't for Nguyễn Bình, who could've done something that big? Then he set up the Urban Action Teams, brought the war right into the enemy's backyard in occupied Sài Gòn... The famous generals Leclerc and Nyo both respect Nguyễn Bình."

Bảy Viễn shook his head:

"I'm not taking the Commander post. This is a 'lure the tiger out of the mountain' trap. I'm used to my Rừng Sác kingdom. Going down to Ðồng Tháp Mười — out in the middle of nowhere, cold as hell!"

Tám Nghệ laughed out loud:

"They gave you the title 'Black Tiger General.' You're a black tiger, king of the jungle. How come today you're sounding so weak? A tiger's a tiger no matter where you go. What, you go down to the plains and turn into a fox or something?"

Bảy Viễn was lifting his glass when he slammed it down on the table, eyes blazing, fists clenched:

"Brother Tám's right! A tiger's a tiger wherever he goes — still king of the jungle! This Bảy Viễn is the Black Tiger General — who the hell am I afraid of that I won't go to Nam Bộ?

But..."

Tám Nghệ knew what was eating him and jumped in:

"You're scared of falling for the 'lure the tiger out of the mountain' trick, right? That's not happening! I figure you and Nguyễn Bình just don't understand each other 'cause you're stationed so far apart. Plus you got people around you with bad intentions, whispering this and that to drive a wedge. If you ask me, Brother Bảy, you ought to go to Nam Bộ and take the post. At the ceremony, all your brothers will be there. Anything ain't clear, we talk it out, even argue it out — clear up every misunderstanding. If you're still not comfortable, bring a couple of strong companies with you."

Bảy Viễn nodded slowly:

"Good idea. I'll bring two heavy-weapons companies. First, to keep me safe on the road. Second, to put the fear of God in Nguyễn Bình."

Tám Nghệ was overjoyed that Bảy Viễn had agreed to come to Nam Bộ and take the Commander post. He reached over and grabbed Bảy Viễn's hand:

"Brother Bảy, you really are the Black Tiger General. I'm proud to call you my friend."

Bảy Viễn, feeling the fire, threw his arm around Tám Nghệ's shoulder:

"Brother Tám, you might not be from the underworld, but you're a legend in War Zone D. I'm damn proud to call you my sworn brother."

The feast wound down. It was hot, and the alcohol was settling in. Tám Nghệ stood up and pulled off his shirt:

"Mind if I take a dip in the river, Brother Bảy? It's burning up out here!"

"Go right ahead, Brother Tám. Make yourself at home."

While Tám Nghệ was splashing around out on the water, those three commando sharpshooters had their submachine guns trained on their target. They were hiding in the boat canopy, poking just their muzzles through gaps in the leaves.

Tư Sang was on edge and asked Năm Tài:

"What do you think, Năm? Should we check with Mr. Bảy first?"

Năm Tài thought it over:

"Ask him and he'll probably say no. What if we just go ahead? Strike first, ask forgiveness later?"

Tư Sang let out a breath:

"We should ask. If we act rash, we might all end up dead right alongside Tám Nghệ."

Năm Tài scrambled inside to find Bảy Viễn:

"Mr. Bảy. This is a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity. Don't let it pass. Tám Nghệ is a Communist. He's Nguyễn Bình's right-hand man. He came down here to pull that 'lure the tiger out of the mountain' poison plan. He walked right into our hands. We've got three shooters in position. If you give the word..."

Bảy Viễn thundered:

"Shut your mouth! Who said you could act on your own? Tám Nghệ is my honored guest. Lucky for you idiots. If you'd made a move, I'd have sacrificed all five of your miserable hides..."

Năm Tài slunk back out, humiliated, and waved Tư Sang off:

"Stand down. Mr. Bảy says he'll sacrifice all of us if we touch him."

Death was circling Tám Nghệ the whole time he was playing in the water. When he finally climbed back on shore, one of the unit men said:

"Sir, that stretch of river's got crocodiles. I was about to warn you, but you'd already jumped in."

Tám Nghệ laughed:

"I knew there were crocs in there. But a croc won't snatch a man who's standing upright."


---


# Agreeing to Go to the South

**Agreeing to Go to the South**

Deputy Zone Commander Tám Nghệ had barely left Rừng Sác when Bảy Viễn welcomed two more distinguished guests: Mười Trí and Bảy Trấn.

Out came the Western liquor again to treat his old jungle buddies, handshakes and smiles all around.

Bảy Viễn threw his arm around Bảy Trấn's shoulder:

"Welcome, Teacher Bảy from Dầu Tiếng."

Bảy Trấn laughed out loud:

"Monsieur le Commandant Hoảnh-Xăng, how you doing!"

Mười Trí raised his glass:

"Here's to the reunion of three outlaws. Man, time flies — it's been five, six years already."

Bảy Viễn, the leader of Bình Xuyên, nodded along.

Bảy Trấn clinked glasses with Bảy Viễn and Mười Trí:

"We were born for big things. Back when we were running protection rackets at Hội đồng Thì's place, who'd have thought we'd end up big shots like this? Damn, all three of us got the golden touch."

Bảy Viễn was pleased but played dumb:

"What do you mean 'golden touch,' anh Bảy?"

Bảy Trấn said:

"Well, let's see — Monsieur Hoảnh-Xăng here is holding down like a thousand titles: Commander of Unit 9, Deputy Zone Chief of Zone 7, and now they've kicked you up to Zone Chief. Old Năm Mắm's got two jobs too: Commander of Unit 4 plus commissioner on the Saigon-Chợ Lớn Resistance-Administration Committee. And me, I've been appointed Political Commissar of Zone 9."

Bảy Viễn was surprised:

"How come you're all the way up here if you're assigned to Zone 9?"

Bảy Trấn laughed:

"Can't a man visit his buddy just 'cause he misses him? Nah, I'm kidding — I came up here on orders from Lê Duẩn himself."

Bảy Viễn was even more confused:

"What does Lê Duẩn want that he'd send you all the way out here to see me?"

Mười Trí jumped in:

"Earlier, Nguyễn Bình sent Tám Nghệ down here to invite anh Bảy to the South to take the Zone Chief position. Lê Duẩn figured anh Bảy doesn't like Nguyễn Bình, so he worried Tám Nghệ's trip wouldn't work. That's why anh Ba reached out to me, and brought Teacher Bảy along too, knowing you two are tight."

Bảy Viễn nodded slowly:

"So that's how it is. But say whatever you want — I'm not taking that Zone Chief post. This is a 'lure the tiger out of the mountain' play, even a kid can see that. I promised Tám Nghệ I'd bring a crack company down to Tháp Mười to show that the 'Black Tiger General' ain't scared of nobody. But taking the position? That I'm gonna have to think about."

Bảy Trấn put on an angry face:

"Anh Bảy, are you treating your old jungle brother like some decoy bird or what? If this was some dirty 'lure the tiger out of the mountain' trick, I'd have stayed put down in Zone 9 where it's safe. Think about it — this Bảy Trấn ain't the type to betray a friend!"

Mười Trí chimed in:

"Anh Bảy. A few years back, I was suspicious of Nguyễn Bình too. But being stationed close to each other cleared things up. Nguyễn Bình is a Northerner, a Communist, sure — but he's solid. You must remember that thing with Sáu Section faking my signature to invite Nguyễn Bình to my place for dinner. Anh Ba Bình came alone in a single sampan, and that trio — Sáu Section, Bùi Hữu Phiệt, and Vũ Tam Anh — ambushed him near the sugar mill right next to my house, sprayed him with an automatic. Lucky for him, he only got a light wound. And after all that, anh Ba still didn't make a big deal out of it with me."

Bảy Viễn said nothing, just kept knocking back drink after drink. Finished one bottle, cracked open another. Mười Trí stopped him:

"Alright, that's enough booze! Keep your head clear for the big talk."

Bảy Viễn's eyes went wide:

"Anh Mười, you drink like a bottomless well — what's gotten into you today?"

Mười Trí laughed:

"New life, new Vietnamese man. Whatever's old and bad, we leave behind. You and me should pinky-swear right now: I quit drinking, you quit women. Deal?"

The party ended on a good note. Bảy Viễn agreed to go to the South — whether he'd actually take the post, that could wait.

The two emissaries, Mười Trí and Bảy Trấn, had to settle for this half-baked result. At least they'd made contact. As for the real heavy business, that'd come later.


---


# The Big Meeting Day

**The Big Meeting Day**

Two crack companies armed with heavy machine guns escorted Deputy Zone Chief Lê Văn Viễn across the Soài Rạp River, over Highway 4, through Vườn Thơm to Cần Vè, then down the Vàm Cỏ Tây along the Dương Văn Dương canal to the village of Nhơn Hòa Lập, where the Southern resistance command was stationed.

Tư Sang picked the area around the church to set up camp.

Out in the yard, the Bình Xuyên soldiers had their guns pointed skyward, belts of ammo dangling red-bright in the sunlight. The villagers came flocking to see the big guns from the Eastern troops.

Every evening, the Unit 9 band would drag themselves out to the wooden bridge in front of the church and play music.

Meanwhile, Bảy Viễn headed to the Southern Resistance-Administration Committee for the meeting.

This was a monumental day in the history of the resistance against the French — May 26, 1948.

The full Standing Committee was present: Phạm Ngọc Thuần, Nguyễn Bình, Ung Văn Khiêm, Nguyễn Thành Vĩnh, Kha Vạn Cân, Lê Quẩn, Diệp Ba, Lê Đình Chi, Trịnh Đình Trọng, Phan Văn Chương.

From the Bình Xuyên side: Lê Văn Viễn, Huỳnh Văn Trí, Trần Văn Đối, Nguyễn Văn Hoạnh. Plus two others — Zone 7 Deputy Chief Huỳnh Văn Nghệ and Zone 9 Political Commissar Nguyễn Văn Trấn.

Ông Thuần chaired the meeting, with ông Trấn and Diệp Ba as secretaries.

Ông Thuần got straight to it:

"The French are playing the divide-and-conquer card. We need to strengthen our internal unity. I propose we lay all our doubts and suspicions on the table and settle them once and for all so we can join forces against the Westerners."

Bảy Viễn stood up and spoke right away:

"The Bình Xuyên fighters have always fought for the unity and independence of the Fatherland, and have always obeyed the government."

Nguyễn Bình stood up to share his thoughts at this meeting — the first one Deputy Zone Chief Bảy Viễn had ever attended. He said:

"Before leaving Zone 7, I want to definitively resolve all the friction between the Zone and the Bình Xuyên brothers. Zone 7 is the big brother war zone with many achievements to its name. Unfortunately, ông Bảy and I haven't been close, which has led to many misunderstandings. Today, I thank ông Tám Nghệ for inviting ông Bảy down here for this meeting to clear the air.

The Bình Xuyên commanders were afraid to come to the Zone because they feared being wiped out. Brothers Năm Hà and Sáu Đối demanded guarantees — they wanted the Zone to send hostages down to Rừng Sác before they'd come to the South. Still, in the end, both ông Năm Hà and ông Sáu Đối came to the Zone to meet me. Only ông Bảy never did. And this is the first time I have the honor of meeting the Deputy Zone Chief.

While I'm at it, let me raise a few things that need addressing: First, Lai Hữu Tài insulted me — the Southern military commissioner — in an open letter, and ông Bảy signed as a witness. Second, Lâm Văn Hậu from the Third Division command entered Bình Xuyên territory and tried to lure our troops back to the city to surrender to the French, and ông Bảy looked the other way. Third, the three men Nguyễn Thành Long, Bùi Hữu Phiệt, and Lai Hữu Tài plotted to assassinate me and then fled inside Unit 9, yet ông Bảy protected them and didn't make arrests. Fourth, Lâm Ngọc Đường and Tư Thiên are secret agents who used the Bình Xuyên name to extort millions, contributing only one percent to Unit 9. Even more dangerous, these two set traps to capture government operatives working inside the city.

These are the big issues. I'm laying them out so from now on both sides understand each other, preserve each other's reputation and honor against the enemy's divide-and-conquer schemes, and tighten our unity."

Finally: "Here's a solution the Southern Standing Committee has unanimously agreed on. I ask for your special attention. For some time, we've learned the French have been trying to co-opt nationalist elements within the resistance, luring them back to the city to surrender to the Westerners under the banner of joining Bảo Đại's national government.

We just captured Phán Huề, an agent of Nguyễn Văn Xuân's government who came out to the countryside to find Deputy Zone Chief Lê Văn Viễn to set up a Rừng Sác national war zone. The enemy has planted many Deuxième Bureau agents inside Unit 9 — men like Tư Sang, Năm Tài — thinking they could easily get their hooks into the Bình Xuyên boys.

To smash the divide-and-conquer game that old fox Bollaert is playing between the nationalists and the Việt Minh, the South has decided to dissolve the Bình Xuyên organization entirely — abolish the Bình Xuyên Command, the Bình Xuyên army. All Bình Xuyên units will become National Guard regiments within the national army."

The Bình Xuyên commanders' faces went red as eggplants when they heard the South was dissolving Bình Xuyên.

Bảy Viễn shot up, roaring:

"No! We reject that decision! For three years, the Bình Xuyên troops have bled for the red flag with the yellow star, following in the footsteps of anh Ba Dương — the man who became the first martyr-general of the underworld to join the resistance."

Nguyễn Bình raised his hand:

"Please, ông Bảy, stay calm and let me finish. The Rừng Sác War Zone under the new organization will be a special war zone — a resistance stronghold. Gentlemen, you can find millions in money anywhere, but the honor of a revolutionary fighter, a national hero — what you have right now — that can't be bought with gold or with empty titles. I hope you will cherish and protect that honor. If you do, I'll be proud to have chosen the right men of talent and virtue for positions worthy of them. I'm truly happy to have comrades and friends as valuable as you."


---


# Tiger Returns to the Plain

**Tiger Returns to the Plain**

The speech Lieutenant General Nguyễn Bình delivered before the Standing Committee of the Southern Resistance-Administration Committee on May 26, 1948, was aimed at resolving the issues of Zone 7 — chiefly the festering bad blood between the Zone Chief and his First Deputy. It ran four and a half pages.

His tone was measured and generous — exactly the voice of a commander steeped in the spirit of "brothers in arms," even when addressing matters of the utmost importance concerning resistance policy and strategy.

The three Bình Xuyên figures Nguyễn Bình questioned were Bảy Viễn, Mười Trí, and Tư Ty.

Mười Trí and Tư Ty calmly explained their actions. Bảy Viễn, though, couldn't sit still — at times he looked ready to jump up and demand a chance to "give tit for tat," but the meeting's chair, lawyer Phạm Ngọc Thuần, had plenty of experience steering stormy sessions and handled things smoothly.

After finishing his presentation, Nguyễn Bình handed his speech to the chair.

Ông Thuần set it aside and read from his notebook the points that needed debate:

"Now I invite Deputy Zone Chief Lê Văn Viễn to respond to the issues the Zone Chief has raised. Specifically:

One — the mutual suspicion between Bình Xuyên and Zone 7.

Two — the employment of Lai Hữu Tài, a man from the Deuxième Bureau.

Three — your association with Maurice Thiên, a man tied to Lieutenant Colonel Savani of the Deuxième Bureau.

Four — your harboring of reactionary elements like Nguyễn Thành Long, Bùi Hữu Phiệt, and Lai Hữu Tài, who are guilty of conspiring to assassinate Lieutenant General Nguyễn Bình, without arresting and turning them over to the judiciary."

Bảy Viễn stood up straight. He didn't look at the chair — he looked straight at Nguyễn Bình:

"Before I answer those four questions from Zone Chief Nguyễn Bình, let me say this straight-up: Bảy Viễn personally does not trust Northerners. And I can't stand those damn political commissars. When we first formed our troops — all the hardship, the dying — where were those commissars? But once the troops were organized, once we had companies and battalions, that's when they showed up, demanding a share of the command."

Mười Trí cleared his throat like he was reminding his buddy not to get too personal, but Bảy Viễn kept rolling:

"Answer number one: I affirm there is suspicion between Bình Xuyên and Zone 7. There've been so many shooting incidents over tax collection that how could commanders from one side dare go into the other's territory? Wear a shirt with red-and-green trim and they'd call you a 'shrimp-dipper' — accuse you of being a spy. I don't trust the Zone. But I came here this time not because of the Zone Chief position you're offering me.

Truth is, this is a 'lure the tiger out of the mountain' play — meant to pull me away from the Rừng Sác war zone. It's a low-class trick, even a kid can see through it. Bảy Viễn knows that and still came because I respect the guts of anh Tám Nghệ. One man, one spear, and he dared to come down to Unit 9 headquarters. Anh Tám had no idea death was waiting for him around every bend, every canal. But Bảy Viễn couldn't let my men lay a hand on an honored guest of the Bình Xuyên underworld — right on my own turf.

Tám Nghệ called me out: 'A tiger in the jungle is a tiger. You mean to tell me if he comes to the plain he turns into a fox?' Bảy Viễn came here too because of the brotherly warmth from Teacher Bảy of Dầu Tiếng and anh Mười Trí.

And while I'm at it, thanks to the three of you — Tám Nghệ, Mười Trí, and Bảy Trấn."

"Now for question two: Yes, I employed the two Lai brothers. The older one, Tư Sang, the younger, Năm Tài. These two have some education, they know how to get things done, so I put them in charge of the paperwork to free myself up for overall command. If you've got proof they're Deuxième Bureau men, I'll handle them myself — no need to hand them over to anyone.

Question three: Maurice Thiên. I've known this half-Chinese bastard for almost twenty years. He's from a rich family, well-educated, married rich, good at sports. When the Westerners came back in September '45, I know they grabbed him and forced him to work for them. But he just coasted, took his paycheck, didn't bust anybody. You say Tư Thiên was using me — but the truth is, Bảy Viễn was using Tư Thiên as cover.

As for harboring those three — Nguyễn Thành Long, Vũ Tam Anh, and Lai Hữu Tài — yeah, I put them up for a few days. But they never told me they'd tried to assassinate Lieutenant General Nguyễn Bình. How was I supposed to arrest them for something I didn't know about?"

Finished with his answers, Bảy Viễn was worn out. He pulled out a handkerchief and wiped his face, his neck, his arms.

Chairman Phạm Ngọc Thuần said:

"Let's take a break. We'll continue this afternoon."

While the delegates were resting, Ông Đốc phủ Phan Văn Chương came and sat down next to Bảy Viễn, fanning him as he spoke:

"I've heard of ông Bảy for so long, and now I finally have the honor of meeting you. If the two of you — the Zone Chief and the Deputy — can find common ground in this meeting, I would be truly, deeply happy. If both of you put the Fatherland's interests above everything, then even though I'm old and frail, I'm ready to serve you both like a beast of burden."

Everyone was moved by those heartfelt words.


---


# Disband the Binh Xuyen Forces?

That afternoon, the meeting was still being chaired by lawyer Pham Ngoc Thuan.

Quick and sharp as always, he called on Muoi Tri to answer Lieutenant General Nguyen Binh's questions:

One: That you hid Vu Tam Anh after this guy killed a guard and then broke out of jail with Tran Quang Vinh, coming up from the Mekong Delta to crash at your Chi Doi 4.

Two: That you covered for Nguyen Hoa Hiep when the Third Division got picked apart by Huynh Van Mot's troops — they'd busted 'em down squad by squad by feeding 'em rice, then sending in militia disguised as civilians to "look at the guns" before snatching those rifles right out of their hands. Nguyen Hoa Hiep's whole command staff ran straight into Chi Doi 4 and hid out for a few days before heading back over to the French side.

Three: That you let Vu Tam Anh and Bui Huu Phiet openly badmouth Ho Chi Minh's government right in front of me, Lieutenant General Nguyen Binh, and Messrs. Nguyen Ngoc Bich and Nguyen Kim Cuong — all while singing the praises of Nguyen Hai Than and Nguyen Tuong Tam — when I came to visit you after the battle at Hamlet Number 10.

Four: That your deputy, Sau Section, faked your signature to invite me to your house for a friendly dinner so somebody could open up on me with a submachine gun along the road. Lucky for me, I only got grazed in the shoulder and arm.

"Please, Mr. Muoi Tri, your thoughts on those four points."

Muoi Tri stood up, looked at Lieutenant General Nguyen Binh, and said in a warm tone:

"Since Chi Doi 4 was stationed near Zone 7, I probably know Brother Ba Binh better than any other Binh Xuyen commander. I've always considered Brother Ba a brilliant leader, someone worthy of being Zone Commander of War Zone 7 — the big brother zone of the whole Southern region. Just look back: when General Leclerc's expeditionary forces were rolling through the central and western provinces, cutting through everything like a knife through butter, and some folks were pushing for 'flee east' or 'flee west' — meaning run to the Eastern region or hop on a boat to Siam — Brother Ba Binh stayed steady. He stood firm on tit-for-tat, like when he hit hard on Western New Year's 1946, storming right into Bien Hoa town and throwing the French into total chaos.

Now let me answer Brother Ba's questions.

First question: Those two escaped convicts broke out of a western-region prison. If they went underground, how the hell was I supposed to know? Our communications back then were crap. If I'd known, I sure wouldn't have put 'em up in my house — wasting chickens and ducks on boozing day and night!

Second question: That business with Nguyen Hoa Hiep getting his guns taken by Huynh Van Mot and running to my place for shelter — I'm the type who plays the generous patron. Somebody shows up asking to crash and mooch a few meals, that's nothing unusual. By the time I realized he'd gone back over to the French, it was already done. If I gotta take a hit for that, fine, I'll own it.

Third thing: Vu Tam Anh and Nguyen Hoa Hiep talking trash about Uncle Ho's government — that's a given. Those two assholes are Vietnam National Party men. My fault was not shutting them up fast enough. I'll take that L too.

As for the fourth thing — now that's the real bombshell. That bastard Sau Section, my own deputy, who'd been with me from the start. The guy barely had any education. So he let Vu Tam Anh and Bui Huu Phiet sweet-talk him with 'one arrow, two birds' — first, take out Lieutenant General Nguyen Binh, and second, frame me so I'd end up in a re-education camp with no idea when I'd ever clear my name. Lucky for me, Brother Ba was sharp enough to spot Sau Section's forged signature. Otherwise I'd be the one in deep shit!"

Nguyen Binh raised his hand to speak:

"I've known for a long time that Brother Muoi has the heart of a generous patron — a necessary quality for a man who knows how to gather comrades and brothers around him. But Brother Muoi, you've taken it a bit too far. You need to think before you pick your friends. The old folks said it right: 'You've got to eat a whole bushel of salt before you know who your real friend is.'"

Lawyer Thuan turned to Tu Ty:

"Mr. Tu Ty, please answer Lieutenant General Nguyen Binh's questions:

One: About the matter where you pushed out Bay Quai, the commander of the An Dien forces in Thu Duc, to take over Chi Doi 25.

Two: About how you sheltered that professor Tran Quoc Buu — the same guy who later jumped ship and became a lackey for the enemy."

Tu Ty shook his head:

"About Bay Quai — Mr. Le Dinh Chi invited him to the Military Justice Department to strengthen their ranks. As for Tran Quoc Buu, the guy came crying and pleading for my protection, saying the Viet Minh might arrest him unjustly. I've always been a chivalrous type — when I see somebody in trouble, my heart goes out to 'em. That's all there is to it!"

The self-criticism session paused there, saving the most important matter for later: to disband the Binh Xuyen forces or not.

That morning, in his heart-to-heart remarks, Lieutenant General Nguyen Binh had only brushed over the issue — but it still set off sparks with the fierce reactions from Bay Vien, Sau Doi, Nam Ha, Muoi Tri...

By the afternoon, acting Chairman Pham Ngoc Thuan laid out the whole picture. So Bay Vien and the other Binh Xuyen commanders were calmer, more polite about it.

In his booming lawyer's voice, Thuan analyzed the pros and cons of maintaining the Rung Sat war zone on Bay Vien's model. The downsides were pretty damn obvious. For a long time, French Intelligence Deuxieme Bureau had been cozy with Bay Vien — the two sides had silently signed a non-aggression pact. You don't hit me, I don't hit you. That meant they'd turned Rung Sat into a highway for transporting weapons and supplies, moving around freely. Even more dangerous: they'd basically gotten a grip on the entire Binh Xuyen Inter-Zone — the fiercest fighting units in the Eastern region — and were about to turn the place into a Nationalist war zone backing Bao Dai against Ho Chi Minh's government. If we disbanded Binh Xuyen, we'd dodge that venomous enemy scheme. Plus, Binh Xuyen's honor wouldn't get soiled by bribes of cash and women.

Thuan hammered home a point that swayed everybody: all this time we've coddled Binh Xuyen, letting them have their own territory, their own army, their own finances, their own tax collection. That's a state within a state — absurd, isn't it?

From now on, dissolve Binh Xuyen. No more Binh Xuyen Command. No more Binh Xuyen troops. Everything merges into the big family of the National Guard.

The sky was already getting dark, but you could see the resentment on every face — from Bay Vien all the way down to the last man at the table.


---


# Falling for the Trick

After acting chairman of the Southern Resistance and Administrative Committee, Pham Ngoc Thuan, laid out the need to disband the Binh Xuyen forces, all the Binh Xuyen commanders stood there dumbfounded, stunned to silence. Nobody knew whether to believe it or not. Maybe Bay Vien was the only one who already knew.

Bay Vien spoke right up:

"This is a major issue. We need to caucus tonight. Tomorrow morning we'll give you gentlemen our answer."

That night, Bay Vien, Sau Doi, Muoi Tri, and Tu Hoanh met at Bay Vien's camp.

Bay Vien was wearing a sarong and a silk undershirt, flicking a paper fan back and forth:

"Goddammit, I knew the communists were pulling the old 'lure the tiger out of the mountain' trick, and I still fell for it like an idiot! I kept wondering — how could Tam Nghe and Bay Tran have the heart to screw me over like this?"

Muoi Tri shook his head:

"Those two didn't set out to hurt you, Brother Bay. From what I know, they genuinely wanted to get you down to the South for one meeting with Nguyen Binh to settle the bad blood between both sides. I don't know Tam Nghe as well as you do, but we all know Bay Tran from our days in Ben Tranh and Dau Tieng — the three of us swore brotherhood after the Southern Uprising."

Sau Doi was getting impatient:

"The South wants to disband Binh Xuyen. What the hell do we do?"

Tu Hoanh was just as hot-headed:

"I don't agree with disbanding Binh Xuyen. This name is our whole inheritance — how can we just dissolve it? Are we sons of bitches or what?"

Bay Vien laughed grimly:

"Don't get hot, you assholes. Nobody here's gonna let 'em disband or dissolve anything. If they leave us alone, we play ball. If they try to shut us down, we each go our own way. I've got my own connections and my own resources — what the hell am I scared of?"

Muoi Tri tried to calm things down:

"That's just us talking among ourselves. Tomorrow morning, Brother Bay, you gotta choose your words carefully. Keep it smooth. As long as there's water, keep paddling. Try to talk 'em out of disbanding Binh Xuyen."

After Bay Vien sent the boys home, he called Tu Sang and Nam Tai over:

"Nguyen Binh keeps insisting you two are Deuxieme Bureau agents. He wants me to hand you over. You better tell me the damn truth so I know what to do."

Tu Sang and Nam Tai both went pale. They dropped to their knees and grabbed his legs, whining:

"Nguyen Binh's telling the truth, Mr. Bay. The two of us are Deuxieme Bureau operatives. Lam Ngoc Duong and Tu Thien placed us in your Chi Doi."

Bay Vien cried out:

"Is that so? Goddammit, Nguyen Binh's intelligence is good! What the hell do I do now? Hand you over to him — is that the right move? If I don't, that's a whole mess of trouble. How am I supposed to eat and talk with Nguyen Binh tomorrow?"

Nam Tai was shaking like a lizard with its tail cut off:

"Mr. Bay, if you hand us over, our lives are as good as finished. Better that I disappear tonight and take a squad back to the city. Before I left, I asked Mr. Tu Thien about this. He said if things went smooth, fine — but if we'd walked right into a trap, I needed to get back ASAP and report so we could arrange for you to pull the whole Binh Xuyen Inter-Zone back to the city."

Bay Vien was startled:

"I haven't even thought about that yet. If I don't take the Zone 7 Commander post, I'll pull the troops back to Rung Sat. Why the hell would I go back to Saigon?" He laughed. "I left with a promise: 'once I leave, I'm never coming back.' Now I'm supposed to turn around halfway, neither fish nor fowl, and slink back with my tail between my legs? What would my folks in Phu Tho think of me? They'd see me as less than a pile of shit!"

Nam Tai turned on the charm, smooth-talking like a politician:

"Mr. Bay, don't you see? You've fallen for the Viet Minh's trick! They invited you down here to appoint you Zone 7 Commander, but then they disband the Binh Xuyen organization. Let me ask you: between the post of Zone Commander and Commander of Binh Xuyen, which one's bigger? Zone 7 Commander still answers to the Southern leadership. But Binh Xuyen? Binh Xuyen bows to nobody!"

Bay Vien nodded thoughtfully. Nam Tai pressed on:

"Now that you know you've been tricked, you've gotta move fast. I'm heading back to the city tonight to sound the alarm. Tomorrow, you pull your forces out. We'll meet up in the Phu Lam — Binh Chanh area and figure out our next move from there."

Bay Vien let out a breath, shook his head, and waved his hand:

"Alright. You go ahead and pave the way. Let me see how tomorrow plays out, then I'll figure it out."


---


# Refusing the Post of Zone 7 Commander

Deep in the Plain of Reeds, in the dead quiet of night, Nam Tai led his squad paddling a four-oar sampan flying like a bullet. The men sat ready with their submachine guns — every time they hit a checkpoint, they'd cut loose with a whole magazine to blast their way through, making a break for the outskirts of Saigon. Meanwhile, Tu Sang was going house to house checking on Chi Doi 9's positions, setting up guards carefully — scared the Viet Minh might snatch them in the middle of the night.

The next morning, the meeting started back up.

Bay Vien answered sharp and hard to every charge General Nguyen Binh threw at him:

"Mr. Zone Commander, yesterday you accused me of having a man named Lai Huu Tai in my Chi Doi 9, saying he's a Deuxieme Bureau agent. I investigated, and yeah, he's one of Tu Thien's guys. But Tu Thien is my close friend. Back when I was broke, he gave me capital to do business. When the French came back, I knew they'd strong-armed him into working for them — his job was just to sniff out who among the big Chinese merchants in Cho Lon was with the Viet Minh. The French wanted to shake down the high-profile Chinese for money. I used to meet him all the time back then, and did he ever point me out to the French for arrest? Never.

You say Tu Thien was using me. The truth is, I was using Tu Thien's open, legal cover.

As for Nam Tai — on this trip, it's like the bastard had some sixth sense. He asked to stay behind and guard the camp. If you want to arrest him, Mr. Zone Commander, send your people back to Rung Sat to get him!"

Sau Doi, Tu Hoanh, and Muoi Tri had to bite their lips to keep from laughing at Bay Vien's three-card monte shuffle — it wasn't fooling anybody, not even Lieutenant General Nguyen Binh. Everyone knew that the two 'hard' companies Bay Vien had brought with him included none other than Mr. Nam Tai, Bay Vien's own right-hand man.

General Nguyen Binh smiled thinly and pivoted to another topic:

"Today we're holding the handover ceremony for the Zone 7 Commander post. I want to take this opportunity to hand over to Mr. Bay a massive legacy — one with more battle honors than any of the other three zones in the South. There have been plenty of victories already, and if we can strengthen our unity within the ranks, victory will follow victory.

So first of all, I want us to clear up all misunderstandings and resolve every point of discord. I believe that you gentlemen are honorable men of the rivers and lakes who have willingly and consciously shed the bad habits of the colonial era to devote yourselves heart and soul to the revolution. I know the colonialists never miss a chance to pull you back onto that dark road you once walked. They throw money, luxury goods, even prostitutes into the zone to tempt you. But I also know they've failed — because you understand that the honor of a man who follows the resistance is recognized by people across the whole country, especially the folks in Saigon-Cho Lon who see you as heroes, as true gentlemen — men who can't be bought with money, can't be bent with force, and can't be shaken by hardship.

I hold you in the highest regard. Money by the millions is easy to come by, empty titles are easy to chase — but the name of a hero, a righteous man that you're holding in your hands right now — that's something damn hard to get. Choosing the man to hand over the Zone Commander post to — I'm proud of it."

The words of the Southern Region's military commissioner were full of genuine feeling. Yet Bay Vien's face stayed stone cold:

"I'm deeply moved by your sincere words, Mr. Military Commissioner, but... unfortunately, all of us — the entire Binh Xuyen command — have an issue. Unless this issue is resolved, we can't proceed with handing over the Zone Commander post. And that issue is the decision to disband Binh Xuyen.

Last night, we discussed it carefully. Not a single one of us will accept the dissolution or disbanding of Binh Xuyen." He paused, then raised his voice. "Binh Xuyen — this name has its own glorious history. How many fighters have fallen under the Binh Xuyen banner, first raised high by the late Major General Ba Duong in the very first hours after we seized power in Saigon on August 25, 1945?

We — Bay Vien, Sau Doi, Muoi Tri — we might die, but the name Binh Xuyen? You can't murder it. I'm asking you to think this over carefully. Is there any way to withdraw the decision to disband Binh Xuyen or not?"

There was a long silence. General Nguyen Binh looked toward acting Chairman Pham Ngoc Thuan, as if yielding the floor. Thuan adjusted his horn-rimmed glasses squarely on the bridge of his nose and spoke solemnly:

"Gentlemen of the Binh Xuyen command. Yesterday I explained this in detail. Today, at your request, I will repeat the main points:

After careful consideration, the Southern Standing Committee has decided to dissolve the Binh Xuyen organization. Why? For the following reasons:

One — there cannot be a state within a state. All this time, the South has been lenient with you gentlemen, which is why we haven't brought up this violation of organizational and administrative principles.

Two — not only is it wrong in principle, it's caused serious disunity. Binh Xuyen does not respect its own superiors — neither Zone 7 nor the Southern leadership.

Orders come down from above, and every other unit carries them out — like disbanding the chi dois to form regiments under the regular army structure. Then there's the tax problem — everybody collecting taxes however they want, making life hell for the river merchants. I know that in Chi Doi 9, you refused to implement the political commissar system and discriminated against Northern cadres.

Now we come to the serious part.

Intelligence reports that the head of the Deuxieme Bureau, Lieutenant Colonel Savani, has detailed two agents — Lam Ngoc Duong and Maurice Thien — to keep close tabs on Deputy Zone Commander Le Van Vien. They've even installed Tu Sang and Nam Tai as the Deputy Commander's 'left and right-hand ministers.' This Nam Tai character even represented Deputy Zone Commander Bay Vien at meetings to form the National United Front against the Viet Minh.

Phan Hue, who was just arrested at Chi Doi 7 of Brother Hai Vinh, also confessed to having contacts with Nam Tai about setting up a Binh Xuyen national war zone.

For all these reasons, the Southern leadership has decided to disband Binh Xuyen to prevent worse destruction down the road."

A long silence again. Bay Vien returned to his same old refrain:

"I had a feeling this trip would decide my fate. I knew damn well this was a 'lure the tiger out of the mountain' play, but I came anyway — so everybody would know that Bay Vien is no coward. As for the French trying to buy me off — that's your judgment call. Do you have any concrete evidence that Bay Vien has betrayed the resistance? I'll tell you straight up: I will not accept the post of Zone 7 Commander if you dissolve Binh Xuyen."


---


# The Verdict

When Bảy Viễn turned down the Zone 7 Commander post, it blindsided the Southern Region Standing Committee. The only way to keep him was to reverse the decision to dissolve the Bình Xuyên organization. But that wasn't something they could give ground on — the French had already made up their minds to turn Bình Xuyên into allies, and they were moving toward setting up a national military zone on orders from High Commissioner Émile Bollaert. So how the hell do you solve a mess like this?

The Standing Committee called an emergency meeting that same night. Two opposing views clashed.

Lieutenant General Nguyễn Bình, speaking as the Southern Region Military Commissioner and Vice Chairman of the Southern Administrative Resistance Committee, pushed to have Bảy Viễn arrested and brought before the Supreme Court. He said:

"I'm a soldier. The revolutionary army's motto is 'reward merit, punish crime.' We've got plenty of proof that Bảy Viễn's been dancing in the dark with the Deuxième Bureau. The Phán Huề case over in Chi đội 7 shows that Nguyễn Văn Xuân's puppet government and Prime Minister Trần Văn Hữu are dead set on wooing Bảy Viễn to get their hands on Bình Xuyên. This is a rare chance — Bảy Viễn's come to the Southern Region for the first time, and it might be the last. We should nab him and put him on trial, fair and square."

Quite a few committee members nodded along.

But Lê Duẩn, head of the Southern Region Militia Department, threw out the opposite view:

"I say let Deputy Zone Commander Lê Văn Viễn go back to Rừng Sác free. Let's not blow this thing up."

Nguyễn Bình shot back at Lê Duẩn.

"Why the hell would we let Bảy Viễn go when we've got all the evidence of his counter-revolutionary activity?"

Lê Duẩn looked around the room for a moment, then laid it out slow and easy:

"Arresting him would be easy. But blood would spill, because Bảy Viễn came prepared when he took the risk of coming here. We sent two or three of his closest men to convince him to come down, but he was dead sure it was a trap — 'lure the tiger out of the mountains.' So he brought two hardcore companies with him, including a 20mm cannon he borrowed from Chi đội 4."

Nguyễn Bình cut him off:

"What the hell are two companies against the dozen regiments we've got scattered across Zones 7 and 8?"

Lê Duẩn went on:

"Let me finish my thought. The Lieutenant General and Military Commissioner is speaking from his military position, and that's fine. But I'm a political officer, so let me lay out my political view. Before this, we made some serious mistakes with the religious sects — the crackdowns on Cao Đài and Hòa Hảo. Lucky for us, we caught ourselves in time when we saw the colonialists' divide-and-conquer game, and we tried to fix things. Now here comes the Bình Xuyên situation. If we arrest Bảy Viễn and put him on trial — and his sentence would definitely be death — what happens next? The colonialists would be clapping their hands and hollering:

'See? Didn't we tell you? Việt Minh thinks they've got the monopoly on patriotism. Việt Minh wipes out the religious sects. Bảy Viễn followed Việt Minh for three years, climbed all the way to Zone 7 Commander, and they still grabbed him and killed him just like that?'

So that's my take on the Bảy Viễn problem. What do you all think?"

A few people nodded in agreement.

One person said:

"Both of you have good points, each speaking from your own position. The military side saying arrest him and put him on trial — that makes sense. The political side worrying about the colonialists spreading propaganda that we've got a monopoly on patriotism and we massacre the sects — that's reasonable too."

Someone laughed:

"Saying it like that, you're sitting on the fence, aren't you? I say we vote, party rules."

Lê Duẩn spoke up:

"Before we vote, let me say one last thing. Bảy Viễn marching his troops back to the French-controlled city — that's him signing his own political death warrant. All this time he followed the revolution, and the people respected him for his patriotism. But now he's suddenly running back to the city, tearing off the mask he's been wearing for three years. Way I see it, the death sentence has already been signed — by Bảy Viễn himself. We don't need to bother dragging him to court and making more trouble for ourselves."

In the end, they took a show of hands, and the majority went with Lê Duẩn.

That same night, two messengers from Chi đội 9 — Hoằng and Cung — raced down to the Southern Region to tell Bảy Viễn that his headquarters had been purged.

The moment he heard the bad news, Bảy Viễn went pale, his legs nearly gave out. His voice came out rough and breathless:

"Purged how? Tell me every goddamn detail!"

Hoằng and Cung both started talking over each other:

"Our two companies had just reached Đồng Tháp Mười when the clean-out order came. Every chi đội got hit. The political commissars were running the show. Over at our Chi đội 9, it was Tám Tâm leading the charge. He had a couple of loyal platoons blocking all the canals in and out of the city. Tư Ty had just brought his wife into town when Trần Công Đức and Lưu Quý Thoái grabbed him. Tám Tâm was hunting down your close men — like Mr. Lâm Ngọc Đường. Word is, Mr. Đường was quick enough to jump on a sampan and head into the marshes. Don't know if he made it out. Ba Rùm, who ran the weapons workshop — Tám Tâm got him too. Word is Ba Rùm put up a fight and called on Mr. Năm Hà to step in."

Bảy Viễn cursed:

"Son of a bitch, that Tám Tâm! If I'd snuffed him out from the start, none of this would've happened."

Tư Sang jumped in:

"Mr. Tư Thiên's read on the situation was spot-on. He said coming down to the Southern Region was walking into the 'lure the tiger out of the mountains' trap. But let's turn their own game against them. Now's the time to pull the whole crew back to the city. Word is, Major General De la Tour will set aside some land for us over by Chánh Hưng, a temporary camp."

Bảy Viễn let out a long sigh:

"Stupid. So goddamn stupid. I knew it was a trick and I still fell for it. Since that's where we're at, you tell 'em to pull back — the faster the better."


---


# Silent Retreat

Bảy Viễn's two hardcore companies slipped away in the dead of night, moving quiet along the Dương Văn Dương canal. Tư Sang's order was simple: hurry up and keep your goddamn mouths shut. Even so, flashlight beams kept flickering on the canal banks now and then in the darkness, proof that the reconnaissance teams from the surrounding regiments had been tipped off and were silently tracking the getaway — orderly as it was — of the former Deputy Zone Commander.

Bảy Viễn usually liked wearing a sarong and a thin three-hole undershirt to stay cool, but on this historic night he suited up in full military uniform, strapped on his weapons proper, ready for whatever might jump out. Every now and then the Bình Xuyên soldiers caught glimpses of the regimental recon teams still tailing them close, but nobody fired a shot. Later they'd find out why: Southern Region Militia chief Lê Duẩn had given the order to let Bảy Viễn pull back to Rừng Sác, but Lieutenant General and Southern Region Military Commissioner Nguyễn Bình had countermanded — order the regiments to stay on his tail, because he knew damn well Bảy Viễn wasn't heading to Rừng Sác but straight back to the city.

When they reached the Đông Thành Military Sector, where Mười Trí's Chi đội 4 was stationed, Bảy Viễn breathed a sigh of relief and ordered his two companies to pull in and take shelter.

"Brother Mười, you got any way to get us out safe? Nguyễn Bình's regimental recon teams are all over our ass. I'm scared we'll trade fire any minute now."

Mười Trí nodded and called Ba Chiêu, his Chi đội 4 office chief, over:

"Go out there and tell the fellas that Brother Mười here will convince Mr. Bảy not to pull back to the city. Ask them to let the retreat go through. If anything happens, Brother Mười will take full responsibility."

Mười Trí had his wife kill some chickens and ducks to throw a feast for Bảy Viễn. The two brothers talked heart-to-heart.

Mười Trí probed:

"Political Commissar Hai Trí's already given the order to cleanse Rừng Sác. What're you gonna do now, Brother Bảy?"

Bảy Viễn let out a breath:

"What the hell am I supposed to do? They tricked me into going down to Tháp Mười so they could clean out my base. Now there's no base left. Only thing left is to go back to the city, borrow some land from the French, and figure things out later."

Mười Trí shook his head:

"You thought this through? Remember last fall, the two of us swore that oath — 'once we step out, we don't come back'?"

Bảy Viễn:

"How could I forget? But things are different now." He sighed. "I got old and stupid. Made one fatal mistake."

"What mistake?"

"Acting before I'd thought things through. I joined the resistance but I never understood how sharp those schemers are — that Hai Trí."

Mười Trí said:

"About heading back to the city — you ought to think it over again. There's still time to bail water out of a sinking boat…"

Bảy Viễn shook his head:

"There's no other road left. My base at Tắc Cây Mắm is gone, and you're telling me not to go back to the city?"

Mười Trí slammed his glass of liquor down on the table:

"It's shameful, man! I'm telling you to reconsider. To the French, what are we? We're bums, we're thieves, we're convicts shipped off to Côn Đảo to break rocks, scrape coral, chop wood up in the mountains. Only by following the resistance do we become somebody. You and me, we're both commanders. You're even better off than me — Deputy Zone Commander, then Zone Commander…"

"I don't give a damn about that bullshit. I'd rather be a rooster's head than a phoenix's tail. Zone Commander — what the hell is that? All I ever wanted was to lead Bình Xuyên. But they're set on dissolving Bình Xuyên. How the hell am I supposed to play ball with that?"

Mười Trí nodded:

"I don't agree with dissolving Bình Xuyên either. If the French are trying to use Bình Xuyên, then we've just got to be sharper than them. Why the hell dissolve it? Keep Bình Xuyên around — any Deuxième Bureau bastard comes sniffing around, we grab him on the spot. The government's in our hands, what're we scared of?"

Bảy Viễn listened to Mười Trí talk, looking like he was thinking it over, but he stayed quiet.

The strange thing was, Mười Trí had quit drinking a long time ago, but at this feast he was putting away glass after glass.

After a while, Mười Trí's voice got slurred:

"Bảy Viễn, listen to me on the city thing. Think it over. Going back to surrender to the French — it's shameful, man. How many lifetimes you been climbing, and in the end, a monkey's still a monkey?"

Bảy Viễn said gloomily:

"You gotta put yourself in my shoes. I've lost everything. What's left for me here?"

"But surrendering to the French is a disgrace that lasts forever. Three years of chopping wood and it all goes up in one hour's fire. If you don't have Tắc Cây Mắm anymore, just stay here with me. I'll vouch for you. We'll build it back up together, you and me. What's the big deal? Go get some sleep. I'm hoping you'll wake up tomorrow with a different mind."


---


# Returning to the City

Mười Trí ordered his eldest son, Company Commander Ly, to set up a mosquito net for Brother Bảy to sleep. Then he called both Ly and Ba Chiêu into the office for a secret talk.

"Tomorrow morning, your Uncle Bảy's pulling his troops back to the city. I've been trying to talk him out of it, but he won't budge. So we've got to limit the damage to the resistance effort…"

Both Ly and Chiêu were taken aback.

"Really, Pa?"

"What're you gonna do, Uncle Mười?"

Mười Trí waved his hand for quiet:

"Let me finish talking first. Nobody knows Bảy Viễn better than me. That bastard Hai Trí ordered the cleanse of Rừng Sác while your Uncle Bảy was down in the Southern Region to take his post as Zone 7 Commander. Me personally? I don't agree with dissolving the Bình Xuyên organization."

"Why dissolve Bình Xuyên, Pa?" Ly asked.

"The Southern Region figures the French planted their Deuxième Bureau agents inside Chi đội 9 — specifically those two, Tư Sang and Năm Tài. The Deuxième Bureau was supplying money, weapons, supplies — even sending young pretty taxi girls down for your Uncle Bảy…"

Ba Chiêu exclaimed:

"That kinda thing too, Uncle Mười? Watch out people start rumors…"

"It's true. I personally took care of one little bitch who called herself 'Madame Deputy Zone Commander' at your Uncle Bảy's own place in Tắc Cây Mắm last year."

Both Ba Chiêu and Ly jumped:

"You took out Uncle Bảy's sweetheart? Didn't he go ballistic at you?"

"When I got there, your Uncle Bảy was at a meeting. The little devil was home alone. Broad daylight and she was parading around half-naked like she was at a nightclub. I was already fed up. When I asked where Bảy Viễn was, she pissed me off worse by snapping back: 'And who the hell are you to ask about the Deputy Zone Commander? I'm Madame Deputy Zone Commander — how come I don't know you?' You hearing this? Makes your blood boil, right? So I pulled out my gun, and my mouth and finger worked at the same time:

'That bastard Bảy Viễn's got a little wife he doesn't know how to train, so let me do it for him!'"

Ly asked nervously:

"When Uncle Bảy got home, how'd he react?"

"About half an hour later your Uncle Bảy got back. I'd left the gun on the table. I hit him first:

'You've got an insolent little wife! She called herself Madame Deputy Zone Commander and addressed me like a dog. You don't know how to train a concubine, so I did it for you. The gun that took care of her is right there on the table in front of you. Whatever you want to do about it, I'll take it.'

Your Uncle Bảy freaked out. He rushed over and picked up the girl, but she'd been dead a while. He was quiet for a long time, then finally said, his voice all hollow:

'How the hell do you train somebody? That was rough! You killed my sweetheart!'

Your Uncle Bảy didn't even know who his sweetheart really was, so I laid it out straight: 'All this time you've been cuddling a poisonous snake and didn't even know it. Hai Bạc's recon team told me this girl was sent down from the Tabarin nightclub by Tư Thiên's bunch to be an informant. You don't believe me? Think back. Since this little devil showed up, how many times has your Chi đội 9 gotten bombed by planes? And how accurate were those bombings?'

That's when your Uncle Bảy finally opened his eyes…"

Mười Trí paused, then shifted to a stern tone:

"Tomorrow morning I'll see your Uncle Bảy part of the way and keep trying to talk sense into him. There's still time to bail water out of a sinking boat. Ly, you keep the Chi đội office secure. Watch out — other regiments might come surround us and pull our guns. If anything happens, keep your cool and tell 'em: 'Wait for my father to get back. Worst case he'll be here by tonight.' And Ba Chiêu, you go find that bastard Xê for me right now. Tell him Bảy Viễn's heading back to the city. Uncle Mười orders that the heavy artillery company Bảy Viễn borrowed from Chi đội 4 stays put — it does not cross the Vàm Cỏ River until Uncle Mười gives the word. You hear me? We've got to limit the damage to the resistance."

Ba Chiêu nodded:

"I'll go find Xê right now. I'll make it clear that as of this moment, Uncle Mười is not lending Bảy Viễn the heavy artillery company anymore. Then I'll come straight back to help Ly secure the office."

All night Mười Trí tossed and turned. Every now and then he'd sit up, open the door, and stare out at the canal bank, lost in thought like he was working something out in his head. Before the sky had even started to lighten, he was already up, dressed neat, boiling water and brewing a pot of strong tea, drinking alone. It was a good while before Bảy Viễn finally woke.

Mười Trí's wife had cooked sticky rice and made lemongrass chili chicken, setting it out for the two brothers to eat before they hit the road — something solid in their bellies for the march.

As soon as he picked up his chopsticks, Mười Trí asked Bảy Viễn:

"A night brings a hundred plans. So? After sleeping on it, you see any new moves on the board?"

Bảy Viễn sighed:

"Those bastards play chess blind compared to the guys watching from the sidelines. I don't have any other road left."

Mười Trí:

"I'll see you part of the way. We'll keep talking while we walk."

Bảy Viễn looked moved:

"Thanks, man. You're my blood brother. Wherever I end up, I'll never forget you."

They hadn't even finished eating when Tư Sang came running up:

"We're in trouble, Mr. Bảy! Xê's heavy artillery company has vanished. I did a head count — we've only got one company left."

Bảy Viễn looked at Mười Trí:

"You ordered Xê to abandon us, didn't you?"

Mười Trí played dumb:

"I've been here with you all night. When did I go outside to order Xê around?" He laughed dryly. "Look, I've been thinking — maybe you see it the same way I do. These soldiers aren't the old-timey gang members we used to have. They've got their own minds. They follow whoever they want to follow. How can we keep 'em? If Xê found out you were heading back to the city, of course he wouldn't come along! Why would I need to say anything?"

Bảy Viễn sighed:

"Going back to the city with just one company — that's too weak. The French won't think I'm worth a damn…"

All along the road from the Đông Thành Military Sector back to Quán Cơm, Vườn Cò hamlet in Hưng Long commune, Bình Chánh district, Bảy Viễn was so bitter he barely spoke a word. Dropping everything halfway and heading back to the city already felt terrible, but doing it with barely one company made it worse. This old gangster's mind kept drifting to the opera about the bandit chief Từ Hải who surrendered to the imperial court, reduced to a confused turncoat. Now Bảy Viễn could see his own dark future coming: the French had been ready to welcome him as Zone 7 Commander of the Việt Minh, or Commander of the Bình Xuyên Interzone with seven chi đội and ten thousand troops — but now with only one company, how the hell was he supposed to face his French masters?

Mười Trí knew exactly what was going through Bảy Viễn's head, so he twisted the knife:

"You ought to think this over real careful. I'm taking you as far as Quán Cơm, then I'm turning back. Going to the city with just one company — that's way too weak. Before, the French rated you high. They saw you as Zone 7 Commander, Bình Xuyên Interzone Commander — that's why they were so eager to court you. But now you show up in the city with one lousy company? That's like a mountain giving birth to a mouse. Not a single general is gonna bother strapping on his uniform to come greet you. Don't you see that?"

Bảy Viễn let out a breath:

"Of course I see it. That's why I'm so goddamn miserable."

Mười Trí didn't let the opportunity slip:

"Think it over. I'm telling you, turn back. Stay at my Chi đội 4. I'll take responsibility for you. Little by little we'll build it all back up. How many times have we faced storms on the open sea and survived every one? This is no different. Think of it as a raft escaping prison getting hit by a tidal wave."

Bảy Viễn gave a smile like a grimace:

"Thank you, man. Really. But I've made up my mind. I can't live with those people anymore. They don't trust me, so they'll never let me off the hook. You try to save me and you'll be the one they accuse. I mean it — I'm worried about you. They're already suspicious you'll come back to the city with me, just watch. You'll catch hell for seeing me this far down the road."

Mười Trí frowned:

"I'm the one worrying about you, and here you are worrying about me. I've got nothing to worry about — my conscience is clean. It's you I'm worried about. You've made up your mind to go back to the city, so consider this the end of the line for me. You're no longer worthy of being my brother on the road, following the revolution. From here on out, we go our separate ways. Alright, I'm heading back."

Bảy Viễn jolted and reached out his hand, but Mười Trí shook his head:

"No need to shake hands. You and me — from this moment, we're on opposite sides. I follow the resistance. You follow the French. I'm ashamed to have a friend who 'climbed for three years and turned into a demon in one hour.'"

Mười Trí turned and headed back, his heart heavy beyond words. He'd failed. He couldn't convince Bảy Viễn. As for Bảy Viễn, he stood frozen, stunned, when Mười Trí left so abruptly without shaking his hand. His heart ached, losing a friend who'd walked through death with him so many times.

He was standing there lost in thought when Tư Sang came running up:

"Bad news, Mr. Bảy! Bảy Cao took two squads out to scout this area, and he led the whole bunch back into the liberated zone. First they fired off a few volleys — 'pretending to fight the French' — then they surrendered to the resistance."

Bảy Viễn went pale:

"Bảy Cao turned on me too? Really?"

"Yes, sir. They just opened fire. We chased after them but couldn't catch up."

Bảy Viễn did the math in his head:

"One company minus two squads. Listen up, you've got to hold it together. Watch out — more squad leaders are gonna take their men and run back into the zone just like Bảy Cao. Shit. Showing up in the city with only a couple platoons left — that's a damn embarrassment."

Tư Sang ordered a roll call of all squads. A little while later he came back with the report:

"Mr. Bảy, we've only got two platoons left. After Bảy Cao, another squad ran back into the marshes too."

Bảy Viễn threw his hands up:

"Just two platoons. How fucking humiliating." He muttered to himself. "Not listening to Mười Trí was a huge mistake." He waved Tư Sang away. "Son of a bitch. Why have I been so foggy-headed these last few days? Throwing away a brother I've lived and died with for two sleazy schemers. A pathetic way to die — that's what I deserve."

Năm Tài had already reached Quán Cơm to meet Bảy Viễn.

Hearing Bảy Viễn's complaints, Năm Tài said:

"Don't worry, Mr. Bảy. I've already talked with Mr. Tư Thiên. He says we don't need to meet General De la Tour right away. We'll set up camp temporarily by the Phạm Thế Hiển market, get organized, rebuild our ranks. The French will launch operations into the liberated zone to bring the other Bình Xuyên chi đội out to the city. Then the French will give us money to recruit more troops, fill out our numbers. After some training, when everything's in order, we'll present ourselves to General De la Tour…"

Bảy Viễn grabbed onto it like a drowning man finding a float:

"You're good. If it really happens like you say, I'll reward you handsomely."


---


# Colonel Bay Vien

Colonel Bay Vien

When Maurice Thien brought Nam Tai to meet Lieutenant Colonel Savani with the news that Bay Vien had suddenly pulled his men back to the city because the Rung Sac war zone got swept clean, that Deuxieme Bureau boss just shrugged with a letdown look on his face. He muttered under his breath:

Bay Vien — the Binh Xuyen chief

"Failure on top of failure! Remember that Thanh Van boat incident, how it got hit right inside Bay Vien's own territory? The Saigon Special Zone Police already broke Savani and Bay Vien's 'sit-tight-and-do-nothing' game back then. And now, with Bay Vien suddenly hauling ass back to the city, Nguyen Binh just wrecked our whole plan to set up a Binh Xuyen national war zone in Rung Sac. Plain and simple, Bay Vien can't get shit done, can he? Don't you think so, Monsieur Maurice?"

Tu Thien, stuck in a spot where he had to smooth things over, said:

"Lieutenant Colonel, you shouldn't let frustration make you sell Bay Vien short. In the Saigon underworld, you gotta hand it to Bay Vien — the man's got the chops to lead the Binh Xuyen no problem. But..."

Savani gave a thin smile:

"But what?"

"There's an old saying — 'even a fierce tiger can't take on a pack of wolves.' How's Bay Vien supposed to stand up to the whole Viet Minh machinery on his own? We should've stepped in right away to help him fight back in time, but the problem is we were miles away from Rung Sac by river. Even by speedboat it takes half a day. Can't put out a fire from far away when the water's nowhere near. Bay Vien's loss is our loss too."

Savani nodded slowly:

"You're right. Bay Vien's loss is our loss — Deuxieme Bureau's loss. Him coming back to town with just two companies — that's way too weak. How the hell do we report that to the Major General commanding Southern Vietnam? Monsieur De la Tour's gonna be real disappointed seeing Bay Vien roll back with only two companies. As Deputy Zone Commander or Zone Commander of War Zone 7, a man's supposed to come back with five or six divisions — ten thousand troops at least."

Savani laughed coldly:

"The General's gonna crack up and say 'the mountain gave birth to a mouse.' All this time we've been hypnotized by those stories about the Binh Xuyen general fighting in the Viet Minh ranks. So what do we do now?"

Tu Thien pointed at Nam Tai:

"This kid here just had a pretty good idea. Bay Vien came back to the city on the defensive — caught flat-footed when the Viet Minh suddenly hit Rung Sac. So he only had two companies with him when he came back to Nam Bo to take up his Zone Commander post. Instead of reporting this to the Major General, we should temporarily move Bay Vien's Binh Xuyen forces over to the Chanh Hung area, around Pham The Hien market, to get them settled with food and shelter. Then the French army can launch an operation into Rung Sac and bring the rest of the Binh Xuyen units back to the city.

If we don't have enough men, we open recruitment offices, outfit them with uniforms and weapons, train them up in a few months, and then hold a ceremony to introduce the national volunteer government."

Savani looked at Nam Tai and nodded approvingly:

"Good idea! At least we got one or two guys who know how to work, like this kid here. I completely agree. I'm putting you in charge of handling everything on my behalf for Bay Vien in this early stage."

Tu Thien and Nam Tai rushed over to Chanh Hung to scope out the area. It was a suburban, semi-rural zone where most folks were laborers who hired themselves out during the off-season. Phan The Hien market was newly set up, with tall buildings rising up that made it look pretty prosperous. There was still plenty of empty land, so Tu Thien had a row of barracks-style housing built right away to take care of the two platoons that stayed loyal to Bay Vien.

Nam Tai chimed in:

"Anh Tu Sang will stay at the Pham The Hien compound to keep an eye on the troops. As for Mr. Bay, we gotta set him up with an office in the commercial district too — after all, Mr. Bay is the Binh Xuyen leader who came over to the central government of Nguyen Van Xuan."

"Naturally! I'll rent Mr. Bay a house on Marins Street — near the Three-Lamp Post."

Just like Nam Tai laid out, the French organized a campaign into Rung Sac to make way for the Binh Xuyen troops still stuck in Viet Minh territory to get back to the city. But by then, all the Binh Xuyen units had been reorganized into regimental structures like everywhere else in the country. Not a single Binh Xuyen company was left untouched — they'd all been broken up and merged into other units, turned into proper, tightly-organized regular regiments.

So even though the French operations looked impressive and big, they didn't get any real results.

Day after day, Bay Vien holed up wearing a national army officer's white khaki uniform but with no rank insignia yet, still waiting on General De la Tour's decision.

Bay Vien wasn't happy when the big French military operations ended without a single Binh Xuyen unit coming back to the city. There was only one option left — open recruitment offices. At Pham The Hien market, Xom Cui, Xom Chi, Cau Chu Y, Cau Tan Thuan, Cau Ong Lanh, and Cau Muoi market, they set up Binh Xuyen recruitment tables. Anyone who signed up got paid on the spot. Captain Nam Be personally oversaw the Binh Xuyen recruiting. However many recruits they got, Tu Sang was in charge of training them. They worked fast to race against time. The sooner they finished, the sooner they could hold the ceremony.

Before you knew it, the big day arrived. The Binh Xuyen army wore brand-new khaki uniforms that still smelled of mothballs fresh out of the quartermaster's warehouse. But the folks in Chanh Hung, Pham The Hien, and Cau Ong Lanh knew these troops all too well. They were all small-time hustlers from the markets — guys who hit rock bottom and got desperate enough to enlist for the signing bonus.

On that big day, Bay Vien stepped up to the podium and read a speech Nam Tai wrote, hammering home that they were "coming back to the national cause because they were sick of the Viet Minh's monopoly on patriotism."

General De la Tour pinned a colonel's insignia onto Bay Vien's shoulder.


---


# In Trouble Because of a Friend

In Trouble Because of a Friend

Just like Bay Vien predicted, Muoi Tri got himself in deep shit because he couldn't help seeing his buddy off. He tried to talk Bay Vien out of it but couldn't. When he got back to his Unit 4 headquarters, he saw all kinds of brother regiments posted around his office. The moment he walked in, his eldest son, Captain Ly, was all scowling and shit-faced:

"If you'd come home any later, I'd've opened fire already. Who the hell do they think they are, surrounding our base?"

Muoi Tri was already down in the dumps and now even more pissed. He didn't say a word, just walked down to the river to wash his face and clear his head, then came back inside and told his wife:

"Brew me a pot of strong tea, will you."

"You came back just in time. They've had our unit surrounded all night. That boy Ly kept saying 'an eye for an eye' — I told him to wait till his daddy got home."

While the tea was steeping, Muoi Tri said real calm-like:

"You two, don't go getting hot under the collar. This one's on me. Out of friendship, I saw Bay Vien off part of the way. We know what we did. But them? How they gonna know? They see me leaving with Bay Vien, they figure we're cooking up a plan to go over to the city side, so of course they surrounded our unit. That's fair. If I'd gone all the way, they'd've attacked, disarmed us, hauled everybody off for questioning... But we're real gold, ain't no fire that can burn us!"

After finishing his cup of tea, he called Ba Chieu over:

"Go out there and tell those boys surrounding us that Uncle Muoi's back. Tell 'em Uncle Muoi tried to talk Bay Vien into staying, but he failed. So Uncle Muoi takes full responsibility — I promised I'd keep him but couldn't. Tell the comrades to report to the Zone and Nam Bo that Muoi Tri is ready to face the music and take whatever punishment they dish out."

Ba Chieu was gone a long time before he came back in:

"They huddled for a good while before they agreed to send an urgent report to the Zone and Nam Bo with everything you said. But they still left a detail watching the perimeter."

Muoi Tri turned to Ly:

"Tell your company to stand down and get some rest. Get some food in you early. I'll handle everything else."

Ba Chieu, seeing the tension ease up, sat down next to Uncle Muoi:

"Uncle Muoi, how do you stay so calm? This is serious stuff, but you handled it so smooth and clean."

Muoi Tri laughed:

"You kids gotta stay calm. And if you wanna be calm, first thing — wash your face to cool off. Then drink a hot cup of tea. Sip it while you blow on it. While you're blowing, you think about what needs to be done — what's the main thing and what's secondary. Handle the main thing first, the side stuff later. Take this situation — the main thing is they think I went back to the city with Bay Vien.

So how do I act to prove I'm clean? Keep a steady nerve, carry yourself with total confidence. Real gold don't fear the fire. If they think you're crooked, you gotta prove you ain't.

A crooked man's got shifty eyes, a jittery walk, a jumpy look about him. But a loyal man stands tall and steady, speaks clear and firm, carries himself with authority... You ever watch cai luong opera? See how different Cao Cao is from Quan Cong, right?"

Muoi Tri's wife shook her head:

"Trouble's about to hit us and here you are going on about Quan Cong and Cao Cao. I'm so worried about you and you're just sitting there cool as a cucumber."

"What's there to worry about?"

"The Zone or Nam Bo's gonna call you up for questioning..."

"Well of course they will! I've been waiting for Nguyen Binh's summons. If you're that worried about me, cook dinner early. I need three bowls of rice in my belly to be sure of myself."

Right as Muoi Tri predicted, a few hours later an invitation letter came from the Nam Bo Military Commissioner Nguyen Binh.

The whole family was worried but Muoi Tri just laughed:

"Meeting Brother Ba Binh is easier to manage than meeting that Hai Tri bastard. And whoever I meet — real gold ain't scared of no fire! Ba Chieu, get the boat ready to take me there, 'cause I'm coming back, you hear."

Muoi Tri didn't take Nam Bo's boat — he took his own. A small thing, but it helped his family worry less about him.

At Nguyen Binh's office, Muoi Tri was treated like a comrade, not like somebody under suspicion.

Brother Ba Binh asked:

"Brother Muoi, you saw Bay Vien off all night — you tired?"

Muoi Tri sighed:

"Tired in the spirit more than anything. I gotta admit I failed — couldn't hold Bay Vien back like I promised you all. I tried to talk sense into him all night, but you guys swept Rung Sac clean, so where the hell was he supposed to stay? And I gotta say, I don't agree with Nam Bo dissolving the Binh Xuyen either. It was that decision that made Bay Vien refuse the Zone 7 Commander post."

Nguyen Binh nodded encouragingly:

"Brother Muoi, say everything that's on your mind. We'll hash it out after."

Muoi Tri said:

"That night when he stayed at my place, Bay Vien opened up to me:

'I joined the resistance with one decision — once I leave, I'm not coming back.' He's my friend, I know him better than anybody. He never wanted to come back to the city while he was in the resistance — that was when his life was really taking off. Coming back to the city, what do the French think he is? Some thug with a long rap sheet, deported to Con Dao three times, scum at the bottom of society. But he had to come back for two reasons: first, you guys dissolved the Binh Xuyen; second, you swept Rung Sac..."


---


# The Open Letter

The Open Letter

Nguyen Binh listened while Muoi Tri laid out all the reasons Bay Vien went back to the city to surrender to the French.

When he was done, Nguyen Binh pulled out a stack of documents and handed them over one by one:

"Brother Muoi, take a look through this pile, then we'll keep talking about Bay Vien."

The first document was a leaflet French planes had dropped all over the Dong Thap Muoi region. In it, Bay Vien denounced the Viet Minh for monopolizing patriotism, wiping out other political parties, and purging religious sects. It was signed by "Colonel Le Van Vien, Commander of the Binh Xuyen, formerly Deputy Zone Commander of War Zone 7."

Muoi Tri shook his head hard:

"That ain't Bay Vien's words — that's Deuxieme Bureau talking. Nam Tai probably wrote that up for Bay Vien to sign."

Nguyen Binh nodded:

"You could be right, Brother Muoi. In all this, Bay Vien's just a pawn of the Deuxieme Bureau. Now take a look at the second document."

The moment Muoi Tri's eyes skimmed a few lines, his face went pale. It was a telegram from the Binh Xuyen Inter-Zone troops sent to the Zone 7 Command, denouncing Bay Vien, Muoi Tri, and Tu Hoanh for exploiting the blood and bone of Binh Xuyen soldiers. The telegram had just four short points:

1. Binh Xuyen fighters have spilled their blood for three years — not for some thugs, bandits, or the Deuxieme Bureau's commandos.
2. Our big brothers are Brother Ba Duong, Mr. Tam Manh, Mr. Nam Ha... As for those guys Bay Vien, Muoi Tri, and Tu Hoanh, they've gone rotten and ain't fit to be our elders anymore.
3. We sacrifice for our country — not for the sake of some unworthy gangster bosses.
4. We demand to be allowed to be revolutionary soldiers, not slaves to a bunch of warlords.

Nguyen Binh asked:

"What do you think about these four points from the thousands of soldiers in the Binh Xuyen Inter-Zone?"

"They're right. But lumping me in with Bay Vien ain't fair, Brother Ba — can't you see that?"

Nguyen Binh smiled:

"There are things the man inside the fight don't see that the outsider sees real clear. Like playing Chinese chess. A while ago you said nobody knows Bay Vien better than you do. That's you being overconfident. I'll grant you know the old Bay Vien — back when you two were a couple of hotshot outlaws. But these last three years, with each of you posted somewhere different, you don't know Bay Vien as well as the French secret police and the Deuxieme Bureau do. And you sure don't know Bay Vien as well as our own police and intelligence do."

Muoi Tri winced, but Ba Binh kept going:

"It's exactly that overconfidence — judging a man with your heart instead of your head — that's why you couldn't see how far Bay Vien had fallen. You both walked the outlaw road together, but you're different, Brother Muoi. First off, you're a farmer at heart — used to working the land, so you know the value of honest labor, you don't take what ain't rightfully yours, and you don't run with crooked people. But Bay Vien? He's city scum through and through — whatever brings profit, he'll do it, don't matter if it's right or wrong, true or false. A man with that kind of crooked instincts is easy pickings for corruption. Tam Tam and his office crew kept thorough reports about the Deuxieme Bureau supplying him with luxury goods — even dancing girls from the clubs sent all the way down to Tac Cay Mam to entertain His Excellency the Deputy Zone Commander.

I heard you even put down one Deputy Zone Commander's wife who had the nerve to talk fresh to her husband's best friend."

Muoi Tri said:

"That Tam Tam character got real lucky. From what I know, Bay Vien suspected Tam Tam was Zone-planted to keep tabs on Unit 9's office. Bay Vien wanted to waste him once, but on the ride out Tam Tam talked his way out of it somehow and Bay Vien softened up and turned the boat around. Bay Vien's like a tiger — his temper flares hot just as quick as it cools down."

Nguyen Binh said:

"I asked you here for some urgent business, Brother Muoi. I need you to write an open letter addressed to Bay Vien — a statement to let the people know that a pair of old outlaw brothers have now gone their separate ways, each following their own ideals. I'll print a bunch and have our city outreach teams distribute them, so folks know Muoi Tri has cut ties the moment Bay Vien went down the wrong road. What do you say?"

"I'll write it right here and now for you, Brother. I sure need to make it clear to the Binh Xuyen Inter-Zone brothers that Muoi Tri put down the bottle and straightened up ever since joining the revolution — a far cry from Bay Vien. I also need to let those Deuxieme Bureau bastards know that Muoi Tri ain't as easy to bamboozle as Bay Vien."

Ba Binh handed him paper and pen:

"Just write what you said."

On a normal day Muoi Tri struggled to write anything, but when it came to defending his honor, he wrote fast.

Ba Binh read it aloud:

STATEMENT:

Mr. Le Van Vien has managed to trick a small number of fighters into surrendering to the French. He recently issued a declaration supporting the puppets Bao Dai and Nguyen Van Xuan. First there was Nguyen Hoa Hiep, Nam Lua, and now Bay Vien. Folks in Nam Bo are no strangers to those who run to the enemy. But since I am Bay Vien's close friend, I must say a few words in my own defense.

I deeply regret that I could not stop a friend who once swore to live and die together with me since childhood. We walked the outlaw path together, we stood against colonial tyranny together, we went in and out of prison together, we escaped from Con Dao many times back to the mainland. Since 1945, we joined the resistance to fight the French for independence.

But we were stationed far apart and grew distant. That's when the reactionary elements crawled in and incited Bay Vien against the Viet Minh. Now Bay Vien has openly gone over to the French, ignoring all my pleas.

Bay Vien has buried his honorable outlaw reputation, he has burned the three-year legacy of our fight against colonialism.

I set our friendship aside and am determined to follow the resistance to the end — to win independence, freedom, and happiness for the Vietnamese nation. For justice, for our people, we will fight and we will win.

Military Office, June 25, 1948.

Regiment Commander, Regiment 303 — Huynh Van Tri.


---


# The Hoa Hao Operation

Finishing up his statement, Muoi Tri felt pretty good when Nguyen Binh said, "Brother Muoi writes real nice," then he passed it straight down to the print shop to set type and print it for sending back to the city.

"You need me to stick around here for anything else, Brother Ba?"

Nguyen Binh nodded. "We gotta talk about something real serious, but before we get into it, let's have a little drink first."

I knew Brother Muoi had quit drinking, and me, ever since I got back from the island, I'd been off the bottle too. But a little something to wet the whistle and pass the time.

Sister Trinh, the code name, brought out a plate of stir-fried intestines, a bottle of black sticky rice wine, and two small glasses.

Sipping slow, Nguyen Binh said:

"Like Brother Muoi was saying earlier, Bay Vien went back to the city because of two mistakes we made: dissolving the Binh Xuyen and clearing out the Rung Sac forest. Let me lay it out straight about those two decisions. These weren't one man's call—they were a collective decision. Why we had to dissolve the Binh Xuyen, Brother Muoi already heard Pham Ngoc Thuan explain in detail. You can't have a country inside a country. Bay Vien was practically king of his own little kingdom inside the Rung Sac territory. Any stranger who wandered in there got taken out right away. Bay Vien didn't like political officers because he didn't want anybody snooping around his royal lifestyle. Section Two had infiltrated pretty deep, not recently, but as far back as the ceremony where Bay Vien got promoted to Deputy Zone Commander two years ago. At that one, Lam Ngoc Duong showed up, Tu Thien, Hộ Pháp Phạm Công Tắc, and even the Hoa Hao founder Huynh Phu So himself. Brother Muoi was there too, so you must've seen those big shots. Section Two kept getting a tighter grip on Bay Vien. That's why we had to dissolve the Binh Xuyen. Brother Muoi, you gotta put aside your personal feelings and see why this decision was necessary."

Muoi Trí nodded along. "But what about the Rung Sac clearing?"

"That was necessary too. Think about it, Brother Muoi. If we hadn't cleared out Rung Sac, Bay Vien would've gone right back there, fortified his little kingdom, and gotten bolder with Section Two. By then we'd have a hell of a time dealing with him. Better to crush the betrayal in the egg. We've got full documentation on every case of Bay Vien sneaking around with Section Two. Like when Bay Vien joined the National United Front, which had all those religious sects, Cao Dai, Hoa Hao... And that secret non-aggression pact Bay Vien signed with Savani—Brother Muoi himself busted that wide open with the attack on the Thanh Van boat. In that case, Brother Muoi played the lead role, but the real strategist was Brother Sau Hoang, Deputy Director of the Southern Public Security Department and Chief of the Special Zone Security. I'm sure Brother Muoi knows that story better than I do."

Muoi Tri laughed, pleased. "I picked two or three ambush spots, but Brother Sau Hoang shot 'em all down and suggested I borrow the Vam Sac canal from Unit 9 as the attack point and split the spoils. Later I found out I was helping Brother Sau Hoang bust up Section Two's plan to get control of Bay Vien."

Nguyen Binh: "Now Brother Muoi understands why we had to dissolve the Binh Xuyen and clear out Rung Sac. Fighting an enemy who shows his face is easier than dealing with an ally who's plotting to betray you. Now let's talk about your situation."

Muoi Tri put his chopsticks down and listened quietly.

"I know you well, but most folks suspect you unfairly. That suspicion got worse when you stood up to guarantee Bay Vien's safety, and then he ended up all the way in the city. That made people think you personally saw Bay Vien off all the way to the outskirts of Saigon. What did the two of you talk about? Lots of members in the Southern Committee swear that sooner or later, Muoi Tri would follow Bay Vien."

Muoi Tri nodded. "It's true, I acted out of friendship, didn't think about the suspicion from people around me. So what are you gonna do with me now?"

Ba Binh raised his glass. "Take it easy. We've worked out a nice smooth solution for Brother Muoi. We're sending you to the Mekong Delta."

"Sending me to the Delta? To do what? Ah, I get it. You all are scared Muoi Tri will follow Bay Vien, so you're pushing me far away. Fine! I'm ready to go to the Delta, but what am I supposed to do there? I can bring my Unit 4..."

"You can only bring a small group, Brother Muoi, maybe about a company. Forget that all the Binh Xuyen units got broken up and reassigned into regiments? We've lined up a real interesting job for you."

"What kind of job?"

"The Hoa Hao Operation. Getting the Hoa Hao followers to side with the Viet Minh against the French."

Muoi Tri's eyes went wide as he looked at Ba Binh, shaking his head. "I can't do that kind of work. First off, I'm not a religious man. Don't know a single prayer or chant. And this is tough work. The Hoa Hao are siding with the French against us in the Delta, same way the Cao Dai in Tay Ninh are with the French against us in the East."

Ba Binh: "That's exactly why we need you, Brother Muoi. Same way a big battle needs a general. I trust Brother Muoi can handle this new job. Don't know the scriptures? Learn 'em. What's so hard about that? You've got a huge advantage you don't even see yourself. The Political Commissar of Zone 9 gave us the idea, and that's how we lined this up for you."

Muoi Tri was even more surprised. "The Political Commissar of Zone 9? Bay Tran? What did Brother Bay suggest?"


---


# The Hoa Hao Uncle

The Southern Committee was going back and forth, not sure what assignment to give Muoi Tri to send him down to the Delta, far away from Bay Vien, when Zone 9's Political Commissar, Bay Tran, threw in his two cents:

"Why not give Muoi Tri the Hoa Hao Operation?"

Everybody was surprised. Bay Tran laughed and said:

"Muoi Tri was once given the title 'Hoa Hao Uncle' by the教主 Huynh Phu So himself. I figure with that title, Muoi Tri'll do a good job getting the Hoa Hao to side with us against the French."

Seeing everybody's puzzled faces, Bay Tran explained:

"I heard this story from Muoi Tri's wife. I double-checked with Nguyen Van Tay, the Political Inspector for the Delta, and it all matched up. So it's可信."

The教主 Huynh had once fled to Ba Queo and crashed with Muoi Tri. Those two got to know each other when the Japanese were keeping Huynh under house arrest in Saigon because the French wanted to ship him off to Laos to prevent future trouble. When the French pushed out to the outskirts of Saigon, Muoi Tri's troops pulled back to the Dong Thanh military zone. One day, planes started firing into Unit 4's bivouac area. The教主's supply company was running around in panic. Brother Muoi said: "Everybody stay with the Teacher. Don't run all over the place." Muoi Tri led Huynh to a pretty roomy house where they could take shelter. The planes fired at a few spots but never hit that house. Feeling grateful, Huynh pointed at Muoi Tri and told the bunch of disciples led by Nam Lua: "This is your Uncle from now on. When I'm far away, your Uncle here takes my place."

From that day on, Nam Lua treated Muoi Tri like his own Uncle.

Everybody cracked up when Bay Tran told that old story. Somebody said, "Sounds like a joke."

But Bay Tran got serious. "No joke. Faith matters. When you're doing people's mobilization work, you gotta respect people's faith."

Hearing Nguyen Binh bring up the "Hoa Hao Uncle" story again, Muoi Tri nodded.

"What Brother Bay Tran told is true. Nam Lua heard it with his own ears and saw it with his own eyes, the Teacher's words, so he sees me, respects me like his Uncle."

"So what does Brother Muoi think about us giving you the Hoa Hao Operation?"

Muoi Tri let out a breath. "How can I say no? I'm in a tight spot, and you all found me a way out. Even if I'm not... too thrilled about it, I'm grateful."

As he saw Muoi Tri out, Nguyen Binh said:

"Brother Muoi, go back and pick a company to bring with you to the Delta. The Southern Committee already wired Long Chau Ha province to help Regiment 304 of the Hoa Hao Uncle set up shop. Once you're settled in, then you can start the work."

Muoi Tri shook hands with Ba Binh: "Thanks, Brother Ba, for all your help."

Ba Binh patted Muoi Tri on the shoulder and said quietly:

"One more thing. Brother Muoi, let us borrow Company Commander Ly for a while..."

"Borrow that kid Ly? For what? And for how long?"

Ba Binh: "We already settled the big stuff nice and smooth. You gonna fight me over something small? Why do we want to borrow Ly? The kid knows this battlefield up here. He saved my life once at Hamlet 10 in Vinh Loc, when those commandos were only about two rice fields away from me. If it wasn't for Ly's FM burst that cut 'em down from the side, those dogs would've killed me. Brother Muoi, let me borrow him. I'll take responsibility for him. As for how long, hard to say. Maybe after one or two campaign seasons, I'll return him to you and your wife."

Muoi Tri nodded and headed back to his boat. But that nod was just politeness. Deep down, Muoi Tri still had questions about this whole thing.

Muoi Tri's wife wasn't as calm as her husband. When she heard him tell the whole story, she cried out:

"It's as plain as day! They suspect you'll follow Bay Vien, so they're shipping you off as far as possible. Long Chau Ha is about as far as it gets. That godforsaken backwater takes a whole day by bus just to get to Saigon. And even that's not enough—they gotta borrow our Ly too. They're taking him hostage, old man. If you go follow Bay Vien, they'll slit that little boy's throat, my precious baby."

"I know what you're thinking. But it ain't like that. How could I abandon the resistance and go to the city to surrender to the French, shameful like Bay Vien?"

That night, Muoi Tri drank again and scribbled something down in his notebook.


---


# The Only Poem

Living all-in for your friends, talking straight when they go wrong, putting your own political life on the line to vouch for them, seeing them off for miles and miles and trying your damnedest to talk sense into them, only to fail completely. Muoi Tri was heartbroken over how Bay Vien's life turned out, and pissed off that everybody was wrongly pointing fingers at him. Now Muoi Tri had to head down to the Delta for a job that was totally foreign to him: getting the Hoa Hao to side with the Viet Minh against the French.

The more he stewed over this jacked-up assignment, the more he mourned for the friend he'd sworn a blood oath with out on the open sea. That hard-to-say sorrow turned a barely-educated man who'd never had a thing to do with poetry into somebody who birthed verses that could shake a person's soul. The only poem Muoi Tri ever wrote in his whole life got jotted down in his notebook on the most painful night he'd ever known: losing a brother bound at the hip, and grieving for his own blurry future.

Ba Chieu, the company commander and Unit 4's chief of staff, was like a nephew to Muoi Tri, so sometimes he let himself rummage through the papers on Uncle Muoi's desk. Flipping open Uncle Muoi's notebook, Ba Chieu read the poem called "To Bay Vien," word for word:

So that's it then. You and me, we're done.
Because the roaming life, my friend, is no more fun.
You locked yourself in a gilded cage of empty fame,
A bird in a cage, craving a warm pillow just the same.
Do you know the enemy's hands are still soaked red
With our people's blood, a fire of vengeance in their stead?
Remember that autumn, remember that season
When history unlocked our nation from its prison?

So that's it then. You and me, two different shores.
Two paths in life that can't be reconciled anymore.
Between us now a churning ocean wide,
Two friends who once stood shoulder to shoulder, now on opposite sides.
The cart ahead broke down, the one behind won't swerve.
Alone I hold onto old times, a word I still reserve,
Because I believe you've got some conscience left to find.
A wandering knight's blood ought to run free and wild —
You gotta know how to live like a true man, fierce and proud.

Muoi Tri had only one week to pack up his whole family and move to the Delta for his new assignment.

He picked Ba Chieu's company to come along. The two of them, uncle and nephew, talked through the new job and really saw eye to eye.

Ba Chieu said: "Uncle Muoi got assigned the Hoa Hao Operation. If you ask me, it came out of nowhere, but when I think about it, we've got more advantages than anybody else."

"What advantages?"

"Back when Teacher Tu, the Hoa Hao leader, was hiding out with our unit, the Delta sent up a supply company led by Nam Lua. In that company there was a female squad that specialized in double swords... When Teacher Tu passed away, the Hoa Hao in the Delta rose up and started fighting alongside the French against the resistance. That supply company got rounded up and locked in Councilman Sam's house. A few months later, Uncle Muoi went and picked up a squad that included Tu Doc, Hong Anh, and two girls, Lan and Dieu. Remember, Uncle?"

"So?"

"This Hoa Hao squad will help Uncle Muoi make contact with the Hoa Hao groups in the Delta. Like Nam Lua's group at Cai Von, Ba Ngo's group at Cho Moi, Hai Ngoan's group at Cai Dau, Ba Cut's group at Bang Tang..."

Muoi Tri nodded. "Good! Thanks for the reminder, you just cleared things up for me. Invite those people over so we can talk. But the urgent thing is for you to find out where Ba Rum is and invite him to come down to the Delta with us. Ba Rum's a carpenter, a mason, a metalworker, damn good at all of it. Down in the Delta, our first job is gonna be building houses. That area's low-lying, floods three months every year, gotta build stilt houses. Gotta be a ace craftsman like Ba Rum to handle that."

Ba Chieu racked his brain, took a minute before he remembered: "When they purged Unit 9, Tam Tam rounded up all of Bay Vien's trusted men. Top of the list was Ba Tuan, who handled tax collection. Next was Ba Rum, who ran the weapons workshop. Those two grabbed hold of Uncle Nam Ha and asked him to intervene."

"Did Nam Ha agree to vouch for them?"

"Uncle Nam Ha's a good-hearted man, anybody he sees wrongly arrested, he takes them in. Last I heard, Uncle Ba Rum is staying with Uncle Nam Ha. If Uncle Muoi sends somebody to claim him, Uncle Nam Ha'll hand him right over, and Uncle Ba Rum'll be happy about it too."

Muoi Tri opened his notebook, tore out a page, and wrote a personal letter to Nam Ha by hand. He told Ba Chieu to pick a fast courier to find Nam Ha.

The day Muoi Tri headed to the Delta with one company, keeping his old rank as Commander of Regiment 304, was a day Muoi Tri would remember for the rest of his life.

Talk about a bolt from the blue, but Muoi Tri forced a happy face so the whole company would head out with spirit. Only his wife wrapped a checkered scarf over her head to hide the scowl of bitterness, because Company Commander Ly, her firstborn son, had been "borrowed" and had already left for his assignment a few days earlier.


---


# Enemy Games

Enemy Games

The purge of the Bình Xuyên rebels in June 1948 hit Rừng Sác like a damn hurricane.

Bảy Viễn's Unit 9, Tư Hoạnh's Unit 21, and Tư Ty's Unit 25 were the main targets of the whole thing, since Tư Hoạnh and Tư Ty were Bảy Viễn's younger brothers.

As for Năm Hà's Joint Unit 2, Mười Trí's Unit 4, and Hai Vĩnh's Unit 7 — they got placed on the outer circle, 'cause those four units weren't in cahoots with Bảy Viễn.

Still, within each of those units there were a few guys that "the old man with the wire beard" Hai Đại — a.k.a. Nguyễn Đức Huy, the Political Commissar of the Coastal Sub-Zone — was keeping a real close eye on. Those were Mười Lực, Bảy Môn, and Tư Huỳnh, who'd all been tight with Bảy Viễn back in the day. Every day Hai Đại would crack open his black-covered notebook, wave his pen around like a goddamn judge, and mark down every suspicious character he figured was one of Bảy Viễn's plants.

Mười Lực got called up to "the old wire-beard's" office for a little "chat":

— Comrade Mười, you're way too close with Bảy Viễn. How come you didn't know he was planning to go to the city?

— That's just your take, Commissar. Truth is, I ain't that tight with Bảy Viễn. Mười Lực and Bảy Môn are Thủ Thiêm fighters, we linked up with Brother Ba Dương and Năm Hà's crew from the beginning. Later on the Thủ Thiêm-Tân Quy allied force turned into Joint Unit 2. I commanded Unit 2, Brother Hai Lung commanded Unit 3.

— Bảy Môn and Tư Huỳnh — they're both in your unit, Mười. Did they hang around Bảy Viễn before?

— Tư Huỳnh I don't know about, he's from Phú Nhuận. As for Bảy Môn, same as me — never had any close dealings with Bảy Viễn.

— Alright, thanks, Mười. I need to understand more about Bảy Viễn, and if you don't know, I'll ask somebody else. You're dismissed.

Bảy Môn got called up next. Same old ambush-style questioning, Hai Đại trying to feel out who among Bảy Viễn's close buddies was still left behind after Bảy Viễn went to the city. Just like Mười Lực, Bảy Môn made it clear he was from a different faction. Hai Đại gave a crooked smile:

— I'm just fucking with you two. I've got plenty of reports about how chummy you both were with Bảy Viễn. Just letting you know so you can conduct yourselves properly. Alright, Brother Bảy, you're dismissed.

Then came Tư Huỳnh's turn.

This Phú Nhuận gangster straight-up admitted he'd once sworn a blood oath with Bảy Viễn.

Hai Đại was thrilled, grinning so hard his beard curled:

— Brother Tư, you really are Bảy Viễn's little brother. Didn't even have to squeeze it outta you!

Tư Huỳnh frowned, annoyed:

— What's so bad about it that I gotta hide? Back in the days of slavery, we formed a group called "Heaven's Will on Earth," following the example of the 108 heroes of Lương Sơn Bạc from the Water Margin — men who dared to think and dared to act. Look, the mark of our oath is right here — Tư Huỳnh held out his pinky, still showing the little scar, for Hai Đại to see.

Hai Đại suddenly got a bright idea:

— Our intelligence says Bảy Viễn went to the city with just two platoons, the French look down on him, he's restless day and night. We want to open an honorable path for Bảy Viễn. Can you help me with this plan?

Tư Huỳnh was taken aback:

— What plan?

— You should go to the city. Whisper to him: Hai Đại, the Political Commissar of the Coastal Sub-Zone, invites Bảy Viễn back to the war zone. All the misunderstandings from before are wiped clean.

Tư Huỳnh thought it over:

— If the Commissar assigns it, I'll do it. But it's gonna be tough. If Tư Huỳnh goes poking around the city, the Deuxième Bureau will grab me on the spot.

Hai Đại laughed:

— Easy. We'll spread the word that Tư Huỳnh deserted the Zone and went to the city to join Colonel Bảy Viễn. With that news, the Deuxième Bureau will welcome you with open arms — they wouldn't dare arrest you! And if they don't buy the desertion story, Bảy Viễn himself will send someone to vouch for you.

A few days later, word got out that Tư Huỳnh had slipped to the city.

Tư Huỳnh was the first to carry out the enemy-infiltration plan — with Bảy Viễn as the main target.

Just like Hai Đại predicted, the minute Tư Huỳnh hit the city, the Deuxième Bureau goons grabbed him.

Tư Huỳnh kept his cool:

— I'm the kid brother of Colonel Bảy Viễn himself. I left the Zone to join Brother Bảy. Report right now that Tư Huỳnh is waiting for Brother Bảy to send someone to pick him up at the Phú Xuân - Nhà Bè station.

A couple hours later, a Jeep pulled up with papers from Colonel Lê Văn Viễn, commander of the Bình Xuyên army, to come get Tư Huỳnh.

That night Bảy Viễn took Tư Huỳnh to a fancy restaurant in Chợ Lớn — just the two of them taking up a whole big room.

Tư Huỳnh was amazed at how well Bảy Viễn was living now:

— Glad to see you living this comfortable, Brother Bảy. Back in the Zone they kept saying you went to the city with only two platoons and the French looked down on you...

Bảy Viễn laughed:

— Of course. When you wanna kill a dog, you gotta yell loud that it's a rabid dog. You're a man of the world, Uncle Tư, you know how people talk.

They ate and drank, and Bảy Viễn asked about how things were in the Zone.

Tư Huỳnh laid it all out:

— The ones close to you, Brother Bảy — like Bảy Môn and Mười Lực — the old wire-beard is watching 'em like a hawk. He's calling 'em up, calling 'em down, interrogating 'em about every damn thing.

Bảy Viễn listened for a while then suddenly asked:

— Did you come here on your own, Uncle Tư, or did the old wire-beard send you on a mission? Give me your right hand, let me see your pinky. The scar from the blood oath is still here. There's no way Tư Huỳnh would betray Bảy Viễn, right?

Tư Huỳnh came clean:

— Brother Bảy's right. I came here because Hai Đại set it up. It's what they call "enemy infiltration" — targeting Bảy Viễn.


---


# Kicking the Rudder

Kicking the Rudder

Bảy Viễn grabbed Tư Huỳnh's hand, held it up in front of his face, and grinned like he'd won the lottery:

— That old wire-beard's a damn whore. But how's he gonna outplay this stinking whore named Bảy Viễn? He picks my own kid brother to run an "enemy infiltration" on me. Listen here, Tư — you and me, we're gonna "kick the rudder" on that old bastard. I'll play his game right back at him. You go back inside, quietly find Mười Lực and Bảy Môn, and tell 'em Brother Bảy says to come to the city with him. Stayin' there ain't a option no more — not with that old wire-beard running things.

Tư Huỳnh thought it over a long while:

— Gonna be straight with you, Brother Bảy — I never planned to come to the city with you. But that old wire-beard handed me this bullshit assignment. Nobody dares talk back to him. So I had to go, even knowing this was a life-or-death job. Now that I've seen you, I'm making up my mind — I'm staying in the city for good. Can't live under that old wire-beard no more. But before I settle in, I gotta help Mười Lực and Bảy Môn see the danger and get the hell out while they still can.

Bảy Viễn raised his glass:

— You're worthy of being Bảy Viễn's little brother. Here's to your success.

Tư Huỳnh went back to the Zone and reported to Hai Đại about the Bình Xuyên situation:

— Carrying out the Commissar's secret infiltration mission, I went to the city, got caught at the Phú Xuân station, Bảy Viễn sent people to pick me up... Stayed with Bảy Viễn three days. I saw things are completely different from what we've been hearing. Bảy Viễn's been made a Colonel by the French. The Bình Xuyên force right now has three battalions, fully equipped by the French — guns, grey khaki uniforms, the whole deal.

Hai Đại waved his hand:

— Forget all that bullshit. Tell me Bảy Viễn's attitude toward my proposal.

Tư Huỳnh shook his head:

— This is life-and-death. How's Bảy Viễn supposed to decide on the spot? After he heard me out, he thought a long time and said: The Việt Minh played me high and low — invited me to Đồng Tháp Mười, offered me the Zone 7 Commander post, then turned around and announced they were dissolving Bình Xuyên. They knew damn well Bảy Viễn would never agree to lose his Bình Xuyên Inter-Zone Commander title, so they purged Rừng Sác. If the Mắm Tree Stronghold was still standing, he wouldn't have gone to the city.

Hai Đại winced:

— So he won't come back into the fold?

Tư Huỳnh sneered:

— How could Bảy Viễn leave the city and come back here now? He already fell for the "lure the tiger out of the mountain" trick once. No way he's making that mistake again. The minute you gave me this assignment, I knew it'd fail. But to show goodwill, I took it on anyway.

Hai Đại nodded:

— Alright, go rest. Consider the mission complete.

That night, Tư Huỳnh secretly went to see Mười Lực and Bảy Môn.

When they saw him, both of them cried out in surprise:

— Tư Huỳnh! We thought you'd skipped off to join Bảy Viễn for good. What're you doing back here? Unless...

Tư Huỳnh nodded rapidly:

— That's right. The old wire-beard assigned me to go to the city and "infiltrate" Bảy Viễn.

Bảy Môn burst out laughing:

— You dumb fuck! You send Bảy Viễn's own little brother to do enemy infiltration — who the hell's gonna listen to that? If you wanna infiltrate, you gotta come from above, cover all the angles.

Mười Lực nodded:

— So it failed?

Tư Huỳnh nodded:

— Damn right it failed (then dropped his voice to barely a whisper). Hai Đại's the one who failed. Us — we won. Brother Bảy told me to tell you both: get the hell to the city fast. Stayin' here, you won't survive that old wire-beard!

Bảy Môn shot a look at Mười Lực, checking his reaction.

Mười Lực nodded:

— Bảy Viễn's right. I've been turning it over and over myself — stay or go.

Bảy Môn looked at Tư Huỳnh:

— And you, Tư Huỳnh?

— I'm bouncing too, right away. You two decide quick. Best if we all go the same night.

All three agreed to "pull the boat out" at the start of the next night's high tide.

But here's the kicker — all three had the same idea, but fate gave 'em three different hands.

Bảy Môn made it to the city smooth as butter. Mười Lực? The French stopped his boat, arrested him, and hauled him to the station. Cornered, he gave his real name and asked the station chief to contact Bảy Viễn to come pick him up. Deep down, Mười Lực wanted to wash his hands of the whole damn war — he was sick of fighting. But the situation forced him to lean on Bảy Viễn.

As for Tư Huỳnh — Hai Đại had already figured he'd go to the city to join Bảy Viễn, so he'd secretly had him tailed. Tư Huỳnh's boat hadn't even made it past the first checkpoint before they grabbed him.


---


# Two Verdicts

Two Verdicts

The Coastal Sub-Zone Court made a big damn deal out of Tư Huỳnh.

Hai Đại, the Sub-Zone Political Commissar, played prosecutor and laid into Tư Huỳnh — called him one of Bảy Viễn's plants left behind to lure away battalion and regiment-level commanders like Mười Lực and Bảy Môn, to get 'em to run to the city and join Bảy Viễn. While he was at it, Hai Đại warned all the regiments that still had Bình Xuyên guys mixed in to watch out for any leftover gangsters still in the Zone.

Tư Huỳnh spoke up from the defendant's stand:

— I'm a dyed-in-the-wool gangster, sure — but that was before the Southern Resistance. Ever since I joined the revolution, Tư Huỳnh has been a battalion-level commander, always leading by example, brave as hell in the face of the enemy. In every battle, I stood straight up to command my men. And now you're calling me a deserter running to Bảy Viễn? Bullshit! They handed me a mission to go to the city and "infiltrate" Bảy Viễn — a completely absurd mission, because I'm Bảy Viễn's own little brother. How the hell is Tư Huỳnn supposed to say anything that Bảy Viễn would listen to? Not to mention Bảy Viễn ain't exactly a stranger to the Việt Minh leadership...

Chief Judge Năm Hà cut him off:

— The accused will only speak about luring Mười Lực and Bảy Môn to follow Bảy Viễn.

Tư Huỳnh:

— I admit I passed along Bảy Viễn's message to Mười Lực and Bảy Môn. Whether they stayed or went — that was their own business.

Also in the dock with Tư Huỳnh was a co-defendant named Ba Bay.

The prosecutor asked him:

— Ba Bay, how come you knew those three were planning to go to the city and didn't report it?

Ba Bay:

— I just happened to walk past Mười Lực's place. All three of 'em were drinking. I heard 'em talking about whether to stay or go, but I figured it was the booze talking, not the men. Why? All three of 'em are senior to me — regiment commanders, battalion commanders, not goddamn children! I figured they were just shooting the shit. And even if they were serious, that's their own damn business.

Prosecutor:

— You knew and didn't report — that makes you an accomplice.

Ba Bay:

— I ain't educated, don't know the law. I'll just say this in court: Ba Bay is a gangster who sent plenty of traitors "shrimping" back in the early days of the resistance. I may not be educated, but the thing I hate most in this world is the snitching trade. So even if I knew they were plotting to go to the city to join Bảy Viễn, I kept their secret. Whatever sentence the court wants to hand down — I'll take it!

The trial wrapped up quick with two verdicts: death sentence for Tư Huỳnh, three years in prison for Ba Bay.

Tư Huỳnh just gave a cold smile:

— I did the crime, I'll do the time. I ask the court for one favor: let me execute myself.

Chief Judge Năm Hà hadn't even had a chance to respond when Prosecutor Hai Đại cut in sternly:

— Denied. A capital offender doesn't deserve the privilege of self-execution.

The execution happened right after that, at the cashew grove in Phước An commune.

Hai Đại's policy of "fight thorns with thorns," "use poison to kill poison" — right from its first strike — had led to this result: Tư Huỳnh executed, Mười Lực and Bảy Môn abandoning the Zone for the city.

After that, Hai Đại sent a few more men — including Năm Chàng and Bảy Rô — but not a single one of 'em managed to infiltrate Bảy Viễn. On the contrary, both of 'em had their lives threatened daily by the Thái Hoàng Minh gang always lurking around. Thanks to his close friendship with Bảy Viễn, Năm Chàng stayed safe. All the slander Thái Hoàng Minh threw at him got shot down by Bảy Viễn.

In Bảy Viễn's book, a wife's nephew ain't more trustworthy than a fellow gangster brother.

As for Bảy Rô — the minute he stepped into Bảy Viễn's mansion, an old buddy whispered to him:

— Get the hell out, now. Watch out for Mr. Năm." (Mr. Năm being Năm Tài, Bảy Viễn's secretary.) Bảy Rô took off running straight to the Mekong Delta and enlisted with Mười Trí, the Hòa Hảo military advisor.

Hai Đại watched Bảy Viễn run wild in Rừng Sác during the post-purge period. Only Tám Tâm — sharp in his judgment, dared to think and dared to act — could see Hai Đại's true dark heart. Something very few people could spot. And even those who did see it didn't dare call it out.


---


# Delegation Goes North

Bảy Viễn ran back to the city, a surrendered warlord dazed and lost. His men got purged by the dozen — some grateful, some bitter. The fortune Bảy Viễn left behind was enormous. All of it was handed over to Political Commissar Hai Đại to look after. Out there in the impoverished war zone, that kind of wealth would blind any greedy eye: fifteen kilos of gold, two million in silver dollars. Back then, silver dollars were precious — the Indochina Bank notes circulated everywhere, while Uncle Hồ's money was only good inside the liberated zones.

Tám Tâm was in charge of the purge operation inside Bảy Viễn's Unit 9. That cash and gold, he had to deliver straight into Hai Đại's hands.

He stood there waiting and waiting, but Hai Đại wouldn't give him a receipt. So he said loud and clear:

"Comrade, sign me a receipt."

Hai Đại stared Tám Tâm dead in the eye:

"You don't trust the Party?"

"I do, of course!"

"Then why make the Party write you a receipt?"

"Sorry, comrade, but you're not the Party... I trust you just fine, but other folks won't trust me. That money and gold — I need a receipt so when higher-ups ask, I can show them I handed it over to the Political Commissar."

Seeing Tám Tâm stand his ground, Hai Đại was pissed but couldn't do a thing about it. Tám Tâm had a rep as the best sharp-shooter in Unit 9, maybe in the whole sub-region.

Hai Đại had to give in:

"I was only testing you, comrade. Let me write the receipt like you asked."

Taking the receipt, Tám Tâm walked off muttering:

"This old bastard with the wire-brush beard is damn suspicious. The way his eyes lit up when he saw that pile of cash and gold, plus his little act of pretending not to hear about the receipt. Real damn suspicious. I gotta keep a real close eye on this guy."

Because he didn't trust Hai Đại, Tám Tâm could see how doomed the whole strategy was — sending the Bình Xuyên underworld guys into the city for enemy infiltration work on Bảy Viễn's behalf. In Tám Tâm's book, that was just pushing your own comrades straight into the grave.

What could a kid brother say to make Bảy Viễn listen? Especially now that Bảy Viễn was like a bird that's been hit by an arrow — scared of every bent branch.

Right after the purge, Hai Đại cleaned out Bảy Viễn's whole office. Every document, every file — even the pretty young secretaries working there. From that day on, if you paid attention, you'd notice the Political Commissar took a lot more care with his own appearance.

**Bảy Viễn — Leader of the Bình Xuyên**

That wire-brush beard got trimmed sharp as a blade. Instead of that moldy old black pajama outfit, now he wore crisp, tailored military uniforms that smelled nice. He looked younger, tougher than before. Some bootlicker paid him a compliment and Hai Đại laughed, stroking his beard:

"I'm feeling stronger these days — my body finally cut out that cancerous tumor called Bảy Viễn."

Tám Tâm hid a mocking smile. Just wait and see. This bastard'll die for money and women too, same as Bảy Viễn.

Sure enough, just like Tám Tâm suspected, Hai Đại "cleaned house" at Bảy Viễn's place. A couple of the girls who couldn't handle Ông Hai's overly familiar hands had asked to transfer to other units.

Tám Tâm was spending day and night tracking the Commissar's every move when word came that he'd been picked for the delegation going North to report on the Bình Xuyên affair. The delegation chief was Comrade Phạm Hùng. The Bình Xuyên members chosen for the North trip included Hai Vĩnh — Commander of Unit 7, the man who'd helped capture Phán Huề and through that got the hard proof of Bảy Viễn's secret dealings with French Intelligence (the Deuxième Bureau). Besides Tám Tâm, there was Political Officer Lưu Quý Thoái and Lieutenant General Nguyễn Bình's personal secretary, Lương Văn Trọng, who handled liaison between Sector 7 Command and the Bình Xuyên Inter-Zone. On this trip, Hai Trọng's role was crucial. A week earlier, Lieutenant General Nguyễn Bình and Hai Trọng had gone over the report outline together — all about leading the underworld army units in the resistance under the name Bình Xuyên. Both were dead tired but fired up.

Nguyễn Bình said:

"History really does repeat itself. The Twelve Warlords era of Đinh Bộ Lĩnh and Lê Hoàn is happening all over again right here in the Eastern Nam Bộ region. To live among these underworld guys while fighting the French, you've gotta be a true hero to earn the trust of the Bình Xuyên leaders. The Ba Nhỏ trial in Long Thành — if I hadn't had the guts to do what I did, things might've turned out real different."

The day before departure, the delegation stopped by Unit 4's office — now Regiment 304. Mười Trí, who was getting ready to head down to the Western region, threw them a farewell feast.


---


# The Language of the Underworld

Mười Trí wanted to report to the Central Committee that he couldn't keep Bảy Viễn in line. His heart was all tangled up, he needed to lay it out for his superiors. While the boys in the delegation ate, Mười Trí barely touched his food, just picked at a couple bites, then excused himself to go write a personal letter to President Hồ.

By the time the others finished eating and had been sitting around drinking tea for a good while, Anh Mười hurriedly stuffed the letter into an envelope and handed it to delegation chief Phạm Hùng:

"Anh Hai, this is a personal letter from Mười Trí to Uncle Hồ. Please have the delegation deliver it for me."

Phạm Hùng took the letter and said:

"Why don't you seal it, Anh Mười?"

Mười Trí shook his head:

"You can't seal a personal letter! Whoever carries it has to be able to read it first — if they think the words are okay, then they'll pass it along. If they think it's no good, they've got the right to tear it up."

Phạm Hùng laughed:

"Anh Mười left the envelope open on purpose so the delegation could censor it, didn't you?"

Mười Trí nodded:

"That's right. Right now, Anh Hai has the authority to open it and read it first."

Phạm Hùng laughed again and said his goodbyes:

"Well, farewell to you, Anh Mười and Chị Mười. Hope your work down in the Western region goes well. We're heading to the Central Committee for a Congress, then coming back South. See you in a year or two."

By the time they reached the Eastern region, Tám Tâm brought up the letter:

"I wanna know what Anh Mười wrote about the Bình Xuyên issue — especially about Bảy Viễn. Those two were tight as brothers."

Lưu Quý Thoái chimed in:

"Anh Hai ought to open it and take a look. Anh Mười didn't seal the envelope so the delegation could see it first — like a review."

Phạm Hùng nodded in agreement and pulled the letter from his briefcase to read.

Everyone watched the delegation chief's face for any reaction. Suddenly they saw him shake his head — their curiosity practically pushed them forward, but they held back out of respect.

Phạm Hùng said:

"This is how Anh Mười wrote it. Who'd dare pass this on to President Hồ?"

Tám Tâm, standing close to the chief, reached out for the letter, read it hungrily, then shook his head too:

"I agree with the delegation chief — we can't send this personal letter to President Hồ."

The letter got passed around to every member of the delegation. Most of them were uneasy, not sure whether it should reach Uncle Hồ or not.

The last to read it was Anh Hà Huy Giáp. Anh Giáp thought for a moment, then said:

"Let's talk this letter over. I want everybody's opinion."

Most of them repeated their view: it shouldn't be given to Uncle Hồ.

Hà Huy Giáp stayed quiet for a beat, then said:

"I see it different from the rest of you. Why shouldn't we deliver Anh Mười Trí's letter to Uncle? I don't see the problem. Here, let me read it out loud so everyone can hear, then we'll decide."

He cleared his throat and read in a loud voice:

*Personal letter respectfully sent to Anh Hồ Chí Minh.*

*Your kid brother Mười Trí sends this letter to wish you good health... That bastard Bảy Viễn has gone over to the French now, and your kid brother is real sorry he couldn't stop him. But your kid brother promises you for sure that he'll keep on fighting the resistance till final victory.*

*Us underworld guys, once we've committed to the revolution, we'll see it through to the end — we'll never have second thoughts.*

*Signed: Huỳnh Văn Trí*
*Commander of Regiment 304*

The letter was stamped with a bright red seal.

After reading, Hà Huy Giáp added:

"You all think the way Anh Mười addresses Uncle is strange, and that's why it feels off to you. But in Anh Mười's underworld circles, writing like that is perfectly normal. Uncle Hồ is thirteen years older than Anh Mười — calling him 'Anh' isn't disrespectful. And anyway, the underworld has always talked rough and familiar. It's only with people they truly respect that they say 'Anh' and call themselves 'your kid brother'..."

Everyone stayed quiet. Anh Giáp went on:

"As for the content, the letter lets Uncle know Bảy Viễn has gone over to the French. Anh Mười admits he's real sad about it and promises to keep fighting till the end. That's more than good enough. Isn't that right, Anh Hai?"

Phạm Hùng nodded:

"Anh Giáp is the real theorist here. My first reaction was that it felt jarring — the way he addresses Uncle. Calling him 'brother' is unusual, hardly anyone talks like that. And then 'your kid brother' — it sounds so underworld!"

Hai Vĩnh laughed:

"The guy's a genuine underworld thug, how can you expect him not to talk like an underworld thug, Anh Hai?"

Hà Huy Giáp spoke up:

"So what's the delegation's consensus? Send it or not? I reckon most of you agree with me now — we should send it. Through this letter, Uncle Hồ gets a real feel for the hearts of the underworld brothers fighting in the resistance. Only Bảy Viễn broke down halfway. The rest of them are standing firm with the revolution till the end. Isn't that right?"

**Nguyên Hùng**


---


# Catching Lost Cows

June 13, 1948 — a big day for Bảy Viễn. General De la Tour pinned colonel's insignia on the commander of the Bình Xuyên army. From that day on, three Bình Xuyên battalions got paid monthly salaries. Bảy Viễn was overjoyed — he knew for sure his future as a surrendered warlord was locked in. Right away, His Excellency the Colonel took care of two things he'd been planning for a while:

First, a memorial ceremony for the comrades who died in the purge. The little temple under the Chứ Y Bridge, right next to the Bình Xuyên office, was handed over for a proper, solemn ceremony. All the commanders attended. Maurice Thiên came to the temple to burn incense and pray real carefully.

Second, a feast for his battle brothers who'd made it through the biggest trial of their lives. All the loyal big fish and little shrimp commanders were invited to the Đại La Thiên Restaurant to live one night like kings — "Emperor for a Night."

Each man got to invite one "easygoing beauty" — Bảy Viễn's own words for the dance hall girls of Chợ Lớn.

Seeing their big brother throw down like that, Bảy Viễn's kid brothers all happily swore they'd stand by him to the death.

General De la Tour gave Bảy Viễn his marching orders:

"As of today, Bình Xuyên is officially the supplementary army of former Emperor Bảo Đại's government. You'll draw monthly salaries just like the royal guards. Rights come with duties. I'm formally assigning the Bình Xuyên to maintain security in Sài Gòn and Chợ Lớn."

Bảy Viễn answered cheerfully:

"The Bình Xuyên brothers have been waiting for you to give us a mission, Major General. Keeping Sài Gòn and Chợ Lớn secure is the perfect job for us — most of us were city boys before we joined the resistance."

General De la Tour went on:

"Việt Minh city action cells often come out here to collect money and medicine to send back into the zones. Your first job is to wipe out those supply networks. Second, you're to provide intelligence on the Việt Minh to the French military. For that, you'll coordinate with the Deuxième Bureau under Major Savani."

Bảy Viễn nodded:

"I accept both missions, Major General."

**Bảy Viễn — Leader of the Bình Xuyên**

From that day on, the people of Sài Gòn saw Bình Xuyên soldiers hanging around every checkpoint at the city's entrances and exits. They were supposedly there to catch Việt Minh cadres slipping in and out of the city, but in reality these thugs were sizing up wealthy families to shake down, stopping pretty women on their way to market to catcall and harass them.

Plenty of brawls broke out in bars just over fighting over girls. The Bình Xuyên soldiers became a terror for decent, honest people.

One day Maurice Thiên came to talk to Bảy Viễn:

"Things are settled now, boss. Time we looked into running the rackets myself, Colonel."

"Running what rackets? Spell it out for me."

"Sài Gòn and Chợ Lớn got two money-printing machines: the Đại Thế Giới entertainment complex and the Kim Chung casino. Up till now, Đại Thế Giới's been run by some Macau tycoons who won the bidding. Those bastards are richer than God."

Bảy Viễn nodded:

"Big boat, big waves. I hear they pay the government a few hundred thousand in taxes every day."

Maurice Thiên laughed:

"After taxes and expenses, they still clear half a million a month. Their business principle is 'moitié moitié' — half and half. The government takes half, they take half."

Bảy Viễn shook his head:

"I don't got the cash to outbid them."

Maurice Thiên waved his hand:

"Money's not the issue. The real question is whether the Colonel is willing to step up and run Đại Thế Giới and Kim Chung yourself."

Bảy Viễn hesitated:

"I like the sight of money, sure, but I hear there's days when a few people kill themselves over losing at sic bo. I'm worried about the bad rep..."

Tư Thiên laughed:

"If you wanna get rich, you gotta leave your conscience at the door. That's what the sages meant when they said 'If you're a good man, you won't be rich' and 'If you're rich, you won't be a good man.'"

Bảy Viễn stayed quiet. Tư Thiên pressed on:

"Better we run these two money-printing machines than leave them in the hands of those Macau scumbags. Every month they send millions back to their homeland. But if that money was in our hands, we could use it for good — build a proper headquarters for Bình Xuyên, instead of this cramped little side street in Chợ Lớn. With that kind of capital, we could do a lot of other useful things too."

Seeing Bảy Viễn starting to come around, Maurice added:

"Tonight I'm taking the Colonel to Đại Thế Giới for a look around. You can check it out yourself and decide if we should run this money machine or not. The fun alone of 'catching lost cows' in there is enough to beat anything."

**Bảy Viễn — Leader of the Bình Xuyên**

Hearing "catching lost cows," Bảy Viễn grinned from ear to ear:

"Deal! Tonight we go catch some lost cows!"


---


# Karmic Debts

The French were real proud of their biggest casino in Saigon — biggest in all of Indochina too — smack in the middle of Chợ Lớn (District 5). Place had everything: rows of game halls, theaters, circus stages, restaurant-dancehalls, open-air drink stalls. But the main attraction was this long stretch of wide-open gambling halls, all lit up bright, each one with its own flavor: the Vietnamese played hốt me, the French played roulette, the Chinese played tài xỉu. Whatever your poison, you just walked up to the hall that called your name.

The roulette hall hired real French women, plus half-breeds too. Over at the tài xỉu hall, they had these young gorgeous Hong Kong dealer girls in tight high-collar cheongsams, cut high up the sides, showing off long smooth white thighs. They'd stand behind the counters, rake in money, pay out winnings...

Taking a stroll around, Bảy Viễn and Tư Thiên stopped at an open-air drink stall to rest. Two girls came by to greet them, brought their drinks, and asked to sit at their table to serve.

Tư Thiên politely thanked them and waved them off:

— We need to talk private business.

Bảy Viễn laughed:

— Man, a few years away from this district and the city's changed so much. These drink stalls are decked out way prettier, and the waitresses are a whole lot more eye-catching too. Especially those tài xỉu halls. All Hong Kong girls, beautiful and sweet-smelling as jackfruit.

Tư Thiên grinned:

— That's how you clean out folks' pockets, Colonel.

Those girls you're complimenting, Colonel — they're decoy birds, singing sweet to lure the blood-red-and-black gamblers into the trap.

Bảy Viễn chuckled:

— Guys who walk into a tài xỉu hall already got gambling in their blood. They're focused on winning and losing, not making eyes at some decoy birds.

Tư Thiên stood his ground:

— Sure, but there's too many halls here, so you gotta use decoys to pull the players in. Nothing like trying your luck while eyeing some beauties — even if you lose, it soothes the soul.

Bảy Viễn nodded:

— You got a point, Brother Tư. So what about this "catching stray cows" business?

Tư Thiên broke into a wide grin:

— I know plenty of fine "stray cows" in here. They're those well-off wives — wives of clerks and secretaries — sneaking away from their husbands to play tài xỉu. When they lose all their money, they hang around hoping to spot someone they know to borrow from, either to win it back or just for cab fare home. If they don't run into anyone, they get desperate enough to do something reckless — pick up a sweet-talker on the spot...

Bảy Viễn laughed, stroking his mustache:

— Every last one of us men has a sweet tooth, am I right, Teacher Tư?

Maurice nodded along:

— Damn right! Only the impotent ones don't crave sugar! Anyway, let's get moving, Colonel. Let's make a game of it. Fifteen minutes — whoever catches a "stray cow" first wins. Loser treats the winner to a night at the dancehall. Deal?

Bảy Viễn waved:

— Fifteen minutes, meet me at the dancehall over there, hear?

Bảy Viễn walked back toward the tài xỉu hall with a big smile on his face:

— In this whole crowd of addicts, I'm the only clear-headed one. Everybody else is busy slaughtering each other, but me — I stay outside the cage. I'm just hunting for a lost beauty wandering through this den of fools.

Then Bảy Viễn turned serious all of a sudden. He'd just spotted a woman — a real stunner, a heavenly vision — heading toward a nearby tài xỉu table. He'd never seen anyone dressed so sharp. She was pushing forty, but her figure was still tight — slim waist, full chest, round hips. She wore a smoke-gray áo dài that made her jet-black lụa pants pop even darker. As she got closer, Bảy Viễn drank in the sight: an elegant oval face, big eyes, long lashes, a high nose bridge, full heart-shaped lips.

She didn't even notice him watching. She stepped up to the tài xỉu counter, pulled a wad of cash from her purse, and put it down on a number.

Bảy Viễn snapped back to reality, squeezed in beside her, and laid his own money next to hers, grinning:

— Let me share in your luck, ma'am!

She was too focused on the dealer shaking the dice cup to pay any mind to the man beside her.

The Hong Kong girl sang out "Hốt a!" — time was up. The dealer set the cup down. Three dice spun like crazy before settling. They announced the result.

The dealer raked in the losers' cash and paid out the winners.

The lady shook her head and sighed, watching her money disappear into the dealer's drawer, then pulled out more to bet again.

Bảy Viễn laid his money down right beside hers:

— Let's ride this number. It's bound to make a comeback.

The lady heard those words — "make a comeback" — and her face lit up, but she didn't turn around to look at the man behind her. This time, they won.

Bảy Viễn made his move:

— I say we bet the whole winnings, stay on the same number. That's our lucky number, trust me.

This time she finally turned to look at him.

Before she could speak, Bảy Viễn pressed on:

— Three rounds and we'll know how the wind blows. If this one wins too, let me buy you a getting-to-know-you dinner.

The lady looked surprised:

— But I don't even know you.

— I figure sharing the same fate for three rounds straight means we're already acquainted. Let's see if this third round goes the way I'm hoping.

The dealer called out "Hốt a!" Again, they won.

Bảy Viễn scooped up the money, handed it all to the beauty, beaming:

— Let me escort you to the dancehall across the way, so we can mark the day we met.

What started as "catching a stray cow" turned into karmic fate.

That lady's name was Hà Thị Tám. She worked as an accountant for MIC Tobacco. Bà Tám would later become Bảy Viễn's last wife.


---


# Bình Xuyên Headquarters

After that visit to Đại Thế Giới, Bảy Viễn decided to bid for it — go head-to-head with those Macau sharks led by Lâm Giống. The bidding war got fierce, but in the end the Macau bunch realized they couldn't compete against Bình Xuyên. The French had put Bình Xuyên in charge of keeping the peace in Sài Gòn - Chợ Lớn, which meant they'd handed life-and-death power right to Bảy Viễn.

What was Lâm Giống compared to the Commander of Bình Xuyên? His bodyguards were all Shaolin martial artists, but what good's kung fu against Bình Xuyên's shock police with grenades and submachine guns?

Lâm Giống backed off. Bảy Viễn won the bid to run Đại Thế Giới. It was a total life-changer — from being a beggar living off the French military's handouts every month, now Bình Xuyên army had a steady income of half a million piasters a day. Money makes you strong, cash makes you bold. Bảy Viễn could finally hold his head high in front of General De la Tour. His favorite moment now was late afternoon, watching the finance clerks hauling silver back to the office. There was even half a squad of female secretaries counting money, stacking bills, locking them in the safe. Every week they had to go deposit at the bank.

All those clerks were women — young and beautiful.

Bảy Viễn figured he was the luckiest man alive: rolling in cash while watching these angelic girls count money faster than machines.

Maurice Thiên came to him with a proposal:

— Now that we've got capital, we ought to build a real headquarters — grand, impressive.

Bảy Viễn nodded:

— I was thinking the same thing. Where's the best spot, Teacher Tư?

Tư Thiên pulled Bảy Viễn over to the city map on the wall:

— We're Bình Xuyên folks — our turf runs along the Kênh Tẻ canal, hugging Trần Xuân Soạn and Phạm Thế Hiển streets. Let's buy this plot here, right under the belly of Chữ Y Bridge. Not many people live there, so we won't have to pay much in compensation.

Bảy Viễn studied the spot Tư Thiên had circled on the map and nodded:

— Good! Teacher Tư, hire an architect to draw it up. The offices need to look flashy — we're gonna do it different from those French barracks.

Tư Thiên thought for a moment:

— If you wanna stand out, Colonel, keep that pond on the property. Turn it into a crocodile lake, just like in Rừng Sác.

Bảy Viễn lit up:

— Hell yeah! That's a golden idea! We'll make those French bastards eat their hearts out. Bình Xuyên is Rừng Sác, and Rừng Sác means crocs... Now that Bình Xuyên has come up in the world, we need some tigers and leopards to make it lively. Right next to the croc pond, let's build iron cages for leopards.

All fired up, Bảy Viễn dragged Tư Thiên into the Jeep and told the driver to head straight for the plot of land they'd picked.

They got out, stood there taking in the whole scene, talking it over:

— I like this piece of land. It's Bình Xuyên territory but right up against the city's armpit. There's one problem though...

— What problem?

— It's right under the bridge. Enemy on the bridge has the high ground.

Tư Thiên nodded:

— The Colonel's got a soldier's eye, alright. We're down low, they could hit us with grenades. But... we'll put a guard post up on the bridge. If things get hairy, we'll put up a "Military Zone" sign and control all the traffic coming through. Easy, Colonel!

Bảy Viễn took a walk around the land with Tư Thiên, then stopped in front of a water spinach pond:

— Turning this into a croc lake would be the shit! Bình Xuyên is Rừng Sác, and Rừng Sác is crocs. Ha! Ha!

A few months later, the folks around Chữ Y Bridge couldn't believe their eyes — Bình Xuyên's headquarters had risen on that abandoned plot of land. That harmless water spinach pond had turned into a crocodile lake, ringed with melaleuca and mangrove trees, and under the branches, crocs as big as small boats poked their snouts up. The whole headquarters compound was fenced in tight. At the bridgehead, a guard post controlled all traffic.

On inauguration day of the Bình Xuyên army headquarters, Bảy Viễn invited General De la Tour to the ceremony, and to really show off, he announced:

— From now on, the Bình Xuyên army stands on its own two feet. No more living off French military welfare. Starting this month, we won't accept any more pay. Still, I thank the General for his generous support back in our early days.

At the private party that night, Bảy Viễn celebrated:

— I've hated the way those French bastards treated Bình Xuyên like nothing for so long. But that's how the world works: the begging hand is always beneath the giving hand. The French looking down on us was only natural. But now — now I've finally settled that score.

De la Tour was shaken when he heard Bảy Viễn was cutting off French military aid. That got him thinking. You couldn't just throw money at Bình Xuyên and order them around anymore.

Around this time, Bảy Viễn couldn't stop praising Tư Thiên — it was his idea to outbid those Macau tycoons that made Bình Xuyên so prosperous. Bảy Viễn wanted to repay Maurice Thiên properly. He planned to recommend to Lieutenant Colonel Savani that Tư Thiên be given a high post — Director of the City Police. But Tư Thiên never got the chance to enjoy that favor from Bảy Viễn.


---


# Who Killed Tư Thiên?

The folks of Sài Gòn - Chợ Lớn had this thing they loved: sitting at a sidewalk café early in the morning. The morning sun was just right — warm but not hot. And at a sidewalk café, you caught all the latest news, local and worldwide.

Maurice Thiên was one of those sidewalk-café addicts. His regular spot was at a Chinese-run joint right at the intersection, in front of the Đại Thế Giới gates. The owner always saved "Mr. Tư" his table — table number one.

Every morning bright and early, Mr. Tư would show up with a few newspapers tucked under his arm. He'd just skim the big headlines, never read the fine print. He'd tell his close buddies with a laugh:

— I don't got much time, so I read the headlines, not the text.

One morning, Tư Thiên was sitting at his usual café when a black Traction came cruising by, hugging the corner tight.

Tư Thiên looked up, coffee cup in hand. The car was moving fast — way over the city speed limit of 40 clicks. Just then it swerved, stopping only about ten feet from Tư Thiên's table. Somebody in the back seat leveled a submachine gun and ripped a burst into Tư Thiên's chest. The Traction gunned it and tore off.

Tư Thiên died on the spot.

People screamed. Cops showed up to file the report. An ambulance hauled Tư Thiên to the hospital.

Tư Thiên's death set all of Sài Gòn - Chợ Lớn buzzing. Who killed Maurice Thiên? Everybody had theories, but the authorities didn't have enough witnesses or evidence to pin it on anyone.

Word of the hit reached Colonel Bảy Viễn. He went straight to Tư Thiên's family. The body had been brought home from the hospital. The doctors couldn't do a thing — Tư Thiên had died instantly from the gunman's burst. The tricky part was that Sài Gòn had thousands of Tractions — Citroen made 'em by the boatload. Hell, Tư Thiên himself owned a blue one.

Bảy Viễn offered his condolences to the family:

— Brother Tư was my friend for twenty, thirty years. He was also my benefactor — and the benefactor of the whole Bình Xuyên army. Everything we've got today, we owe to Brother Tư. I was about to recommend to the French that he be made Director of the City Police, but I didn't get the chance before he left us. I can't tell you how sorry I am.

Rumors started flying that the killer was Tư Sang. Whether that's true or not, people started digging into the background of the Lai brothers. Lai Văn Sang was from Cao Lãnh, served in the French army, fought in the Xiêm campaign in February 1941 as a platoon leader, studied radio repair, then went to the Phan Thiết Sports School and became a trainer. He joined the Tiền Phong Youth movement. At the time of the murder, Tư Sang was Bảy Viễn's military assistant and carried a Ten submachine gun. Lai Hữu Tài was also from Cao Lãnh, had his baccalaureate. Went to Phan Thiết Sports School too. He worked at the Criminal Investigation Department tracking down criminals. And he was a French secret agent.

So why was Lai Văn Sang suspected of killing Tư Thiên? Tư Thiên and his younger brother Năm Tài had thrown in with Bảy Viễn after Nguyễn Hòa Hiệp pulled the Third Division out of Cao Lãnh. Most of the Third Division units got their weapons taken by Brother Huỳnh Văn Một's 15th Company at Đức Hòa. Nguyễn Hòa Hiệp's command staff fled to the city to surrender to the French. Among them were the two Lai brothers. Sang and Tài were introduced by Tư Thiên into Bảy Viễn's 9th Company. When Bảy Viễn moved to the city, both Lai brothers followed, continuing to serve him. Once they were in the city, Tư Sang had his eye on the Director of City Police post. When he found out Bảy Viễn was planning to give that job to Tư Thiên, Tư Sang got scared he'd lose his shot — so he took out the competition.

The Deuxième Bureau was dead certain the killer was Bảy Viễn himself. Here's what they laid out: When General De la Tour — French army commander and Republic of Cochinchina Commissioner — gave Bảy Viễn control of the Chợ Lớn district, Bảy Viễn became a big shot among the millionaire Chinese merchants there. Plenty of them backed Bình Xuyên with millions, billions in cash to grease their business deals. That money was handed to Tư Thiên to pass on to Bảy Viễn. Tư Thiên got greedy and skimmed three million off the top. Unfortunately for him, the scheme got exposed. Every single Chinese guild leader in Chợ Lớn ratted Tư Thiên out. Bảy Viễn set up an investigation panel — put Năm Tài and his own son Major Lê Paul on it. Their conclusion: Tư Thiên had indeed embezzled three million. So Bảy Viễn ordered him taken out. The Deuxième Bureau even quoted what Bảy Viễn said when he gave the order to kill the man he'd called his benefactor so many times. The words went like this:

"Back when we became friends, we swore an oath — whoever betrays the other dies. Now you've betrayed me, so I have to kill you. Then I'll bring flowers to your grave."

With all these conflicting stories, the death of Maurice Thiên remains a mystery to this day.

After Tư Thiên's death, General De la Tour gave Bình Xuyên another heavy responsibility: clearing Route 15 to connect Sài Gòn with Vũng Tàu.


---


# Highway 15

Highway 15

Back at the start of the resistance, the local militia of Bien Hoa and Ba Ria provinces were tearing up Highway 15 — the one hundred twenty kilometer stretch linking Saigon to Vung Tau — to cut the enemy's supply lines. The section from Long Thanh to Ba Ria took the worst beating. The militia would dig up the asphalt, scrape off the gravel, and plant bamboo right over it. After a few rainy seasons, the bamboo grew thick and dark, and Highway 15 just vanished. And on top of that, where they couldn't plant bamboo fast enough, they'd chop the trees lining both sides so they'd fall criss-crossed into each other. Every bridge — iron, concrete, didn't matter — they blew it all up. Even the Co May Iron Bridge, the longest one on the whole Saigon-Vung Tau route, had its middle span knocked clean out.

Surveying the damage on Highway 15, Bay Vien said to General De la Tour:

"Tens of bridges and culverts, all wrecked. Binh Xuyen boys aren't engineers — how we supposed to rebuild all this?"

De la Tour just shrugged.

"Bridge-building's for the engineers. Your job's keeping the peace in the area so the engineers can work. As you clear the road, you throw up blockhouses to hold it — make sure the Viet Minh don't sneak out at night with their hoes and shovels digging it up again."

Bay Vien put his wife's nephew, Thai Hoang Minh, in charge. When word got around, Muoi Luc and Bay Mon laughed between themselves.

"That parade-ground soldier boy gets it done? I'll chop my own head off," Muoi Luc said.

Bay Mon let out a breath. "That French bastard's got a mean streak. He's setting kin against kin, ain't he?"

And sure enough, that's exactly what happened. Binh Xuyên troops pushed deeper into the liberated zone clearing Highway 15, and they got picked off one by one — sniper fire, tripwire grenades, casualties piling up every day. Some squads walked straight into ambushes when they pushed too far into enemy territory.

The fresh Binh Xuyen recruits Bay Vien had scraped together were nothing but market hustlers — drifters and dice-rollers. How could they know the terrain like the local guerrillas did?

The more men fell, the lower morale sank. Thai Hoang Minh nearly resigned half a dozen times — scared of being laughed at. But there was another reason to keep him on: every kilometer cleared meant easier access to the timber. Years of war had stopped the logging crews cold, and the forest had grown up thick — junk wood tangled with precious hardwood.

Thai Hoang Minh was a lousy soldier but a sharp businessman. That timber made the Thai family a fortune. Every stretch his men cleared, they'd fell the trees, saw them into firewood, hire boats, and haul it all back to the city to sell. All along Tran Xuan Soan Road, running alongside the Kenh Te canal, woodyards popped up like mushrooms. The Binh Xuyen's money machine was humming along pretty damn nicely.

Word of this operation reached the Deuxième Bureau. General De la Tour chewed Bay Vien out again.

"I gave you Highway 15 to clear, not to strip the forest for firewood to line your pockets. Clearing that road is critical. Opening the Saigon-Vung Tau artery means breathing life back into the capital of Cochinchina."

That road was the weekend getaway for civil servants and the diplomatic corps. It was like Highway 4 — the lifeline feeding the bellies of a million people in Saigon-Cholon.

Bay Vien, freshly roasted by the Frenchman, turned around and lit into Thai Hoang Minh.

"Cut the logging, you hear me! That bastard De la Tour just chewed my ass out."

Thai Hoang Minh lost his appetite. Take the shit to get the xoi, and then the xoi's gone cold — why the hell would he stick around getting killed for nothing? So he cooked up a scheme: shoot himself in the arm, then ask his uncle-in-law to let him go to France for treatment.

That child's trick wouldn't fool an old fox like Bay Vien, but family's family. He let Thai Hoang Minh go to France for treatment and put his own son, Major Le Paul, in charge instead.

The Thai man let out a huge sigh of relief — dumped the burden and got himself a free trip to France, paid for with all that firewood money he'd already pocketed.

It took three full years to clear Highway 15.

De la Tour was so damn happy he threw a big ceremony for the reopening. That very afternoon, the French organized a motorcade of sightseers heading down to Vung Tau for the weekend. Vung Tau, quiet as a fishing village all this time, suddenly found itself buzzing, rolling out the welcome mat for a bunch of big shots — French and Vietnamese both — plus a few Chinese merchants who'd been stuck in Saigon. Weekend beach trips were the favorite pastime of the bourgeois crowd who'd picked up a little French culture.

Without Tu Thien as his financial advisor, Bay Vien had his wife — a woman who knew how to make money. That was Ba Lua, the favorite daughter of Councilor Dong.

"Tourists are flooding into Vung Tau. I got an idea — tell me what you think."

"What idea?"

"We buy a couple of hotels out there, renovate them, and put up the guests."

Bay Vien nodded. "Good. Good idea. What else?"

"More. We should buy a bus line, bring guests straight from Saigon to Vung Tau. If they ride with us, they don't have to get off at the station and catch a cyclo to the hotel."

Bay Vien wrapped his arms around Ba Lua's head.

"A brilliant financial mind was sleeping right next to me all this time and I never knew. Good, good! I'm putting you on both: open hotels in Vung Tau and start a bus company running passengers from Saigon."

A month later, tourists saw a sign go up: "Hoa Binh Hotel," right in the heart of Vung Tau. Saigon travelers riding the Nghia Hiep buses got better service than any other line, and they were driven straight to the Hoa Binh Hotel's doorstep.


---


# Throwing Stones While Hiding Your Hand

Throwing Stones While Hiding Your Hand

The dynamic duo Muoi Luc and Bay Mon got word from Bay Vien and slipped back into the city. Staying out there meant that old goat with the wire beard would find a way to do them in. Bay Mon got lucky and slipped through clean. Muoi Luc? The French caught him. He sat in the Phu Xuan lockup waiting for Bay Vien to send someone to pick him up. Both Muoi Luc and Bay Mon were put on assignments Bay Vien trusted them with. But that crew of parade-ground boys under Thai Hoang Minh — scared of losing their own perks — started trash-talking the two of them.

Nam Tai, always on the lookout, whispered poison to Boss Bay.

"Watch those two, Colonel sir. Pretty sure the other side sent 'em back here to lie low and spy."

Bay Vien laughed it off.

"Don't talk nonsense, man! You want to accuse somebody, bring proof. How the hell would you know those guys better than I do?"

Nam Tai was burning mad that Bay Vien shut him down like that. So he ran straight to the Deuxième Bureau and suggested they "borrow" Muoi Luc and Bay Mon to extract intel about the situation in the liberated zone.

Next day, an official request came across Bay Vien's desk from the Deuxième Bureau, asking to borrow two Viet Minh cadres who'd just left the zone to report to the Binh Xuyen commanding colonel.

Bay Vien was furious but what could he do?

"Go on over, the both of you," he told them. "Just hang tight — I'll send the car to bring you back."

The Deuxième Bureau interrogators questioned each man separately. Bay Mon and Muoi Luc were careful with their answers, feeding them intel about old base locations — knowing full well that when someone defected, the unit packed up and moved somewhere else right away.

Come evening, the car rolled up and picked them up, just like Bay Vien promised.

The minute Muoi Luc saw Bay Vien, he lit into him.

"We came back here trusting you'd protect us, and those damn Frenchmen haul us in for interrogation, grilling us about all kinds of bullshit?"

Bay Vien got hot too.

"God-fucking-dammit. That was a 'throw-stones-hide-your-hands' move by Nam Tai. He's the one who cooked up this whole Deuxième Bureau 'borrowing' scheme. Borrow, my ass! These are human beings, not tools — what do you mean, 'borrow'?"

Knowing Muoi Luc was stewing, that night Bay Vien brought over a bottle of Martell.

"I know you two are still sore about what happened. I'm ten times more pissed off than you — I'm the Binh Xuyen commanding colonel and I couldn't even protect you. Truth is, the Frenchman was within his rights. Anybody who comes out of the liberated zone, they've got the authority to investigate. They have to — in case the other side sent someone to lie low in the city. I trust you, but the Bureau and the secret police? They wouldn't trust their own father-in-law."

Muoi Luc gave a cold laugh.

"Their own blood father they don't trust — what's a father-in-law?"

The word "wife" made Bay Vien's face change. He looked off for a moment, then asked:

"When they did that sweep, did Huanh's mother and kids get caught in the crossfire, Brother Muoi?"

"Sister Bay and the little ones are fine. What you did — nobody's gonna take it out on your wife and kids. At first Sister Bay stayed with Brother Nam Ha's family. Then Muoi Tri sent someone up to take Ba Rum back to the Mekong Delta, and Sister Bay asked to go down there too, to live with Muoi Tri and his family."

Bay Vien nodded. "Good. That puts my mind at ease. Muoi Tri sent me a poem breaking our oath sworn at sea, but I know Brother Muoi's a damn good friend. Even if he won't call me comrade anymore, he still took in my wife and kids."

Somewhere in the drink, Muoi Luc blurted out:

"Anh Bay, you've got too many wives. You ever sit down and count how many wives and kids you've actually got?"

Bay Vien laughed.

"Hardly ever sit down and count 'em like you're asking. But sure, let's try. I've always been a man of many wives. So far I've got five official ones, not counting the side pieces. The first wife's from Can Duoc, mother of two kids — oldest is Tinh, youngest is Dinh. Wife number two's from An Phu, mother of Le Paul. Wife number three's Huanh's mother — his French name's Vincent — plus three little girls: Ba, Tu, and Nam. Wife number four is Ba Lua, Councilor Dong's daughter, married after we took power. Wife number five — married after coming back to the city. Met her at a sic-bo table in the Dai The Gioi district. Went to catch a cow, ended up catching a wife."

Muoi Luc shook his head.

"Anh Bay, you're too deep. Ain't you scared of sleeping in a pigsty?"

Bay Vien cracked up.

"We sure have a saying for everything, don't we? 'One wife, you sleep squeezed tight. Two wives, you sleep curled up. Three wives, you sleep in a pigsty.' I'm up to five — where the hell do I sleep, Brother Muoi?"

Without waiting for Muoi Luc to answer, Bay Vien kept going.

"Sleeping in a pigsty — that's the fate of some poor bastard who can't afford his habits. Me? I've got enough to buy each wife a proper house. The two wives in Can Duoc and An Phu already have homes down in the countryside. Huanh and his mother are staying with Muoi Tri's family. And the last two wives — I bought each of them a villa."

Muoi Luc teased him.

"Good thing we don't have kings anymore. If Anh Bay were emperor, you wouldn't be far off from Minh Mang himself!"

Bay Vien roared with laughter.

"That old man had a hundred and-something wives, ranked from the main palace down to the lowest concubines. I've got Minh Mang's own herbal wine recipe. One day I'll treat you, Brother Muoi — let's see if it really works."

"Works for what?"

"Works like they say: 'One night, five wives, four sons born.'"

Muoi Luc pushed his chair back and stood up.

"Ah, come on, old man! I've got one wife and I'm already floored! Where the hell would I find five to test it out like some emperor?"

But getting loose with the liquor and shooting the shit like that helped Muoi Luc forget, even for a little while, the bitterness of a resistance fighter who'd deserted and run to the city.


---


# Why Be Scared of Mr. Nam?

Why Be Scared of Mr. Nam?

When Muoi Luc heard from Bay Vien that Nam Tai had put Lieutenant Colonel Savani up to "borrowing" him and Bay Mon for interrogation, his blood boiled. He was dead set on teaching Nam Tai a lesson — make the bastard know that Muoi Luc wasn't somebody you fucked with. He talked it over with Bay Mon.

Bay Mon, always slow and measured, said:

"That Nam Tai's got a lot of nerve. Yeah, he needs a lesson. But it ain't easy, hear."

"Why's it hard? Tell me."

"Above him, Boss Bay. Below him, Mr. Nam. Haven't you heard the soldiers talkin'?"

"Fuck Mr. Nam. I'm Mr. Muoi. Why should I be scared of Mr. Nam?" The more he talked, the hotter Muoi Luc got.

Before Muoi Luc could make his move, another provocation hit — this one with Nam Tai pulling the strings behind the scenes. Same target: Muoi Luc.

Here's how it went down.

Bay Vien threw a banquet to welcome his two friends Bay Mon and Muoi Luc into the Binh Xuyen fold. Invites only went out to squad leaders and above. Somebody arranged the seating so that Muoi Luc ended up right across from Savani — close enough to reach out and touch each other.

A few rounds of drinks in, Savani switched to a mocking tone and asked Muoi Luc a question. His French-accented Vietnamese came out all twisted, grating on the ears.

"Mr. Muoi, now that you're here with us, party after party, Martell and Cognac flowing like tap water — you planning on going back into the zone?"

Muoi Luc's face went white. The Deuxième Bureau chief was obviously trying to rile him up. He shot to his feet, shoved his chair back, locked eyes with Savani and near-shouted:

"I came back to the city for personal reasons — my own. But definitely not because of parties with Martell and Cognac, like the Lieutenant Colonel says. You need to drop that condescending attitude, sir! This Muoi Luc is Vietnamese, but when it comes to defending my honor, I'll fucking harakiri like a Japanese if I have to."

As he said it, he tore open his shirt, grabbed a knife off the table and raised it.

Bay Mon, sitting next to him, snatched the knife away.

Everybody at the table was stunned by Muoi Luc's explosive reaction.

Even Savani was caught off guard. He quickly backtracked.

"I was just joking. I didn't expect Mr. Muoi to take it so personally. My apologies, Mr. Muoi."

He raised his glass for a toast to make peace. Muoi Luc ignored him.

"Some kind of joke is that? Talk shit about a man's family and just walk away?"

Muoi Luc shoved his chair aside and walked out.

Bay Mon chased after him.

"Brother Muoi, cool down! Remember, we're honored guests at this party. If you walk out, Anh Bay's gonna be real upset."

While Bay Mon was pulling Muoi Luc back, Nam Tai sidled up to whisper something to Bay Vien. Bay Vien cut him off.

"Shut up! Your boss was the one running his mouth — that's why Muoi Luc blew up. Go back to your seat. It's nothing!"

Bay Vien clapped when Bay Mon managed to drag Muoi Luc back to his seat. The party went on, but the mood was dead.

Bay Vien signaled the band to rip into a few raucous numbers to chase away the chill that argument had left hanging in the air.

That night, Muoi Luc stayed until the very end to ask Bay Vien:

"Who arranged the seating? Was it Tai? He put Savani up to 'borrowing' me and Bay Mon for interrogation like a couple of deserters — and then he tried to use Savani's mouth to insult me too. Was it him, Anh Bay?"

Bay Vien walked Muoi Luc out to the crocodile pond for a heart-to-heart.

"It was him. He handled everything at tonight's banquet. I don't know if he had some scheme putting you two face to face like that. What'd he say to you when I chewed out his boss? Did he tell you to yell at me?"

"Forget it, Brother Muoi. Let it go. Don't bother with that guy."

Muoi Luc shook his head.

"No. I can't let this slide. He's humiliated me twice. I've got to settle this."

The Kim Chung casino was packed that night with gamblers riding hot and cold streaks. Binh Xuyen soldiers came as soon as they got paid, testing their luck.

Bummed out, Muoi Luc showed up there too.

Bay Vien had heard Nam Tai's reports about gambling running rampant in the Binh Xuyen ranks. Foreseeing the damage it could do, he'd banned it outright. Anybody caught red-handed faced discipline. And Nam Tai himself was head of the disciplinary committee.

One night, Muoi Luc was placing his bets when a soldier ran up and whispered.

"Mr. Nam's coming, Brother Muoi!"

Muoi Luc looked up and saw Nam Tai marching straight toward him. He smiled at the soldier.

"Fuck Mr. Nam. I'm Mr. Muoi. Why should I be scared of Mr. Nam?"

And fast as lightning, an idea shot through his head: payback — tit for tat. He pretended to be completely absorbed in the game, oblivious to everybody.

Nam Tai walked up beside him and slapped his shoulder.

"Caught you red-handed..."

Right at that instant, Muoi Luc drove his elbow hard into Nam Tai's ribs. The man let out a grunt and doubled over, clutching his side. Muoi Luc played his act perfectly.

"Holy shit — Mr. Nam? I thought it was some broke-ass soldier coming to borrow money... I was in the middle of a hot streak..."

Nam Tai clutched his ribs for a long, long time. When the pain finally eased up enough, he shuffled off alone. After that, he steered clear of Muoi Luc.


---


Major General Bay Vien

Once Highway 15 from Saigon to Vung Tau got cleared in 1951, General Bondis — the commander of French forces in Cochinchina — was over the moon.

See, this highway had been a pain since 1949 under General De la Tour, dragged through General Chanson's term, and didn't get finished till Bondis took over. A three-year headache spanning three commanders.

So Bondis pinned Major General stars on Bay Vien to thank him for the job.

Bay Vien had never felt this alive in his life. A gangster who'd been in and out of jail more times than he could count, escaped hell on Con Dao island three times to make it back to the mainland — and now he's wearing two French army stars? A dream he'd never even dared to have. Now a general with three battalions under his command, all run by his most trusted men, Bay Vien's daily routine was just swinging by the finance office every afternoon to watch the girls count cash, stack it up, and lock it in the safe.

One night at a fancy restaurant, Bay Vien ran into an old benefactor from way back, from when he first got out of prison, dead broke and wandering the streets.

It was Huynh Dai — a Chinese merchant with the heart of a prince, the kind of man who'd saved Bay Vien's life once upon a time.

Huynh Dai's story sounded a lot like the billionaire Hui Bon Hoa — folks called him Uncle Hoa. Everyone in Saigon knew that mansion of his down in Cho Cu, second only to the Governor General's palace.

Huynh Dai came from Guangdong too (Teochew stock), showed up in Saigon with a bamboo pole and two baskets, spent his days scraping by as a junk buyer. Saved up a little, started selling candy in front of schools. Bit by bit his business picked up, he opened a coffee-and-noodle joint out in the suburbs along Tau Hu Canal.

That's exactly when Huynh Dai had taken Bay Vien in.

By the time Bay Vien ran into him again, his old savior was the owner of Dai La Thien Restaurant — one of the fanciest joints in all of Cho Lon. Bay Vien grabbed his hand, threw an arm around his shoulder, beaming.

"Well, well — if it ain't my own Prince of Patrons! Seeing you again, man, it's got me thinking of those old Chinese stories."

Huynh Dai laughed.

"The Major General's thinking of Han Xin and the Washerwoman, right? Eat today's rice, don't dwell on yesterday's bowl. Real glad to see my little brother come home in glory."

Huynh Dai sent for champagne, popped the cork himself nice and loud to celebrate the reunion.

Raising his glass, he said:

"After the fall of '45, I kept an eye on your moves. Company Commander of Company 9, then Commander of the Binh Xuyen Sector, then Deputy Zone Commander, then Zone Commander of War Zone 7. Now you're back in the city as a Colonel, and now a General. Damn, brother, your luck's been burning hot."

Bay Vien clinked glasses with Huynh Dai and grinned.

"And I'm toasting you too, big brother — from noodle-shop owner on Tau Hu Canal straight up to master of Dai La Thien Grand Restaurant."

Over drinks, Huynh Dai threw out a business idea.

"A general's title looks mighty impressive, but it ain't as sweet as being a businessman. I'm telling you, brother — while you've got rank and power, jump into the market. That's where the real money's made, faster."

Bay Vien laughed loud.

"With Dai The Gioi, I'm already pulling in half a million a day. What more you want me to chase, big brother? That's enough headache."

Huynh Dai smiled.

"When opportunity knocks, you gotta swing the door wide open. Remember — a man's only got one season in his life. That season's got a shelf life. You gotta milk it for everything it's worth."

"What are you telling me to do, big brother?"

"You gotta think big. With millions, billions in capital, you should start a bank, buy up real estate — one coin in, four coins out. Take a page from Uncle Hoa's book. Half the streets in Saigon-Cho Lon belong to that man."

Bay Vien's eyes lit up.

"Damn, that's a hell of an idea, big brother. I'll go home and chew on it."

Then he cracked up, cupped his hand, and whispered in Huynh Dai's ear:

"What you said reminds me of the old folks' saying — 'A buried pot of gold ain't worth a breeding sow.' Park your money in the bank and it don't grow like putting it to work."

Huynh Dai slapped Bay Vien on the shoulder.

"You still the same old salty dog, huh? Never change."

Bay Vien was tickled.

"What's better than that in this life? The Chinese have a way of thinking I really go for. When you go to a friend's celebration for striking it rich, folks whisper in each other's ear: 'You're loaded now, got more money than you can spend. Go get yourself a young wife!'"

Huynh Dai got serious again.

"I'm showing you the path to business. But first you need someone to back you up. You ever made contact with the former Emperor Bao Dai — now the Chief of State?"

Bay Vien was startled.

"No! Never even crossed my mind. The man's up on his high horse — how's a guy like me supposed to reach him?"

Huynh Dai gave a sly grin.

"High as he is, the man still needs money."

"Ain't that what the French government pays him for?"

Huynh Dai leaned in.

"A man used to living like a king — what salary's ever enough? Listen to me. You ought to set aside a monthly cut of your earnings and send it to the former Emperor, call it a social fund contribution. Money that walks ahead is smart money. The classics say: 'Receiving money is like receiving arrows.' The former Emperor takes your cash, he'll find a way to repay you proper. All you need from him is to stand as your canopy, your umbrella, so you can do business big."

Bay Vien nodded like a bobblehead.

"Good, good! Big brother, you sure know people. From now on, I'm calling you my master... I'll follow that advice."


---


Greasing the Wheels for an Umbrella

Taking Huynh Dai's advice, Bay Vien set out to make contact with former Emperor Bao Dai.

The rule of the game was still the same: money that walks ahead is smart money. But greasing the wheels ain't easy. You gotta do your homework on the mark first. Like the French saying goes: "How you give matters more than what you give."

Bribing a Chief of State? That's no small thing.

Bay Vien ordered Nam Tai to put together a full dossier on the former Emperor.

Emperor Khai Dinh passed away, and Vinh Thuy took the throne in 1925 while he was still studying in France. He didn't come back to rule until 1932. As king, Bao Dai only cared about hunting — he left all the governing to Pham Quynh, a real French puppet. In August 1945, the Viet Minh took power, and Bao Dai handed over the seal and sword to Viet Minh representative Tran Huy Lieu.

With that legendary declaration: "I would rather be a citizen of an independent country than king of a slave nation."

President Ho Chi Minh named Citizen Vinh Thuy as Advisor to the Government of the Democratic Republic of Vietnam. But stepping down from the throne didn't kill his craving for a royal life, so under the new regime, Vinh Thuy couldn't get comfortable. When the Advisor got sent to China on diplomatic work, Vinh Thuy left Hanoi and flew straight to Hong Kong.

Vinh Thuy ditched the advisor title and picked up a new one: former Emperor Bao Dai. The former Emperor played hard and waited for his moment.

The hawk faction kept losing with their blitzkrieg strategy. Admiral d'Argenlieu got replaced. Bollaert took over and went nowhere either. France started thinking about playing the Bao Dai card. The former Emperor was used to living like royalty, plus he'd married the daughter of a big Catholic landowner, connected to the local capitalist powers down south. So the machinery to use Bao Dai started turning.

On December 7, 1947, aboard the warship Duguay Trouin, the Bao Dai solution took shape: Bollaert signed an accord with Bao Dai where France would grant independence to Vietnam under Chief of State Bao Dai.

On March 26, 1948, Bao Dai agreed to form a provisional central government with Nguyen Van Xuan as Prime Minister. Two months later, on May 25, 1948, Prime Minister Xuan announced the cabinet.

On June 5, 1948, at Ha Long Bay, Bao Dai and High Commissioner Bollaert signed a formal treaty recognizing Vietnam as an independent country. The word "independence" — France had never used that word in any negotiation with the Democratic Republic of Vietnam. They only ever used "free state."

The ironic twist: after "winning independence," the former Emperor didn't come home to serve as Chief of State. He went back to France to live in exile. To the press, Bao Dai declared he'd only return when Cochinchina was formally handed back to Vietnam.

The French government saw Bollaert wasn't getting anywhere, so they put Pignon in to sweet-talk Bao Dai (October 1948). Four months later, President Vincent Auriol signed the March 8, 1949 accord with Bao Dai — only then did Bao Dai agree to come back to Vietnam. The former Emperor picked Da Lat as the spot for his Chief of State office.

After reading the full background on Chief of State Bao Dai, Bay Vien decided to grease the wheels so he'd have a royal canopy over his head, just in case things went sideways. Knowing Bao Dai trusted his little brother Vinh Can, Bay Vien found someone to make the connection.

Through that middleman, Bay Vien pledged 240,000 piasters a month to the Chief of State.

Funny thing: even as Chief of State, Bao Dai still drew a salary from the French government — top of the payscale — but no amount was ever enough for the way he threw money out the window. Bay Vien's contribution came at just the right time. Just like Huynh Dai had predicted, Bao Dai figured out a way to repay Bay Vien proper.

On April 22, 1952, when General Bondis proposed promoting Bay Vien to two-star general, Bao Dai signed off right away.

In early 1954, Bao Dai accepted Bay Vien as his sworn brother — after Bay Vien coughed up half a million US dollars so Bao Dai could keep his mistresses happy, including a Hong Kong dancer named Jenny Dong.

1954 was the year Bay Vien's star shined brightest. Becoming the former Emperor's sworn brother — that made him happier than the two-star Major General title.

With the biggest umbrella in the country over his head, Bay Vien and Huynh Dai went all in on the entertainment industry in Saigon-Cho Lon.

Dai The Gioi expanded way beyond its early days.

D'Argenlieu's strategy had been to open up entertainment zones aggressively, getting people in French-controlled areas to bury themselves in the casinos so they'd forget their civic duty — namely, joining the resistance.

Phu Tho racetrack reopened, buzzing with action.

The French press couldn't stop praising Dai The Gioi: a city within a city.

Dai The Gioi had multiple gambling halls, two movie theaters, three cai luong opera houses, Tieu Quang theater, and a dance hall. It also ran three branches: one on Boresse Street (now Calmette), two inside Cho Lon.

Governor Tran Van Huu wouldn't sign off on opening Dai The Gioi, but Chief of State Bao Dai signed it, arguing the place had already existed since High Commissioner d'Argenlieu's time and had been expanded further under Bollaert.

Only then did Bay Vien really appreciate his master Huynh Dai.

Later on, Bay Vien would need that Chief of State umbrella even more, when the political winds turned dead against the Binh Xuyen.


---


The Sworn Brother of the Former Emperor

Following his master Huynh Dai's lead, Bay Vien consulted a fortune teller before every big move. Back when he first came into the city under the French, facing General De la Tour's cold shoulder, Bay Vien was lost — so Huynh Dai hooked him up with a Chinese fortune teller who read the stars and mapped out the future. The old man swore that Bay Vien's year — the Wood Dragon, 1904 — was a fate of three lows and seven highs, like a dragon that soars and plunges by turns. Age 44 would be the big turning point of his life. The years after that would bring a surge in both money and fame. At first Bay Vien didn't believe it — those months of lying low and waiting, he felt like his life was about to hit the skids. But then things started looking up: he got promoted to Colonel, put in charge of city security, then assigned to clear Highway 15 from Saigon to Vung Tau.

He thought that was good enough — then out of nowhere, April 1952 he got promoted to Major General, and by late 1953, Bao Dai accepted him as a sworn brother.

Bay Vien asked Huynh Dai why the former Emperor picked him specifically. Huynh Dai gave a sly chuckle.

"That word 'bao de' the papers use — it's wrong. In Sino-Vietnamese, 'bao de' means biological brothers, same mother and father. The right word here is 'nghia de' — sworn brother, brother by covenant. That's the accurate term."

Bay Vien nodded slowly.

"Master's right. The former Emperor speaks French better than Vietnamese, so getting the word wrong makes sense. But I still wonder — why me? The man's surrounded by plenty of sycophants, most of them intellectuals."

Huynh Dai spoke with confidence.

"This is delicate. At first I thought it was because you sent him 240,000 piasters monthly, then dropped half a million dollars for him to slip to his girlfriends as lucky money. But later, through my sources close to Bao Dai, I got to know him better."

Bay Vien was even more curious.

"Spell it out for me, then."

Huynh Dai laid it out.

"France wanted to use Bao Dai against the Viet Minh. Bao Dai knew that full well, and he played along — using France right back to upgrade his life in exile. High Commissioner Pignon assigned two experts to stay by Bao Dai's side. Both had worked closely with him before. But Bao Dai asked Pignon to pull them both out — he didn't like their interference. To prove he wasn't France's puppet, Bao Dai flat-out refused to set up shop in Hanoi, even though France offered him the Puginier Palace where all the French Governor Generals had lived. He also turned down Saigon because France wouldn't give him the Norodom Palace — the Governor General's residence before 1945. He didn't want to be number two.

"So Bao Dai picked Da Lat as the spot for his Chief of State office. Up in that fancy resort town, he started opening up entertainment halls, dance clubs, casinos — just like Hong Kong and Macau. Naturally, the French pushed back: a Chief of State couldn't be some 'international playboy.' His plan to turn Da Lat into an international casino got blocked. That's why the former Emperor chose Bay Vien as his ally — to work Dai The Gioi together. You get it now?"

Bay Vien finally saw the light.

"So the former Emperor took me under his wing because we shared the same business strategy — milking people's gambling blood."

Huynh Dai pressed on.

"That's not all. Earlier you asked why Bao Dai didn't pick intellectuals as allies instead of a gangster like you. Truth is, Bao Dai got sick and tired of those shameless intellectuals. Life at the Chief of State's villa was like a miniature imperial court, full of flunkies coming and going to line their pockets — especially Phan Van Giao, the Central Vietnam Governor. This guy once told Bao Dai: 'Your chef might be Viet Minh. If you don't mind, please let me taste your food beforehand to protect you.'"

Bay Vien cracked up laughing.

"Damn, that guy's sucking up so hard there's no room left for anybody else to flatter. So what'd Bao Dai think?"

"Phan Van Giao was the one who brought girls to Bao Dai for entertainment at the villa — all kinds: Vietnamese, French, mixed, blind singers, even hill tribe women. Bao Dai valued Phan Van Giao but secretly despised an intellectual with no backbone. There were plenty more Phan Van Giaos swarming around Bao Dai, so he grew scared of the 'ill-bred' intellectual type. That's why he leaned toward gangsters who had more class."

Huynh Dai wrapped it up.

"You picked Bao Dai as your canopy, so take the time to really understand him. Everybody likes flattery — but you gotta do it with art. Flatter in a way that the man being flattered doesn't feel embarrassed.

"Bottom line: Bao Dai is a complicated man. You need to keep a close watch. We've got a long road of business ahead with him."

— Nguyen Hung


---


Choosing the Chief of Staff

Bảo Đại called up a handful of politicians to his Đà Lạt palace to pick their brains about who should be Chief of Staff for the National Army of Vietnam.

According to the Bảo Đại–Vincent Auriol treaty, France recognized Vietnam as an independent country with its own sovereignty and its own army. The army was already there — they just needed to pick a Chief of Staff. Among the names the Chief of State put forward for the job was Bảy Viễn. At the Langbian Palace Hotel, where all those politicians were staying, whispers were flying.

Bảy Viễn — a bandit turned general, the French trusted him to fight fire with fire.

Central Vietnam's governor Phan Văn Giáo was the one slamming Bảy Viễn harder than anybody else.

Tough luck for him, though — he badmouthed Bảy Viễn right in front of Lai Văn Sang, Bảy Viễn's man at that very meeting.

Tư Sang shot a telegram straight to Bảy Viễn, filling him in on the gossip around the Chief of Staff vote, especially Phan Văn Giáo's trash talk.

Bảy Viễn flew into a rage, jumped into his Jaguar — that brand-new racing model fresh off the line, the latest thing in Vietnam. The road from Sài Gòn to Đà Lạt, past Bảo Lộc, twists through high passes with cliffs dropping off the side, hairpin turns with little shrines to the souls who'd died there. Didn't matter. Bảy Viễn floored that Jaguar, pushing damn near a hundred klicks an hour. The second he pulled up to Langbian Hotel, he jumped out looking for Tư Sang.

"Which son of a bitch is this Phan Văn Giáo, daring to talk shit about Bảy Viễn?" — Even as he asked, his hand was already sliding into his pocket where his snub-nose was waiting to spit fire.

Governor Phan Văn Giáo was a regular VIP at the hotel. A bellboy quick-footed it to tip him off, and Phan Văn Giáo slipped out the back, flagged a taxi, and hauled ass to the palace to beg Bảo Đại for protection.

When he realized his prey had slipped, Bảy Viễn grabbed Tư Sang and they tore out of there, burning rubber to the palace.

Phan Văn Giáo had vanished, of course. Bảo Đại had to step in and smooth things over.

But even after almost getting killed, Phan Văn Giáo kept right on trash-talking Bảy Viễn to the other politicians stuffing their faces at the meetings:

"I don't know about you gentlemen, but me — I'd be mortified if some bandit got picked as Chief of Staff of the National Army of Vietnam. You mention Bảy Viễn, you gotta mention Đại Thế Giới — the biggest entertainment palace in all of Indochina. Back in the day, the French let a Macau guy named Lâm Giống run it. To force Lâm Giống to hand Đại Thế Giới over, Bảy Viễn didn't blink before ordering his Bình Xuyên boys to throw grenades into the Kim Chung casino — killed sixty people. Then he kidnapped Đại Thế Giới's whole board of directors for ransom. A character with a reputation like that — how the hell does he have the right to be Chief of Staff of Vietnam's National Army?"

He kept going, getting hotter by the second:

"Did you gentlemen know — the minute he took over as head of police and security for Sài Gòn–Chợ Lớn, Bảy Viễn set up Bình Khang Village, a full-on whorehouse district, wide open, servicing the troops."

Funny thing was, the more Phan Văn Giáo trashed Bảy Viễn, the more he showed his own ass. Folks from Central Vietnam — especially Huế and Đà Lạt — all knew the governor started as a pharmacist, got rich peddling Western medicine on the black market. Phan Văn Giáo kissed up to Bảo Đại to shore up his own power. Bảo Đại had plenty of bootlickers, but Phan Văn Giáo was numero uno.

Meanwhile, through Huỳnh Đại as the middleman, Bảy Viễn was linking up with the Corsican crew, led by the owner of the Continental Hotel, to run some international operations.

The Corsicans — Corsica's an island off the south of France — they're born to ride the crest of the wave, both literally and figuratively. Most of them made their living smuggling, running bars, and working as prison guards.

The top dog was Andréani, who owned the Croix du Sud (Southern Cross) bar at the head of Catinat Street, diagonal from the Majestic Hotel (Cửu Long).

That place was the meeting spot for every Corsican smuggler in town. Andréani had rubbed out plenty of rivals, mostly Chinese. But kidnapping people and taking them out to the suburbs for a hit had been getting busted by the cops too often, so he'd gone to ground.

The one flying the Corsican banner highest was the owner of the Continental Hotel, a man named Franchini.

The cops had Franchini's whole life story: started out as a cabin boy on Marseille–Sài Gòn ocean liners, taught himself on the job, eventually landed a gig at the Bank of Indochina, then jumped into import-export and became a heavyweight on the Sài Gòn business scene. And on top of all that, he got lucky — he charmed the spoiled daughter of a high-ranking mandarin when he was down in the Mekong Delta...


---


New Alliance

The luck of a lifetime came when Franchini was working as a traveling salesman for a big company, going from Sài Gòn down to Mỹ Tho. That's where the daughter of a high-ranking mandarin set her sights on him. She asked her family to let her marry Franchini, but the mandarin's family didn't want to give their daughter to a Westerner.

But young love burned too hot. They ran off together to set up their own nest. Couldn't let their precious daughter live like a drifter, so the mandarin and his wife sent people to find them, brought them back, threw a proper wedding, and even gave them a few hundred acres of land to make a living. Just like that, the Corsican boy became a landowner. Bad luck hit, though — his wife died giving birth to their first child. Even so, the mandarin still treated Franchini like a son-in-law and let him keep all that land.

Then came strike number two: the Continental Hotel was going under. They put an ad in the paper to sell. Nobody wanted to buy because the place was falling apart, bleeding money nonstop. But Franchini saw it different — the Continental sat right on Catinat Street, next to the Municipal Theater, smack in the heart of Sài Gòn. And the price was a steal.

He borrowed cash from the Missionary Society to buy it, then hustled loans from friends in the Corsican community. The Continental got fixed up, renovated into a five-star hotel, and turned into an international press hub. Heavyweight journalists and writers like Lucien Bodard, Pierre Dareourt, Jean Lacouture, and Philippe Devillers all came there to get the war news. The English writer Graham Greene even set up base there to write his famous novel The Quiet American.

Once he became a billionaire hobnobbing with the press at home and abroad, Franchini turned into a major player in Sài Gòn politics. But the security services never stopped tailing him. They didn't buy his story — you don't get rich that fast just running a hotel. In ten years, from 1940 to 1950, a dead-broke guy becomes a billionaire. There had to be some secret deals going on. Corsicans were the kings of smuggling.

So the cops and security kept leeching onto Franchini like starving leeches. But no luck. Not a shred of evidence that Franchini was involved in any smuggling rings.

One incident showed just how much juice Franchini had: a reporter named Armorin came from Bordeaux to interview the owner of the Continental. The guy came in swinging: "There's stuff in the French papers saying you got rich fast by money-laundering and currency exchange on the black market. True?" Without a word, Franchini stood up and slapped him right across the face. Then his boys piled on to "discuss things with the gentleman." The reporter ran for his life — he thought he'd walked into a Corsican gangster's den. Later he wrote an article accusing the Continental owner of trying to murder a journalist. A few weeks later, on his way back to France, his plane went down. No body recovered.

Franchini knew Bảy Viễn was on the rise, backed by Chief of State Bảo Đại, so he made the first move to get tight. He showed up at the dancehall inside Đại Thế Giới with his Corsican buddies after a boozy dinner.

The next day, he went to Bảy Viễn and laid it out straight:

"Last night we stopped by your dancehall, Major General. I want to help you build it up to proper international standards. You need a Filipino band to change up the vibe. The dance floor needs expanding, and the whole interior needs redoing."

Bảy Viễn knew Franchini was a smuggling kingpin that the cops could never nail with evidence or witnesses — a true master worth learning from. So he shook his hand warmly:

"I'm honored! Having you on board, brother — nothing beats it. Aside from this little dancehall, I'm hoping we can work together on bigger things. I've got close friends in the Chinese community in Chợ Lớn, and you've got your Corsican crew. You and me team up — I reckon we'll go far."

Franchini nodded:

"That's right. The dancehall's just the first step. We'll talk more after."

And just like that, Bảy Viễn had tied himself to one of the biggest power players in Sài Gòn.


---


Burying the Name Bảy Viễn

Chief of State Bảo Đại kept holing up in Đà Lạt while the war situation got more desperate by the day. The French were getting crushed up north — worst of all was losing their two crack battalions, Lepage and Charton, on Route 4. Down in Sài Gòn, Lieutenant General Nguyễn Bình's suicide squads were running wild. The French leaned on Bảo Đại to come back to Sài Gòn and actually do his damn job as Chief of State.

Bảo Đại had no choice but to listen to his colonial masters.

The day Bảo Đại flew down from Đà Lạt to Sài Gòn, civil servants and schoolkids were herded to Tân Sơn Nhất Airport to welcome him.

All the religious sect forces showed up too.

Every person who came to greet the Chief of State got five đồng as a participation bonus. The ones who had it worst were the cops and security — they lined the whole route from the airport to downtown, a guard every five meters, in case anything went down.

On this occasion, Bảy Viễn had the honor of hosting the Chief of State. Beforehand, Năm Tài had worked with Nguyễn Đệ, the Chief of State's Chief of Staff, to draw up a welcome plan. Bảy Viễn put together nights fit for a king — parties all over town, from Đại Thế Giới to Đại La Thiên. Master and servant shared the same tastes, so Bảy Viễn had the owner of Đại La Thiên, billionaire Huỳnh Đại, pick out a bunch of beauty queens from the dancehalls to serve the Chief of State. The festivities also featured a special variety show — beautiful young singers with all the charm, crooning love songs in Vietnamese, Chinese, and even French. Then came a dance routine straight out of a fairy tale, a cancan number, and to top it off, a strip-tease finale.

Before the night was over, the host handed the honored guest an album of beautiful women, each one numbered, for him to take his pick.

That very first night in Sài Gòn, Bảo Đại was already crazy about Bảy Viễn and decided he'd repay the favor in a big way.

Under the Bảo Đại–Auriol treaty, the whole administrative apparatus was being handed over to the Vietnamese. The Chief of State had full authority to pick his people from top to bottom. Bảo Đại didn't like General Nguyễn Văn Xuân, so he handed the Prime Minister post to Nguyễn Phan Long. Mr. Long was an intellectual landowner who followed the Cao Đài faith. He was obsessed with séances — one of those hobbies Cochinchina intellectuals picked up to commune with the spirits and sages. He was also a journalist who wrote French better than the French themselves. On the personal side, Mr. Long had all the vices — hooked on all four: booze, women, money, and opium. One thing about him, though — he didn't like the French. The minute he took office as Prime Minister of the State of Vietnam, he tried reaching out to the Việt Minh to propose a peaceful solution, but the Việt Minh flat-out refused to negotiate with the Bảo Đại government.

The police and security apparatus also had to be handed over to the Vietnamese. Before this, the French Sûreté, led by Perrier, occupied a whole row of ten buildings at the far end of Catinat Street, right next to the Notre-Dame Cathedral.

The folks in Sài Gòn had a dark joke about the colonizers: "Right next to heaven is hell."

The Catinat station was infamous as a torture house. Any Việt Minh cadre or suspect who got picked up was taken there to be interrogated and squeezed, their statements written up, then shipped off to the Grand Prison. Even after being handed over to Nguyễn Phan Long's government, the French Sûreté still operated alongside it, still run by Perrier, supposedly to protect the French expeditionary forces.

Bảo Đại put Major General Lê Văn Viễn in charge of national security and police.

Ever since he got promoted to Major General, the Bình Xuyên Commander ordered the name "Bảy Viễn" buried — it brought back bad memories. He wanted everybody to call him Lê Văn Viễn instead — sounded more respectable, more proper.

Lai Văn Sang got appointed Director of the Metropolitan Police by Bảy Viễn, taking his throne at the Catinat station. Tư Sang's big dream had finally come true. But the title sounded fancy while his actual know-how was pretty thin, so he had to learn on the job. And Tư Sang's way of learning was something special: he pulled out the personnel files of his predecessors and studied them to pick up tips.

First up was Nguyễn Văn Tâm, the man folks down in Cochinchina called "the Tiger of Cai Lậy."

District Chief Tâm sat in the Cai Lậy district seat before becoming Police Director. He earned the nickname "Tiger of Cai Lậy" back in 1940, the year of the Southern Uprising. Nguyễn Văn Tâm got promoted to the mandarin rank of Đốc phủ by the French because he wiped out more Communists than anybody else. Back then, District Chief Tâm only had about twenty native militia men.

Thousands of protesters marched into Cai Lậy town.

Tâm knew he was outgunned, so he ordered his militia to hold their positions and not shoot. He called for the protest leaders to come negotiate — and had his trusted men secretly photograph the people at the front of the march. Then he arrested a few hundred of them cold, dragged them into the interrogation rooms. District Chief Tâm handled the questioning himself, medieval style.

When the Japanese overthrew the French, Tâm got captured by the Japanese and beaten just as brutally as he'd tortured the Communists before. When the Việt Minh took power, Tâm finally learned what fear was — the tables had turned. But he got lucky. British troops arrived in Sài Gòn to disarm the defeated Japanese, and they intervened to free the French and their Vietnamese collaborators from the Grand Prison.

You'd think surviving that would've made him smarter. But District Chief Tâm stayed exactly the same. The French put him in as Security Minister in the puppet governments of Nguyễn Văn Thinh, Lê Văn Hoạch, and Nguyễn Văn Xuân...

So what did Tư Sang learn from his predecessor Nguyễn Văn Tâm?

Tâm was always on defense. Even in his own office, he kept expert marksmen posted. At the smallest sign of trouble, the shooters would open fire on whoever was sitting across from him.


---


# Born of the Trade, Die by the Trade

Born of the trade, die by the trade.

After studying up on the lessons of "the Cai Lay tiger," Tu Sang dug into the file on Coq Bazin—a damn terror who'd been ruling the roost over at the Catinat station for a good long while.

Sitting there plopped down in Bazin's own armchair, reading the report on how Bazin met his end, Tu Sang couldn't help but shiver. He said to himself: "Born of the trade, die by the trade."

Bazin lived and worked like a damn machine, always on alert, always watching his back. Bazin lived on the second floor of a high-rise on Catinat Street. Every day, he'd come downstairs, walk down Catinat, turn onto Le Thanh Ton Street, and climb into his car—his driver already waiting in the parking lot in front of the Saigon City Hall (what's now the Ho Chi Minh City People's Committee building).

On April 28, 1950, the shock squad of La Van Liem's plainclothes police had set up an ambush along that short stretch—less than a kilometer. One of the squad members, wearing a brown felt hat, pretended to read a newspaper on a bench in the little flower garden right in front of Bazin's building. When Bazin stepped out onto Catinat Street, this guy waved his hat as a signal to the team leader standing at the intersection of Catinat and d'Espagne, right by La Pagode restaurant. That team leader tipped his hat too, signaling a pistol-wielding squad member already waiting at the Metropole pharmacy on d'Espagne Street. When Bazin walked past, walking with an Air Force captain, that squad member quietly trailed him, hanging back a few steps. All of a sudden the Air Force captain turned around, just as this guy reached into his pants pocket. Realizing he'd run into a Viet Minh, the captain lunged forward and kicked the squad member. The guy slammed into the wall but still fired from inside his pocket. The bullet hit the captain square. Right then he pulled his gun out and emptied the rest of the magazine into Bazin's chest. After the shooting, he ran over to a Traction Avant parked in the lot in front of City Hall. Another squad member whose job was to cover him took a shot at Bazin's driver, but that driver was quick—he dropped and crawled under the car. They both jumped in the car and tore off, heading straight for the rendezvous point to make their way back to the Delta.

The whole hit went down fast, inside of five minutes—no security force had time to react. The first volley wounded Air Force Captain Roger, tore right through La Pagode restaurant, and shattered the glass cabinets full of liquor and pastries. All the well-to-do folks having breakfast there hit the floor, hugging the tiles. By the time the cops showed up, the hit squad's Traction Avant was long gone.

The French secret police only found out about one of the guys in that hit squad by pure chance. Some French guy who'd been walking past City Hall that morning had seen a coworker from the Tan Son Nhat airport sitting behind the wheel of a Traction Avant parked in front of City Hall. Later, when he heard stories about the shooting and how the killers got away in that very same model of car, this Frenchman went to the police station and ratted out the driver as the guy he worked with. The secret police launched an investigation right away and printed wanted posters with photos and a big reward for anyone who could help catch the men. But no matter how fast they moved, they were already late. The trio, including the driver, had already slipped into the Delta that very afternoon.

Tu Sang pulled out his handkerchief and wiped the sweat off his forehead. He knew all about those urban action units. Every single one of them was a suicide soldier, trained to the bone to strike deep and hit hard. And the scariest part was their spirit—their guts, their audacity, looking at death like it was lighter than a feather.

Even the women had their own suicide unit, the Minh Khai Platoon, the ones who lobbed grenades into the Majestic movie theater (now the Cuu Long). That time, over fifty naval officers were killed or wounded by two grenades thrown by three schoolgirls who'd gone out to the Delta for military training and then come back to Saigon to operate.

What could you learn from Bazin's death?

Tu Sang figured Bazin died because he lived like a machine. Wake up, come downstairs, get in the car, get to the office—same time, every day. Made it easy as pie for the Viet Minh to draw up their plans.

From then on, Tu Sang kept changing his routes and his schedule. Even being that careful, the specter of Bazin's death never left him alone. That just shows you—being Director of City Police was nobody's idea of an easy gig. That armchair of Bazin's he was sitting in felt like it had only three legs.

The nightmares weren't over yet. On July 31, 1951, news broke that shook both Saigon and Paris to the core: General Chanson—Commander of French forces in Southern Vietnam—and Governor Thai Lap Thanh, who'd replaced Tran Van Huu, had been taken out by a suicide attacker during an inspection trip to Sa Dec province.

They'd barely made it to the reviewing stand in the provincial stadium when a soldier came charging through the security fence straight at them. Three yards away, he pulled his hand out of his pants pocket and snapped to attention, saluting sharp as a military academy cadet. Then the grenade he'd already primed inside his pocket blew. Half his body went flying. Up on that stand, General Chanson and Governor Thai Lap Thanh died on the spot. A bunch of officers, including a colonel, were badly wounded.

Tu Sang launched an investigation right away, but he couldn't for the life of him figure out which agency or unit that suicide attack belonged to. Later, the French secret police swore it was Colonel Trinh Minh The's handiwork—the head of the Cao Dai Alliance, a splinter group that broke off from the Holy See in Tay Ninh and leaned pro-American.

Military Intelligence Unit Two also figured, based on the attacker's Cao Dai uniform, that it was the pro-American Cao Dai faction trying to "put the French in their place."

Tu Sang, even though he didn't have a shred of witness or physical evidence, still concluded it was a Viet Minh "achievement." Because nobody else would sacrifice themselves for a greater cause the way the Viet Minh would.

Tu Sang realized he'd already climbed onto the tiger's back. Jump off and you die anyway—might as well kill them before they kill you.

The Binh Xuyen police from then on made a name for themselves as even more ruthless than the "Cai Lay tiger" or Coq Bazin.


---


# The Monte Carlo Casino

The military situation was getting tighter by the day. The French were losing in the border regions and then the highlands too, so they had to ship Marshal de Lattre de Tassigny over to Vietnam to serve as High Commissioner and Commander-in-Chief of French forces in Indochina.

But even France's most brilliant general was hard-pressed to turn the tide.

France was already in a bind after World War II, and now they had to shoulder the cost of the Indochina war—101 billion francs in 1946, up to 136 billion by 1948. What scared the French most was the prospect of never pacifying the region before the Viet Minh got reinforced by the Chinese Red Army rolling up to the border. In the middle of that boiling-over mess, France sent a general over to Indochina to study the situation from every angle and help the French government figure out how to handle the Vietnam war.

The man given that mission was General Revers.

On May 16, 1949, General Revers touched down in Saigon.

Revers toured the battlefields, consulted with all the staff commands, trying to see as much as he could and hear as much as he could to get a full picture. Beyond military matters, Revers was also interested in politics, culture, and society.

The atmosphere in Saigon at the time hit the Inspector General square in the face. It was an atmosphere of pure pleasure-seeking and zero responsibility. French officials were busy getting rich by transferring funds and buying and selling Indochinese piasters at the bank. On top of the currency trading, Saigon was crawling with entertainment venues and high-class nightclubs, the biggest of them being Dai The Gioi (The Great World), which Bay Vien was operating under a concession with the backing of Chief of State Bao Dai.

On the military front, France was up against a lot of disadvantages. The Viet Minh used guerrilla tactics, and the French army wasn't used to that kind of fighting. On top of that, Indochina fell under the jurisdiction of the Ministry of Overseas France (formerly the Colonial Ministry), represented by the High Commissioner, but the Commander-in-Chief of the expeditionary forces was a general appointed by the Ministry of Defense.

With that kind of crossed-wire arrangement, you had one group marching left and another marching right.

Revers proposed they should pick someone from the Ministry of Overseas France to hold both civilian and military power. That someone ought to be a general.

As for strategy, France should pull its lines in tight, concentrate maximum forces in northern Vietnam, and fortify the positions around Hanoi.

When Nam Tai reported all this to him, Bay Vien was only really concerned about one thing—his "cash cow," Dai The Gioi, which some French politicians back in the home country were starting to eye.

Bay Vien was terrified someone was going to smash his rice bowl. And the one who'd smash it would have to be the French President himself, because Bay Vien already had Chief of State Bao Dai as his umbrella overhead.

Worried about this, Bay Vien wired the palace up in Da Lat to consult with the former emperor, but Nguyen De told him the Chief of State had already flown to France.

As anxious as he was, Bay Vien couldn't help but chuckle to himself.

"Now that's what I call a playboy. The war's raging everywhere and the Chief of State's only worried about flying around having a good time. He got tired of Da Lat, so he flew over to Cannes, that resort town on the Mediterranean coast."

Bay Vien made up his mind to fly to France himself, just once, to see what it was all about. Going to the West had been a longtime dream of this country-boy-turned-gangster, and besides, the trip was also about securing his own interests.

He had to convince the Chief of State to hold onto Dai The Gioi, no matter what.

One week in France was the most glorious week of Bay Vien's life. The former emperor threw a big welcome banquet for the owner of Dai The Gioi. They went up to Paris to watch the can-can with all those legs and backsides flying high, then down to Saint-Tropez beach to see the topless girls sunbathing. But the thing he enjoyed most was tagging along with the former emperor to the Monte Carlo casino in Monaco. As a dyed-in-the-wool gambler himself, Bay Vien had to tip his hat to the former emperor's way of playing—he'd stand there watching for a good long while before putting his money on a number at the roulette table. A little while later, some gambler who'd gone broke after riding a single number walked out of the casino, and the former emperor immediately put his money on that very same number—and the ball landed right on it.

Bao Dai nodded thoughtfully and said to Bay Vien:

"That's probability theory. The way the math works, each number has its own cycle. It disappears for a while, then shows up again. That fellow rode the number 13 for over a dozen spins. By the time he went bust, the number 13 came up again."

Bay Vien shook his hand and praised the former emperor:

"That probability theory of yours is something else. If you think about it bigger, in life you got to know how to wait for your moment."

Bao Dai nodded along:

"That's right! Doing politics, you got to know how to wait for your moment too. Like when I was holed up in Hong Kong. So many politicians came calling, inviting me to get involved in government. But I wouldn't listen. I still had to read the situation. I waited until France got tired of the warmongering crowd like d'Argenlieu and Leclerc. Once those boys got replaced, only then did I agree to talk with High Commissioner Pignon. Signed the agreement and I still stayed in Cannes, waiting until France really followed through on handing over democratic freedoms before I'd come back to Vietnam. But not Hanoi or Saigon—I flew straight to Da Lat."

Bay Vien brought up the matter of Dai The Gioi, and Bao Dai said:

"Don't you worry. Nobody's gonna shut that place down.

All civilized countries have entertainment for their people. Look at Monaco right here."


---


# Marshal De Lattre

At the end of October 1950, High Commissioner Pignon went from Saigon up to Hanoi and saw the capital was in a tight spot.

The city was surrounded by the Viet Minh on all four sides. Meanwhile, the French press was running stories that High Commissioner Pignon was about to be replaced because the "communist forces" were winning everywhere. The French President called on the famous generals to go save Indochina. General Juin, who was living like a king in Morocco, didn't want to "drop the bird in hand for the one in the bush." General Koenig agreed to go to Indochina only if he got reinforcements, but France had no reserve troops. President Auriol called on Marshal De Lattre.

De Lattre knew how hopeless the situation was, but he took the mission because duty called.

On December 17, 1950, De Lattre arrived in Saigon. This five-star general was a tough son of a bitch—soon as word got out he was coming, every officer in the place got nervous. Having already read Inspector General Revers's report, De Lattre knew how bad things were in Indochina. The army had no talented commanders. Even his own son, Second Lieutenant Bernard, had written him, complaining that "there's no leadership in Vietnam." Everybody was busy getting rich and chasing pleasure. De Lattre saw Dai The Gioi as a thorn in his side. He couldn't stand the idea of people partying while the soldiers were suffering. How were the troops supposed to fight when everybody around them was just out having a good time?

De Lattre's first order of business was to straighten everybody out. He went to Chasseloup High School and gave a speech, calling on the youth to enlist and defend the "free world." Then the High Commissioner and Commander-in-Chief of French forces ordered a general mobilization.

When he couldn't find Chief of State Bao Dai in Hanoi or Saigon, De Lattre rushed up to Da Lat. Lucky for him, Nguyen De had already cabled Cannes to call the former emperor back to his post when he heard De Lattre was on his way to Vietnam.

Bao Dai knew damn well De Lattre was hot-tempered, and now that he'd been appointed High Commissioner and Commander-in-Chief, he was nothing to mess with. Bao Dai flew straight back to Da Lat.

Bao Dai received General De Lattre with great ceremony. Knowing De Lattre liked hunting, Bao Dai organized a hunting trip up in Ban Me Thuot, then took him to Lac Thien Lake. It was the finest place in all Indochina for rest and hunting. The highland forests were full of wild game—tigers, elephants, leopards, and especially rhinoceroses. Bao Dai's little stilt house was decked out with all the comforts: armchairs upholstered in tiger skin, footrests made from elephant feet. Deer antlers and elephant tusks hung on the walls. In the back there was a bookcase with a couple hundred volumes. And plenty of single beds with thick mattresses and white sheets. If you needed a pretty girl, all you had to do was pick up the phone and a few hours later, one would show up.

While drinking rice wine through bamboo tubes with Bao Dai, De Lattre took the chance to straighten out the former emperor:

"Your life here is most enjoyable, but you ought to spare a little time for affairs of state."

Bao Dai laughed:

"What would you have me do in this situation, Marshal? The mighty French army with its famous generals—Leclerc, Salan, Valluy—couldn't stop the red tide. What could a deposed emperor like me possibly do?"

De Lattre said:

"At the very least, you shouldn't be in bed with that gangster-turned-general Bay Vien, running the Dai The Gioi right in the middle of Saigon."

Bao Dai shook his head:

"Marshal, you've been far away and don't have the full picture. Dai The Gioi wasn't started by Bay Vien or by me. Admiral d'Argenlieu signed the permit when the French army first arrived here. If this entertainment venue is immoral, then you ought to reprimand the Admiral first. A man of the cloth, and he dared to sign a permit for a nightclub. Maybe he figured every country needs a place for people to have fun. If you don't concentrate it all in one spot where you can keep an eye on it, people will just do it on the sly. Brothels ought to be out in the open too, so the ladies can get proper check-ups from doctors. If you ban it, they'll run things underground, and that'll hurt the people even worse—starting with the soldiers."

De Lattre shook his head:

"No matter who approved it, Dai The Gioi has no moral standing."

Bao Dai said:

"Let me finish my thought. By the time High Commissioner Bollaert came around, and then Pignon, they both re-signed the extension permits for Dai The Gioi. Before, Lam Gieng, a Macau Chinese, held the concession. Every quarter, they sent millions of piasters back to the home country. When Bay Vien won the bid, that revenue started going to public works—like funding the troops. The Binh Xuyen don't take French salaries like the Cao Dai and Hoa Hao sect armies. The Binh Xuyen also used that money to build their headquarters and family housing, just as nice and fancy as the personal guard barracks—sometimes even nicer."

De Lattre kept shaking his head:

"You shouldn't get so deep into business dealings with Bay Vien. He's one thing, you're another. You can't let the world judge the Chief of State as just some playboy. It's bad for your reputation.

Tomorrow I'm heading to Hanoi. I'd like you to come with me. A Chief of State needs to be seen where the bullets are flying, alongside the troops. That would serve you well."

Bao Dai politely declined:

"As I said, the fighting is the business of famous generals like yourself, Marshal. What good would I do there? I wish you a safe journey."


---


# Prime Minister Ngô Đình Diệm

Prime Minister Ngô Đình Diệm

From his farewell at the Đà Lạt palace, Bảo Đại kept a close eye on the war front.

The French were on a losing streak. Couldn't win a goddamn fight, so their generals switched to the hedgehog tactic—bunching up in fortified positions, baiting the enemy's main force to come close so they could blow 'em to bits. Bảo Đại wasn't a military man, but all he had to do was look at the list of French big shots getting shipped out one after another to know things were going to shit: Leclerc, Valluy, Sa Lan, Blaizot, Carpentier, De Lattre, and now Navarre—the poor bastard stuck with the whole Điện Biên Phủ siege mess.

Bảy Viễn, the Bình Xuyên Boss

To keep tabs on the situation, Bảo Đại decided to hop over to France. The way the Former Emperor saw it, Vietnam's fate was gonna be settled at the conference table where the big powers were meeting in Geneva.

Reading the papers every damn day, Bảo Đại got more and more disappointed.

General Bedell Smith cut right to the chase:
"I don't believe you and the French can pull off an honorable ceasefire with the communists. They don't want to split Vietnam, and neither do you. You oughta keep fighting while also negotiating toward peace. President Eisenhower can't directly intervene—domestic politics, same story as the Korean War. He got elected president by promising to bring American boys home from Korea. He can't send U.S. troops to Vietnam now, but we can give you the strong Vietnamese army you need. We'll train your troops. All you gotta do is appoint the generals and the staff. Lean on the French to let us handle training for your new divisions. That's as far as we can go."

To put Bảo Đại at ease, Bedell Smith added:
"Since the Secretary of State—head of the negotiating team, Foster Dulles—flew back to the States, I've gotta fill in. If he were still in France, he'd have come to see you himself to hash out these confidential matters like we're doing today."

Bảo Đại kept his cards close to his chest with the American proposal. The Former Emperor had been through enough storms in his life to learn how to be damn careful. Not getting tied down—that was the name of the game.

A few days later, the Americans came back with a new offer for Bảo Đại: they wanted him to appoint Ngô Đình Diệm as Prime Minister after the Geneva Accords split Vietnam at the 17th parallel.

Bảo Đại didn't like Ngô Đình Diệm one bit—they'd butted heads over politics before. But with his back against the wall, he had to pick his old enemy as Premier. Little did the Former Emperor know this decision would bring disaster crashing down on his head just a few years later.

Things moved so fast it made your head spin.

Bảy Viễn was always sniffing around for news about the Former Emperor's fate—that was the umbrella shielding the Bình Xuyên-born Major General.

When word got out that Bảo Đại had invited Ngô Đình Diệm back to run the country as Prime Minister, Bảy Viễn got worried. Word had it Diệm was a devout Catholic, with his big brother Archbishop Ngô Đình Thục. Bảy Viễn was scared this pious Premier would shut down all the gambling joints and entertainment halls—meaning the Bình Xuyên would lose the "milk and honey" that'd been feeding them for years.

Ngô Đình Diệm came back to the country at the end of June 1954. Everybody knew Diệm was American "shelf stock."

Bảy Viễn, the Bình Xuyên Boss

When they brought Diệm in, the U.S. jacked up their aid to 500 million bucks a year, but they handed it straight to Vietnam—no more going through the French. They also sent over a military advisory team to train fresh recruits, slowly building up a powerful national army.

Diệm had left the country and lived in a monastery in America for years, biding his time, until the U.S. pressured Bảo Đại to invite him back as Prime Minister. Diệm figured he'd "attained enlightenment through his修行." First thing he did was head back to Huế for a big "glorious homecoming ceremony."

Here's the kicker—in the Imperial city, damn near nobody knew who this Ngô "patriot" was except for a handful of big-shot court officials. But when his plane was about to touch down in the ancient capital of Huế, Ngô Đình Diệm's eyes lit up: a red carpet rolled all the way from the landing strip to the terminal reception hall.

Who the hell had the bright idea to welcome the Prime Minister with such a fancy ceremony? Soon as he stepped off the stairs, Diệm asked the mayor of Huế. The mayor pointed to an officer—a second lieutenant from the Security Corps named Tôn Thất Đính.

Diệm nodded and said:
"Take care of that kid for me."

Back then, Tôn Thất Đính was just a nobody officer. Thanks to that brainwave of running to Đông Ba Market to buy cheap seven-span mats, dye 'em red, and stitch 'em into a carpet, he later rose like a kite in a storm.

But the very guy who owed Diệm everything would end up playing the key role in Operation Bravo II, which finished off the Ngô dynasty's brutal reign ten years later.


---


# Crushing the Sects

Crushing the Sects

To square off against Prime Minister Ngô Đình Diệm, Bảy Viễn teamed up with Lieutenant Colonel Savani of the Deuxième Bureau.

Bảy Viễn told Năm Tài:
"Sun Tzu said: know your enemy and know yourself, and you'll win a hundred battles. Our enemy now is Ngô Đình Diệm. We gotta get the full picture—what makes him tick, his strengths and weaknesses—and we gotta hit him first. He just got here, he hasn't cemented his power yet. 'Strike first, strike hard.' I'm putting you on Savani's ass. Stick to him like glue and dig up every single detail about the Ngô family."

Năm Tài spent his days at Lieutenant Colonel Savani's office. Every evening he'd come back with a full report on everything Bảy Viễn wanted to know about the man who was the enemy of National Chief Bảo Đại—and the damn irony was, under American pressure, the Former Emperor had to appoint him Prime Minister.

Bảy Viễn, the Bình Xuyên Boss

October 10, 1954. The French rolled up their flag and pulled out of Hà Nội. Trailing the French army were tens of thousands of civilians fleeing south. American warships shuttled refugees back and forth like shuttles on a loom. French military planes hauled out their civil servants' families and military personnel who'd worked for them. There were plenty of deadly accidents—women with kids on their shoulders wading through muddy water, chasing after evacuation boats.

First thing Premier Diệm did was grab land for the migrating Catholics to settle down and get their lives together. After that, Diệm started squeezing the opposition factions. He followed the old playbook his mentors taught him: stick out the carrot to bribe the sect leaders. If they took the deal, great. If not, out came the stick—beat people into line.

Only then did the Cao Đài and Hòa Hảo realize they should've thought twice before cozying up to the French army. But regrets come too late.

Diệm had American advisors breathing down his neck the whole time. The main one was Intelligence Colonel Edward Lansdale—a specialist in "coaching" big shots angling for the presidency. Right from the start, Lansdale had been sent by the U.S. to be Diệm's "military consultant."

The kicker? Lansdale had actually opposed picking Diệm as Prime Minister. Unclassified files show Lansdale thought the decision was straight-up nuts. But the U.S. government went with Diệm anyway because he was the "adopted son" of Senator Mike Mansfield—one of the most powerful men in Washington at the time.

Two messengers—Bill Gigson and David Banh—were sent to Cannes to inform Bảo Đại about this decision.

Bảo Đại didn't agree. He tried to buy time, begging French Minister of Associated States Letourneau for help. But Letourneau just shrugged: "You've got your independence now—it's your call."

Bảo Đại fought the decision to pick Diệm as Prime Minister.

But the U.S. leaned hard on the French government: "You lost the war, and you lost half of Vietnam. We've pumped in way too much money and weapons to save this situation. If you can't hold South Vietnam together, then step the hell aside and don't get in our way while we back Diệm."

So Prime Minister Bidault ordered Letourneau to tell Bảo Đại: "There's no other way. You've gotta sign the appointment decree for Ngô Đình Diệm."

That's how Bảo Đại ended up bringing Diệm back as Prime Minister.

Advisor Lansdale personally handled the "soul-winning" of the sect leaders.

The first guy Lansdale reached out to was Trịnh Minh Thế, commander of the Cao Đài Liên Minh faction in Bến Cầu, Tây Ninh. Thế had long cut ties with the Tây Ninh Holy See, fought independently, and leaned pro-American.

Lansdale used a Deuxième Bureau agent named Nghĩa as the go-between. Meeting Thế in Bến Cầu, Lansdale went through an interpreter chain: Nghĩa translated Vietnamese into French, then Captain Redink translated French into English for

Bảy Viễn, the Bình Xuyên Boss

Lansdale. The bargaining went all the way up to two million dollars.

Thế agreed to pull 2,500 Cao Đài soldiers out of the alliance and join Premier Ngô Đình Diệm's government. That was February 13, 1955.

Lieutenant Colonel Savani tipped off Bảy Viễn that Diệm was dead set on wiping out the religious sects and the Đại Việt Party in Central Vietnam. The French ordered Bảy Viễn to stand up a National Total Force Front, gathering the sects—Cao Đài, Hòa Hảo, Catholics—and the Bình Xuyên together to fight Diệm. They called it the Cao-Thiên-Hòa-Bình alliance for short.

Back then, the Tây Ninh Cao Đài had an army under General Nguyễn Thành Phương that was ten times bigger than Trịnh Minh Thế's Cao Đài Liên Minh force, and also ten times bigger than Bảy Viễn's Bình Xuyên troops. But Bảy Viễn was still the big cheese in the National Total Force Front because the Bình Xuyên army had a reputation as stone-cold fighters—three years living in Việt Minh territory meant they'd been in plenty of blazing firefights with the French army.

Later on, the Đại Việt forces in Central Vietnam—who were getting slaughtered by Diệm—also begged to join the National Total Force Front. But the real scary muscle behind the Cao-Thiên-Hòa-Bình was the French army, now under four-star General Ely, who was both High Commissioner and Commander-in-Chief.

The balance of power between the Unified Total Force Front and Diệm's army was ridiculously lopsided. Diệm only had two Nùng battalions—mercenaries who'd fight for whoever paid top dollar. But behind Diệm stood America, with aid money piling up so high it'd make your eyes pop. And scariest of all was advisor Lansdale, who went by the nickname "president maker." Magsaysay of the Philippines was his handiwork. Lansdale had already bought Trịnh Minh Thế, and he was eyeing Nguyễn Thành Phương. The Americans bought Phương for 3.6 million U.S. dollars—1.6 million more than Trịnh Minh Thế, since the Tây Ninh Cao Đài had 25,000 men. As for Năm Lửa—real name Trần Văn Soái—they bought him for 1 million for himself and another 1 million for the Hòa Hảo army in Cái Vồn. These numbers were leaked by journalist John Osborne in the British weekly Life magazine on May 13, 1957.

As for the Bình Xuyên, Lansdale authorized General O'Daniel to head over to the headquarters at the Y-bridge to negotiate. This buyout was a real bitch because Bảy Viễn was both the leader of the Cao-Thiên-Hòa-Bình Front and Bảo Đại's sworn brother.


---


# The Tripod Situation

The Tripod Situation

by Nguyên Hùng

The day the Geneva Accords took effect, Bình Xuyên soldiers fired their guns like fireworks to celebrate: "No more dying?" That's what they all shouted. Years spent holed up in bunkers along Route 15 from Sài Gòn to Vũng Tàu, getting picked off or shelled by the Việt Minh, living like dogs, scared shitless day and night. Some guys were so broken they'd say "dying would be better." So now, suddenly getting a ceasefire, no more dodging bullets and shrapnel—what joy could beat that?

Lieutenant Colonel Tư Hiểu talked it over with Bảy Viễn about bringing in the Thành Tôn opera troupe to entertain the troops. First, to hold a ceremony at the communal house, praying for the souls of Bình Xuyên soldiers who'd died in the war years. Second, to enjoy the peace.

The artists of the Thành Tôn opera troupe could never forget those three nights performing at Bình Xuyên headquarters. They put on the show in a huge hall that could hold 500 people. Up front, instead of the fancy seats you'd find in a regular theater, they set up writing desks and armchairs—one table per big shot. On each table sat a bottle of Martell or Cognac, a bottle of soda, and two glasses, big and small. The big one for drinking your liquor, the small one for doing shooters.

Whenever a scene called for a drinking toast, Tư Hiểu would grab the Martell bottle and the little shot glass, hop up on stage, snatch the prop right out of the actor's hand, pour a full glass of booze, and shove it at 'em:
"Those props your stage managers set up are fake. This here's the real deal. Drink up! Bottoms down!"

Poor actors. Martell downed in shots—how the hell were they supposed to sing and perform? Singing off-key, the orchestra missing every beat. The whole night turned into a damn circus.

After the show, they'd stay for chicken congee with mung beans, and then another round of drinking. This time it was serious drinking, going as hard as your tolerance allowed. Half-drunk, Tư Hiểu would order his men to haul out crates of guns and hand them out to every actor and actress—one rifle each—to fire off in celebration of peace. Poor actresses, squeezing the trigger with one hand and covering their ears, shaking like a leaf.

Decades later, the Thành Tôn performers still talked about those three nights entertaining the Bình Xuyên.

Those gangster types sure had a unique way of enjoying stage performances. But you gotta give 'em credit for their hearts: they partied with everything they had, they poured drinks with everything they had, and the envelopes they handed out were stuffed fat.

One day a politician came looking to meet General Lê Văn Viễn. He handed over his business card: "Trịnh Khánh Vàng, former Deputy Zone Commander of Zone 9."

Bảy Viễn tried to recall not-too-distant memories. The guest introduced himself:
"Back in the resistance days, I was a colleague of the Major General. You were Deputy Zone Commander of Zone 7, I was Deputy Zone Commander of Zone 9. I'm Trịnh Khánh Vàng..."

Bảy Viễn nodded, shook his hand again:
"Yeah, I remember now! I had a close buddy named Bảy Trấn, who was Political Commissar down there. You're looking for me today—must be something worth discussing?"

Trịnh Khánh Vàng smiled politely:
"That's right. The Geneva Accords split the country in two. Not just the old resistance fighters are pissed off—

Bảy Viễn, the Bình Xuyên Boss

even the folks in the liberated zones are talking shit. Our beautiful mountains and rivers suddenly torn in half—who wouldn't be furious? We gotta take a stand."

"A stand about what?"

"Things are gonna turn bad for us. The Americans brought Ngô Đình Diệm back as Prime Minister. They'll push Bảo Đại and the pro-French crowd out. They won't implement the Geneva Accords, they won't reunify the country. The South is gonna become American territory—part of the 'Free World,' a U.S. puppet."

Bảy Viễn was shaken by the picture Trịnh Khánh Vàng painted.

"Can you explain more?"

Trịnh Khánh Vàng continued:
"If the Major General wants to hear it, this afternoon scholar Hồ Hữu Tường will come here himself to lay out the world and domestic situation for you. Mr. Tường is the head of our group."

At two in the afternoon, a car brought Hồ Hữu Tường to the Bình Xuyên assembly hall. Bảy Viễn and his general staff gave the number one scholar of the South a grand welcome.

Up on the podium, Hồ Hữu Tường spoke at length about the tripod situation, mesmerising the whole audience:

"We're standing at a boiling point. France is getting consumed by the North African battlefield day and night, so they'll hand over the South to the Americans to free themselves up for suppressing the independence movement back home. The U.S. didn't sign the Geneva Accords, so they're not obligated to hold general elections to reunify the two halves. Vietnam will be split permanently. Our South will forever be an outpost of the 'Free World' under American control. That's the big picture..."

Taking a sip of water, the scholar continued:
"Now here's what's relevant to us. I'm talking about the sect issue. France was weak, so they propped up the sects as allies to fight the Việt Minh. But America is strong, so they don't need to share power with anybody. They'll wipe out the sects first, and then they'll go after the Việt Minh—what they now call the Việt Cộng. That's America's strategic and tactical playbook for the coming years. So what should our stance be? When I say 'our,' I mean the sects. Right now, on the chessboard, we've got three forces fighting for power. One: America through Diệm. Two: the Việt Cộng, who're going underground. Three: the sects—Bình Xuyên, Hòa Hảo, Cao Đài. France is as good as beaten; for now they're just hanging around the South keeping order and enforcing the Geneva Accords, but in reality they've already sold out to the Americans.

So what I'm saying is this: we're in a tripod situation right now, just like the Three Kingdoms story."


---


Already Bought Everyone ... Only Bình Xuyên Left

Hồ Hữu Tường rubbed his hands together, pacing back and forth on the stage, and boomed out:

"Here I figure everybody's read that Chinese epic at least a couple times. You know the one — Romance of the Three Kingdoms. Tells the story back in China when there was three kingdoms: Wei under Tào Tháo, Shu under Lưu Bị, and Wu under Tôn Quyền. What made those three kingdoms special was they all had strategists. They didn't just fight with armies — they fought with schemes. Schemes are a hundred times more dangerous than weapons. Like working folks say, 'One man who knows how to plan is worth nine who just do the grunt work.'

Tào Tháo's strategist was Tư Mã Ý, Lưu Bị's was Khổng Minh, and Tôn Quyền's adviser was Châu Du. Winning or losing all came down to these three strategists. And their playbooks were identical: ally with one side to hit the other, then turn around and link up with that side to hit the first one. In soccer terms, it's two-on-one. Dead simple, but it works like a charm..."

Now let's shift over to our situation: Right now we got our own "three kingdoms." One is USA-Diệm, two is Việt Cộng, and three is us, the religious sects. Việt Cộng's keeping their powder dry for now, respecting the Geneva Accords. So the sects gotta figure out who to ally with to fight USA-Diệm. Naturally, our Việt Cộng brothers are quietly backing us. Our big ally right now is France. Nine years of fighting, and in the end they got kicked out of the game — France is hurting bad. America claims to be France's ally, gave 'em money and weapons, but really all they wanted was to keep France weak in Indochina so America could jump in later. That's how it goes between empires — they hate each other's guts. So France hates America bad. America brought Diệm back to Vietnam to push Bảo Đại out and push France out too. So of course France will back us if we've got the guts to stand up to Diệm. And standing up to Diệm means standing up to America.

Bảy Viễn's ears perked up when he heard France was backing Bảo Đại:

"Is this just guesswork, or do you got hard proof?"

Hồ Hữu Tường was confident:

"I'm basing my argument on concrete evidence. Our friend Trịnh Khánh Vàng here's gonna share some good news."

Trịnh Khánh Vàng jumped up, all excited:

"A friend of ours — name stays confidential — is giving the Major General a radio station so we can trade fire with Radio Saigon. I also managed to talk Văn Thiệt, a top-notch announcer, into leaving Radio Saigon to join us. Pretty soon the Ngô family clan's gonna hear Văn Thiệt dish it out from their great-great-granddaddy all the way down to Ngô Đình Khả."

Hồ Hữu Tường's speech was the kickoff for a parade of big-shot politicians joining the Bình Xuyên forces. The lineup included scholar Hồ Hữu Tường, then Trần Văn Ân, followed by Trịnh Khánh Vàng, and then Jean Baptiste Đồng, a wheeler-dealer who knew his way around the black market.

Bảy Viễn and his inner circle couldn't hide their joy.

For a long time the Bình Xuyên boys felt self-conscious about their lack of education, always coming up short against those bourgeois intellectual types. But now, suddenly, with Hồ Hữu Tường's group flocking to their banner, Bình Xuyên couldn't be called a bunch of empty-headed dummies anymore.

Preparations for going to war with USA-Diệm kicked into high gear.

Trịnh Khánh Vàng officially took charge of the Bình Xuyên radio station. French military engineers stashed the equipment on a barge so it could move around the canals when the fighting broke out on land. Trần Văn Ân handled war bulletins to broadcast Bình Xuyên's victories. Hồ Hữu Tường and Jean Baptiste Đồng made contact with the other sects to push toward forming a Unified National Front against Ngô Đình Diệm's dictatorship.

With Diệm threatening to wipe them out, the sects rushed to join the Unified National Front against the Ngô clan. The Bình Xuyên radio station ran day and night, blasting curses at the Ngô family while singing their own praises, hinting darkly that they had powerful allies backing them.

When Diệm crushed the Đại Việt groups in Central Vietnam, those groups fled south and joined the National Full-Force Front, which boosted the Front's reputation and scared the Ngô family. American adviser Lansdale tried to calm things down:

"The USA's 'carrot and stick' strategy has never failed. In the current situation, Việt Cộng's gone underground, buried their weapons, and gone to ground — respecting the Geneva Accords they never even signed. Us, we never signed from the start, and we advised the Republic of Vietnam not to sign either. So we got no obligation to follow those accords. As for the sects, we just peel 'em off one by one and buy 'em off. Colonel Thế already swallowed two million dollars. So we've split that Liên Minh group away from Tây Ninh. That Nguyễn Thành Phương character with his 25,000 troops — he sold his soul for 3.6 million dollars. Then Năm Lửa took a million for himself and another million for his Hòa Hảo boys in Cái Vồn. So we've already bought both Cao Đài and Hòa Hảo. Only Bình Xuyên left."


---


Into Independence Palace

Diệm once told Lansdale:

"It's Bình Xuyên and that Bảy Viễn fella who worry me most."

Lansdale smiled, full of confidence:

"I've handed the buying job over to General O Daniel. Don't know how he's coming along. How about we invite Bảy Viễn into the Palace and test the waters?"

Ngô Đình Diệm came back to Saigon to take power on July 7, 1954, right when Vietnam was at its most explosive. France had just fallen at Điện Biên Phủ, and the conference on Vietnam was underway in Geneva, Switzerland.

Diệm lamented: "I came back to a Vietnam the way France was in the time of Joan of Arc."

But Diệm was a sharp politician.

On the Geneva Conference, Diệm saw ahead that Vietnam would be divided for a long time, so he ordered Foreign Minister Dr. Trần Văn Đỗ, head of the "State of Vietnam" negotiating team, to issue this final statement: "The State of Vietnam reserves for itself the right to act freely to protect the sacred right of the Vietnamese people to territorial unity, national independence, and freedom."

When he took the Prime Minister's seat, Diệm used people loyal to his faction and pushed out the pro-French crowd.

Right from the start, Diệm ran into opposition from the religious sects. The sects' power base was the army France had built, led by General Nguyễn Văn Hinh, son of former Prime Minister Nguyễn Văn Tâm — both father and son were pro-French.

Following adviser Lansdale's advice, Diệm took the initiative and ordered General Hinh to go to France. Hinh not only refused the order but started planning a coup.

Lansdale saved Diệm's skin by cabling President Magsaysay to invite General Hinh's staff officers on a week-long pleasure trip to the Philippines. That's how the coup got scuttled. This was mid-September 1955. After that, Lansdale moved to buy off Generals Trịnh Minh Thế and Nguyễn Thành Phương, then bought General Trần Văn Soái (Năm Lửa) too.

But Bảy Viễn was a hard buy because he was tight with Chief of State Bảo Đại.

American journalist Robert Shaplen swore Bảo Đại had sold the exclusive rights to run Đại Thế Giới, Kim Chung, and Bình Khang casinos for 40 million piasters — chump change, barely 80 days of Đại Thế Giới's take.

Since Bảy Viễn was hard to buy, Diệm played a higher card: he invited Cao Đài to send two representatives into the government. Hòa Hảo also got two seats in Prime Minister Ngô Đình Diệm's cabinet. But these four ministerial posts were all title and no power — all the real authority stayed in the Ngô family's hands. Even so, by showing favor to Cao Đài and Hòa Hảo, Diệm wanted everyone to see that the Ngô clan had isolated Bảy Viễn's Bình Xuyên.

Following his strategist Lansdale's advice, at the end of December 1954, Diệm reluctantly invited Bảy Viễn into Independence Palace. Bình Xuyên's command staff worried it was a trap Diệm had set to snatch their leader, but Bảy Viễn insisted on meeting Diệm:

"If somebody invites you and you don't show, that means you're scared. And don't forget, behind Bình Xuyên stands General Paul Ély, France's Commander-in-Chief and High Commissioner in Indochina."

So Bảy Viễn got in the car and headed to Independence Palace.

Năm Tài immediately called Lieutenant Colonel Savani to let General Ély know, in case Diệm kidnapped Bảy Viễn and held him inside the Palace.

When he arrived, Bảy Viễn could feel the hostile atmosphere. The security officers at the Prime Minister's residence were armed to the teeth, staring at the Bình Xuyên leader like they wanted to jump him and bite his head off.

Bảy Viễn laughed contemptuously, making it clear "I walked in here, so I'm treating you all like dirt."

When he met Diệm, Bảy Viễn said:

"The Prime Minister invited me here, must be something important?"

Diệm:

"The Geneva Accords split our country in two. The North has fallen to the Communists — we can't let them swallow the South too. To fight Communism, we need to unite the religious sects. Cao Đài and Hòa Hảo have already agreed to send their troops to strengthen the Army of the Republic of Vietnam. What about your Bình Xuyên?"

Bảy Viễn spoke calmly:

"Fighting for our country's independence has always been the goal of the Bình Xuyên army. We fought the Việt Minh the same as we fought the French. We've got our own way of fighting, one that fits the times, the terrain, and the people. If the Prime Minister wants to merge us into the Republican army, I'm afraid the Bình Xuyên troops won't fight as effectively as before. Better for the Prime Minister to do what the French did."

Diệm wasn't much of a diplomat, so when Bảy Viễn gave him a sideways answer that dodged the question, it got on his nerves.

The meeting ended fast, going nowhere.

In less than thirty minutes, Bảy Viễn was out of Independence Palace.

Not willing to lose, twenty days later Diệm had General O Daniel go to Bảy Viễn's headquarters to try again. But this time things had changed. The Unified National Front had put on heavy pressure — four Cao Đài and Hòa Hảo ministers had resigned, demanding Diệm change a government that was way too nepotistic. Saigon papers called it the sects' ultimatum, forcing Diệm to overhaul his administration within five days.

Ngô Đình Nhu immediately organized the National Revolutionary Civil Servant movement to back the government, while also working on America to increase aid money for their operations.

At the start of 1955, Diệm dealt Bình Xuyên a death blow: he ordered the Đại Thế Giới entertainment palace closed. Bảy Viễn's — and Bảo Đại's — practically endless cash cow was suddenly cut off.

Immediately, Bảy Viễn flew to France to meet Bảo Đại and figure out their next move.


---


Who Killed General Trịnh Minh Thế?

General O Daniel was sent by the US President to Saigon to help Ngô Đình Diệm whip the army into shape. General O Daniel backed Ngô Đình Diệm one hundred percent, whether Diệm was right or wrong.

When he met Bảy Viễn, General O Daniel got straight to the point:

"We need to settle the sect problem quick and clean."

Bảy Viễn shook his head:

"We got to be thorough. Wipe out the sects carelessly and you'll push 'em into a corner — civil war's gonna be unavoidable. And they might just jump over to the Communists."

O Daniel couldn't convince Bảy Viễn, even though he made the effort to come back several times.

By the time Diệm shut down Đại Thế Giới, both Bảy Viễn and Bảo Đại were dead set on fighting Diệm.

Bình Xuyên only had three battalions, but the only ones worth a damn in a fight were Mười Lực's and Bảy Môn's — the other two, Thái Hoàng Minh's and Tư Hiểu's, were just parade soldiers. To beef up Bình Xuyên's forces, Cao Đài loaned them one battalion, Năm Lửa sent one battalion, and General Hinh chipped in one paratroop battalion. All together, the National Full-Force Front's military strength came to six battalions, and they marched out with real swagger.

Bảo Đại was determined to take out Ngô Đình Diệm before Diệm could turn on him.

On May 9, 1955, Bảo Đại cabled Diệm, summoning him to France, while handing the post of Commander-in-Chief of the armed forces to General Nguyễn Văn Vỹ.

Diệm asked his patron Lansdale for advice and was told to ignore Bảo Đại's order and move ahead with plans to bring Bảo Đại down. General J. Lawton Collins, assigned by the US President to stay by Diệm's side, didn't like Diệm — their personalities clashed. He saw Diệm had no popular support and his government was just the Ngô family clique. So Collins agreed with General Ély that he'd go back to the US, file his report, and ask for a replacement. While Collins was in the US, the fighting had already broken out in Saigon.

Months earlier, Việt Minh had seen the tension brewing between Diệm and Bình Xuyên and dispatched cadres to help. After the 1954 Geneva Accords, two cadres named Bảy Khánh and Chín Đạo were assigned to make contact with Mười Lực, Bảy Môn, and Năm Chàng to pull Bảy Viễn's Bình Xuyên onto their path. Năm Yên, an intelligence cadre, linked up with Bảy Môn and persuaded him to hand over one company from his 3rd Battalion to Ba Chậm's command. Ba Chậm's company would be the fuse that would blow the conflict between the Ngô clan and Bình Xuyên wide open.

The tension between the two sides hit the breaking point. Bảy Viễn ordered Bảy Môn to fire first. The plan to hit Diệm had been carefully discussed. All the bridges leading to Chánh Hưng Island were secretly rigged with explosives — when the time came, they'd just flip the switch. On the afternoon of April 28, Bảy Môn opened up with mortars, targeting Independence Palace and the police stations of Mai Hữu Xuân, who'd just flipped to Diệm's side.

Minutes later, Diệm's forces returned fire, targeting Chánh Hưng. The Nùng soldiers attacked the bridgeheads. As for the Tân Thuận bridge, Diệm handed that to Trịnh Minh Thế's Cao Đài Liên Minh forces.

Bảy Môn hit the switch and blew the bridges. Only the Nhị Thiên Đường bridge stayed standing.

Things kept getting worse. The Nùng troops made it across the Chánh Hưng area in those final days — Diệm had bought off some units, boosting his forces to four battalions. The operation commander for wiping out Bình Xuyên was Lieutenant Colonel Dương Văn Minh, who'd transferred from the French army to the Army of the Republic of Vietnam and got a special promotion to Colonel.

They had to slug it out for four days before Dương Văn Minh's troops could cross the Chánh Hưng area and break into Bảy Viễn's headquarters. Bình Xuyên forces pulled back to the Tân Thuận bridge — the bridgehead for retreating down to Rừng Sác.

The Tân Thuận bridge front was the toughest. Wave after wave of charges across the bridge got stopped cold. The worst were the Bình Xuyên riverboats raking the bridge with heavy machine-gun fire. Then Bảy Môn's mortars zeroed in on General Thế's formation and hammered away.

Then came news that shook everyone: Trịnh Minh Thế was hit. An American journalist described Thế's death. Standing on his command jeep, Thế's head dropped and he collapsed. A bullet had hit him right in the head. In his pocket was an uncashed check for 70 million đồng. Who killed Trịnh Minh Thế? Diệm was flustered — if someone who'd come over to his side died like that, so mysteriously, who'd ever dare come over to him again? As for Nhu, he was delighted — the money America paid to buy Thế was American money, and the cash in Thế's pocket would end up in the Ngô family's coffers.

Bảy Viễn miscalculated. He'd been dead certain the French army would back him, but France couldn't do a thing — they were scared America would cut their aid. Even General Ély was powerless when he called Diệm to urge caution about attacking Bình Xuyên. Intelligence reports said that on April 28, 1955, the phone in the High Commissioner's office rang. Ély picked up. Diệm's panicked voice reported that Bình Xuyên had opened fire on Independence Palace. One person dead, many wounded. Diệm announced he was sending the full army to wipe out Bình Xuyên. Ély urged Diệm to think it over, not to drag the country into the fires of war. But Diệm hung up. The battle exploded ferociously. On the government side, initial casualties were 26 dead and 152 wounded, but Diệm's forces held the city. The fighting lasted five days. The losing Bình Xuyên forces gathered at Tân Thuận, boarded boats and ships, and retreated into Rừng Sác. Mười Lực and Bảy Môn were both hit by bullets. France secretly put them in Grall Hospital (Đồn Đất) for treatment. A few days later, Diệm's men got wind of it, but Mười Lực and Bảy Môn had already escaped to Rừng Sác to rejoin their comrades. Bảy Viễn was overjoyed to see his two battle buddies again.

Retreating to Rừng Sác was Bình Xuyên's big mistake: drinking water was scarce, they were surrounded for days, their reserves ran dry, causing hardship for thousands of soldiers. On top of that, mortars from naval warships pounded them, and without solid fortifications, the infantry took heavy losses.

In that desperate situation, two Việt Minh cadres, Bảy Khánh and Chín Đạo, bravely cut through the jungle to find Bảy Môn and showed him their only way out: ally with the Eastern Zone troops (these soldiers hadn't regrouped to the North and had the mission of protecting the people when Diệm refused to implement the Geneva Accords). Bảy Môn happily reported to Bảy Viễn, and Bảy Viễn immediately sent Bảy Môn to invite two senior Việt Minh representatives for a meeting. Knowing that Tài and Sang wouldn't approve of shaking hands with the Communists, Bảy Viễn kept them out of this important meeting.

Bảy Khánh and Chín Đạo were stunned when Bảy Môn led them onto a brand-new barge France had given Bảy Viễn to use as his command post in Rừng Sác. At Bảy Viễn's side was Trịnh Khánh Vàng, the man running the Bình Xuyên radio station that blasted the Ngô family day and night.

Bảy Khánh got straight to the point:

"Representing the Vietnam Liaison Committee, we've been tracking the activities of the Cao-Thiên-Hòa-Bình National Full-Force Front, where Bình Xuyên is the locomotive. Under the Geneva Accords, we can't openly send troops to help you, Brother Bảy, but we can give you two outstanding military cadres — Ba Thu and Tư Thước. Brother Ba Thu will be the political officer for Brother Bảy Môn's 3rd Battalion, and Brother Tư Thước will handle communications between you and us. And if Diệm's forces surround you for too long and your rations run dry, he'll lead Bình Xuyên troops up to the Eastern Zone."

The two sides drafted a joint statement, basically declaring that the Bình Xuyên army belonged to the people and its mission was to fight the American imperialists and their lackey Ngô Đình Diệm.

Ba Thu and Tư Thước arrived just in the nick of time.

Diệm launched Operation Hoàng Diệu to blast Bình Xuyên out of Rừng Sác. All six of Bình Xuyên's battalions were stuck in a ring of encirclement. Soldier morale sank under the relentless artillery barrages. Bình Xuyên's weak spot was that a ton of soldiers' wives and kids had followed along, making it hard to fight.

The three paratroop, Hòa Hảo, and Cao Đài battalions that had come to reinforce Bình Xuyên weren't used to real combat — their first time out they faced such brutal firepower they dropped their guns and ran. To straighten out the troops' morale, Bảy Môn dragged Major Bay, the Cao Đài battalion commander, before a military council for court-martial.

Tư Thước stepped in to plead:

"The Cao Đài army's been around a long time but they lack battle experience. This fight was way too brutal. The soldiers weren't used to the Rừng Sác battlefield, panicked and ran — Major Bay couldn't hold 'em no matter what. Please give Major Bay a chance to redeem himself."

The ring of American-Diệm naval warships kept tightening. Bình Xuyên soldiers ran out of drinking water and had to eat dried rice. Morale sank lower by the day.

Ba Thu talked it over with Bảy Môn:

"It's time we cut through the jungle and move the troops up to the Eastern Zone. The situation's critical."

Bảy Môn suggested to Bảy Viễn that the 3rd Battalion blast a bloody path up to Phú Mỹ as a springboard to move the whole force into the Eastern war zone.

Bảy Viễn hesitated because his two trusted Lại-family advisers disagreed.


---


# All These Damn Fools Meeting All Day Long

All These Damn Fools Meeting All Day Long

The hushed back-and-forth between Bay Vien and those salon politicians — Ho Huu Tuong, Tran Van An, Trinh Khanh Vang — was driving the Lai brothers up the wall. Ever since they'd fled to the Rừng Sác mangrove forest, Bay Vien stopped asking for their input altogether, spending all his time whispering and scheming with that bunch of noodle-and-pork-belly politicos. And honestly? It made sense. The Lai brothers were French pawns, and France had already packed up and gone home — couldn't do jack for the Bình Xuyên anymore. Bay Vien had to look elsewhere for help.

When they caught wind that Bay Vien was reaching out to the Việt Minh, those two Phòng Nhì agents saw red. Didn't dare go after Bay Vien himself, so Nam Tài aimed his venom at Bay Môn instead:

"What the hell are you thinking, moving the 3rd Battalion up into the jungle? You planning to ally with the Việt Minh? You forget we nearly died at their hands a few years back?"

Bay Môn shot back:

"Who the hell are you to interfere with my command? What, should the 3rd Battalion just sit here with our arms folded and let ourselves get slaughtered in this encirclement like you two? I'm cutting a blood trail back to Phú Mỹ so we can get every last Bình Xuyên man out of this trap, whether you like it or not. I've got Brother Bay's blessing on this."

Nam Tài ran straight to Bay Vien.

Bay Vien confirmed it:

"I gave Bay Môn the green light to take the 3rd Battalion through the jungle to Phú Mỹ. We've got to break this siege somehow. France can't help us anymore — we've got to save our own skins."

Nam Tài was shaking:

"But Brother Bay, have you forgotten how the Việt Minh tried to screw us over before?"

Bay Vien just laughed:

"How could I forget? But times change. Right now Diệm's got a knife to our throats, and the Việt Minh are sending people to pull us out of this ring. Only a fool wouldn't take that chance. What happens between us down the road — we'll sort that out when we get there."

Bay Vien's logic was solid as a rock, but those two Phòng Nhì boys were terrified. They knew damn well that the death sentence for traitors who sold out to the French colonialists was still sitting in the files of the communist security squads — the same ones who'd wasted Cò Bazin.

While Bay Môn was cutting through the jungle with the 3rd Battalion heading for Phú Mỹ, Lieutenant Colonel Chiêu — commander of the Biên Hòa sector — hit them hard. But the 3rd Battalion knew that terrain like the back of their hand: Bàu Bông, Vũng Gấm, Phước An, Long Thành. They fought like cornered tigers. When the enemy couldn't beat them, they tried to sweet-talk them into surrendering. Colonel Chiêu sent an emissary to Bay Môn with a letter from General Dương Văn Minh — freshly promoted to Major General for pushing the Bình Xuyên back into the mangroves — offering Bay Môn a deal: come back to Prime Minister Ngô Đình Diệm's side, get all his property returned, and a promotion to Major General, just like Trịnh Minh Thế.

Big mistake bringing up Trịnh Minh Thế. Everybody knew Thế had sold out to the Americans for two million dollars and never got to spend a single cent of it before he dropped dead under mysterious circumstances. Lead car breaks down, the one behind it swerves.

Bay Môn sent word back:

"I'm just a soldier. The general says fight, I fight. Don't waste your time coming back with offers."

Couldn't buy Bay Môn off, so General Minh threw everything he had at them, pounding the 3rd Battalion so hard they had to pull back into the swamps.

Diệm made a huge mistake getting trigger-happy with the religious sects. Before he'd even finished off the Bình Xuyên, he started in on the Cao Đài and the Hòa Hảo. On May 25, 1955, Operation Thoại Ngọc Hầu rolled west to crush the Hòa Hảo. Major General Dương Văn Minh got tapped for command — Diệm trusted him that much. This time around, they threw fifty battalions into the fight.

The campaign dragged on for two months and got nowhere. Taking on the Hòa Hảo was a whole different ball game than the Bình Xuyên. First off, the Hòa Hảo army under Năm Lửa (Trần Văn Soái) and Ba Cụt (Lê Quang Vinh) was ten times bigger than the Bình Xuyên. Second, the western battlefields were wide open — nothing like the cramped mangroves of Rừng Sác. Third, the Hòa Hảo faithful fed and sheltered their own. So the American-Diệm forces got bogged down in the Mekong Delta mud.

Then out of nowhere: Thái Hoàng Minh up and left Sài Gòn, ran straight into Rừng Sác, and begged Bay Vien to forgive him. Bay Vien's heart softened — the guy was his nephew by marriage after all — so he let it slide. But the Bình Xuyên soldiers weren't about to forgive a man who'd gone over to the enemy and stabbed them in the back. It was Thái Hoàng Minh who'd pocketed American money and then failed to blow up the Nhị Thiên Đường bridge, letting the Nùng soldiers cross over to Chánh Hưng and putting the whole headquarters at the Chữ Y bridgehead in a world of hurt. The boss could forgive, but fate wouldn't. Thái Hoàng Minh got taken out, his body floated down the river. And that was the end of another traitor.

Even with the Americans and Diệm busy duking it out with the Hòa Hảo out west, giving the folks in Rừng Sác a little breathing room, everybody knew their number was up. Like fish trapped in a bamboo cage — they could pull you out anytime they wanted.

Bay Vien spent more and more time picking the brains of his advisors: Hồ Hữu Tường, Trần Văn Ân, Trịnh Khánh Vàng.

Nam Tài knew these opportunists inside out — sunshine patriots who'd shade whoever was on top. Scared they'd talk Bay Vien into running to the Việt Minh, Nam Tài fumed under his breath:

"All these damn fools meeting all day long."

The inner circle didn't care much for the Lai brothers, so they snitched: "Brother Nam's been talking smack about Brother Bay."

Already on edge and now hearing that his own trusted man was badmouthing him, Bay Vien flew into a rage and ordered Nam Tài arrested.

Nam Tài went white as a sheet, dropped to his knees, and kowtowed to Bay Vien like he was praying to the stars.

Tư Sang saw his brother's life hanging by a thread and dropped down beside him, begging for mercy.

Bay Vien saw the Lai brothers groveling and waved them off with a light scolding:

"I'm letting you bastards live. Remember: don't get cocky and talk shit about everyone around you being fools, or one day your bodies won't be found in one piece."


---


# Bay Vien Flees to France

Bay Vien Flees to France

While Ngô Đình Diệm's army was busy flushing out the Hòa Hảo in the Mekong Delta, Tư Thước talked it over with Bay Môn — time to cut through the jungle and break out one more time.

Bay Môn was all in. He talked it over with Mười Lực:

"You and me, brother — we've been through hell together, on the streets and in the resistance. I'm taking the 3rd Battalion out first. If things go smooth, I'll send word and come get you."

Mười Lực warned him:

"Be careful, hear? Don't let those two bastards Tài and Sang know about it. They've been sniffing around, watching me like hawks the last few days. Good luck. I'll be waiting day and night."

Thanks to Tư Thước — a man who knew the jungle like his own backyard — the 3rd Battalion blazed a trail through the woods all the way to Phú Mỹ without running into a single Diệm soldier. Bay Môn's first move was to send a runner back into the jungle immediately to bring Mười Lực's battalion up to the Eastern region. But bad luck — the enemy had caught wind of Bay Môn's escape route and locked down the area tight.

Meanwhile, the French artillery was pounding the Bình Xuyên positions hard.

Back when Bay Vien had spared the lives of the Tài-Sang brothers, plenty of people said he was too soft for not finishing off those two venomous snakes. But Bay Vien just smiled.

It wasn't time yet to cross those two Phòng Nhì agents off the list. They could still help Bay Vien slip the noose — by contacting Lieutenant Colonel Savani and getting the French military to intervene at the last minute.

Bay Vien's hunch was dead right.

Just as Diệm's troops were tightening the noose, ready to bag the entire Bình Xuyên command alive, Năm Tài's radio crackled to life.

Savani's voice came through crystal clear: "Mr. Tài and Mr. Sang — take the Major General with you, follow our men through the jungle to Phú Mỹ. There'll be a car waiting to take you to Bà Rịa."

And just like that, the three of them slipped through the eye of a needle. At Phú Mỹ, they crawled into a bunker the French had built years back. Tư Sang radioed the French in Vũng Tàu. The next morning, a French military jeep picked them up and drove them to Bà Rịa, where a helicopter flew them out to Vũng Tàu.

The French flew their three important puppets to Laos on a military plane, then from there to Paris on Air France.

On November 7, 1955, Bay Vien arrived in the French capital as quietly as any tourist. No official welcome — the French were keeping their support for the anti-Diệm sect forces under wraps.

So that was it. Bay Vien's fight against the Ngô tyranny had stretched on for just over half a year — from April 28 to November 7, 1955.

International observers rated Bay Vien higher than the salon politicians and the two other sects — the Cao Đài and the Hòa Hảo, most of whom flipped sides for CIA dollars. Bay Vien stayed true to his old patron, France. And that's why the French government — no matter which party held the prime minister's seat — felt obliged to keep Bay Vien fat and happy, along with ex-Emperor Bảo Đại. They were, after all, "good friends of France."

But Bay Vien had a roof over his head and food on the table without any peace of mind. His wives were still stuck back in Việt Nam, and he had to keep begging his French patrons for favors. Eventually, three of his official wives made it over to France to reunite with him: Mrs. Lúa — daughter of Councilor Đống from Đa Phước; Mrs. Hà Thị Tám — former accountant at the MIC tobacco company; and Mrs. Hoa. But what really kept Bay Vien up at night was that his son, Major Lê Paul, was trapped inside the encirclement and captured by Diệm. Every single Bình Xuyên general and every salon politician was rounded up, put on trial, and sentenced to Côn Đảo prison island. But Lê Paul — Ngô Đình Nhu had him separated and locked up in a special detention facility in Phú Lâm.

Why did Nhu single out Lê Paul? Bay Vien would only find out later from journalist Hilaire Du Berrier. During the final brutal phase of fighting — from September 20 to October 12, 1955 — the entire Bình Xuyên command was captured alive. When Diệm's forces seized Bay Vien's headquarters at Chánh Hưng, a squad led by Lieutenant Nguyễn Văn Tâm stumbled onto a hidden treasury. One soldier smashed his rifle butt through a plywood wall, and out tumbled brick-sized packages wrapped in paper. They picked them up and realized they were Bay Vien's silver bars — he hadn't had time to get them to the bank. The paratroopers stuffed their military jackets full. Lieutenant Nguyễn Văn Tâm naturally helped himself to the biggest share. He also bagged a pair of ivory tusks over a meter and a half long as a gift for Prime Minister Diệm. For this exploit, Tâm got promoted to Captain and appointed District Chief of Hốc Môn.

Ngô Đình Nhu seized every asset Bay Vien had in the banks. He was convinced Bay Vien had more hidden treasure stashed somewhere, so he used Lê Paul as a hostage to force Bay Vien to cough it up to save his son's life.

A father's love running deep, Bay Vien told Nhu everything — all the money in the banks, take it all, just give back Lê Paul. But Nhu was too greedy. He figured the bank money already belonged to the Republic of Việt Nam anyway. To ransom Lê Paul, Nhu wanted the treasure Bay Vien had cleverly hidden somewhere inside the country — except there wasn't any hidden treasure.

So the fate of Bay Vien's 27-year-old son was settled the old underworld way: On April 14, 1956, a police van drove Lê Paul out of the Phú Lâm station, headed toward Phú Định. Somewhere along the road, they shoved him out and shot him dead.


---


# Life Is But a Dream

Life Is But a Dream

A restless man who'd spent his whole life charging into hails of bullets, Bay Vien couldn't stomach the cushy existence of what the French political class called "inventory stock" — a figure kept on the shelf, well-fed and waiting, to be dusted off and trotted out whenever needed. Sitting around doing nothing, Bay Vien started thinking, and he realized his fate wasn't so different from his sworn brother, the ex-Emperor Bảo Đại. They were both "inventory stock." Only difference: Bảo Đại was sick to death of dancing and spinning on the political stage, while Bay Vien still had fire in his belly.

During his exile, Bay Vien's favorite pastime was sitting at a Paris sidewalk café, reading the newspapers and keeping tabs on what was happening back in Sài Gòn. Learning that his entire general staff had been captured alive and exiled to Côn Đảo, Bay Vien remembered his own glory days decades back — when he was in prison and still managed to befriend the 76-year-old jail warden's wife, turning her into his inside woman for prison breaks that gambled with the open sea and the storms. Gangster brothers washing up on prison islands was nothing new. What got to him was the salon politicians — Hồ Hữu Tường, Trần Văn Ân, Trịnh Khánh Vàng and the rest — all rotting away on the island.

The years slipped by. The Ngô family blazed hot and burned out fast — lording it over the country for only nine years, from 1954 to 1963. Then the coup came. Diệm and Nhu died inside an M113 armored personnel carrier on the road from Father Tam's church in Chợ Lớn back to Sài Gòn. And the youngest brother, Ngô Đình Cẩn, was executed by Nguyễn Khánh.

Bay Vien saw life as nothing but a dream — glory one day, disgrace the next. Nobody had strutted prouder than Ngô the Patriot, climbing from Prime Minister to President, knocking down Chief of State Bảo Đại with his American puppeteers pulling the strings from behind the curtain.

But here's the twist of fate: those same American puppeteers were the ones pulling the strings for the generals who turned against the Ngô family. The more he thought about it, the more Bay Vien believed his luck had held. He should've died at the hands of the Ngôs, but at the eleventh hour his French patrons reached out and saved his skin.

He was sitting in a café, yawning his head off, when a piece of news snapped him wide awake: America was shifting its whole strategy in Việt Nam — now backing a military junta. Back in the Ngô days, Washington's game plan was to wipe out the religious sects and run a monopoly on power. They'd failed. Now the Americans did a complete one-eighty — cozying up to the sects, trying to band together against the communists. Word came from Côn Đảo prisoners who'd been released — including some Bình Xuyên boys — that Trần Văn Ân had been recruiting Bình Xuyên officers to rebuild their army and help the military government fight the Reds.

Hearing this, Bay Vien was over the moon. He thought it over for a few days, then wrote a letter to President Lyndon Johnson, promising to secure Sài Gòn and Highway 15 connecting Sài Gòn to Vũng Tàu, just like in the old days. He mailed it off, hoping for a warm reply. Nothing came.

Seeing her husband pacing back and forth, sighing, Mrs. Hoa spoke up:

"In 1960 you wrote to President Eisenhower. Did he ever write you back? Now you're writing to President Johnson. Don't get your hopes up — you'll just be wasting your time."

Bay Vien snapped:

"How do you know they won't answer me?"

Mrs. Hoa said calmly:

"The Americans have to run it by their pet generals before they reply to your letter. And Dương Văn Minh and Nguyễn Ngọc Thơ — those are the men who served Ngô Đình Diệm when he crushed your Bình Xuyên. You think they'd ever let the Americans bring you back? Don't you see? And even if you did come back, who's left to help you fight the Việt Minh? Mười Lực and Năm Chẳng — after all those years of exile on Côn Đảo — they're worn out. Trần Văn Ân invited them to rebuild the Bình Xuyên army and they wouldn't even take the gig. If you ask me, they've got more sense than you. A man's time comes only once. That time is gone. You can't bring it back. Remember Brother Mười Trí?"

"What about Mười Trí?"

"Mười Trí was smarter than you. If you'd listened to him back then, you wouldn't have ended up broken and disgraced like you are today."

From that day on, Bay Vien lived with nothing but regret — gnawing at himself, mourning the time he'd spent in the Rừng Sác forest fighting alongside the Việt Minh fighters, tough and unbending. Mourning that he hadn't been as lucky as Bay Môn, who'd gotten the 3rd Battalion through to the Eastern region. If that breakout had gone through, Bay Vien's whole life would be different today. God — just one moment... and a man's whole life can swing from one extreme to the other.

In 1970, Bay Vien died an old man in Paris. Nobody gave a damn. Still, foreign journalists wrote down his story and his life for posterity.

References:
- "Bay Vien, le mai tre de Cholon" — Pierre Darcourt
- "Background to Betrayal" — Hilaire du Berrier
- "The Lost Revolution" — Robert Shaplen
- "La guerre d'Indochine" — Lucien Bodard
